{"course_id": "pm_to_controls_lead", "readings": [{"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From SAP Project Manager to Controls Lead"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Course Package README"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Eight-week bridge programme for an experienced SAP project manager who will lead an SAP controls project at an oil and gas company  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}]}, {"title": "1. What This Package Is", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "1. What This Package Is"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The package contains everything needed to run the course: the Course Guide, the bridge manuals for what a project manager lacks, the planning module and estimating model, the capstone, the assessments and the instructor materials, together with the core method materials and the Corvane Energy Group case file on which the course is built. Start with the Course Guide."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Fact", "Value"], ["Duration", "Eight weeks, about 85 hours, with a weekly 90-minute live session"], ["Audience", "SAP project and programme managers new to security, GRC and audit"], ["Outcome", "Ready to scope, plan, staff, run and defend an SAP controls project"], ["Cohort", "8 to 15 participants; one mentor per four"], ["Environment", "Excel and Word only; no SAP system access needed"]]}]}, {"title": "2. Folder Map", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "2. Folder Map"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Folder", "Contents", "Who uses it"], ["00_Start_Here", "Course Guide: purpose, modules, schedule, reading map, assessment and package status", "Everyone"], ["01_Bridge_Manuals", "Bridge Manual A (Modules 0 and 1); Bridge Manual B (Modules 2 and 3)", "Participants"], ["02_Planning_Module", "Module 9 manual and the Controls Project Estimating Model (Excel)", "Participants"], ["03_Capstone_Participant", "Module 10 capstone brief (Verrin Energy Partners)", "Participants, released in week 7"], ["04_Assessment_Participant", "Quizzes for Modules 5 to 8 and case reviews CR-5 to CR-8", "Participants"], ["05_Instructor_Only", "Instructor Guide and Answer Key; Capstone Assessor Guide; Capstone Scoring Sheet", "Instructors and assessors only: do not share"], ["06_Core_Materials", "Method Manual (five parts), Workbook Primer (three parts), Controls Lead Playbook, Corvane Story, Weekly Activity Plan, Deployment Guide, video outline", "Participants, as set by the reading map"], ["07_Corvane_Case_File", "The zipped Corvane case file: 14 populated workbooks, blank templates and the practice package (08_Practice_Workbooks)", "Participants"]]}]}, {"title": "3. Module Map", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3. Module Map"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Module", "Title", "Main file"], ["0", "Orientation and self-assessment", "Bridge Manual A"], ["1", "Controls and audit for the SAP project manager", "Bridge Manual A"], ["2", "SAP security and GRC without the jargon", "Bridge Manual B"], ["3", "Oil and gas on SAP", "Bridge Manual B"], ["4", "The workbook toolkit", "Workbook Primer Parts 1 to 3; practice package"], ["5", "The method: from foundation to test plan", "Method Manual Parts 1 and 2"], ["6", "Reading and directing the testing", "Method Manual Parts 3A and 3B"], ["7", "Evaluating, remediating and reporting", "Method Manual Part 4"], ["8", "Leading the team and the stakeholders", "Corvane Story; Controls Lead Playbook"], ["9", "Planning and estimating a new controls project", "Module 9 manual; Estimating Model"], ["10", "Capstone", "Capstone brief; Assessor Guide; Scoring Sheet"]]}]}, {"title": "4. How to Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "4. How to Start"}]}, {"title": "Participants", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Participants"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Read the Course Guide, then complete the self-assessment in its Appendix A."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Book the week 1 mentor call, then follow the eight-week schedule in the Course Guide."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Use the reading map in the Course Guide to decide what to read fully and what to skim."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Unzip the Corvane case file when you reach Module 4."}]}, {"title": "Instructors", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Instructors"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Read the Instructor Guide and the Capstone Assessor Guide. Keep the instructor folder separate from the participant folders."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Run the weekly sessions using the agendas in Part A of the Instructor Guide."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Mark CR-6 and CR-7 with the model answers; score the capstone with the Scoring Sheet."}]}, {"title": "5. Assessment and Certification", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "5. Assessment and Certification"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Element", "Weight"], ["Module quizzes (Modules 1 to 3 and the Module 4 Primer assessment)", "15%"], ["Practical (Find the Errors and the trace exercise)", "15%"], ["Case reviews (CR-6 and CR-7 scored)", "20%"], ["Planning pack (Modules 8 and 9)", "20%"], ["Capstone: written pack and steering presentation", "30%"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "To be certified: 75 percent overall, at least 70 percent in every element, a capstone total of 70 or more out of 100, and at least 21 of 30 on the capstone presentation. The Capstone Scoring Sheet includes a certification tracker that applies these rules."}]}, {"title": "6. Notes and Cautions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "6. Notes and Cautions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Corvane Energy Group, Redwater Pipeline and Terminals and Verrin Energy Partners are fictitious. All people and figures are invented."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The Estimating Model's unit efforts are illustrative planning assumptions calibrated to the Corvane case, not industry benchmarks. Replace them with your own history."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "SOX and similar requirements depend on a company's listing and size. Confirm the requirement with the client's finance and audit teams on any real project."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The instructor folder contains model answers. Release participant materials by week, and keep the capstone brief until week 7."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The video outline in the core materials covers the main method course; the new modules have no video outline yet."}]}, {"title": "7. Version", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "7. Version"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Package", "From SAP Project Manager to Controls Lead, version 0.1 (Draft)"], ["Date", "October 2026"], ["Format", "Word (.docx) for documents; Excel (.xlsx) for the estimating model, scoring sheet and workbooks"], ["Owner", "TIB Systems LLC"]]}]}], "word_count": 381, "id": "readme-pm-to-controls-lead-course", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/00_Start_Here/README_PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course.docx", "filename": "README_PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course.docx", "category": "Start Here", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From SAP Project Manager to Controls Lead"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Course Guide: an eight-week bridge programme for leading an SAP controls project at an oil and gas company"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Course design and curriculum map  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Built on the Corvane Energy Group case file, the five-part method manual, the workbook primer, the Controls Lead Playbook and the practice package."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "1. Purpose and Audience", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "1. Purpose and Audience"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "This course takes an experienced SAP project manager who is new to security, GRC and audit, and prepares them to lead an SAP controls project at an oil and gas company: to scope it, plan it, staff it, run it through its gates, direct the testing, evaluate the findings, manage the client and the auditors, and close it with a result the client can defend."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Who it is for", "What they already have", "What they lack"], ["SAP project and programme managers; delivery leads moving from implementation or support into assurance and controls", "Planning, budgeting, staffing, stakeholder and risk management; SAP module and landscape awareness; steering committee experience", "SOX and audit vocabulary; SAP security, GRC and change controls; how controls are tested and how deficiencies are evaluated; oil and gas control risks; the method and tools of a controls project"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["THE PRINCIPLE OF THE COURSE A controls lead does not need to test every control. A controls lead needs to know what good scoping, evidence and conclusions look like, to see when a team is cutting a corner, and to hold the line with the client and the auditor. The course therefore teaches lead-depth: enough to direct and challenge, not enough to replace the testers."]]}]}, {"title": "2. What You Will Be Able to Do", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "2. What You Will Be Able to Do"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "By the end you will be able to", "Assessed by"], ["1", "Explain SOX, ITGC, ITAC, ITDM, IPE, SoD, TOD and TOE to a CFO and to an SAP basis lead in each one's language", "Module 1 and 2 quizzes; capstone briefing"], ["2", "Describe an oil and gas SAP landscape and name the control risks that come with joint ventures, production volumes, payments and capital projects", "Module 3 quiz; capstone scoping"], ["3", "Open, read and check any of the 14 foundation workbooks and follow a key from a server to a conclusion", "Workbook practice and error hunt; trace exercise"], ["4", "Scope a controls project: systems, interfaces, processes, risks, controls and owners, with the gates and what each must show", "Module 5 exercises; capstone scoping pack"], ["5", "Direct testing without doing it: set the test order, sample and evidence rules, and read results critically", "Module 6 case reviews"], ["6", "Evaluate deficiencies, group them by cause, decide on reliance and report with a defensible denominator", "Module 7 exercises; capstone evaluation"], ["7", "Lead a team of 15 and a set of senior stakeholders through nine gates", "Module 8 scenarios; capstone plan"], ["8", "Plan, staff and estimate a new controls project and write the engagement conditions that protect it", "Module 9 plan; capstone plan"], ["9", "Present a project to a steering committee and defend it under challenge", "Capstone presentation"]]}]}, {"title": "3. Course Structure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3. Course Structure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The course has ten modules over eight weeks, about 85 hours of study and practice (around 11 hours a week alongside a day job). Modules 1 to 3 and 9 and 10 are new. Modules 4 to 8 reuse what we have already built, read at lead depth."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Module", "Title", "Hours", "Source", "Format"], ["0", "Orientation and self-assessment", "2", "New (Appendix A)", "Self-assessment; mentor call"], ["1", "Controls and audit for the SAP project manager", "7", "New", "Reading, short exercises, quiz"], ["2", "SAP security and GRC without the jargon", "7", "New", "Reading, walkthrough role-play, quiz"], ["3", "Oil and gas on SAP: landscape, processes and control risks", "6", "New plus Corvane W01 to W04", "Reading, workbook tour, quiz"], ["4", "The workbook toolkit", "10", "Workbook Primer Parts 1 to 3; practice package", "Hands-on labs; error hunt"], ["5", "The method: from foundation to test plan", "12", "Method Manual Parts 1 and 2; Corvane W01 to W10", "Reading, exercises, case review"], ["6", "Reading and directing the testing", "8", "Method Manual Parts 3A and 3B; W10 to W12", "Case review of workpapers and results"], ["7", "Evaluating, remediating and reporting", "8", "Method Manual Part 4; W13, W14", "Evaluation exercise; reporting drill"], ["8", "Leading the team and the stakeholders", "8", "The Corvane Story; Controls Lead Playbook; Weekly Activity Plan", "Reading, scenario discussions"], ["9", "Planning and estimating a new controls project", "8", "New, built on the Weekly Activity Plan", "Plan-building workshop"], ["10", "Capstone: a new oil and gas project", "9", "New case; rubric", "Written pack and steering presentation"]]}]}, {"title": "Eight-week schedule", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Eight-week schedule"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week", "Modules", "Hours", "Milestone"], ["1", "0 and 1", "9", "Self-assessment complete; controls vocabulary quiz passed"], ["2", "2 and 3", "13", "Security and oil and gas quizzes passed"], ["3", "4", "10", "Primer labs done; Find the Errors scored 15 or more out of 18"], ["4", "5", "12", "Scoping exercise submitted"], ["5", "6", "8", "Workpaper case review submitted"], ["6", "7", "8", "Evaluation exercise submitted"], ["7", "8 and 9", "16", "Stakeholder map and draft project plan submitted"], ["8", "10", "9", "Capstone pack and steering presentation; certification decision"]]}]}, {"title": "4. From Project Manager to Controls Lead: What Transfers and What Is New", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "4. From Project Manager to Controls Lead: What Transfers and What Is New"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Project management strength", "How it transfers", "What is new", "Module"], ["Planning and scheduling", "The Weekly Activity Plan is a project plan with gates", "Gates are evidence-based and the order of tests is set by reliance logic", "5, 9"], ["Scope control", "Same discipline: one-page scope signed at steering", "Scope is a landscape of systems, interfaces and processes validated with evidence, not assumed", "5"], ["Stakeholder management", "Same: map, engage, escalate", "Auditors and internal audit are both users and critics; findings are spoken before written", "8"], ["Risk and issue management", "Same RAID discipline", "The deliverable itself is a risk register; project risks include incomplete evidence and defensive technical owners", "5, 8"], ["Team and resource management", "Pods, leads, capacity", "A tester never reviews their own work; the data and evidence role sits between testing and filing", "8"], ["Quality management", "Gate reviews and checks", "Checks tabs measure completeness, not correctness; the lead must read for sense", "4, 5"], ["Status reporting", "Weekly and fortnightly reporting", "Report the denominator; lead with what works", "7, 8"], ["Estimating and commercials", "Effort and cost models", "Controls effort depends on landscape, key controls and retest operating periods", "9"]]}]}, {"title": "5. Reading Map for the Existing Materials", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "5. Reading Map for the Existing Materials"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The lead does not read everything at the same depth. Use these three levels. Core: read all of it and do the exercises. Skim: read the purpose, the worked result and the exception evaluation, and skip the step-by-step SAP navigation. Reference: use when needed."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Material", "Core", "Skim", "Reference"], ["Workbook Primer Part 1 (Sessions 1 to 5)", "Sessions 1, 2, 5", "Sessions 3, 4", "Appendix B (tabs)"], ["Workbook Primer Part 2 (Sessions 6 to 10)", "Cards for W04, W05, W08, W09, W10, W13, W14", "Cards for W01, W02, W03, W06, W07, W11, W12", "Answer key"], ["Workbook Primer Part 3 (Sessions 11 and 12)", "Both, including the trace exercises", "Final assessment", "Quick reference, glossary"], ["Practice package", "Task 7: Find the Errors (a lead must be able to review)", "Tasks 1 to 5 (build Corvane Lite)", "Answer keys"], ["Method Manual Part 1 (Sessions 1 to 6)", "Sessions 1, 4, 5, 6", "Sessions 2, 3", "Appendix key register"], ["Method Manual Part 2 (Sessions 7 to 16)", "Sessions 8, 9, 10, 11, 14, 15, 16", "Sessions 7, 12, 13", "—"], ["Method Manual Part 3A (Sessions 17 to 24)", "Session 17", "Sessions 18 to 24 (read \"what the control must achieve\", the worked result and \"evaluating exceptions\")", "Navigation tables"], ["Method Manual Part 3B (Sessions 25 to 34)", "Sessions 25, 32, 33", "Sessions 26 to 31 and 34", "Navigation tables"], ["Method Manual Part 4 (Sessions 35 to 40)", "All sessions", "—", "—"], ["Corvane Story and Controls Lead Playbook", "All chapters and sections", "—", "Appendices"], ["Weekly Activity Plan", "Weeks 1 to 38 as a planning template", "—", "Pod_Activity view"], ["Deployment Guide", "Sections 4 to 6 and 11", "Sections 3, 7 to 10", "Troubleshooting"]]}]}, {"title": "6. Assessment and Certification", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "6. Assessment and Certification"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Element", "Content", "Weight"], ["Module quizzes", "Modules 1 to 3: short quizzes; Module 4: Primer final assessment (pass 80)", "15%"], ["Practical", "Find the Errors (15 of 18 or more) and the trace exercise", "15%"], ["Case reviews", "Workpaper review (Module 6) and deficiency evaluation (Module 7), marked against model answers", "20%"], ["Planning pack", "Stakeholder map, risk list, gate plan and 38-week outline for a new project (Modules 8 and 9)", "20%"], ["Capstone", "Written scoping and plan pack for the new oil and gas project, plus a 20-minute steering presentation with questions", "30%"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "To be certified as ready to lead a controls project, a participant needs an overall score of 75 percent, at least 70 percent on every element, and a pass on the capstone presentation, where a panel of two assessors plays the CFO and the external audit partner."}]}, {"title": "Capstone in outline", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Capstone in outline"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A new fictitious oil and gas company with a different landscape, a different set of stakeholders and a hidden problem or two in the facts."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The participant produces a scoping pack (systems, interfaces, processes, key risks), a project plan with gates, a team structure, a stakeholder map and the first 90 days."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The panel challenges scope choices, staffing, how success will be measured and how findings will be handled."}]}, {"title": "7. How to Run the Course", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "7. How to Run the Course"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Cohort size: 8 to 15 participants, with one mentor per four participants."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Mentors: current or former controls leads who read the planning pack and sit on the capstone panel."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Rhythm: a weekly 90-minute live session for discussion of scenarios and the exercises; the rest is self-study."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Materials: every participant receives the Corvane case file folder, the three-part primer, the method manual, the playbook, the practice package and the new modules."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Environment: Excel is all that is required. No SAP system access is needed; SAP screens appear as screenshots in the materials."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Entry conditions: experience managing an SAP programme, and completion of the self-assessment in Appendix A."}]}, {"title": "8. Package Contents and Status", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "8. Package Contents and Status"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every piece of the course is built. The package is organised in the folders shown in the README."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Status"], ["Method Manual (five parts), Workbook Primer (three parts), practice package, Controls Lead Playbook, Corvane Story, Weekly Activity Plan, Deployment Guide, video outline", "Built"], ["Course Guide (this document)", "Built"], ["Bridge Manual A: Module 0 and Module 1", "Built"], ["Bridge Manual B: Module 2 and Module 3", "Built"], ["Module 9 manual and the Controls Project Estimating Model", "Built"], ["Module 10 capstone: participant brief, assessor guide, scoring sheet", "Built"], ["Assessment Pack for Modules 4 to 8 (participant) and Instructor Guide with answer keys", "Built"], ["Video outline for the new modules", "Not built: optional"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Assessment status: the quizzes for Modules 5 to 8 and case reviews CR-5 and CR-8 are formative (feedback only). Case reviews CR-6 and CR-7 are scored in the case-review element. Module 4 is assessed by the Primer Final Assessment. The capstone is scored with the Capstone Scoring Sheet."}]}, {"title": "Appendix A. Self-Assessment for Entry", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix A. Self-Assessment for Entry"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Score each statement from 1 (not at all) to 5 (fully). A total below 40 suggests extra preparation in the matching bridge module. This is a starting point for the mentor conversation, not a pass or fail."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Statement", "Score 1–5"], ["1", "I have led an SAP project or programme with a steering committee", ""], ["2", "I can read a project plan and spot a gate that is not realistic", ""], ["3", "I can explain what the audit committee expects from IT controls", ""], ["4", "I know what SOX requires of management", ""], ["5", "I can explain the difference between a risk and a control", ""], ["6", "I know what a user role and an authorisation are in SAP", ""], ["7", "I understand why segregation of duties matters", ""], ["8", "I know how a change reaches an SAP production system", ""], ["9", "I have worked with auditors on evidence requests", ""], ["10", "I can describe the oil and gas value chain", ""], ["11", "I know what a joint venture means for accounting", ""], ["12", "I am comfortable using Excel filters, lookups and dropdowns", ""], ["13", "I have managed a team of 10 or more", ""], ["14", "I have delivered bad news to a senior sponsor", ""], ["15", "I can estimate effort for a project I have not run before", ""], ["", "Total (maximum 75)", ""]]}]}], "word_count": 635, "id": "tib-pm-to-controls-lead-course-guide", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/00_Start_Here/TIB_PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course_Guide.docx", "filename": "TIB_PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course_Guide.docx", "category": "Start Here", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From SAP Project Manager to Controls Lead"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Bridge Manual A: Orientation (Module 0) and Controls and Audit for the SAP Project Manager (Module 1)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Course manual  |  Weeks 1 of the eight-week programme  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Examples come from the completed Corvane Energy Group case file, a fictitious oil and gas company."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "Module 0 — Orientation and Self-Assessment", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Module 0 — Orientation and Self-Assessment"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Describe what a controls lead does and decides. Rate yourself against ten lead competencies. Agree a personal learning plan with your mentor."]]}]}, {"title": "0.1 How the course works", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "0.1 How the course works"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "You are an experienced SAP project manager. You already know how to plan, staff, report and escalate. This course adds what you do not yet have: how controls and audit work, how SAP security and change controls fit in, what the oil and gas landscape looks like, and how a controls project is run from scoping to a defensible conclusion. It runs for eight weeks and about 85 hours. The Course Guide gives the schedule, the assessments and the reading map."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "This manual covers week 1: this orientation and Module 1, \"Controls and audit for the SAP project manager\". By the end of week 1 you can talk to a CFO and an audit partner in their own words."}]}, {"title": "0.2 What a controls lead does", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "0.2 What a controls lead does"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Responsibility", "What it means in practice", "Decisions you make"], ["Scope", "Agree what is in and out: systems, interfaces, processes, period, materiality basis", "Which systems and processes are in scope; what counts as validated"], ["Plan", "Build the project plan, gates and dates; set the order of testing", "General controls before automated controls; sample sizes; retest dates"], ["Team", "Design pods, assign testers and reviewers, protect capacity", "Who leads each area; who reviews whom"], ["Stakeholders", "Keep the sponsor, the CIO, the controller, Internal Audit and the external auditor aligned", "What to say, to whom, and in what order"], ["Quality", "Hold the rules: population before sample, evidence before conclusion, retest before closure", "When to say no; when a gate is not passed"], ["Evaluation", "Group findings by cause, rate severity, decide reliance", "What is a deficiency; how serious; what can be relied on"], ["Reporting and closure", "Present results with the denominator; say what is still open", "What goes to the steering committee and the audit committee"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["THE LEAD'S THREE RULES (REPEATED THROUGHOUT THE COURSE) 1. Nothing is a surprise.  Findings are spoken before they are written. 2. Nothing is sampled, filed or closed without a check. 3. Everything is traceable.  Any conclusion can be followed back to a server and any server forward to a conclusion."]]}]}, {"title": "0.3 Ten lead competencies", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "0.3 Ten lead competencies"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Rate yourself on each competency today. Use 1 (no exposure), 2 (aware), 3 (working: can do with guidance), 4 (confident), 5 (can teach it). The course aims to move every competency to at least 3, and the ones marked with a star to 4."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Competency", "Taught in", "Today (1 to 5)", "Target"], ["1", "Controls and audit vocabulary and logic", "Module 1", "", "4 *"], ["2", "SAP security, GRC and change controls (conceptual)", "Module 2", "", "3"], ["3", "Oil and gas SAP landscape and control risks", "Module 3", "", "3"], ["4", "Reading and checking the workbooks", "Module 4", "", "4 *"], ["5", "Scoping and the foundation method", "Module 5", "", "4 *"], ["6", "Directing and reading the testing", "Module 6", "", "3"], ["7", "Evaluating deficiencies and reliance", "Module 7", "", "4 *"], ["8", "Leading a team and stakeholders", "Module 8", "", "4 *"], ["9", "Planning and estimating a controls project", "Module 9", "", "4 *"], ["10", "Presenting and defending the project", "Module 10", "", "4 *"]]}]}, {"title": "0.4 Self-assessment scoring", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "0.4 Self-assessment scoring"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Complete the 15-statement self-assessment in Appendix A of the Course Guide. Then add up the scores by theme. A theme with a low score tells you where to spend extra time."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Theme", "Statements", "Maximum", "Your score", "If below"], ["Controls and audit", "3, 4, 5, 9", "20", "", "12: allow extra time in Module 1"], ["SAP security and change", "6, 7, 8", "15", "", "9: allow extra time in Module 2"], ["Oil and gas", "10, 11", "10", "", "6: allow extra time in Module 3"], ["Excel toolkit", "12", "5", "", "3: do the Primer labs twice"], ["Leadership and planning", "1, 2, 13, 14, 15", "25", "", "15: focus on Modules 8 and 9"]]}]}, {"title": "0.5 Personal learning plan and the mentor call", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "0.5 Personal learning plan and the mentor call"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Your answer"], ["Which two competencies are you most keen to build?", ""], ["Which theme scored lowest on the self-assessment?", ""], ["What project are you hoping to lead, and when?", ""], ["How many hours a week can you commit?", ""], ["What would success look like for you at week 8?", ""]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Mentor call (30 minutes, week 1): review the self-assessment, agree the two focus areas, confirm the weekly hours, and book the week 4 and week 8 check-ins."}]}, {"title": "Module 1 — Controls and Audit for the SAP Project Manager", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Module 1 — Controls and Audit for the SAP Project Manager"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Module 1 has six short sessions, about seven hours in all. Each has outcomes, an explanation, an example from the Corvane case, a \"PM lens\" that compares the idea with project management, an exercise and three questions. A quiz and an answer key close the module."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Session", "Topic", "Hours"], ["1.1", "Why controls projects exist", "1"], ["1.2", "The vocabulary of controls", "1.5"], ["1.3", "IT controls in three layers", "1.5"], ["1.4", "How controls are tested", "1.5"], ["1.5", "Deficiencies and severity", "1"], ["1.6", "What the auditor needs from you", "0.5"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 1.1 — Why Controls Projects Exist", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 1.1 — Why Controls Projects Exist"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Explain internal control over financial reporting in plain language. Say who does what: management, internal audit, external audit and the audit committee. State what a controls project delivers and to whom."]]}]}, {"title": "1.1.1 The problem", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.1.1 The problem"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The financial statements of a listed company are produced largely by systems. If the systems post a wrong number, or let someone post a number they should not, the statements are wrong. The company has to show that it has designed and operates controls that would prevent or detect such errors in time. When a company is subject to the Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX) in the United States, management must assess and report on the effectiveness of its internal control over financial reporting, and the external auditor of larger companies must also give an opinion on it. Other countries have similar regimes. The rules depend on the company's listing and size, so on a real project always confirm the requirement with the client's finance and audit teams."}]}, {"title": "1.1.2 The four parties", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.1.2 The four parties"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Party", "Role", "What they want from a controls project"], ["Management (CFO, controller)", "Owns the controls and must assess them", "A defensible assessment and no surprises"], ["Internal audit", "Independent assurance for management and the audit committee", "An audit trail they can rely on and reuse"], ["External auditor", "Audits the statements; for larger companies, also opines on internal control", "Evidence that automated controls and reports can be relied on, so they can reduce their own testing"], ["Audit committee", "Oversees reporting and audit for the board", "A clear picture of what is fixed, what is open and how serious it is"]]}]}, {"title": "1.1.3 What a controls project is", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.1.3 What a controls project is"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A controls project builds and proves the foundation for that assessment. It identifies the systems and processes that matter, states the risks, defines the controls that answer them, tests whether those controls are designed well and operate, evaluates what fails, and helps fix and retest it. Its output is not a long report but a result the client can defend: what is covered, what was tested, what failed, what was fixed and what remains."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane Energy Group is a US-listed oil and gas company with one production S/4HANA system for four company codes. Its CFO, Eleanor Whitcombe, sponsored the project; Internal Audit and the external audit partner were stakeholders from week one. The project ran for 38 calendar weeks and ended with 30 deficiencies logged, 21 closed after a retest and 9 open, no material weakness."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS A controls project has a normal project life cycle, but its deliverable is evidence. A late milestone can be recovered; an unsupported conclusion cannot."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 1.1 In your own words, write two sentences each on what the Corvane project delivers to (a) the CFO, (b) the external audit partner and (c) the head of Internal Audit."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Who owns the controls: management, internal audit or the external auditor?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why does the external auditor care whether automated controls can be relied on?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What is the difference between the output of an audit and the output of a controls project?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Management owns the controls and must assess them; auditors give independent assurance. A controls project builds the foundation for that assessment. Its deliverable is a defensible result, not a report."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 1.2 — The Vocabulary of Controls", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 1.2 — The Vocabulary of Controls"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Define risk, control, assertion, key control, preventive and detective. Write a control in five parts. Classify a control by nature and frequency."]]}]}, {"title": "1.2.1 Risk and control", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.2.1 Risk and control"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A risk is something that could go wrong and cause the financial statements to be misstated, written as: there is a risk that something happens because of a cause, resulting in an impact. A control is one person or system doing one thing, at a stated frequency, producing evidence, that prevents or detects the risk. One risk can need several controls; one control can answer several risks."}]}, {"title": "1.2.2 Assertions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.2.2 Assertions"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "An assertion is a claim the financial statements make: that transactions occurred (occurrence), nothing is missing (completeness), amounts are right (accuracy), they are in the right period (cut-off), balances exist (existence) and are valued correctly (valuation). Each risk is tied to the assertions it threatens."}]}, {"title": "1.2.3 Kinds of control", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.2.3 Kinds of control"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dimension", "Values", "Plain meaning"], ["Type", "Preventive; detective", "Stops the error happening; finds it after it happens"], ["Nature", "Automated; IT-dependent manual; manual", "The system does it; a person does it using a system report; a person does it without a system"], ["Frequency", "Continuous, event-driven, daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, annual", "How often it operates; frequency drives sample size"], ["Key", "Yes or no", "Relied on to address a SOX-relevant risk; key controls are formally tested"]]}]}, {"title": "1.2.4 The five-part description", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.2.4 The five-part description"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A good control description answers five questions: who performs it, what they do, when or how often, how (system, report or criteria), and what evidence results. If you cannot say what evidence it produces, it is not yet a control."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weak", "Five parts"], ["Access is managed appropriately", "The IT Security Administrator (who) locks and end-dates the SAP user of every leaver (what) within two business days of the HR termination date (when), using the daily HR leavers report and SU01 or GRC (how). Evidence is the leavers report annotated with lock dates and the SU01 change history."], ["Vendor bank changes are checked", "The AP Analyst (who) verifies every vendor bank-detail change by calling the vendor on an independently sourced number before the change is saved (what) for every change (when) using the call-back procedure and log (how). Evidence is the call-back log with approver."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE The risk \"a fictitious vendor is created and paid\" (R-P2P-001) is answered by several controls: the segregation-of-duties analysis (C-ITGC-SOD-001), the weekly vendor change review (C-BPC-P2P-001) and the call-back control (C-BPC-P2P-002). The first removes the conflict, the others detect or prevent misuse. None of them alone covers the risk."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS A control is like a task in a plan: it has an owner, a frequency and an output. A control with no evidence is like a milestone with no deliverable."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 1.2 A.  Rewrite each in five parts, inventing sensible details: \"Access is reviewed regularly.\" and \"The system checks invoices.\"  B.  For each of these, say whether it is preventive or detective and automated, IT-dependent manual or manual: (i) the system blocks an invoice outside the price tolerance; (ii) the controller reviews a report of large journals each month; (iii) the AP analyst phones the vendor before changing bank details."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What are the five parts of a control description?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Which is typically tested more often: a daily control or an annual control, and why?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What makes a control \"key\"?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Risk: what could go wrong. Control: who does what, when, how and with what evidence. Assertions say what the risk threatens. Frequency drives the sample size."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 1.3 — IT Controls in Three Layers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 1.3 — IT Controls in Three Layers"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Distinguish ITGCs, ITACs and IT-dependent manual controls with reports (IPE). Explain why general controls protect automated controls. Read a reliance decision."]]}]}, {"title": "1.3.1 Three layers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.3.1 Three layers"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Layer", "What it covers", "SAP examples"], ["ITGC: IT general controls", "The environment that all automated controls and reports depend on: access, change, operations (and, when a project is in the period, development)", "User provisioning, leaver removal, access reviews, privileged and Firefighter access, transports, production change settings, job and interface monitoring, backup"], ["ITAC: IT application controls", "Automated controls built into SAP configuration", "Three-way match tolerances, duplicate invoice check, posting periods, release strategies, depreciation keys"], ["ITDM and IPE: IT-dependent manual controls and information produced by the entity", "Controls performed by people using a system report; the report itself must be complete and accurate", "The controller's review of a report of large journals; a BW reconciliation report"]]}]}, {"title": "1.3.2 The four ITGC domains", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.3.2 The four ITGC domains"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Domain", "Question it answers"], ["Access to programs and data (APD)", "Who can get in, and what can they do?"], ["Program change (PC)", "How does a change reach production, and who approves it?"], ["Computer operations (CO)", "Do jobs, interfaces and backups run and get fixed when they fail?"], ["Program development (PD)", "Were new implementations and migrations controlled? (relevant only when one is in the period)"]]}]}, {"title": "1.3.3 Reliance: why ITGCs protect ITACs", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.3.3 Reliance: why ITGCs protect ITACs"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "An automated control such as the three-way match is only a setting in a table. A test of it shows the setting is right today. To say it was right all year, you need the change and access controls around it to have worked, or other evidence that nobody changed it. That dependency is called reliance. If a general control fails, the automated control is not automatically wrong, but the presumption of reliance is gone and extra procedures are needed."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE C-ITAC-001, the three-way match, relies on three general controls: transports (C-ITGC-PC-001), system and client settings (C-ITGC-PC-002) and privileged access (C-ITGC-AC-005). All three had exceptions, so reliance was recorded as \"supported with additional procedures\": a full-period review of table change logs and of the transports that touch the tolerance tables, and a usage analysis of the users who held SAP_ALL. The auditors accepted the reliance."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS Think of the ITGCs as the platform and the ITACs as the workloads running on it. If the platform is unmanaged, you cannot vouch for the workloads without extra checks."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 1.3 A.  Classify each as ITGC, ITAC, ITDM or manual business-process control: (i) password length parameter; (ii) three-way match; (iii) quarterly user access review; (iv) AP supervisor's weekly review of the vendor change report; (v) call-back to the vendor before a bank change; (vi) transport approval; (vii) daily batch job monitoring; (viii) monthly reconciliation of BW to the ledger.  B.  The transport approval control failed. What does that do to your reliance on the three-way match, and what would you do?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Name the three ITGC domains that are tested on almost every project."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why is a test of one enough for an automated control?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does \"supported with additional procedures\" mean?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS ITGCs are the environment; ITACs are settings; ITDMs are people using reports. Reliance links the layers. A failed ITGC removes the presumption of reliance; other evidence can restore it."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 1.4 — How Controls Are Tested", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 1.4 — How Controls Are Tested"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Explain the test of design and the test of operating effectiveness. Choose a population, attributes and a sample for a simple control. Name the four kinds of test procedure."]]}]}, {"title": "1.4.1 Two tests per key control", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.4.1 Two tests per key control"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["", "Test of design (TOD)", "Test of operating effectiveness (TOE)"], ["Question", "If performed as described, would this control address the risk?", "Did the control actually operate throughout the period?"], ["Method", "Walkthrough of one instance with the performer", "Inspection or reperformance of a sample across the period"], ["If it fails", "The control has a design deficiency", "The control has an operating deficiency"]]}]}, {"title": "1.4.2 Procedures", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.4.2 Procedures"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Procedure", "What you do", "Strength"], ["Inquiry", "Ask the performer", "Weakest alone; never sufficient by itself"], ["Observation", "Watch the control being performed", "Good for a moment in time"], ["Inspection", "Examine documents, records or system output", "Strong"], ["Reperformance", "Do the control again yourself", "Strongest"]]}]}, {"title": "1.4.3 Population, sample and attributes", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.4.3 Population, sample and attributes"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Population: the complete list of items the control applies to for the period (all leavers, all transports imported, all payment runs). It must be complete and accurate: that is the IPE check."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Sample: items drawn from the population. Sample size depends on how often the control operates. Typical practice: 25 to 60 for event-driven controls, 20 to 40 for daily, 5 to 15 for weekly, 2 to 5 for monthly, 2 for quarterly, 1 for annual. Follow the auditor's methodology where there is one."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Attributes: the conditions each item must meet to count as a pass, written from the control description."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Evidence: what you obtain to prove each attribute."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE For C-ITGC-AC-002 (leavers locked within two business days) the population was the HR leavers report, 214 leavers from 1 January to 31 May 2026, not the list of locked SAP users. Starting from SAP would only have shown users who were removed. The sample was 25. The attributes were: A the leaver is on the HR report; B the user is locked or end-dated; C the lock is within two business days of termination; D there is no logon after termination. Six of 25 were locked late, because the HR report reached Security weekly, and one had a display-only logon."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — START FROM THE SYSTEM OF RECORD The population for a test comes from the authoritative source for the thing being controlled: HR for leavers, what production received for changes. Starting from the process that is supposed to produce the record can never find a bypass."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS A population is like the complete work breakdown structure: if it is incomplete, no sample from it tells you about the whole."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 1.4 For the control \"every vendor bank-detail change is verified by a call-back before it is saved\":  A.  What is the population and where does it come from?  B.  Write four attributes.  C.  The control is event-driven: what sample size range is typical?  D.  Name one procedure that is stronger than inquiry and say how you would use it."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What is the difference between a population and a sample?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why is inquiry never enough on its own?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why must a population be checked before sampling?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Two tests per key control: design, then operating. Population, sample, attributes and evidence are the four building blocks. Start from the system of record."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 1.5 — Deficiencies and Severity", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 1.5 — Deficiencies and Severity"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Tell an exception from a deficiency. Use the three severity levels. Explain grouping, compensating controls, remediation and retest."]]}]}, {"title": "1.5.1 From exception to deficiency", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.5.1 From exception to deficiency"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "An exception is a fact: a sample item did not meet an attribute. A deficiency is a judgement: the control, as designed or as it operated, does not let management prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis. Between the two sits an evaluation: is the exception real, is it a design or an operating failure, is the number of failures above what the sample tolerates, what could go wrong, what else compensates, and does it connect to other findings."}]}, {"title": "1.5.2 Three levels of severity", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.5.2 Three levels of severity"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Level", "Meaning"], ["Control deficiency", "The control does not allow management or employees to prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis"], ["Significant deficiency", "Less severe than a material weakness but important enough to merit the attention of those overseeing financial reporting"], ["Material weakness", "There is a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement of the financial statements will not be prevented or detected on a timely basis"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Severity depends on how large the potential misstatement is, how likely it is, whether fraud is possible, whether other controls compensate, and, for general controls, whether automated controls and reports that depend on them can still be relied on."}]}, {"title": "1.5.3 Group by cause", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.5.3 Group by cause"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Thirty separate findings overwhelm a client. Group them by cause and process and evaluate the group. Several modest deficiencies with one cause can together be significant."}]}, {"title": "1.5.4 Remediation and retest", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.5.4 Remediation and retest"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every deficiency needs an owner, a date and a fix that addresses the cause, not only the instance. A deficiency is closed only when a retest shows the remediated control operating. How long a retest needs depends on the control: roughly five weeks for a weekly control, two month-ends for a monthly one, two quarters for a quarterly one."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane logged 30 deficiencies in seven groups. Group A, privileged access governance, included nine dialog users with SAP_ALL, 14 role assignments made outside GRC, two openings of production without a ticket and late Firefighter reviews. Usage analysis showed no misuse, so it was rated a significant deficiency, not a material weakness. Group B, the payment process, combined conflicting access, weak bank-change verification and an unprotected payment file; a review of every bank change and payment run found nothing improper, and it too was rated significant. After remediation and retest, 21 deficiencies were closed and nine remained open, all at the lowest severity."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS Evaluation is like impact assessment of a project risk: probability, impact, mitigation and the combined effect of related risks, with the difference that the standard you are held to is set by the auditor."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 1.5 A client has these facts: (i) nine dialog users hold SAP_ALL; (ii) usage analysis shows they only displayed data; (iii) 14 role assignments were made outside the request workflow; (iv) production was opened twice for changes without a ticket.  A.  Should you treat these as four findings or one group, and why?  B.  What would make you rate the group a material weakness?  C.  What would you need to see in a retest before closing it?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What is the difference between an exception and a deficiency?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Name the three severity levels in order."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why is a deficiency closed only after a retest?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS An exception is a fact; a deficiency is a judgement. Group by cause, then rate the group. Close only on a retest."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 1.6 — What the Auditor Needs from You", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 1.6 — What the Auditor Needs from You"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES List what an external auditor needs from a controls project. Prepare the first conversation with the audit partner."]]}]}, {"title": "1.6.1 The auditor's view", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.6.1 The auditor's view"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "External auditors may use the work of management and internal audit if it is reliable. The more it can be relied on, the less the auditors need to test again themselves. They look for four things: clear scope that ties to the significant accounts, evidence that is complete and traceable, tests designed to the auditor's own standards of attribute and sample, and an honest evaluation with the denominator stated."}]}, {"title": "1.6.2 The week-one conversation", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.6.2 The week-one conversation"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "On a controls project the single most useful conversation in week one is with the audit partner. Agree what they will accept before you test. Questions to ask:"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Which significant accounts and processes do you consider in scope, and what materiality do you use?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What test attributes and sample sizes will you accept for each type of control?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What do you need to rely on automated controls, and which general controls do you expect to see tested?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What operating period do you need after a fix before you will accept a retest?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How will you classify and aggregate deficiencies, and when do you want to hear about a possible significant deficiency?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What are your timing constraints and which evidence format do you prefer?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE At Corvane the lead asked the external audit partner in week one which attributes and sample sizes she would accept and wrote them into the plan. In week 34 he agreed the retest sample sizes and operating periods with her before issuing the evidence requests. In week 31 the auditors accepted 49 reliance decisions with additional procedures, 11 fully supported, and 1 not relied on."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS This is stakeholder requirements gathering with an unusually clear customer: the auditor tells you the acceptance criteria. Ask before you build."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 1.6 You are about to meet the external audit partner of a new oil and gas client. List your five most important questions and, for each, say what you will do with the answer."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why does the auditor care how reliable management's work is?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What is the benefit of agreeing sample sizes in week one?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Who should hear about a possible significant deficiency first, and why?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS The auditor can rely on your work only if it is complete, traceable and tested to their standard. Agree attributes, samples and operating periods before you test. Tell the client first; no surprises."]]}]}, {"title": "Module 1 Quiz", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Module 1 Quiz"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Fifteen questions, 2 marks each. Pass mark 24 out of 30 (80 percent). Answers follow."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Who owns the controls over financial reporting?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Name the four parties in a controls project."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Write the sentence structure for a risk statement."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. What is an assertion? Give two examples."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Name the five parts of a control description."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "6. What is a key control?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "7. Name the three layers of IT controls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "8. Name the three main ITGC domains."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "9. Explain \"reliance\" in one sentence."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "10. What is the difference between a test of design and a test of operating effectiveness?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "11. Why must a population be complete?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "12. Name the four test procedures from weakest to strongest."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "13. What is the difference between an exception and a deficiency?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "14. Name the three severity levels."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "15. Why group deficiencies by cause?"}]}, {"title": "Quiz answers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Quiz answers"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Q", "Answer"], ["1", "Management"], ["2", "Management, internal audit, external auditor, audit committee"], ["3", "There is a risk that [event] because [cause], resulting in [impact]"], ["4", "A claim the financial statements make, such as occurrence, completeness, accuracy, cut-off, existence or valuation"], ["5", "Who, what, when or how often, how, and what evidence"], ["6", "A control relied on to address a SOX-relevant risk; it is formally tested"], ["7", "ITGCs, ITACs, and IT-dependent manual controls with reports (ITDM and IPE)"], ["8", "Access to programs and data, program change, computer operations"], ["9", "Trusting an automated control or report because the general controls around it worked or other evidence shows it did not change"], ["10", "Design asks whether the control would work as described; operating asks whether it did work throughout the period"], ["11", "A sample from an incomplete population tells you nothing reliable about the whole"], ["12", "Inquiry, observation, inspection, reperformance"], ["13", "An exception is a fact about one item; a deficiency is a judgement about the control"], ["14", "Control deficiency, significant deficiency, material weakness"], ["15", "A client can fix a cause; related findings together can be more serious than each alone"]]}]}, {"title": "Answer Key to the Session Exercises", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Answer Key to the Session Exercises"}]}, {"title": "Session 1.1", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 1.1"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Model answers. (a) CFO: a defensible assessment of internal control and a clear statement of what is fixed and what is open, with no surprises. (b) External audit partner: evidence that automated controls and reports can be relied on, tested to their standards, so their own testing can be reduced. (c) Head of Internal Audit: a complete audit trail and a foundation they can reuse in their own audits. Check yourself: (1) Management. (2) Because reliance reduces how much they must test themselves. (3) An audit gives an opinion on the statements; a controls project builds and proves the foundation for management's assessment."}]}, {"title": "Session 1.2", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 1.2"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Model answer"], ["A: Access is reviewed regularly", "Role owners (who) review all users and role assignments (what) every quarter (when) through a GRC user access review campaign generated from the authorisation tables (how); rejected access is removed within 10 business days. Evidence is the campaign listing, reviewer decisions and removal records."], ["A: The system checks invoices", "The system (who) blocks payment of any vendor invoice outside the tolerance limits for price, quantity and receipt (what) at every invoice posting (when), comparing purchase order, receipt and invoice (how). Evidence is the tolerance configuration and the blocked-invoice records."], ["B (i)", "Preventive; automated"], ["B (ii)", "Detective; IT-dependent manual (a person using a report)"], ["B (iii)", "Preventive; manual"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) Who, what, when or how often, how, evidence. (2) A daily control, because there are many more occurrences, so the sample is larger. (3) It is relied on to address a SOX-relevant risk."}]}, {"title": "Session 1.3", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 1.3"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Answer"], ["A (i)", "ITGC (access to programs and data); automated"], ["A (ii)", "ITAC"], ["A (iii)", "ITGC (access); detective, IT-dependent manual"], ["A (iv)", "ITDM (a manual control that relies on a system report)"], ["A (v)", "Manual business-process control"], ["A (vi)", "ITGC (program change)"], ["A (vii)", "ITGC (computer operations)"], ["A (viii)", "IPE-related control (reconciliation of a report used in the close)"], ["B", "The presumption of reliance on the three-way match is gone. Do extra work: review the change log of the tolerance tables for the full period, inspect any transports touching them, and analyse who could change the settings, then document whether reliance is still supported"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) Access, change, operations. (2) Because the setting is the same for every transaction, provided it did not change. (3) The general control had exceptions, but other documented procedures show the configuration did not change, so the automated control can still be relied on."}]}, {"title": "Session 1.4", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 1.4"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Model answer"], ["A", "All vendor bank-detail changes in the period, from the change documents in SAP (for example table CDPOS), reconciled to the call-back log"], ["B", "A call-back log entry exists; the number called was independently sourced; the call was made and approved before the change was saved; the change agrees to the request"], ["C", "25 to 60"], ["D", "Inspection (examine the log entry and the change request) or reperformance (re-contact a vendor from the independent number to confirm)"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) A population is everything the control applies to; a sample is the part you test. (2) Because the performer may be mistaken or may overstate what happens; it needs corroboration. (3) A sample from an incomplete population says nothing about what is missing."}]}, {"title": "Session 1.5", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 1.5"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Model answer"], ["A", "One group. The four facts share a cause (access and change rights granted during an upgrade and never withdrawn) and one team. Together they describe a team that can bypass access and change controls; each fact alone understates that"], ["B", "Evidence of misuse (for example changes to configuration or master data by the privileged users), or no way to show that automated controls and reports were not altered, so reliance cannot be restored"], ["C", "New controls operating for long enough: no SAP_ALL on dialog users, no role assignments outside the workflow, an alert on every production opening, and a sample of recent events passing the same attributes"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) An exception is a fact about an item; a deficiency is a judgement about the control. (2) Control deficiency, significant deficiency, material weakness. (3) Because only a retest shows the remediated control operating."}]}, {"title": "Session 1.6", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 1.6"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Model answer: use the six questions in 1.6.2. For example, \"What sample sizes will you accept?\" tells you whether to follow the standard guide or the auditor's own; \"What operating period do you need after a fix?\" tells you when to schedule retests; \"When do you want to hear about a possible significant deficiency?\" tells you how to plan escalation. Check yourself: (1) Because the less they can rely on, the more they must test themselves. (2) It avoids rework and disagreement at the end. (3) The client (the CFO and the CIO for matters touching IT), in person, because no one should learn of a finding from a report."}]}, {"title": "Appendix — Glossary for Module 1", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix — Glossary for Module 1"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Term", "Meaning"], ["Assertion", "A claim the financial statements make: occurrence, completeness, accuracy, cut-off, existence, valuation"], ["Audit committee", "The board committee that oversees financial reporting and audit"], ["Compensating control", "Another control that reduces the exposure left by a deficient one"], ["Control", "One person or system doing one thing at a stated frequency, producing evidence"], ["Control deficiency", "A control that does not let misstatements be prevented or detected on time"], ["Detective / preventive", "Finds an error after it happens / stops it happening"], ["Exception", "A sample item that failed an attribute"], ["ICFR", "Internal control over financial reporting"], ["IPE", "Information produced by the entity: a report or population that must be proven complete and accurate"], ["ITAC / ITGC / ITDM", "Automated application control / general control / IT-dependent manual control"], ["Key control", "A control relied on to address a SOX-relevant risk"], ["Material weakness", "A reasonable possibility that a material misstatement will not be prevented or detected on time"], ["Population / sample", "The complete list / the items tested"], ["Reliance", "Trusting a control or report because the controls around it worked or other evidence shows no change"], ["Retest", "A repeat test after a fix, needed to close a deficiency"], ["Significant deficiency", "Less severe than a material weakness but important enough for those overseeing reporting to know"], ["SOX", "The US law requiring management to assess internal control over financial reporting"], ["TOD / TOE", "Test of design / test of operating effectiveness"]]}]}], "word_count": 2221, "id": "tib-bridge-manual-a-modules-0-1", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/01_Bridge_Manuals/TIB_Bridge_Manual_A_Modules_0-1.docx", "filename": "TIB_Bridge_Manual_A_Modules_0-1.docx", "category": "Bridge Manuals", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From SAP Project Manager to Controls Lead"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Bridge Manual B: SAP Security and GRC without the Jargon (Module 2) and Oil and Gas on SAP (Module 3)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Course manual  |  Week 2 of the eight-week programme  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Examples come from the completed Corvane Energy Group case file, a fictitious oil and gas company."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "How to Use This Manual", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "How to Use This Manual"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "This manual covers week 2. Module 2 gives you enough SAP security, GRC and change knowledge to direct a controls team and challenge a basis lead, without turning you into a security administrator. Module 3 gives you the oil and gas picture: the business, the landscape, the processes and the control risks that are particular to the industry. Each session has outcomes, an explanation, a Corvane example, a PM lens, a question set called \"What a lead asks\", an exercise and a self-check. The answer key is at the back."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Module", "Sessions", "Hours"], ["2 SAP security and GRC without the jargon", "2.1 to 2.6", "7"], ["3 Oil and gas on SAP", "3.1 to 3.5", "6"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT YOU WILL NOT BE ASKED TO DO You will not be asked to maintain roles, run a GRC analysis or write a transport. You will be asked to understand them well enough to know what a good answer sounds like when your team or the client gives one."]]}]}, {"title": "Module 2 — SAP Security and GRC without the Jargon", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Module 2 — SAP Security and GRC without the Jargon"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Session", "Topic", "Hours"], ["2.1", "How SAP decides who can do what", "1.25"], ["2.2", "Segregation of duties and GRC", "1.25"], ["2.3", "The access lifecycle: joiners, movers, leavers and privileged IDs", "1.25"], ["2.4", "How a change reaches production", "1.25"], ["2.5", "Operations and the layers beneath SAP", "1"], ["2.6", "A walkthrough toolkit for the lead", "1"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 2.1 — How SAP Decides Who Can Do What", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 2.1 — How SAP Decides Who Can Do What"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Explain users, roles, authorisations and user types in plain language. Say why SAP_ALL is dangerous. Ask the right first question about access."]]}]}, {"title": "2.1.1 Users, roles, authorisations", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.1.1 Users, roles, authorisations"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every person or program that logs on to SAP has a user record. What the user may do is decided by authorisations. Authorisations are grouped into roles, and a user is given one or more roles. Inside a role, an authorisation object (for example, one for posting a journal or maintaining a vendor) has fields, and the field for the activity says whether the user may create (01), change (02) or display (03). The permission to do something is therefore a combination of the transaction and the authorisation values behind it."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Concept", "Plain meaning", "Everyday comparison"], ["User", "An identity that can log on", "A badge"], ["Role", "A bundle of permissions for a job", "A job profile"], ["Authorisation object and values", "The exact rights: which action, on which organisation, for which data", "The detail of what the badge opens"], ["Profile", "The generated technical form of a role", "The programmed badge"]]}]}, {"title": "2.1.2 Types of user", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.1.2 Types of user"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["User type", "Used for", "Control point"], ["Dialog", "A person logging on", "Must be a named individual; one person, one user"], ["System", "Background jobs and technical processing; no dialog logon", "Should not be usable by a person"], ["Communication", "Calls between systems (RFC, services); no dialog logon", "Narrow authorisations; credentials protected"], ["Service", "Anonymous or shared dialog access for a defined service", "Tightly limited"], ["Reference", "Holds authorisations only; nobody logs on with it", "Used to share authorisations across users"]]}]}, {"title": "2.1.3 SAP_ALL and the powerful profiles", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.1.3 SAP_ALL and the powerful profiles"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "SAP_ALL is a profile that grants every authorisation in the system. A user holding it can bypass every application control, post any journal, change any master record and, with the right rights, change configuration. SAP_NEW grants the new authorisation checks that arrive after an upgrade. Neither should be held by a normal dialog user. Technical or emergency users may need broad access, but it is then controlled through a log and a review."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE At Corvane nine dialog users held SAP_ALL and four generic IDs were active. The extra detail that mattered was not the number but what the users had done. Usage analysis showed display transactions and two header-text changes, no configuration or master-data change. That evidence decided the severity. After remediation SAP_ALL existed only on Firefighter IDs."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT A LEAD ASKS Who has broad access, and why? Can I see a list of users holding SAP_ALL or SAP_NEW in production? What have those users done in the period?"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS Treat user access like site access on a project: a badge for every person, rights tied to the job, and a log of what was opened."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 2.1 A.  Say which user type fits: (i) a finance analyst approving invoices; (ii) the account that posts bank statements; (iii) a nightly batch job.  B.  Explain to a CFO in three sentences what SAP_ALL means and why a dialog user should not have it.  C.  A basis lead says \"we need SAP_ALL for support\". What three follow-up questions do you ask?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What is the relationship between a user, a role and an authorisation?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Which user types should not be usable for a person to log on?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why does it matter what a privileged user has done, not only that they have the access?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Users get roles; roles contain authorisations with actions and values. Different user types have different control points. SAP_ALL bypasses everything; usage analysis decides how serious holding it is."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 2.2 — Segregation of Duties and GRC", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 2.2 — Segregation of Duties and GRC"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Explain segregation of duties with examples. Name what the GRC Access Control components do. Say what a mitigating control must be."]]}]}, {"title": "2.2.1 Segregation of duties (SoD)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.2.1 Segregation of duties (SoD)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Segregation of duties means that no one person can complete a sensitive process alone. The classic conflict is creating a supplier and paying that supplier. Two or three conflicts matter most in finance: create and pay vendors, post and approve journals, raise an order and receive the goods. An SoD rule is a pair of functions, each defined by transactions and authorisation values, that must not be held by the same user. Rules written only at transaction level over-report; rules at permission level check the underlying authorisation values and are more accurate."}]}, {"title": "2.2.2 What GRC adds", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.2.2 What GRC adds"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["GRC Access Control component", "What it does"], ["Access Risk Analysis (ARA)", "Runs the SoD rules against users and roles and shows the conflicts"], ["Access Request Management (ARM)", "A workflow for requesting, approving and provisioning access, with an SoD check before approval; also the user access review"], ["Emergency Access Management (EAM)", "Firefighter IDs: a user checks out a privileged ID with a stated reason; every action is logged and a controller reviews the log"], ["Business Role Management (BRM)", "Designs and maintains roles"]]}]}, {"title": "2.2.3 Mitigating controls", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.2.3 Mitigating controls"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "When a conflict cannot be removed (for example, a small team where one person must do both tasks) the business may accept it with a mitigating control: another control that would detect misuse. A mitigation counts only if the control exists, has an owner, is tested and found effective, and has a valid period. Critical conflicts that threaten fraud are normally removed, not mitigated."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane's approved ruleset had 16 rules over 20 functions. Four rules were critical (such as vendor maintenance with the payment run) and could not be mitigated. Twelve mitigations covered the lower-risk conflicts. In the baseline test 61 users held unmitigated critical conflicts; after roles were redesigned by duty, none did. Usage analysis showed no combined use that caused a loss."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT A LEAD ASKS Is there an approved SoD ruleset, who approved it and when? Are custom transactions and Fiori apps included? Which conflicts are mitigated, by which control, and has that control been tested?"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS SoD is the \"four-eyes\" principle you already use for budget approval and release management, written as machine-readable rules."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 2.2 A.  Which of these is an SoD conflict? (i) create a vendor and run the payment; (ii) display a vendor and display an invoice; (iii) post a journal and release the same journal; (iv) raise a purchase order and receive the goods; (v) change a bank account and reconcile it.  B.  A team of three cannot separate raising and receiving. What must be true of the control the business offers to mitigate?  C.  Why does a transaction-only rule produce false conflicts?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What does ARA do and what does EAM do?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why are critical conflicts removed instead of mitigated?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What must be true of a mitigating control?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS SoD stops one person completing a sensitive process alone. GRC runs the rules (ARA), approves access (ARM) and controls emergency access (EAM). A mitigation is valid only if the control exists, is owned, tested and current."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 2.3 — The Access Lifecycle", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 2.3 — The Access Lifecycle"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Describe the controls over joiners, movers and leavers. Explain access reviews and privileged access controls. Say what auditors test for each."]]}]}, {"title": "2.3.1 The lifecycle controls", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.3.1 The lifecycle controls"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Stage", "Control", "What the auditor tests", "Population comes from"], ["Join or change", "Access is requested, approved by the line manager and role owner, SoD-checked and then provisioned", "Approval before provisioning; roles granted equal roles approved", "All role assignments added in the period"], ["Leave", "The account is locked promptly after termination", "Lock within the policy time; no logon after termination", "The HR leavers report, not the SAP lock list"], ["Review", "Role owners review all users and roles at a set frequency and remove inappropriate access", "The review listing is complete; reviewers are authorised; rejections are actioned", "The campaign listing, tested against the user tables"], ["Privileged", "Powerful access is limited to approved IDs and reviewed", "No dialog users with SAP_ALL; generic and default IDs controlled", "All users with the powerful profiles"], ["Emergency", "Firefighter use is approved and every log is reviewed by a controller", "Session approval, review timeliness and whether the activity matches the reason", "All emergency sessions in the period"], ["Password and logon", "System parameters enforce policy on every server", "Values on every application server; no change in the period", "The parameter values per server"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane's HR report reached IT Security weekly, so six of 25 sampled leavers were locked late, up to 14 business days. The fix was a daily feed from HR into GRC that locks the account automatically. The quarterly access review tested effective and was the compensating control that kept the finding at the lowest severity. Fourteen role assignments had been made directly by Basis outside the GRC workflow, which is why the test started with the population of changes, not the sample of requests."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT A LEAD ASKS Where do leavers come from, and how often does Security see them? Can anyone assign roles outside the request workflow? Who reviews privileged access, how often, and what do they do with what they find?"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS This is joiner-mover-leaver management you know from resource management, with an auditable trail."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 2.3 A.  A client says \"we lock leavers promptly\". What population do you ask for, and why not the list of locked users?  B.  The monthly privileged-access review was missed twice. What other control could compensate and what would you need to see?  C.  What questions would you ask about direct role assignment by Basis?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Where does the population for a leavers test come from?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What does a good user access review start with?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What are generic IDs and why are they a control point?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Each lifecycle stage has a control, a test and a population. Test from the authoritative source; look for the bypass. A compensating control keeps a finding in proportion."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 2.4 — How a Change Reaches Production", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 2.4 — How a Change Reaches Production"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Describe the development, quality and production landscape. Explain transports, change workflow and production settings. Say why direct changes are the main risk."]]}]}, {"title": "2.4.1 The landscape and the transport", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.4.1 The landscape and the transport"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Changes to programs and configuration are built in a development system (DEV), tested and signed off in a quality system (QAS) and then moved to production (PRD) by a transport. The transport route enforces the order: nothing should reach production without passing through QAS. A change-management tool such as ChaRM in Solution Manager adds an approval workflow and holds the evidence: request, approval, test sign-off and who imported."}]}, {"title": "2.4.2 The change controls", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.4.2 The change controls"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control", "What it ensures", "Typical failure"], ["Approved, tested, independent import", "Every transport has an approved request, test evidence and is imported by someone other than the developer", "Sign-off held outside the tool; developer imports own change"], ["Production closed to direct change", "Production is set to not modifiable and openings need an approved ticket and a time limit", "Production opened for urgent fixes without a ticket"], ["Debug-change and table-edit rights restricted and logged", "Only emergency IDs can change data in memory or edit tables directly", "Rights held by ordinary users; no log review"], ["Emergency changes approved quickly", "Urgent changes are approved within a short time after the event", "Retrospective approval chased late"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — START FROM WHAT PRODUCTION RECEIVED The population for a change test is what arrived in production (the import history), not the list of approved tickets. A ticket list can only show changes that went through the process; only the import history shows changes that did not."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane's production was opened twice without a ticket, in February and April, and two custom tables were changed in the first window. The cause was the 2025 upgrade: access and openings granted for speed were never withdrawn. The fix: an alert to the CIO and the controls team on every production opening, and a ticket made mandatory. Two of 27 sampled transports lacked test sign-off, and three emergency transports were approved 5 to 9 days late; the first was closed after retest, the second stays open until an emergency change occurs to demonstrate the new workflow."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT A LEAD ASKS Show me the transport route: is there any path to production that skips QAS? Who can import to production and can a developer? How often has production been opened for changes, and who approved each?"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS Change control in an SAP landscape is release management with the same questions you ask at go-live: who approved, was it tested, and who moved it?"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 2.4 A.  Why is the import history, not the ticket list, the right population for a change test?  B.  Production was opened for six hours on 14 April with no ticket. List three things you would look at.  C.  Why does a failed change control matter to the reliance on the three-way match?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What is a transport?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why should production normally be closed to direct change?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does an emergency-change control try to prevent?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Changes flow DEV, QAS, PRD through transports and an approval workflow. Production is closed to direct change; openings are controlled. The import history is the population for change tests."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 2.5 — Operations and the Layers beneath SAP", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 2.5 — Operations and the Layers beneath SAP"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Describe job, interface, backup and database controls. Explain why the database and the cloud integration layer are control points. Understand a service organisation report."]]}]}, {"title": "2.5.1 Operations", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.5.1 Operations"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Area", "Control", "Why it matters"], ["Batch jobs", "Critical jobs are monitored, failures are ticketed and rerun; jobs run under controlled technical users", "A failed job leaves postings incomplete; a job carries the access of its user"], ["Interfaces", "Errors on financial interfaces are monitored and resolved; files are reconciled; payment files are protected", "Data entering or leaving SAP can be lost or altered without any user action"], ["Backup and recovery", "Backups are monitored and a restore is tested", "Financial records must be recoverable"], ["Database and operating system", "Database administrators and operating-system accounts are named, limited and logged", "They can change financial tables outside SAP, bypassing application controls"]]}]}, {"title": "2.5.2 Integration and cloud services", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.5.2 Integration and cloud services"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Banks, customers, partners and payroll providers connect through interfaces: files, IDocs, RFC calls, and often a cloud integration platform. Each is a path for data. A provider that runs part of the landscape (hosting, integration) is a service organisation. It usually issues an independent report on its own controls, called a SOC 1 report. The client must check the report covers the period, read the exceptions, and perform its own \"complementary user-entity controls\" listed in it."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane found four AP users with write access to the payment file directory. A payment file is a list of who gets paid and how much; anyone who could edit it could redirect money. The fix was to limit the directory to the integration service account and add a hash check that alerts if the file the bank receives differs from the file SAP created. The integration platform's provider SOC 1 report covered the period and its client controls were mapped into the controls matrix. A database user called SYSTEM had been used for routine work on 22 days; that deficiency remains open until two quarterly reviews have operated."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT A LEAD ASKS How do you know when a critical job or interface fails, and what happens next? Who can write to the payment file after SAP creates it? Who holds database administrator rights, and is their activity logged and reviewed?"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS This is the operations handover you ask for at go-live: monitoring, support, recovery and a named owner for every moving part."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 2.5 A.  Why is the payment file directory a control point even though no SAP user touches the file?  B.  What do you check in a provider's SOC 1 report?  C.  Why does a database administrator matter to the financial statements?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Name four operations controls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What is a service organisation report?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why does a batch job's user matter?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Jobs, interfaces, backup and database access are all control points. Data can be altered outside SAP, so the layers beneath matter. Rely on a provider only after reading its report against your period and your own controls."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 2.6 — A Walkthrough Toolkit for the Lead", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 2.6 — A Walkthrough Toolkit for the Lead"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Run a walkthrough with a technical owner. Know what evidence to expect and what to treat as a red flag."]]}]}, {"title": "2.6.1 The walkthrough in four steps", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.6.1 The walkthrough in four steps"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Ask them to show, not tell. Ask to see the system, the report or the log."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Follow one real instance end to end, from trigger to evidence."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Ask how they would know if it failed."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Write it down in their words before you judge it."}]}, {"title": "2.6.2 Questions, evidence and red flags", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.6.2 Questions, evidence and red flags"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Area", "Ask", "Expect to see", "Red flag"], ["Access", "Who can get in and with what rights? Who approves?", "A request with approvals; role owner list", "Approvals by email only; roles assigned outside the tool"], ["Leavers", "How does Security learn of a leaver?", "A daily feed or report; lock dates", "Weekly report; manual lock"], ["Privileged", "Who holds broad access and what have they done?", "An approved list; a review; usage analysis", "Dialog users with SAP_ALL"], ["Change", "Show a transport from request to import", "Approved request, QAS sign-off, independent import", "Developer imports; sign-off by email"], ["Production", "When was production last opened and why?", "Ticket, time limit, closure, log review", "Opened without a ticket"], ["Operations", "What happens when a critical job fails?", "Ticket, rerun, confirmation", "Reliance on an email alert"], ["Interfaces", "How do you know a file arrived complete and unchanged?", "Monitoring, reconciliation, restricted directory", "\"The bank would tell us\""]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane's lead asked the payment-file owner \"how would you know if it failed?\" and got a pause followed by \"I suppose treasury would notice if the bank complained\". That answer became a risk (R-P2P-006) in week three, long before any test."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 2.6 Role-play. One person plays the Basis lead of an oil and gas company, the other the controls lead. Prepare six walkthrough questions on change, access and operations, say what evidence you expect for each and what would worry you. Swap roles."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why ask \"how would you know if it failed?\""}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why write the answer in the owner's words before judging it?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Name two red flags in the change area."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Show, follow, ask about failure, write it down. Know the expected evidence for each area. Red flags turn into risks early."]]}]}, {"title": "Module 2 Quiz", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Module 2 Quiz"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Fifteen questions, 2 marks each. Pass mark 24 out of 30. Answers follow the module 3 quiz."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What is the relationship between a user, a role and an authorisation?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Name the five SAP user types."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does SAP_ALL grant?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. What is segregation of duties?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. What do ARA, ARM and EAM do?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "6. What must be true of a mitigating control?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "7. Where does the population for a leavers test come from?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "8. Why is the population for a change test the import history?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "9. What is a transport?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "10. Why should production normally be closed to direct change?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "11. Why does the database layer matter to financial reporting?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "12. What is a SOC 1 report?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "13. What does a controls lead ask after \"show me\"?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "14. Name two red flags in the change area."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "15. Why does a failed ITGC matter to an automated control?"}]}, {"title": "Module 3 — Oil and Gas on SAP", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Module 3 — Oil and Gas on SAP"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Session", "Topic", "Hours"], ["3.1", "The oil and gas value chain and what it means for finance systems", "1"], ["3.2", "The SAP landscape of an oil and gas company", "1.25"], ["3.3", "The business processes and where the money moves", "1.5"], ["3.4", "Control risks that are particular to oil and gas", "1.25"], ["3.5", "Reading the Corvane foundation and a mini scoping exercise", "1"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 3.1 — The Value Chain and What It Means for Finance Systems", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 3.1 — The Value Chain and What It Means for Finance Systems"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Describe upstream, midstream and downstream. Explain joint ventures and why they shape the control environment."]]}]}, {"title": "3.1.1 Three parts of the chain", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.1.1 Three parts of the chain"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Part", "What happens", "Typical finance consequences"], ["Upstream (exploration and production)", "Finding and producing crude oil and gas", "Large capital projects; depletion of oil and gas properties; production volumes; joint ventures with partners"], ["Midstream", "Gathering, pipelines, storage, processing", "Tariff revenue; throughput volumes; asset-heavy; long-lived capital"], ["Downstream (refining and marketing)", "Refining crude into products and selling them", "Inventory of crude and products; commodity pricing; large sales volumes; supply chain"]]}]}, {"title": "3.1.2 Joint ventures", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.1.2 Joint ventures"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Many oil and gas assets are owned by several companies under a joint operating agreement (JOA). One party is the operator and incurs the costs; the others are partners who pay their share. The operator bills partners for their share (joint interest billing) and collects cash through cash calls. Joint venture accounting therefore carries receivables and payables with partners and revenue shared by equity. The equity shares and cost-allocation rules must match the agreements. This is a distinctive control area."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane is an integrated company with upstream, midstream and refining and marketing businesses and joint-venture partners in most of its assets, under four company codes. Joint venture accounting was brought into scope in the first steering committee because JV receivables, payables and revenue share are significant accounts. About 40 partners receive monthly statements."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS A joint venture is a client with partners who audit you: expect scrutiny of cost allocations, which makes accuracy controls a priority."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 3.1 Draw the value chain in three boxes and put under each one: (a) its main business processes in finance, (b) one control risk it creates. Compare with the answer key."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What distinguishes the upstream business from the downstream business?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Who bills whom in a joint venture?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why are JV equity settings a control point?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Three parts of the chain create different finance consequences. Joint ventures add partner receivables, payables, billing and equity rules. Allocation accuracy is a priority control area."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 3.2 — The SAP Landscape of an Oil and Gas Company", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 3.2 — The SAP Landscape of an Oil and Gas Company"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Name the components of a typical landscape. Read the Corvane landscape and say which systems are in scope and why."]]}]}, {"title": "3.2.1 Corvane's landscape", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.2.1 Corvane's landscape"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SYS-ID", "SID / client", "Env", "Purpose", "In scope"], ["SYS-01", "CED / 100", "DEV", "Development client: configuration and custom code; origin of all transports", "Y"], ["SYS-02", "CEQ / 200", "QAS", "Quality client: testing and business sign-off before production", "Y"], ["SYS-03", "CEP / 300", "PRD", "Primary financial system: FI/CO, MM, SD, PM, PS, JVA and HCM posting for all four company codes", "Y"], ["SYS-04", "GRP / 001", "PRD", "Access requests, SoD risk analysis, Firefighter (EAM) and user access review", "Y"], ["SYS-05", "FEP / 100", "PRD", "Fiori launchpad for invoice entry, journal posting and approvals", "Y"], ["SYS-06", "HDP / n/a", "PRD", "Database for CEP; database-level privileged users can bypass SAP security", "Y"], ["SYS-07", "BWP / 100", "PRD", "Consolidation and management reporting; feeds financial close reports (IPE)", "Y"], ["SYS-08", "CPI / n/a", "PRD", "Integration hub for bank, EDI, JV partner and invoice flows", "Y"], ["SYS-09", "SMP / 100", "PRD", "Change request management and transport approval workflow; evidence source for Program Change", "Y"], ["SYS-10", "CES / 400", "SBX", "Sandbox for experimentation; no production data", "N"], ["SYS-11", "CEQ / 210", "TRN", "End-user training client in QAS with masked data", "N"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Two systems are excluded with a rationale (the sandbox and the training client: no financial data and no transport path to production). The database and the cloud integration tenant are in scope because each can change or carry financial data without any SAP user."}]}, {"title": "3.2.2 Interfaces", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.2.2 Interfaces"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["INT-ID", "Name", "Source to target", "Technology", "Monitoring at scoping"], ["INT-01", "Bank statements inbound", "Banks (host-to-host) to SYS-03", "CPI", "CPI monitor"], ["INT-02", "Vendor payment files outbound", "SYS-03 to Banks (host-to-host)", "CPI", "Other documented"], ["INT-03", "Field production volumes", "Production allocation system (external) to SYS-03", "SFTP file", "Email alert"], ["INT-04", "Payroll journal", "Payroll provider (external) to SYS-03", "SFTP file", "Email alert"], ["INT-05", "Customer nominations and orders (EDI)", "Customers (EDI) to SYS-03", "IDoc", "WE02/WE05"], ["INT-06", "Supplier invoices (scanning platform)", "Invoice scanning platform (external) to SYS-03", "OData", "Other documented"], ["INT-07", "GRC to S/4HANA connector", "SYS-04 to SYS-03", "RFC", "SM58"], ["INT-08", "BW extraction", "SYS-03 to SYS-07", "RFC", "Other documented"], ["INT-09", "JV partner statements", "SYS-03 to JV partners (portal)", "CPI", "CPI monitor"], ["INT-10", "ChaRM to S/4HANA transport connector", "SYS-09 to SYS-03", "RFC", "SM58"], ["INT-11", "Journal upload program ZFI_JE_UPLOAD", "Excel files (finance users) to SYS-03", "Manual upload (LSMW/LTMC)", "None"]]}]}, {"title": "3.2.3 Oil and gas specific flows", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.2.3 Oil and gas specific flows"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Production volumes arrive daily from a field allocation system and drive revenue and joint venture billing."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Bank files in and out through a cloud integration platform carry statements and payment instructions."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Partner statements go out monthly to joint venture partners."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Payroll is run by an external provider; SAP receives a posting journal."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE The scoping of the landscape found things that were not on the diagram: a Fiori front-end server that posted invoices, a sandbox, a training client, and a cloud integration platform. Eight systems on the diagram became eleven. Six of the eleven interfaces had weak or no monitoring or reconciliation and became risks."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS A landscape diagram is a requirements baseline. Validate it against the live systems, as you would validate a scope baseline."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 3.2 A.  From the table, which systems are in scope and which two are excluded? Why?  B.  Which interfaces would worry you most for revenue and cash?  C.  Which question do you ask for each interface?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why is the database layer in scope?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why were two systems excluded?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What do you ask about each interface?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS The diagram is a claim; validate it. Interfaces are paths for data without a user. Weak monitoring becomes a risk."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 3.3 — The Business Processes and Where the Money Moves", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 3.3 — The Business Processes and Where the Money Moves"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Name the main processes in an oil and gas finance landscape. Map processes to financial statement accounts."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["BP-ID", "Process", "Modules", "Owner"], ["BP-R2R-01", "Manual journal entries and journal upload", "FI", "Financial Controller"], ["BP-R2R-02", "Period-end close and consolidation", "FI, CO", "Financial Controller"], ["BP-R2R-03", "Fixed assets and capitalisation (DD&A)", "FI-AA, PS", "Capital Projects Controller"], ["BP-P2P-01", "Vendor master maintenance", "MM, FI", "Head of AP"], ["BP-P2P-02", "Purchasing, service entry, receipt and invoice", "MM, FI", "Head of Procurement"], ["BP-P2P-03", "Vendor payment", "FI", "Treasury Manager"], ["BP-INV-01", "Inventory movements and valuation (spares, crude, products)", "MM", "Supply Chain Manager"], ["BP-O2C-01", "Customer master and credit management", "SD, FI", "Credit Manager"], ["BP-O2C-02", "Crude and product sales: order, delivery, billing, revenue", "SD, FI", "Commercial Manager"], ["BP-JVA-01", "Joint venture accounting: cost allocation, cash calls and billing", "FI-JVA", "JV Accounting Manager"], ["BP-PM-01", "Maintenance orders and spares consumption", "PM, MM", "Maintenance Manager"], ["BP-PS-01", "Capital projects: AFE budgeting and settlement", "PS", "Capital Projects Controller"], ["BP-H2R-01", "Payroll posting to FI", "HCM, FI", "HR Director"], ["BP-TRE-01", "Bank and treasury: statements and bank reconciliation", "FI-BL", "Treasury Manager"], ["BP-TECH-01", "User administration and access", "Basis/Security", "IT Security Manager"], ["BP-TECH-02", "Change management", "Basis", "Basis Lead"], ["BP-TECH-03", "IT operations: jobs, interfaces, backup", "Basis", "Basis Lead"], ["BP-TECH-04", "Database and operating-system privileged access", "Basis", "Basis Lead / DBA"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The same processes map to the financial statement accounts that matter. A controls project starts from the accounts and works back to the processes that create their balances."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI", "Account", "Processes that feed it"], ["FSLI-01", "Cash and cash equivalents", "BP-TRE-01, BP-P2P-03"], ["FSLI-02", "Trade receivables", "BP-O2C-01, BP-O2C-02"], ["FSLI-03", "Joint venture receivables", "BP-JVA-01"], ["FSLI-04", "Inventory (spares, crude, products)", "BP-INV-01, BP-PM-01"], ["FSLI-05", "Property, plant and equipment (oil and gas properties)", "BP-R2R-03, BP-PS-01"], ["FSLI-06", "Construction in progress", "BP-PS-01, BP-R2R-03"], ["FSLI-07", "Trade payables and accruals", "BP-P2P-01, BP-P2P-02, BP-P2P-03"], ["FSLI-08", "Joint venture payables", "BP-JVA-01"], ["FSLI-09", "Revenue (crude, products, tariffs)", "BP-O2C-02, BP-JVA-01"], ["FSLI-10", "Operating expenses", "BP-P2P-02, BP-PM-01"], ["FSLI-11", "Depreciation, depletion and amortisation", "BP-R2R-03"], ["FSLI-12", "Payroll expense and accruals", "BP-H2R-01"], ["FSLI-13", "Consolidated equity and reserves", "BP-R2R-02"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — MODULES ARE NOT PROCESSES Procure-to-pay crosses purchasing and finance modules. Sales of crude cross sales, finance and an external volume feed. Always scope by process and account, then note the modules."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS This is a work breakdown structure for finance: accounts at the top, processes below, systems underneath."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 3.3 A.  Which processes feed \"Revenue\" and \"Joint venture receivables\"?  B.  Which technical areas are included and why?  C.  Name the process whose scope was unclear at the start of the project and how it was resolved."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why start from the accounts?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Name three processes that cross more than one module."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why do technical areas get their own rows?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Scope from accounts to processes to systems. A process often crosses modules. Technical areas anchor the general controls."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 3.4 — Control Risks Particular to Oil and Gas", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 3.4 — Control Risks Particular to Oil and Gas"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Write risk statements in the standard formula for oil and gas areas. Link each to the control that answers it."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Area", "Risk statement (abridged)", "Corvane control and result"], ["Production volumes", "Revenue incomplete or inaccurate because allocated volumes are not completely or correctly loaded (R-O2C-002)", "Daily volume reconciliation C-BPC-O2C-001: design gap found (spreadsheet with no independent review); redesign in the workflow tool; open"], ["Joint ventures", "JV costs billed incorrectly because equity shares and allocation rules can be changed without review (R-JVA-001)", "Locked equity configuration C-ITAC-009 effective; quarterly change review C-BPC-JVA-002"], ["JV statements", "Partner statements incomplete or altered because not reconciled to the billing (R-JVA-002)", "Monthly reconciliation C-BPC-JVA-001: one month lacked control totals; open"], ["Capital projects", "Operating costs capitalised because settlements are not reviewed for eligibility (R-R2R-005)", "Eligibility review C-BPC-R2R-003: one settlement lacked documentation; open"], ["Depletion", "DD&A miscalculated because asset data and unit-of-production inputs can change without review (R-R2R-006)", "Depreciation configuration effective; input file unlocked (design gap); open"], ["Payments", "Payment file altered between creation and bank (R-P2P-006)", "File integrity C-BPC-P2P-003: directory access and hash check added; closed"], ["Vendor fraud", "Fictitious vendor created and paid; bank details changed to divert payment (R-P2P-001, R-P2P-002)", "SoD rules, call-back and weekly review; closed after retest"], ["Inventory", "Inventory adjusted without authorisation (R-INV-001)", "Weekly adjustment review; explanations required; closed"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane's risk register has 8 interface risks, 4 SoD risks and 6 automated-control risks among its 36. Interfaces matter because so many oil and gas flows (volumes, banks, partners) arrive or leave without a user."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS Industry risk is domain knowledge: the same project method, applied to risks only an oil and gas person would think of."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 3.4 A.  Write two risk statements in the standard formula for (i) production volumes and (ii) JV billing.  B.  For each, name a control and say who owns it.  C.  Which of the eight areas above would you test first, and why?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why are production volumes a revenue risk?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What is joint interest billing?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why is depletion a control risk?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Oil and gas risks centre on volumes, joint ventures, capital, depletion and payments. Interfaces carry many of them. Each risk has a control and an owner."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 3.5 — Reading the Corvane Foundation and a Mini Scoping Exercise", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 3.5 — Reading the Corvane Foundation and a Mini Scoping Exercise"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Find the Corvane facts in W01 to W04. Do a first-pass scoping of a different oil and gas company."]]}]}, {"title": "3.5.1 A map of the Corvane foundation", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.5.1 A map of the Corvane foundation"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Where to look"], ["Which systems are in scope?", "W01 System_Inventory"], ["Which interfaces carry financial data and how are they monitored?", "W02 Interface_Register"], ["Which processes and accounts?", "W03 Process_Scope and FSLI_Map"], ["What could go wrong?", "W04 Risk_Register (36 risks, 32 SOX-relevant)"], ["Which controls answer the risks?", "W05 Control_Register (47 controls, 38 key)"]]}]}, {"title": "3.5.2 Mini scoping exercise", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.5.2 Mini scoping exercise"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Redwater Pipeline and Terminals (fictitious) is a US-listed midstream company with one S/4HANA production system, a GRC system and a separate treasury system. It runs crude pipelines and storage terminals, earns tariff revenue and storage fees, and buys capital equipment for pipeline projects. Throughput volumes arrive daily from metering systems. Customers are billed monthly. Banks connect through host-to-host files. Payroll is outsourced. The CFO wants a SOX controls project and has asked you to lead it."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 3.5 In one page, list: (a) the likely systems; (b) the likely interfaces; (c) the processes and accounts; (d) five risks written in the standard formula; (e) three stakeholders and what each wants; (f) the first three questions you would ask the CFO."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Where would you find the list of interfaces and their monitoring in the Corvane file?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why is throughput volume to a midstream company like production volume to an upstream one?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What is the first thing you would validate on a new client's landscape diagram?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Use W01 to W05 as the map of a foundation. Volumes, banks, capital and payroll recur across oil and gas companies. Scope from the accounts and validate the diagram."]]}]}, {"title": "Module 3 Quiz", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Module 3 Quiz"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Twelve questions, 2 marks each. Pass mark 20 out of 24. Answers follow."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Name the three parts of the oil and gas value chain."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What is a joint operating agreement?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Who bills whom in a joint venture?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Why is a payment file a control point?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Name two oil and gas processes that rely on an external volume feed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "6. Why are two of Corvane's eleven systems out of scope?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "7. Why start scoping from the financial statement accounts?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "8. Name three technical areas that anchor the general controls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "9. What is depletion?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "10. Why is a capital project a control risk?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "11. Why does a database administrator matter?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "12. What is the first check on a landscape diagram?"}]}, {"title": "Answer Key to Module 2", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Answer Key to Module 2"}]}, {"title": "Session 2.1", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 2.1"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: (i) dialog; (ii) communication; (iii) system. B: SAP_ALL gives a user every right in the system, so that user can bypass every control, post any entry and change any record; a person who logs on every day should not have that power because there would be no way to tell a legitimate action from a harmful one without reviewing everything they did. C: Who holds it and why? Can it be replaced by a controlled emergency ID? What has the user done with it in the period?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) Users are given roles; roles contain authorisation objects with fields and values that decide exactly what the user may do. (2) System, communication and reference users cannot be used by a person to log on to the screen; service users allow shared dialog access, so they need tight limits. (3) Usage shows whether the risk materialised."}]}, {"title": "Session 2.2", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 2.2"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: Conflicts: (i), (iii), (iv), (v). (ii) is not. B: It must exist, have a named owner, be tested and found effective, and be valid for the period. C: It treats holding a transaction as the same as being able to do something, ignoring whether the authorisation values allow the action, so it reports conflicts that cannot actually happen."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) ARA runs the SoD rules; EAM controls emergency (Firefighter) access. (2) Because a mitigation only detects after the event and these conflicts allow fraud. (3) See B."}]}, {"title": "Session 2.3", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 2.3"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: The HR leavers report, because the HR report is the system of record for terminations; a list of locked users shows only those that were removed and cannot show leavers who were missed. B: The quarterly access review, if it tested effective and was precise enough to find leavers and privileged holders; see the listing reconciled to the user tables and the removals. C: Who can do it, why, how many were made, whether each had an approved request later, and what the users did with the access."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) The HR leavers report. (2) A listing that is proven complete against the user tables. (3) Shared or technical IDs with no individual owner; they remove accountability."}]}, {"title": "Session 2.4", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 2.4"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: Because only the import history shows changes that bypassed the process. B: The log of who opened it and why; the table change log for the window; the transports and users active at that time. C: Because the three-way match is a setting; if changes were not controlled it may have been altered, so the presumption of reliance is gone and extra procedures are needed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) A package that moves a change from one SAP system to the next. (2) Because direct changes bypass approval, testing and the audit trail. (3) Emergency changes that go unapproved for too long."}]}, {"title": "Session 2.5", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 2.5"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: Because anyone with write access to the file can alter payments after SAP creates it, with no SAP user and no SAP log. B: That it covers your period (or has a bridge letter), the exceptions noted and whether they affect you, and the client controls it lists that you must perform. C: A database administrator can change financial data outside SAP, bypassing every application control."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) Job monitoring, interface monitoring, backup and restore, database access. (2) An independent report on a provider's controls (SOC 1). (3) A job carries the authorisations of its user."}]}, {"title": "Session 2.6", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 2.6"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Model questions: Show me a transport from request to import; who can import to production; when was production last opened and who approved; how does Security learn of a leaver; who holds SAP_ALL; what happens when a critical job fails. Expect: tool evidence for each. Worry about: approvals held outside the tool, openings without tickets, dialog users with SAP_ALL, weekly manual feeds, reliance on emails. Check yourself: (1) The answer becomes the risk register. (2) To avoid judging before you understand. (3) Developer imports own change; sign-off held outside the change tool; production opened without a ticket."}]}, {"title": "Module 2 quiz answers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Module 2 quiz answers"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Q", "Answer"], ["1", "Users get roles; roles contain authorisations that decide what they can do"], ["2", "Dialog, system, communication, service, reference"], ["3", "Every authorisation in the system"], ["4", "No one person can complete a sensitive process alone"], ["5", "ARA analyses SoD risk; ARM runs access requests and reviews; EAM controls emergency access"], ["6", "It exists, is owned, tested effective and current"], ["7", "The HR leavers report"], ["8", "It shows everything that reached production, including changes that bypassed the process"], ["9", "A package that moves a change between SAP systems"], ["10", "Direct changes bypass approval, testing and the audit trail"], ["11", "It can change financial data outside SAP"], ["12", "An independent report on a service provider's controls"], ["13", "\"How would you know if it failed?\""], ["14", "Any two: developer imports own change; sign-off outside the tool; production opened without a ticket"], ["15", "It removes the presumption that the setting did not change; additional procedures are needed"]]}]}, {"title": "Answer Key to Module 3", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Answer Key to Module 3"}]}, {"title": "Session 3.1", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 3.1"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Upstream: capital projects, depletion, production volumes, joint ventures; risk: capitalising operating cost or incomplete volumes. Midstream: tariff revenue, throughput volumes, long-lived assets; risk: wrong volumes or tariffs. Downstream: inventory of crude and products, commodity pricing, sales volumes; risk: misvalued inventory or unauthorised adjustments. Check yourself: (1) Upstream finds and produces; downstream refines and sells. (2) The operator bills partners for their share. (3) Wrong equity or allocation rules misstate partner receivables and payables."}]}, {"title": "Session 3.2", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 3.2"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: Nine are in scope; the sandbox (SYS-10) and training client (SYS-11) are excluded because they hold no financial data and have no transport path to production. B: Production volumes (INT-03), the payment file (INT-02), bank statements (INT-01), partner statements (INT-09). C: How would you know if it failed, and who looks? Check yourself: (1) It can change financial tables outside SAP. (2) No financial data and no route to production. (3) How would you know if it failed."}]}, {"title": "Session 3.3", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 3.3"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: Revenue: BP-O2C-02 and BP-JVA-01. JV receivables: BP-JVA-01. B: BP-TECH-01 to 04 (access, change, operations, database), because the general controls cut across all processes. C: Payroll: it was unclear until HR confirmed that payroll runs externally and SAP receives only the posting journal. Check yourself: (1) Because the statements are what the assessment is about. (2) Procure-to-pay, order-to-cash, payroll posting. (3) The general controls have no business owner and need an anchor."}]}, {"title": "Session 3.4", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 3.4"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: (i) There is a risk that revenue is incomplete or inaccurate because allocated production volumes are not completely or correctly loaded, resulting in misstated revenue and receivables. (ii) There is a risk that joint venture costs are billed to partners incorrectly because equity shares and allocation rules can be changed without review, resulting in misstated JV balances. B: Daily volume reconciliation (Commercial Manager); locked equity configuration and quarterly change review (JV Accounting Manager). C: Production volumes or the payment file: the highest value, the least user involvement. Check yourself: (1) Revenue is built on them. (2) The operator bills partners their share of costs. (3) A wrong depletion rate misstates asset values and expense."}]}, {"title": "Session 3.5", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 3.5"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Model answer for Redwater. (a) S/4HANA production, GRC, treasury system, metering feed, integration layer, change system, database. (b) Metering to SAP (throughput), customer billing, bank files in and out, payroll journal. (c) Processes: R2R, P2P, O2C (tariff and storage billing), capital projects, treasury, payroll posting, technical areas; accounts: revenue, receivables, PP&E, construction in progress, payables, cash. (d) For example: throughput volumes loaded incompletely resulting in understated revenue; payment file altered; fictitious vendor created and paid; capital cost recorded as expense or the reverse; leavers keeping access. (e) CFO (clean opinion), CIO (team treated as partners), audit partner (reliance). (f) Success measure, access for the team, materiality and scope. Check yourself: (1) W02 Interface_Register. (2) Both feed revenue directly and arrive from an external system. (3) That every system is real and in scope."}]}, {"title": "Module 3 quiz answers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Module 3 quiz answers"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Q", "Answer"], ["1", "Upstream, midstream, downstream"], ["2", "An agreement between parties that jointly own an oil and gas asset and set shares and costs"], ["3", "The operator bills the partners for their share"], ["4", "Anyone who can alter it can redirect payments"], ["5", "Revenue, JV billing"], ["6", "They hold no financial data and have no transport path to production"], ["7", "The statements are what the assessment is about; processes are scoped to explain them"], ["8", "Access, change, operations, database"], ["9", "The charging of the cost of oil and gas properties against production, typically by unit of production"], ["10", "Operating cost can be capitalised wrongly, overstating assets"], ["11", "Can change financial data outside SAP"], ["12", "Is every system real and in scope?"]]}]}], "word_count": 2980, "id": "tib-bridge-manual-b-modules-2-3", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/01_Bridge_Manuals/TIB_Bridge_Manual_B_Modules_2-3.docx", "filename": "TIB_Bridge_Manual_B_Modules_2-3.docx", "category": "Bridge Manuals", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB Controls Project Estimating Model — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC | From SAP Project Manager to Controls Lead"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Controls Project Estimating Model"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A driver-based model that sizes the effort, duration and team of an SAP controls project from counts of systems, interfaces, processes and controls. It is calibrated so that the Corvane Energy Group case reproduces its actual team (16 people), duration (38 weeks) and utilisation (80 percent)."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use it"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Inputs tab: enter your project in column D. Leave the optional risk, control, key-control and SoD fields blank to derive them from the number of processes using the Corvane ratios; type a number to override."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Factors tab: the unit efforts and ratios. Replace them with your own firm's history as soon as you have it."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Estimate tab: read effort by phase, total effort, duration, phase weeks, the retest-window check and a suggested pod structure. Columns B (Corvane) and C (Redwater example) stay as references."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow with blue text = input. Grey = formula. Do not overwrite grey cells."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Important limits"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The factors are illustrative planning assumptions calibrated to one case, not industry benchmarks. Use the model to structure a conversation and to compare options; present results as a range and state the assumptions. Landscape complexity, client readiness and the operating period your retests need can move the result materially."}]}], "word_count": 220, "id": "tib-controls-project-estimating-model-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/02_Planning_Module/TIB_Controls_Project_Estimating_Model.xlsx", "filename": "TIB_Controls_Project_Estimating_Model.xlsx", "category": "Planning Module", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From SAP Project Manager to Controls Lead"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Module 9: Planning and Estimating a New Controls Project"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Course manual  |  Week 7 of the eight-week programme  |  with the Controls Project Estimating Model (Excel)  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Examples come from the completed Corvane Energy Group case file, a fictitious oil and gas company."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "About Module 9", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "About Module 9"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "You can already plan a project. This module shows what is different about planning a controls project: the plan is built around evidence and retest periods, the size depends on counts of systems, processes and controls rather than on a build specification, and the conditions that protect the project must be agreed before it starts. It has six sessions and about eight hours of work, and comes with an Excel estimating model."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Session", "Topic", "Hours"], ["9.1", "Planning logic: gates, order and the retest calendar", "1.5"], ["9.2", "Sizing the project from its drivers", "1.5"], ["9.3", "Estimating effort and duration with the model", "1.5"], ["9.4", "Staffing and pod design", "1"], ["9.5", "Engagement conditions, governance and RAID", "1"], ["9.6", "Building the planning pack (workshop)", "1.5"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["A WARNING ABOUT THE NUMBERS The unit efforts in this module are illustrative planning assumptions calibrated so that the model reproduces the Corvane case (16 people, 38 weeks, 80 percent utilisation). They are not industry benchmarks. Replace them with your own history as soon as you have it, show your estimate as a range, and always state the assumptions."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 9.1 — Planning Logic: Gates, Order and the Retest Calendar", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 9.1 — Planning Logic: Gates, Order and the Retest Calendar"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Lay out a controls project in phases and gates. Explain why the plan is worked backward from the retest. Use the Corvane Weekly Activity Plan as a template."]]}]}, {"title": "9.1.1 The shape of the project", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.1.1 The shape of the project"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A controls project follows the same nine stages and gates every time. What changes is how long each takes. At Corvane the 38 calendar weeks were distributed as follows:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Phase", "Weeks", "Gate at the end", "What it proves"], ["Mobilise", "1", "—", "Scope signed; team and access in hand"], ["Environment", "5", "G1 (week 4), G2 (week 6)", "Systems, interfaces and processes validated"], ["Risk and control design", "7", "G3 (week 8), G4 (week 11), G5 (week 13)", "Risks agreed; controls and SoD rules approved; owners confirmed"], ["Evidence and test planning", "9", "G7 (week 19), G6 (week 22)", "Test plan approved; populations passed IPE"], ["Testing", "7", "G8 (week 29)", "All tests done and reviewed"], ["Evaluation", "2", "—", "Deficiencies evaluated, grouped and rated; reliance decided"], ["Remediation", "3", "—", "Fixes implemented"], ["Retest and close", "4", "G9 (week 37), close-out (week 38)", "Retests done; conclusion signed"]]}]}, {"title": "9.1.2 The three planning rules", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.1.2 The three planning rules"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "General controls before automated controls. Their results decide how much reliance the automated controls can have. The Corvane access and change tests ran in weeks 23 to 26 and the automated tests in weeks 27 to 29."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Population before sample. Evidence requests are in waves: scoping and configuration, populations, then samples. Nothing is sampled before its population has passed the completeness check."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Work backward from the retest. A deficiency is closed only when a retest shows the remediated control operating, and a control needs a minimum operating period before a retest means anything."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control frequency", "Typical minimum operating period before retest"], ["Daily or event-driven", "About four to five weeks of occurrences (20 or more items)"], ["Weekly", "About five weeks"], ["Monthly", "Two month-ends"], ["Quarterly", "Two quarters"], ["Annual", "One year; often cannot be retested in the project"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane planned remediation for August to September and underestimated how long weekly and monthly controls need to operate. The result was nine deficiencies still open at the end, all at the lowest severity: five monthly or quarterly controls awaiting more cycles, one awaiting an emergency change, two design fixes still in progress and one awaiting qualifying settlements. In his own account the lead said that pulling weekly and monthly fixes forward by three weeks would have closed at least two of them. That is the retest calendar working against a plan built forward."]]}]}, {"title": "9.1.3 The Weekly Activity Plan as a template", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.1.3 The Weekly Activity Plan as a template"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The Corvane Weekly Activity Plan lists 287 discrete activities across 38 weeks, with a phase, a gate, an owner, supporters, client stakeholders, the workbook and the output for each. Copy it, change the start date on the Setup tab, and replace the activities for your project. Keep the structure: one owner per activity, an output for each, and the gates in the weeks they must fall."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS This is a stage-gate plan with an unusual critical path: the longest chain is not the build but the sequence evidence, testing, evaluation, fix, operate, retest. Plan the operating period as a task in its own right."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 9.1 A client needs the assessment to be remediated by 30 November. Weekly controls need about five weeks of operation before the retest ends and monthly controls need two month-ends. Assume the retest must finish by 23 November to leave a week for review.  A.  By what date must fixes for weekly controls be live?  B.  By what date must fixes for monthly controls be live, and which month-ends do they cover?  C.  What do you say about quarterly controls?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why do general controls come before automated controls in the plan?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What is a wave of evidence requests?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why is the longest chain of a controls project not the testing?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS The nine gates are constant; the durations change. Order: general controls, then populations, then samples. Work backward from the retest and the operating period."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 9.2 — Sizing the Project from Its Drivers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 9.2 — Sizing the Project from Its Drivers"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Name the drivers that determine the size of a controls project. Assess landscape complexity and client readiness."]]}]}, {"title": "9.2.1 Drivers, not deliverables", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.2.1 Drivers, not deliverables"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A controls project does not have a build specification. Its size comes from counts. The more systems, interfaces, processes and key controls there are, the more there is to scope, request evidence for, test and evaluate."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Driver", "Corvane", "What it drives"], ["Systems in scope", "11 (9 in scope)", "Landscape validation, evidence, owners"], ["Financial interfaces", "11", "Interview, monitoring, risks"], ["Processes and technical areas", "18", "Walkthroughs, mapping, owners"], ["Risks", "36 (32 SOX-relevant)", "Workshops, rating"], ["Controls / key controls", "47 / 38", "Descriptions, links, tests"], ["SoD rules", "16", "Ruleset design and analysis"], ["Evidence requests / IPE checks", "95 / 45", "Evidence handling and population checks"], ["Deficiencies", "30", "Evaluation, remediation support, retest"]]}]}, {"title": "9.2.2 Early sizing from what you know", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.2.2 Early sizing from what you know"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "At proposal stage you know only a few things: how many systems and interfaces the client has and roughly how many processes. From the Corvane ratios you can derive the rest: about two risks per process, 1.3 controls per risk, 0.8 key controls per control, 2.5 evidence requests per key control and 0.8 deficiencies per key control. The Estimating Model does this when you leave the optional fields blank."}]}, {"title": "9.2.3 Complexity and readiness", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.2.3 Complexity and readiness"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Two clients with the same counts can need very different effort. Rate readiness with ten questions. Count the answers that are No."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Readiness question", "Yes / No"], ["1", "Is there a current landscape diagram and a system owner for each system?", ""], ["2", "Are there documented process narratives for the key processes?", ""], ["3", "Is there an existing risk and control matrix?", ""], ["4", "Has an SoD ruleset been approved and is a GRC tool in use?", ""], ["5", "Is there a change-management tool with approval evidence?", ""], ["6", "Do HR leavers reach Security daily?", ""], ["7", "Can the team get read-only access to every system in scope?", ""], ["8", "Is there a named executive sponsor who will attend steering?", ""], ["9", "Do the control and evidence owners know what is expected of them?", ""], ["10", "Has the external auditor stated what it will accept?", ""]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Number of No answers", "Complexity setting in the model"], ["0 to 2", "Low (multiplier 0.8)"], ["3 to 5", "Medium (1.0)"], ["6 or more", "High (1.25)"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane answered No to several: the landscape diagram missed three systems, the leavers feed was weekly, owners did not know their evidence, and the extracts had gaps. It was set as Medium, and the model was calibrated on it. A client with more No answers would need a larger multiplier."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS Readiness is the controls equivalent of requirement maturity: the less defined it is, the larger the contingency."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 9.2 Redwater Pipeline and Terminals (from Module 3) has seven systems, six financial interfaces and 12 processes and technical areas. It has a current landscape diagram, documented process narratives for the main processes, no risk and control matrix, a GRC tool without an approved ruleset, a change tool, a daily HR feed, difficulty arranging access, a sponsor, owners who are not briefed, and an auditor who has not stated expectations.  A.  Count the No answers and set the complexity.  B.  Derive the number of risks, controls and key controls using the ratios."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Name four drivers of effort."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why derive counts from processes at proposal stage?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does a high number of No answers do to the estimate?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Size comes from counts of systems, interfaces, processes and controls. Ratios let you derive the rest early. Readiness sets the complexity multiplier."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 9.3 — Estimating Effort and Duration with the Model", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 9.3 — Estimating Effort and Duration with the Model"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Use the Controls Project Estimating Model. Explain how it is calibrated and where it is weak. Present an estimate as a range with assumptions."]]}]}, {"title": "9.3.1 How the model works", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.3.1 How the model works"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The model multiplies each driver by a unit effort, adds the phase totals, applies a complexity multiplier and adds overhead for project management and quality review. It then divides by the team's weekly capacity to get a duration."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Unit effort (person-days)", "Value"], ["Per system in scope", "8"], ["Per financial interface", "6"], ["Per process or technical area", "10"], ["Per risk", "2"], ["Per control", "4"], ["Per SoD rule", "6"], ["Per owner confirmation", "1"], ["Per evidence request", "3"], ["Per IPE check", "2"], ["Per key-control test (design plus operating)", "20"], ["Per deficiency (evaluation, remediation support, retest)", "12"], ["Overhead", "10 percent"]]}]}, {"title": "9.3.2 Calibration and its limits", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.3.2 Calibration and its limits"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "With the Corvane counts the model gives 2,455 person-days. The Corvane team of 16 working 38 weeks at 80 percent utilisation had 2,432 person-days of capacity, a difference of under 1 percent, and the model returns a duration of 38 weeks. That is calibration, not proof. It shows the model reproduces one case. It does not show that the unit efforts are right for another client, another landscape or another firm. Treat it as a structured starting point."}]}, {"title": "9.3.3 Worked example: Redwater", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.3.3 Worked example: Redwater"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Redwater result"], ["Systems / interfaces / processes", "7 / 6 / 12"], ["Derived risks / controls / key controls", "24 / 31 / 25"], ["SoD rules / evidence requests / IPE checks", "11 / 63 / 30"], ["Expected deficiencies", "20"], ["Effort by phase: environment", "212"], ["Risk and control design", "269"], ["Evidence", "249"], ["Testing", "500"], ["Evaluation, remediation and retest", "240"], ["Subtotal / overhead / total", "1,470 / 147 / 1,617 person-days"], ["Team of 10 at 80 percent: capacity per week", "40 person-days"], ["Duration", "40 weeks"], ["Phase weeks", "1, 5, 7, 9, 7, 2, 3, 6"], ["Retest window (remediation plus retest)", "9 weeks: covers a 5-week operating period"]]}]}, {"title": "9.3.4 Ranges, scenarios and what to say", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.3.4 Ranges, scenarios and what to say"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Give a range. A plus or minus 20 percent range on 1,617 person-days is 1,294 to 1,940."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Show the levers. A team of 14 instead of 10 cuts the duration to 29 weeks. A High complexity setting raises the total to about 2,021 person-days and, with a team of 10, the duration to 51 weeks."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "State the assumptions. Counts of systems and processes, readiness, utilisation, the operating period the retests must cover, and what is excluded."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Revisit at gates. Re-estimate at G2, when the scope is known, and at G4, when the controls are known."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE The Corvane lead gave the sponsor a plan with a single start and end date and no range. A range would have told the CFO early that closing nine deficiencies depended on operating periods, not on effort. That is the point of the retest-window check on the Estimate tab."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS This is parametric estimating with a calibration case. Treat the first estimate as a class 4 estimate and tighten it as scope and controls become known."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 9.3 Open TIB_Controls_Project_Estimating_Model.xlsx.  A.  In the Inputs tab, enter in column D the Redwater counts from 9.2 (7, 6, 12) with Medium complexity, a team of 10, 80 percent and a 5-week retest period. Check that you get 1,617 person-days and 40 weeks.  B.  Change the team to 14. What is the duration?  C.  Change complexity to High with the team at 10. What are the total and the duration?  D.  Change the operating period to 9 weeks. What does the retest-window check say and what does that mean for your plan?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What does \"calibrated to the Corvane case\" mean and not mean?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why give an estimate as a range?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does the retest-window check protect against?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Drivers times unit efforts, a complexity multiplier and overhead give effort; capacity gives duration. Calibration to one case is a starting point, not a benchmark. Give ranges, show levers and state assumptions."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 9.4 — Staffing and Pod Design", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 9.4 — Staffing and Pod Design"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Design a pod structure for 8, 16 and 25 people. Apply span-of-control and capacity rules."]]}]}, {"title": "9.4.1 Pods by team size", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.4.1 Pods by team size"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Pod", "8 people", "16 people (Corvane)", "25 people"], ["Lead", "1", "1", "1"], ["Programme office", "1 (with evidence)", "1", "2"], ["Training and change", "—", "1", "1"], ["Security and GRC", "2", "3", "4"], ["ITGC (change and operations)", "2", "3", "5"], ["Finance controls", "2", "5", "8"], ["Data and evidence", "—", "2", "3"], ["Integration and BW specialists", "—", "—", "1"], ["Total", "8", "16", "25"]]}]}, {"title": "9.4.2 Rules", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.4.2 Rules"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Each pod has a lead with decision rights, so the lead manages four to six conversations, not fifteen."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A tester never reviews their own work. The reviewer is the pod lead; the lead reviews the pod leads."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Put data and evidence in the middle. In larger teams, an evidence coordinator and a data analyst stand between testing and filing. In the smallest teams, combine them with the programme office."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Plan to 80 percent of nominal hours. The peaks are test planning and sampling, and the deficiency evaluation fortnight."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Name a deputy for each pod lead and for the evidence coordinator."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IN THE CORVANE CASE Corvane's pods were Security and GRC, ITGC, Finance controls, and Data and evidence, plus the programme office and a training and change champion. During the general-control tests the finance pod had few tester activities and used the time for walkthrough summaries and owner guides. The Weekly Activity Plan's Pod_Activity view shows exactly where each pod is busy and where it can help another."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PM LENS Pods are the project teams you already know, with one difference: the data and evidence role gates what the testers may sample."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 9.4 Design a team of 12 for Redwater: list the pods, the number in each, the leads, the reviewer for each tester and the deputies. State where you would combine roles."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why must a tester not review their own work?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What is the role of the data and evidence pod?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why plan at 80 percent utilisation?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Pods with leads keep the span of control manageable. Data and evidence sit between testing and filing. Plan at 80 percent and name deputies."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 9.5 — Engagement Conditions, Governance and RAID", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 9.5 — Engagement Conditions, Governance and RAID"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Write the conditions that protect a controls project. Set up governance and a first RAID log."]]}]}, {"title": "9.5.1 Engagement conditions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.5.1 Engagement conditions"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Agree these before the project starts, in the contract or in a signed scope page. The Corvane lead set three on day zero (a named sponsor, read-only access, and success measured by issues closed). A fuller list:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Condition", "Why it matters"], ["1", "A named executive sponsor who attends steering fortnightly", "Unblocks access and decisions"], ["2", "Read-only access for every tester to every in-scope system before testing", "Without it you cannot validate or test"], ["3", "Success measured by issues closed, with the denominator stated", "Keeps the project from becoming a findings report"], ["4", "Control owners and evidence owners named by a stated week", "Avoids stalls in evidence"], ["5", "An evidence turnaround standard (for example 10 working days)", "Makes delays visible and escalable"], ["6", "Agreement with the external auditor on attributes, sample sizes and operating periods", "Avoids rework at the end"], ["7", "A scope change procedure", "Keeps scope from creeping"], ["8", "A retest calendar and the operating periods it assumes", "Sets expectations on what can close"], ["9", "The client owns remediation; the project supports it", "Keeps accountability with the owners"], ["10", "Written assumptions and exclusions", "Prevents misunderstanding about what is and is not included"]]}]}, {"title": "9.5.2 Governance", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.5.2 Governance"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A steering committee every second week, with decisions on the agenda and the sponsor present."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A gate review at each of the nine gates, recorded and archived."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A monthly note for the audit committee through the CFO."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A decision log kept by the programme office."}]}, {"title": "9.5.3 The first RAID log", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.5.3 The first RAID log"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk or issue", "Early sign", "Response"], ["Evidence arrives late", "Requests past due at the weekly sync", "Escalate at level 1 after two days; ask the sponsor at steering"], ["Extracts are incomplete", "IPE result not Pass", "Return with parameter evidence; do not sample"], ["Defensive technical owner", "Slow access", "Pair the owner with a pod lead; show findings first"], ["Scope creep", "Requests outside the signed scope", "Say no or swap; log the decision"], ["Key client person leaves", "Owner changes", "Re-confirm owners; name deputies"], ["Remediation too late to retest", "Fixes landing in the last weeks", "Work back from the retest calendar"], ["Team overload at test planning and sampling", "Late workpapers", "Move people between pods"], ["Auditor changes expectations", "New request for attributes or samples", "Agree in writing; record changes"], ["Personal data in extracts", "Files in shared folders", "Restrict access; no personal data in reports"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A note on commercials: this module gives the effort and duration view that a price needs. How you price (fixed fee, time and materials, or a cap) is a commercial decision for you and your firm, and the conditions above are what make any of them workable."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 9.5 Write the engagement conditions you would insist on for Redwater, ordered by importance. For each, say what goes wrong if it is refused."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why agree attributes and sample sizes with the external auditor before testing?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Who owns remediation?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does a retest calendar protect against?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Ten conditions protect the project. Governance is steering, gates, a monthly note and a decision log. Start the RAID log with the nine standing risks."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 9.6 — Building the Planning Pack (Workshop)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 9.6 — Building the Planning Pack (Workshop)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Produce a complete planning pack for a new project. Defend it to a challenging reviewer."]]}]}, {"title": "9.6.1 The planning pack", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.6.1 The planning pack"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Part", "Contents", "Tool"], ["1 One-page scope", "Systems, interfaces, processes, period, materiality basis, exclusions", "Word or the scope page of the Corvane case"], ["2 Estimate", "Effort, duration and range with assumptions and the retest-window check", "The Estimating Model"], ["3 Plan", "Phases, gates and the first 12 weeks in detail", "The Weekly Activity Plan (copy)"], ["4 Team", "Pods, leads, reviewers and deputies", "Section 9.4"], ["5 Stakeholder map", "Who, what they care about, how you engage them", "The Controls Lead Playbook, section 4"], ["6 Engagement conditions", "The ten conditions, tailored", "Section 9.5"], ["7 RAID", "Top ten risks and issues", "Section 9.5"], ["8 First 90 days", "What must be true at day 30, 60 and 90", "The Playbook, section 12"]]}]}, {"title": "9.6.2 Workshop steps", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.6.2 Workshop steps"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Work in pairs on Redwater, using the facts in Module 3.5 and the readiness answers in 9.2."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Spend 45 minutes on parts 1 to 4, 30 minutes on parts 5 to 8, and 15 minutes preparing to present."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Present for five minutes. The reviewer challenges the estimate, the order of work and the conditions."}]}, {"title": "9.6.3 Marking", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.6.3 Marking"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Criterion", "Marks"], ["Scope: clear, validated, with exclusions", "15"], ["Estimate: model used correctly, range given, assumptions stated, retest window checked", "20"], ["Plan: gates in the right weeks, general controls before automated controls, retest calendar visible", "20"], ["Team: pods with leads, reviewers, deputies; sensible size", "15"], ["Stakeholders and conditions: specific, prioritised", "15"], ["RAID and first 90 days: realistic, tied to the plan", "10"], ["Presentation under challenge", "5"], ["Total", "100"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 9.6 Complete the planning pack for Redwater and present it. Assessors use the marking guide above. Pass: 70 or more."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS A planning pack has eight parts, from scope to the first 90 days. The estimate, the plan and the team must agree with each other. Expect challenge on the retest window and on reliance."]]}]}, {"title": "Module 9 Quiz", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Module 9 Quiz"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Ten questions, 2 marks each. Pass mark 16 out of 20."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Name the three planning rules of a controls project."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What does \"work backward from the retest\" mean?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. How long does a weekly control typically need to operate before a retest?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Name four drivers of effort."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. What does the complexity multiplier represent?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "6. What does \"calibrated to the Corvane case\" mean?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "7. Why should an estimate be a range?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "8. Name three conditions to agree before starting."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "9. Why must a tester not review their own work?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "10. What does the retest-window check show?"}]}, {"title": "Answer Key", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Answer Key"}]}, {"title": "Session 9.1", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 9.1"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: Weekly controls need about five weeks of operation before 23 November, so fixes must be live by about 19 October. B: Monthly controls need two month-ends before the retest ends on 23 November, which means the 30 September and 31 October month-ends, so fixes must be live by about 1 September (the 30 November month-end falls after the retest ends). C: Quarterly controls need two quarters; that cannot be done within the project, so say plainly that their deficiencies will stay open pending more cycles and give the retest dates. Check yourself: (1) Their results decide how much reliance the automated controls can have. (2) A batch of evidence requests of one kind, such as configuration, populations, samples or follow-up. (3) The longest chain is evidence, testing, evaluation, fix, operating period and retest, because the operating period cannot be shortened."}]}, {"title": "Session 9.2", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 9.2"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: The No answers are questions 3 (no risk and control matrix), 4 (no approved ruleset), 7 (access difficult), 9 (owners not briefed) and 10 (auditor has not stated expectations): five No answers, so Medium. B: With 12 processes: 24 risks, 31 controls (24 × 1.31) and 25 key controls (31 × 0.81). Check yourself: (1) Any four of systems, interfaces, processes, risks, controls, key controls, evidence requests, deficiencies. (2) Because it is all you know at that stage and the ratios let you derive the rest. (3) It raises the complexity multiplier and the estimate."}]}, {"title": "Session 9.3", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 9.3"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A: Total 1,617 person-days; duration 40 weeks. B: Team of 14: capacity 56 per week; 1,617 / 56 = 28.9, so 29 weeks. C: High complexity: subtotal 1,470 × 1.25 = 1,837.5; overhead 183.75; total about 2,021 person-days; with a team of 10 (40 per week) duration is 51 weeks. D: With a 9-week operating period the window of 9 weeks is shorter than 9 plus 2, so the check reads \"SHORT: expect deficiencies to stay open\"; the plan must either lengthen the retest window or accept that some deficiencies will remain open. Check yourself: (1) It reproduces the Corvane staffing and duration; it does not make the unit efforts a benchmark. (2) Because the inputs are uncertain and a single number hides that. (3) Planning to close deficiencies that cannot be retested in time."}]}, {"title": "Session 9.4", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 9.4"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Model team of 12: Lead 1; Programme office and evidence coordinator 1; Security and GRC 3 (lead, SoD analyst, tester); ITGC 2 (lead and tester, with the Basis support coming from the client); Finance controls 4 (lead, P2P and O2C analyst, R2R and projects analyst, tester); Data and IPE analyst 1. Reviewers: pod leads for testers; lead for pod leads. Deputies: the Security lead for ITGC and the finance lead for the data analyst. Combine the programme office with evidence, and training with the finance lead. Check yourself: (1) They would not see their own errors. (2) It checks populations before sampling and files the evidence. (3) To allow for leave, rework and peaks."}]}, {"title": "Session 9.5", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 9.5"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Model order for Redwater: sponsor; access; success measure; auditor agreement on attributes and samples; named owners; evidence turnaround; retest calendar; scope change control; remediation ownership; assumptions and exclusions. If refused: no sponsor means access and decisions stall; no access means no validation; no success measure turns the project into a findings list; no auditor agreement means rework at the end; and so on. Check yourself: (1) To avoid rework and disagreement at the end. (2) The client's owners. (3) Promising closures that cannot be retested in time."}]}, {"title": "Quiz", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Quiz"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Q", "Answer"], ["1", "General controls before automated controls; population before sample; work backward from the retest"], ["2", "Plan the fix dates from the date the retest must end and the operating period needed"], ["3", "About five weeks"], ["4", "Any four of systems, interfaces, processes, risks, controls, key controls, evidence requests, deficiencies"], ["5", "Landscape complexity and client readiness"], ["6", "The model reproduces the Corvane case (team, duration, utilisation); it is not an industry benchmark"], ["7", "Because the inputs are uncertain"], ["8", "Any three of the ten conditions"], ["9", "They would not see their own errors"], ["10", "Whether the time from remediation start to project end covers the operating period plus build time"]]}]}], "word_count": 1947, "id": "tib-module-9-planning-and-estimating", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/02_Planning_Module/TIB_Module_9_Planning_and_Estimating.docx", "filename": "TIB_Module_9_Planning_and_Estimating.docx", "category": "Planning Module", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From SAP Project Manager to Controls Lead"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Module 10 Capstone: Participant Brief. Verrin Energy Partners"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Week 8 of the eight-week programme  |  Written planning pack and steering presentation  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Verrin Energy Partners is a fictitious company. All people and figures are invented."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "1. Your Task", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "1. Your Task"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "You have been asked to lead an SAP controls project at Verrin Energy Partners (VEP), a US-listed oil and gas company. You have two days of preparation time and nine hours in all. You must deliver a planning pack and then present it to a panel who will play the CFO and the external audit partner. The panel will challenge you."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["What", "Detail"], ["Written planning pack", "Up to 12 pages: the eight parts below plus an assumptions and issues log"], ["Presentation", "20 minutes with up to 10 slides, followed by 15 minutes of questions"], ["Tools you may use", "The Estimating Model, the Weekly Activity Plan, the Controls Lead Playbook, the Method Manual, the primer and the bridge manuals"], ["Rules", "Individual work. State any assumption you make. If a fact is missing, do not invent it: list it as a question"], ["Submit", "Two days before the presentation, by the route your mentor gives you"]]}]}, {"title": "The planning pack: parts to produce", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "The planning pack: parts to produce"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Part", "Contents"], ["0", "Assumptions and issues log: what you noticed in the case, why it matters, what you will do about it"], ["1", "One-page scope: systems, interfaces, processes, period, exclusions"], ["2", "Estimate: effort, duration and range, with assumptions; the retest-window check"], ["3", "Plan: phases, gates and the first 12 weeks in detail"], ["4", "Team: pods, leads, reviewers and deputies"], ["5", "Stakeholder map and how you will engage each person"], ["6", "Engagement conditions, tailored to the case"], ["7", "Risks and RAID: your first risks in the standard formula and the project RAID log"], ["8", "First 90 days"]]}]}, {"title": "How you will be marked", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "How you will be marked"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Element", "Marks"], ["Written pack: scope, estimate, plan, team, stakeholders and conditions, risks, first 90 days, judgement on the issues", "70"], ["Presentation and questions: clarity, handling challenge, credibility, time", "30"], ["Pass", "70 or more overall and at least 21 of 30 on the presentation"]]}]}, {"title": "2. The Company", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "2. The Company"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "VEP is a US-listed independent oil and gas company with revenue of about USD 2.6 billion. It operates offshore platforms in the Gulf of Mexico with joint venture partners, holds non-operated interests in onshore shale, and owns two gas processing plants. It has three company codes: VEP Holdings (1000), VEP Offshore (2000) and VEP Processing (3000), the last acquired 18 months ago. About 25 joint venture partners receive monthly statements. The annual audit planning meeting is in 20 weeks."}]}, {"title": "2.1 What IT showed you in the kickoff", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.1 What IT showed you in the kickoff"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The Head of IT presented a landscape diagram with six systems:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System", "Description"], ["S/4HANA production", "Company codes 1000 and 2000; hosted under RISE with SAP"], ["S/4HANA quality", "Test system for the above"], ["S/4HANA development", "Development system"], ["GRC Access Control", "Access requests, SoD and emergency access; connected to S/4HANA"], ["BW/4HANA", "Management reporting and consolidation"], ["Cloud integration platform", "Connects banks, customers and partners"]]}]}, {"title": "2.2 Interfaces as described", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.2 Interfaces as described"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Interface", "How it works"], ["1", "Bank statements", "Daily files in through the integration platform"], ["2", "Payment instructions", "Files created in a third-party treasury workstation and sent to the banks through the integration platform"], ["3", "Production volumes", "Field accountants send daily volumes as spreadsheets; a custom upload tool loads them"], ["4", "Joint venture statements", "Monthly statements to partners"], ["5", "Payroll journal", "From an external payroll provider"], ["6", "Legacy trial balance", "Group Reporting uploads a spreadsheet each month"], ["7", "Gas customer nominations", "EDI messages"], ["8", "GRC connector", "Provisioning from GRC to S/4HANA"], ["9", "Integration platform to a partner portal", "Statements and cash calls"]]}]}, {"title": "2.3 Processes in scope as described", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.3 Processes in scope as described"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Record to report (journals, close, fixed assets); procure to pay (vendor master, purchasing and invoices, payment); gas sales; joint venture accounting; capital projects (AFE budget and settlement); inventory; treasury and bank reconciliation; payroll posting; and four technical areas (access, change, operations, database): sixteen in all."}]}, {"title": "3. Your First Week: Notes", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3. Your First Week: Notes"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "These are your notes from the first week of interviews and meetings. Some matter more than they first appear."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Who", "What you noted"], ["N1", "Basis analyst, in passing", "\"We also keep the old Harbor ledger running for plant accounting until the migration finishes next year.\""], ["N2", "Head of IT", "The S/4HANA system is on RISE with SAP: \"SAP looks after the database and backups.\""], ["N3", "CFO", "Wants the assessment evidence ready in 20 weeks, before audit planning"], ["N4", "CIO", "A security review means your team can have read-only access to production only from week 8"], ["N5", "Production accountant", "Daily volumes arrive as spreadsheets that the field accountants email in"], ["N6", "Treasury Manager", "\"Payment files are encrypted, so there is no risk.\""], ["N7", "JV Accounting Manager", "\"No issues.\" Two partners disputed billings last year"], ["N8", "HR", "The leavers list is emailed to IT Security each week"], ["N9", "Controller", "The prior-year management letter listed two IT points: late removal of leavers and privileged access"], ["N10", "Head of Internal Audit", "Used to work for the audit firm. The audit partner is new to the account and has not yet said what she will accept"], ["N11", "Group Reporting", "The legacy trial balance is uploaded to S/4HANA by spreadsheet each month"]]}]}, {"title": "4. The People", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "4. The People"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Person", "Role", "What they say or want"], ["CFO", "Executive sponsor", "A clean result; impatient; expects a date"], ["CIO", "Owns IT", "Wants his team treated as partners, not suspects; defensive about the upgrade"], ["Controller", "Owns finance controls", "Supportive but stretched; worried about close"], ["Head of Internal Audit", "Independent assurance", "Wants an audit trail he can reuse"], ["External audit partner", "Audits the statements and opines on controls", "New to the account; has not set expectations"], ["Treasury Manager", "Payments", "Confident; says there is no risk"], ["JV Accounting Manager", "Partner billing", "Says there are no issues"], ["Head of Basis", "Runs the platform", "Cooperative but short of time"]]}]}, {"title": "5. Readiness Check", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "5. Readiness Check"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Your readiness assessment for the model (ten questions from Module 9.2):"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Question", "Answer"], ["1", "Is there a current landscape diagram and a system owner for each system?", "No"], ["2", "Are there documented process narratives for the key processes?", "Yes"], ["3", "Is there an existing risk and control matrix?", "No"], ["4", "Has an SoD ruleset been approved and is a GRC tool in use?", "No"], ["5", "Is there a change-management tool with approval evidence?", "No"], ["6", "Do HR leavers reach Security daily?", "No"], ["7", "Can the team get read-only access to every system in scope?", "No"], ["8", "Is there a named executive sponsor who will attend steering?", "Yes"], ["9", "Do the control and evidence owners know what is expected of them?", "No"], ["10", "Has the external auditor stated what it will accept?", "No"]]}]}, {"title": "6. Constraints", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "6. Constraints"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A team of up to 12 including you is budgeted. More people need the CFO's approval, and the CFO will ask for the case."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Fiscal year-end is in 24 weeks. The audit planning meeting is in 20."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The Head of Internal Audit will attend steering. The audit partner has agreed to a first meeting in week 1."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "You have the course materials, the Estimating Model and the Weekly Activity Plan template."}]}, {"title": "7. Presentation", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "7. Presentation"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "You have 20 minutes. Tell the panel what you will do, why, how long it will take, with whom, and what you need from them. The panel will play the CFO and the external audit partner and will challenge your scope, estimate, plan, team and conditions. Be ready to concede where you are wrong and to hold your position where you are right."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Suggested structure", "Minutes"], ["The situation and what you noticed", "3"], ["Scope and the issues log", "4"], ["Estimate and the options for the 20 weeks", "5"], ["Plan and team", "4"], ["Stakeholders, conditions and the first 90 days", "4"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Good luck."}]}], "word_count": 493, "id": "tib-module-10-capstone-participant-brief", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/03_Capstone_Participant/TIB_Module_10_Capstone_Participant_Brief.docx", "filename": "TIB_Module_10_Capstone_Participant_Brief.docx", "category": "Capstone Participant", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From SAP Project Manager to Controls Lead"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Assessment Pack for Modules 4 to 8: Quizzes and Case Reviews (Participant Version)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Weeks 3 to 7 of the eight-week programme  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Answers and model solutions are in the separate Instructor Guide and Answer Key."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "1. How This Pack Is Used", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "1. How This Pack Is Used"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Module", "Status", "Pass mark"], ["Module 4 assessment", "4", "The Primer Final Assessment (in the Workbook Primer Part 3); counts toward the course quiz score", "80 of 100"], ["Quiz 5", "5", "Practice: feedback only", "20 of 24"], ["Case review CR-5: review a draft risk register and controls", "5", "Practice: feedback only", "21 of 30"], ["Quiz 6", "6", "Practice: feedback only", "20 of 24"], ["Case review CR-6: review a draft workpaper", "6", "Scored: counts toward the case-review element", "21 of 30"], ["Quiz 7", "7", "Practice: feedback only", "20 of 24"], ["Case review CR-7: evaluate a set of test results", "7", "Scored: counts toward the case-review element", "21 of 30"], ["Quiz 8", "8", "Practice: feedback only", "20 of 24"], ["Case review CR-8: five lead scenarios", "8", "Practice: feedback only (feeds your planning pack)", "21 of 30"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Work individually and in your own words. Use the course materials. Where a case asks you to decide, give your reasons: the marks are for the reasoning."}]}, {"title": "Quiz 5: The Method (Module 5)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Quiz 5: The Method (Module 5)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Twelve questions, 2 marks each. Pass mark 20 out of 24."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Name the key created in W01, the key created in W03 and the key created in W05."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why do technical areas such as access, change and operations get BP-IDs?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What is wrong with listing \"User access\" as a control?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Write the risk statement formula."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. What does the ITGC_Dependency tab record?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "6. Why must a critical SoD conflict with no mitigation be removed rather than mitigated?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "7. Name the four waves of evidence requests."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "8. Why must a population pass its IPE check before sampling?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "9. Why are general controls scheduled before automated controls?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "10. Why does every key control get two tests?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "11. What must be true at gate G4?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "12. What does the scoping workpaper state when it is issued at gate G5?"}]}, {"title": "Quiz 6: Directing the Testing (Module 6)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Quiz 6: Directing the Testing (Module 6)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Twelve questions, 2 marks each. Pass mark 20 out of 24."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why does a leavers test start from the HR leavers report and not the SAP lock list?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why does a change test start from what production received?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does a usage analysis of SAP_ALL holders tell you?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. What is a \"test of one\" for an automated control, and what else must you show?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Why prove that QAS equals production before reperforming a control in QAS?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "6. What is the difference between an exception and a deficiency?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "7. Name two signs of a weak workpaper."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "8. What should happen to an extract that is incomplete?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "9. Why test the listing used in an access review before testing the review itself?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "10. What does a reliance decision \"supported with additional procedures\" require?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "11. Why test the logic of a report that a control relies on?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "12. A tester finds no exceptions in a control you know is weak. What do you do?"}]}, {"title": "Quiz 7: Evaluating, Remediating and Reporting (Module 7)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Quiz 7: Evaluating, Remediating and Reporting (Module 7)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Twelve questions, 2 marks each. Pass mark 20 out of 24."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. List the steps from an exception to a severity."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Name the three severity levels in order."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Name four factors that raise severity."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Why group deficiencies by cause?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. What is a compensating control and what must you show about it?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "6. How long must weekly, monthly and quarterly controls typically operate before a retest?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "7. Why state the denominator when you report a reduction?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "8. What makes a deficiency closed?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "9. Why report what works before what does not?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "10. What do you do before rating a privileged-access finding?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "11. What must be true before the W14 conclusion is supportable?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "12. What do you say in the close-out about open deficiencies?"}]}, {"title": "Quiz 8: Leading the Team and the Stakeholders (Module 8)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Quiz 8: Leading the Team and the Stakeholders (Module 8)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Twelve questions, 2 marks each. Pass mark 20 out of 24."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why organise a team into pods with leads?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Who reviews the pod leads?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What is the role of the data and evidence pod?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Name three standing meetings and their purposes."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Why speak a finding before writing it?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "6. Give the four levels of the escalation ladder."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "7. Why hold the rule \"no sample before IPE Pass\" even under time pressure?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "8. How do you approach a defensive technical owner?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "9. Name three things you would ask the sponsor to decide at steering."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "10. How should you present partial success to the board?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "11. What do you do about peaks in the team's workload?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "12. What does a good handover look like?"}]}, {"title": "Case Review CR-5: Review a Draft Risk Register and Controls", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Case Review CR-5: Review a Draft Risk Register and Controls"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A junior analyst has drafted the following extract for a client. For each item, say what is wrong, then rewrite it correctly. 30 marks (about 3 for each of nine items, plus 3 for a covering note)."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Draft item"], ["a", "Risk: \"Leavers are not removed from SAP.\""], ["b", "Risk: \"Fraud risk in accounts payable.\""], ["c", "Risk: \"There is a risk that vendors might be paid incorrectly.\""], ["d", "Control: \"Access is reviewed regularly by IT.\""], ["e", "Control listed as \"Change management\" in the control register"], ["f", "Automated control \"Three-way match blocks out-of-tolerance invoices\" with the Dependent ITGCs column blank"], ["g", "SoD rule: create vendor against run payment; mitigation: \"management oversight\""], ["h", "A SOX-relevant risk of unauthorised journals has no key control in the Coverage_Check tab"], ["i", "Control: \"The system validates uploads\" with expected evidence blank"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Finally, write a three-sentence covering note to the analyst that tells them what to fix first and why."}]}, {"title": "Case Review CR-6: Review a Draft Workpaper", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Case Review CR-6: Review a Draft Workpaper"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A tester has submitted the following draft for the leavers control C-ITGC-AC-002 (leavers locked within two business days of termination). You are the reviewer. Identify every flaw, state what should have been done, give the conclusion the evidence supports, and say what you would ask the tester to do next. 30 marks: flaws up to 20, supported conclusion 5, next steps 5."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Header", "Draft"], ["Test", "C-ITGC-AC-002-TOE"], ["Tester / reviewer", "S. Boateng / S. Boateng"], ["Population", "List of locked SAP users, 1 January to 31 May: 231 records (SU01 and the lock flag in USR02). No IPE check recorded"], ["Sample", "12 users, chosen haphazardly; no seed"], ["Policy applied", "Lock within 10 business days"], ["Attributes", "A on the HR report; B locked or end-dated; C lock within policy; D no logon after termination"], ["Evidence", "No evidence IDs recorded against the items"], ["Conclusion", "Effective: all attributes met for all 12 items. No deficiency to raise"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Termination", "Lock date", "Business days to lock", "A", "B", "C", "D"], ["1", "12 Jan", "13 Jan", "1", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"], ["2", "20 Jan", "21 Jan", "1", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"], ["3", "3 Feb", "12 Feb", "7", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"], ["4", "10 Feb", "11 Feb", "1", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"], ["5", "24 Feb", "25 Feb", "1", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"], ["6", "3 Mar", "17 Mar", "10", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"], ["7", "11 Mar", "12 Mar", "1", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"], ["8", "19 Mar", "20 Mar", "1", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"], ["9", "2 Apr", "15 Apr", "9", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met (note: user logged on 8 Apr, display only)"], ["10", "14 Apr", "15 Apr", "1", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"], ["11", "21 Apr", "22 Apr", "1", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"], ["12", "5 May", "6 May", "1", "Met", "Met", "Met", "Met"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The control description in the matrix reads: \"The IT Security Administrator locks and end-dates the SAP user of every leaver within two business days of the HR termination date.\" The sample guide for an event-driven control is 25 to 60."}]}, {"title": "Case Review CR-7: Evaluate a Set of Test Results", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Case Review CR-7: Evaluate a Set of Test Results"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "You lead the controls project for Redwater Pipeline and Terminals. Overall materiality is USD 12 million and the clearly trivial threshold is USD 600,000. The testing is complete and gives these results. Answer parts A to F. 30 marks (5 each)."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Result"], ["R1", "Five dialog users hold SAP_ALL. Usage analysis shows display transactions for four of them. The fifth changed the bank details of three vendors; two of the changes have no vendor support and USD 340,000 was paid to those two accounts in April"], ["R2", "Nine role assignments were made directly by Basis outside the GRC workflow"], ["R3", "Production was opened once without a ticket; no table changes were found in that window"], ["R4", "Twenty-two users hold the vendor-maintenance and payment-run combination with no mitigation"], ["R5", "The weekly vendor change review was not performed in 4 of 12 sampled weeks"], ["R6", "The quarterly user access review operated effectively"], ["R7", "Two of 25 leavers were locked late; no logons after termination"], ["R8", "One of three monthly bank reconciliations was reviewed late"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Part", "Task"], ["A", "What do you do first about R1, before rating anything?"], ["B", "Group the eight results by cause. Explain each group"], ["C", "Rate each group (control deficiency, significant deficiency or material weakness) and give your reasons"], ["D", "Identify the compensating controls and say whether each reduces the exposure"], ["E", "What do you say about reliance on the automated controls that depend on the access and change controls?"], ["F", "Outline remediation and the retest plan, stating the operating periods and which deficiencies may stay open"]]}]}, {"title": "Case Review CR-8: Five Lead Scenarios", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Case Review CR-8: Five Lead Scenarios"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "For each scenario say what you do, what you say and to whom, and why. 30 marks (6 each)."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Scenario"], ["1", "Two pod leads disagree about the severity of a deficiency. The security lead says control deficiency because a compensating review operated; the finance lead says significant deficiency because fraud is possible"], ["2", "The CIO emails you: a draft finding about SAP_ALL was shared with his deputy before he saw it, and he is angry"], ["3", "Evidence for six key populations is a week overdue and gate G6 is on Friday"], ["4", "In week 20 the external audit partner tells you she now wants samples of 40, not 25, for event-driven controls"], ["5", "The CFO asks you to rate a group of deficiencies as control deficiencies so that it does not have to go to the audit committee"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Submit CR-6 and CR-7 through your mentor. CR-5 and CR-8 are for feedback in the weekly session."}]}], "word_count": 970, "id": "tib-assessment-pack-modules-4-8-participant", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/04_Assessment_Participant/TIB_Assessment_Pack_Modules_4-8_Participant.docx", "filename": "TIB_Assessment_Pack_Modules_4-8_Participant.docx", "category": "Assessment Participant", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The Controls Lead Playbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Leading a 15-Person Team and Managing Stakeholders on an Oil and Gas SAP Controls Project"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Practical guide for a new controls lead  |  Built from the Corvane Energy Group case  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Companion to: The Corvane Story, the five-part manual, the deployment guide and the completed Corvane workbook set (W01 to W14)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "1. The Role of the Lead", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "1. The Role of the Lead"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "You are the person who owns the outcome. You do not own the workbooks, the tests or the findings: your team does. You own four things: the scope and its defence, the team and its capacity, the relationship with every stakeholder who can help or hinder, and the conclusion that goes to the client. This playbook shows how one lead did that for a 15-person team on a US-listed oil and gas company, and turns it into reusable routines."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["The lead owns", "The lead does not own", "How you know it is working"], ["Scope, gates and dates", "Individual workbook entries", "Every gate passes on its stated date with the Checks tabs reading ALL CHECKS PASS"], ["Team design and capacity", "Every test", "Pod leads make decisions without escalating; you review conclusions, not drafts"], ["Stakeholder trust", "The client's remediation work", "No stakeholder first hears a finding in writing"], ["The conclusion and its denominator", "The client's opinion", "Every number can be traced to a workbook row"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["THE LEAD'S THREE RULES 1. Nothing is a surprise.  Findings are spoken before they are written. 2. Nothing is sampled, filed or closed without a check.  Population passes IPE before sampling; evidence has an ID before it counts; retest passes before a deficiency closes. 3. Everything is traceable.  Any conclusion can be followed back to a server, and any server forward to a conclusion."]]}]}, {"title": "2. Designing the Team of Fifteen", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "2. Designing the Team of Fifteen"}]}, {"title": "2.1 The pod model", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.1 The pod model"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Corvane used four pods and two cross-cutting roles. Each pod has a lead with decision rights, so you manage four conversations, not fifteen."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Pod", "Members (role)", "Owns", "Workbooks"], ["Security and GRC", "Tunde Adeyemi (lead), Lerato Dlamini (SoD analyst), Samuel Boateng (access tester)", "Access, privileged access, Firefighter, SoD ruleset", "W06; access tests in W10 to W12"], ["ITGC: change and operations", "Mei Lin (lead), Ivan Petrov (Basis consultant), Elena Rossi (change and operations tester)", "Transports, system settings, jobs, interfaces, backup, database", "W01, W02; change and operations tests"], ["Finance controls", "Carlos Mendes (lead), Fatima Khan (P2P), Arjun Mehta (O2C and JVA), Grace Liu (R2R, assets, projects), Kofi Mensah (ITAC and business-process tester)", "Processes, risks, RCM, automated and review controls, reliance", "W03, W04, W05; ITAC and BPC tests"], ["Data and evidence", "Nadia Haddad (data and IPE analyst), Olivia Grant (evidence coordinator)", "Populations, IPE, evidence index, PBC register", "W08, W09, W12"], ["Cross-cutting", "Hannah Brooks (programme office), Ruth Abara (training and change champion)", "Plan, risks, reporting; plain-language guides and handover", "W14 support; guides and training"]]}]}, {"title": "2.2 Who does what by stage", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.2 Who does what by stage"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "R = does the work, A = accountable, C = consulted, I = informed."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Stage and gate", "Lead", "PMO", "Security / GRC", "ITGC", "Finance", "Data / evidence", "Client owner"], ["1 Environment (G1)", "A", "I", "C", "R", "C", "C", "C"], ["2 Process scope (G2)", "A", "I", "C", "C", "R", "I", "C"], ["3 Risks (G3)", "A", "I", "C", "C", "R", "I", "C"], ["4 Control design and SoD (G4)", "A", "I", "R", "C", "R", "I", "C"], ["5 Owners (G5)", "A", "R", "C", "C", "C", "I", "R"], ["6 Evidence and IPE (G6)", "A", "C", "C", "C", "C", "R", "R"], ["7 Test plan (G7)", "A", "R", "C", "C", "C", "C", "I"], ["8 Testing (G8)", "C", "I", "R", "R", "R", "R", "C"], ["9 Evaluate and conclude (G9)", "A", "C", "R", "R", "R", "C", "C"]]}]}, {"title": "2.3 Onboarding and capacity", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.3 Onboarding and capacity"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Week one for every new member: read the Deployment Guide, work through the Session 17 test cycle on a dummy control, and shadow one walkthrough. Testers practise on a dummy before a real test."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Reviews: testers never review their own work; the pod lead reviews testers; you review the pod leads and every deficiency before it leaves the team."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Capacity: plan 80 percent of nominal hours. The two peaks are G7 to G8 (test planning and sampling) and the deficiency evaluation fortnight. Move people between pods deliberately at those points."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Cover: name a deputy for each pod lead and for the evidence coordinator. A single point of failure in evidence is the quickest way to slip a gate."}]}, {"title": "3. Operating Rhythm", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3. Operating Rhythm"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Cadence", "Forum", "Purpose", "Output"], ["Daily, 15 minutes", "Team stand-up", "Blockers only; no status reading", "List of blockers and owners"], ["Weekly", "Pod review (Wednesday)", "Checks tab status, open items, risks to the gate", "Updated Checks tabs; items escalated"], ["Weekly", "Lead one-to-ones with pod leads", "Decisions, coaching, workload", "Decision log entries"], ["Weekly", "Client sync (Thursday)", "Evidence due, dependencies, access", "Updated PBC register and W14 dependencies"], ["Fortnightly", "Steering committee (Friday)", "Progress against gates; decisions only the sponsor can make", "Minutes; decision log; one-page status"], ["At each gate", "Gate review", "Confirm gate conditions are met", "Signed gate record; archived workbook copies"], ["Monthly", "Audit committee note (via CFO)", "Headline status and open items", "One-page note"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cancel any meeting that has stopped producing decisions. Replace status reading with the Checks tabs: every workbook reports its own state."}]}, {"title": "4. Managing Stakeholders", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "4. Managing Stakeholders"}]}, {"title": "4.1 The stakeholder map", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.1 The stakeholder map"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Corvane's main stakeholders are shown below. The names are fictitious; the roles recur on almost every oil and gas controls project."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Stakeholder", "What they care about", "Influence", "How the lead engaged", "Cadence"], ["Eleanor Whitcombe, CFO (sponsor)", "Clean opinion; no surprises", "Very high", "Decisions only, with a recommendation and two options; never ask her for what others can give", "Steering; ad hoc 1:1 before bad news"], ["Raymond Okafor, CIO", "His team treated as partners", "Very high", "Findings about his team spoken to him first, with evidence", "Weekly 20 min"], ["Priya Raman, Group Financial Controller", "Controls her accountants can operate", "High", "Treated as the customer of every control design; she approved the ruleset", "Weekly"], ["Martin Kessler, Head of Internal Audit", "An audit trail he can rely on", "High", "Invited to every gate review; shared the coverage matrix openly", "Each gate"], ["Sofia Lindqvist, external audit partner", "Evidence that automated controls can be relied on", "Very high", "Asked in week one which attributes and sample sizes she would accept; wrote them into the plan", "Fortnightly"], ["Viktor Hale, Basis Lead", "Not being blamed for upgrade shortcuts", "High (can stall access and extracts)", "Made Mei and Ivan his working partners; saw all evidence before any finding was written", "Daily contact"], ["Neil Abernathy, IT Security Manager", "Access controls he owns", "High", "Co-wrote the remediation plans; guides from Ruth", "Weekly"], ["Process owners (AP, procurement, treasury, JV, commercial, capital projects, supply chain, HR)", "Their own risks; time", "Medium", "One 20-minute walkthrough in their office and a one-page summary", "As needed; gate reviews"], ["Integration, BI and IT applications leads", "Interfaces, reports and custom code", "Medium", "Invited to evidence and IPE sessions early", "Weekly during evidence"], ["Audit committee (through the CFO)", "Assurance", "High, remote", "Monthly one-page note", "Monthly"]]}]}, {"title": "4.2 Communication principles", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.2 Communication principles"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Speak before you write. For any finding, meet the owner first with the evidence on the table."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "One page per audience. The CFO gets decisions; process owners get what they must do; the auditors get attributes, populations and reliance decisions."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Lead with what works. Report effective controls first so the client knows what to protect."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "State the denominator. \"Thirty logged, nine open\" always, never \"70 percent\"."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Keep a decision log. Every steering decision is dated, owned and recorded."}]}, {"title": "4.3 Difficult conversations", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.3 Difficult conversations"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Situation", "Approach", "Opening line (adapt)"], ["Telling the CIO that nine dialog users hold SAP_ALL", "Meet in person; bring the usage analysis; separate the facts from blame", "\"Here is what we found, here is what these users actually did, and here is what I suggest we do together.\""], ["A defensive technical owner won't share extracts", "Ask them to teach you how it works; make them a co-owner of the fix", "\"Show me how this runs today in your words; I will write it down before I judge it.\""], ["Telling the CFO nine deficiencies stay open", "Show what closes each, and when; resist rounding up", "\"Twenty-one are closed. Nine remain at the lowest severity. Here is the date each closes and the retest that proves it.\""], ["Agreeing retest sample sizes with the external auditor", "Bring the guide and the evidence of operating occurrences", "\"These are the occurrences since the fix. Here is the sample size the guide gives. Do you accept it?\""], ["An owner asks to relax the population check to save a week", "Hold the line; offer to give time back later", "\"I will give you that week back by not having a single disputed sample.\""], ["Internal Audit wants control areas as the unit of testing", "Offer a trade: areas to group, controls to test", "\"Let's keep your areas as headings and test the controls under them. Can you describe this control in five parts?\""]]}]}, {"title": "4.4 Escalation ladder", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.4 Escalation ladder"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Level", "Who", "Trigger", "Time limit"], ["1", "Pod lead and client owner", "Evidence or access not provided by due date", "2 working days"], ["2", "Lead and client function head", "Level 1 not resolved; gate at risk", "2 working days"], ["3", "Sponsor (CFO) at steering or by call", "Gate will slip, or a finding disputed", "Next steering or within 48 hours"], ["4", "CFO and CIO jointly", "Dispute about scope or access that threatens the engagement", "As needed"]]}]}, {"title": "5. Phase-by-Phase Guide for the Lead", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "5. Phase-by-Phase Guide for the Lead"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Gate", "Your actions as lead", "Do not pass if"], ["G1 Environment", "Sign the one-page scope at steering; have Basis confirm every system; personally read W02 open items", "Any in-scope system is Documented only without an open question; no transport route recorded"], ["G2 Process scope", "Resolve scope arguments early (for example JV accounting); confirm owners for each process", "A significant account maps to no process; a process is guessed instead of Outstanding"], ["G3 Risks", "Attend two owner walkthroughs; check risk statements follow the formula", "Any process without a risk; any risk that is really a finding"], ["G4 Controls and SoD", "Defend the control-versus-area rule; approve the ruleset version with the Controller", "Any SOX risk without a key control; ITAC or report control without ITGC dependencies"], ["G5 Owners", "Issue W14 in scoping form; confirm access for testers", "Owners Outstanding for key controls"], ["G6 Evidence", "Hold the population-before-sample rule; escalate late requests at level 2", "Any population with an IPE result other than Pass"], ["G7 Test plan", "Approve the order: ITGCs first; confirm sample sizes with the auditor", "ITAC tests scheduled before the last ITGC test ends; samples outside the guide without a reason"], ["G8 Testing", "Read every deficiency draft; hold the daily blocker list", "Exceptions without a deficiency or rationale; unreviewed workpapers"], ["G9 Conclude", "Run follow-up analyses before rating severity; group by cause; meet the CFO before the committee", "Coverage checks not all PASS; a deficiency closed without a retest"]]}]}, {"title": "6. Quality Control", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "6. Quality Control"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Three lines of review: tester, pod lead, you. The tester and reviewer in W10 must differ; the Checks tab enforces it."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Use the Checks tabs as your dashboard. Read the overall status of each workbook every Wednesday and ask only about REVIEW lines."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Re-paste before you read. A stale Ref_ tab causes most false alarms (Deployment Guide section 4)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Spot checks: each week re-perform one test attribute yourself on one sample item and compare to the workpaper."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Deficiency review: you read every deficiency for the four-part statement, severity logic and compensating evidence before it goes to the client."}]}, {"title": "7. Managing Project Risks", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "7. Managing Project Risks"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Project risk (seen at Corvane)", "Early sign", "Response"], ["Evidence arrives late", "Requests past due at the weekly sync", "Escalate at level 1 after two days; ask the sponsor to chase at steering"], ["Extracts are incomplete (locked users, short date range)", "IPE result not Pass", "Return with the parameter evidence; never sample until Pass"], ["Defensive technical owner", "Slow or partial access to systems", "Partner the owner with your pod lead; show findings first"], ["Scope creep (extra testing requests)", "Requests outside the signed scope", "Say no or swap; log in the decision log"], ["Key client person leaves", "Control owner changes", "Re-confirm owners in W07 immediately; name deputies"], ["Remediation dates too late for retest", "Weekly or monthly controls fixed in the last weeks", "Work back from the retest calendar; agree operating periods with the auditor early"], ["Team overload at G7 and G8", "Late workpapers; errors in samples", "Move people between pods; protect review time"], ["Auditor changes expectations", "New request for attributes or samples", "Agree methodology in writing in week one; record changes"], ["Personal data in extracts", "Files in shared folders", "Restrict folder access; no personal data in reports"]]}]}, {"title": "8. Reporting and Key Measures", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "8. Reporting and Key Measures"}]}, {"title": "8.1 Weekly measures for the steering one-pager", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "8.1 Weekly measures for the steering one-pager"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Measure", "Source", "Why it matters"], ["Gate status and next gate date", "Gate record", "The only thing the sponsor must decide on"], ["Checks tab status by workbook", "Checks tabs", "Quality at a glance"], ["Evidence requests: raised, received, overdue", "W08", "Leading indicator of slippage"], ["Populations passed / total", "W09", "Readiness to sample"], ["Tests: planned, done, reviewed, with exceptions", "W10, W11", "Progress and quality"], ["Deficiencies by severity and status", "W13", "The headline outcome"], ["Open dependencies and days overdue", "W14", "What is blocking the project"]]}]}, {"title": "8.2 The close-out one-pager", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "8.2 The close-out one-pager"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A table of what was fixed and what remains, with the owner and retest date for each open item."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The headline with its denominator: for Corvane, 30 deficiencies logged in the baseline cycle, 21 closed after retest, 9 open (70 percent reduction), no material weakness."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Before and after measures from the cases in the Corvane story (Appendix B of the story)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The reliance statement from the external auditor."}]}, {"title": "9. Managing Remediation and Closure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "9. Managing Remediation and Closure"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Write plans with the owner, not for them. Add a one-page plain-language guide per owner (Ruth's role at Corvane)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Fix the cause, not the instance. Ask what removed the control or created the weakness in the first place."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Work back from the retest. Weekly controls need about five weeks of operation, monthly controls two month-ends, quarterly controls two quarters. Agree this with the auditor."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Close only on a retest. Create a retest TEST-ID in W10; sample from the post-remediation population only; update W13 and W14."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Say plainly what is still open. List each open item with the event that will close it."}]}, {"title": "10. Team Health", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "10. Team Health"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Recognise the unglamorous work. Evidence coordination and IPE checks protected the project more than any single test. Name them in the steering committee."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Coach with the workpaper. The first workpaper from every tester has a gap. Review it with them in person and show what a good one looks like."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Watch for the quiet failure. A tester who stops raising questions is probably stuck. Check in at the weekly one-to-one."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Disagreement is a signal. If two pod leads disagree on a severity, ask each to state the evidence and decide yourself within a day."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Protect weekends. If the team is working weekends in G7 and G8, your plan is wrong; move scope or add people."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Hand over deliberately. Leave the workbooks with the client and train the owners; a dependence on you is a failure."}]}, {"title": "11. Decision Log from Corvane", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "11. Decision Log from Corvane"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Decision", "Reason", "Result"], ["1", "Agree success measure (issues closed) before contract", "Avoid a findings-only project", "Aligned with sponsor from day zero"], ["2", "Listening week before planning", "Map conflicting priorities", "Plan accepted at first steering"], ["3", "Put JV accounting in scope", "Significant accounts", "Avoided later rework"], ["4", "Four pods with decision rights", "Manage four conversations, not fifteen", "Pod leads reviewed each other by month two"], ["5", "No sampling before IPE Pass", "Prevent disputed samples", "Zero disputed samples"], ["6", "Controls, not control areas, as the unit of testing", "Controls are testable", "47 controls with five-part descriptions"], ["7", "General controls tested before automated controls", "Reliance logic", "Auditors accepted reliance with documented procedures"], ["8", "Findings spoken before written", "Protect relationships", "No surprises at steering"], ["9", "Group findings by cause before presenting", "Make thirty findings legible", "Seven groups; two rated significant"], ["10", "Follow-up analysis before severity", "Usage analysis and substantive review", "No material weakness"], ["11", "Retest calendar sets remediation deadlines", "Realistic operating periods", "21 closed; 9 honestly left open"], ["12", "Leave workbooks with the client", "Avoid dependence on consultants", "Client owns the foundation"]]}]}, {"title": "12. Your First 30, 60 and 90 Days", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "12. Your First 30, 60 and 90 Days"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Period", "Focus", "Deliverables"], ["Days 1 to 30", "Listen, scope, build the team", "Stakeholder map; one-page scope signed; pods named; W01 to W03 under way; access for every tester"], ["Days 31 to 60", "Foundation and design", "Gates G1 to G4 passed; risk register agreed; RCM with reliance links; SoD ruleset approved"], ["Days 61 to 90", "Evidence and test plan", "G5 to G7 passed; populations passed IPE; test plan approved with sample sizes agreed with the auditor"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Self-check at day 90: can you trace any control back to a server and any server forward to a test? If not, the foundation is not complete and testing should wait."}]}, {"title": "Appendix A. Meeting Templates", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix A. Meeting Templates"}]}, {"title": "Steering agenda (45 minutes)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Steering agenda (45 minutes)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Decisions needed (10 minutes): the only items on the agenda that require the sponsor."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Gate status and next gate (5 minutes)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Coverage and Checks summary (5 minutes)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Deficiencies and remediation (10 minutes): by group, not by item."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Dependencies and blockers (10 minutes): owner, date, escalation level."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Next fortnight (5 minutes)."}]}, {"title": "Weekly client status note (outline)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Weekly client status note (outline)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Three lines on progress, three on blockers, one decision requested."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Evidence: requests due this week and overdue."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Next week's walkthroughs and who must attend."}]}, {"title": "Finding conversation checklist", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Finding conversation checklist"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Evidence ready and brought in print or on screen."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Usage analysis done where access is involved."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Condition, criteria, cause, effect written, with the compensating evidence."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Suggested remediation and who would own it."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The owner's response recorded in W13 the same day."}]}], "word_count": 1108, "id": "controls-lead-playbook-15-person-team", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/Controls_Lead_Playbook_15_Person_Team.docx", "filename": "Controls_Lead_Playbook_15_Person_Team.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The Corvane Story"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A Controls Lead's Account of Cutting Control Issues by 70%"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Case study and training narrative for a new controls lead  |  Told by Daniel Reyes, Controls Lead  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Fictitious case: Corvane Energy Group is an invented company. All people, figures and events are illustrative and built to match the completed workbook set."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "Prologue — The Phone Call", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Prologue — The Phone Call"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The call came on a Tuesday in early December. Eleanor Whitcombe, the CFO of Corvane Energy Group, did not waste words. \"Our auditors have told us that our SAP controls are our biggest risk going into the next audit cycle,\" she said. \"We moved to S/4HANA last year. We have a management letter with three IT points, an internal audit list of forty open findings, and a board that wants to hear the word remediated. Can you lead this?\""}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Corvane is an integrated oil and gas company: upstream fields offshore and onshore, a pipeline business, a refinery and a marketing arm, four company codes, joint venture partners in most of its assets, and a US listing that makes it subject to Sarbanes-Oxley. It runs one S/4HANA production system for all four company codes, with GRC for access, a Fiori front end, BW for consolidation, a cloud integration platform for banks and partners, and Solution Manager for change."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I had spent thirty-odd years in SAP security and controls work. I knew what a project like this looked like when it went well and, more often, when it did not. The failures had a pattern. Teams jumped to testing before they agreed what was in scope. They tested what was easy instead of what mattered. They found problems, wrote long reports, and left the client with a list instead of a result."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I told Eleanor I would take it on under three conditions: a named executive sponsor who would attend a steering committee every two weeks, read-only access for my team to every system in scope, and a rule that we would measure success by how many issues were actually closed, not how many we found. She said yes to all three before I had finished the sentence."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "This is the story of what happened next: how I built the team, how we built the foundation, what we found, what it took to fix it, and why thirty deficiencies became nine. I have written it for the person who will lead the next project like this. I will tell you what I decided, why, and where I got it wrong."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT The sponsor, access and measure-of-success conditions were agreed before the contract was signed. A lead who negotiates these later will negotiate them from a weaker position, with a team already waiting."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED Agree how success is measured on day zero. \"Issues closed\" is a different project from \"issues found\"."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What three conditions would you set before accepting a similar engagement?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you define \"success\" in numbers for your client?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 1 — Week One: Listening Before Planning", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 1 — Week One: Listening Before Planning"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I started on 12 January 2026. My first week had no workbooks in it at all. I spent it with people."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I met the CFO, the CIO Raymond Okafor, the Group Financial Controller Priya Raman, the head of Internal Audit Martin Kessler, and Sofia Lindqvist, the external audit partner. I asked each of them the same four questions. What worries you most? What have you already tried? What would make this project a success in your eyes? What must I not do?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The answers were consistent in a way that surprised me. Eleanor wanted a clean opinion and no surprises. Raymond wanted his IT team treated as partners, not suspects. Priya wanted the controls to be simple enough for her accountants to operate. Martin wanted an audit trail he could rely on. Sofia wanted one thing above all: evidence that the automated controls could be trusted, so her team could rely on them and reduce their own testing."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Raymond's answer to \"what must I not do\" stayed with me. \"Do not email my Basis lead a list of his failures,\" he said. Viktor Hale, the Basis Lead, had run the S/4HANA upgrade the previous year, and everyone knew some shortcuts had been taken to meet the go-live date. Viktor was proud and tired and, I would learn, frightened of what we would find."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I wrote down the project in one page: scope in plain words, the nine stages and gates from the foundation manual, the dates I could commit to, and the one measure. Then I took it to the first steering committee and asked everyone in the room to sign it. Not because the signature mattered, but because reading it aloud made people argue about scope on day five instead of week twelve. The CFO challenged whether joint venture accounting belonged in scope. I said yes, because JV receivables, payables and revenue share were all significant accounts. She agreed. That argument, early and cheap, saved us from a far more expensive one later."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT I chose a listening week over a planning week. The project plan I wrote on Friday was better than the one I would have written on Monday, and the stakeholders felt heard before anything had been tested."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED The same four questions to every senior stakeholder gives you a map of conflicting priorities. Write it down before you design anything."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Whose answer in your first week is most likely to change your plan?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What would you ask the Basis lead that is not an accusation?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 2 — Building a Team of Fifteen", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 2 — Building a Team of Fifteen"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The budget gave me fifteen people. It sounds like plenty. It is not, for a landscape with eleven systems and four company codes. The real question was how to organise them so that no one waited for anyone and no work was done twice."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I split the team into four pods plus two cross-cutting roles, each with a lead who could make decisions without me. The Security and GRC pod: Tunde Adeyemi as lead, Lerato Dlamini on segregation of duties, and Samuel Boateng as tester. The ITGC pod for change and operations: Mei Lin as lead, Ivan Petrov as Basis consultant, and Elena Rossi as tester. The finance controls pod: Carlos Mendes as lead, with process analysts Fatima Khan for procure-to-pay, Arjun Mehta for order-to-cash and JV accounting, and Grace Liu for record-to-report, fixed assets and projects, plus Kofi Mensah as the tester for automated and business-process controls. The data and evidence pod: Nadia Haddad as data and IPE analyst and Olivia Grant as evidence coordinator. Across all of them, Hannah Brooks ran the programme office and Ruth Abara was our training and change champion."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Three design choices mattered. First, every pod had its own tester, and testers never reviewed their own work. Reviews went to the pod lead, and the pod leads' work came to me. Second, Nadia and Olivia sat in the middle of everything: nothing was sampled until Nadia had passed the population, and nothing was filed until Olivia had indexed it. That one rule is why we never had a sampling argument with the auditors. Third, Ruth joined on day one rather than at the end. She wrote the plain-language guides to each control as we designed it, so that by the time we reached remediation, the client's people had already read about what they would be asked to do."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "My own role was narrower than I expected. I did not build workbooks. I removed obstacles, made the scoping decisions, ran the steering committee, and read every deficiency before it went to a client. In the first month I made the mistake of reviewing too much. By the second month the pod leads were reviewing each other's work, and I focused on conversations with the client."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The rhythm we kept for thirty-seven weeks was simple: a fifteen-minute stand-up each morning, a pod review each Wednesday, a one-to-one with each pod lead each week, a client sync on Thursdays, steering every second Friday. When a meeting stopped producing decisions, I cancelled it."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT I gave each pod a lead with decision rights rather than managing fifteen people directly. The cost was less visibility of detail; the gain was speed. I replaced detail with the Checks tabs: every workbook told me its own status."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED A team of fifteen needs four conversations, not fifteen. Design the pods first and the individual roles second."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you organise fifteen people across the nine stages of the foundation?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Which two roles would you put in the middle so nothing is sampled or filed unchecked?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 3 — Mapping the People", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 3 — Mapping the People"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I treat stakeholders as a landscape of their own. Before the first workbook, I drew a map: who decides, who influences, who is affected, and who might quietly stop us. Over thirty-seven weeks the names did not change, but my view of them did."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The sponsor was Eleanor, and her job was to unblock. I never asked her for anything I could get elsewhere. When I did ask, it was for a decision, and I came with a recommendation and two options. Raymond, the CIO, was the owner of the systems and the people whose controls we would test. I made a rule early: findings about his team went to him in person before they went in writing. Priya, the Group Financial Controller, was the person who would operate most of the finance controls after we left, so I treated her as the customer of everything we designed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Martin Kessler in Internal Audit was both a stakeholder and a risk. If we found things he had missed, he might feel exposed. I invited him to every gate review and shared the coverage matrix openly. He became the most useful challenger on the project. Sofia Lindqvist, the external audit partner, I treated as a user of our output from the first week: I asked what test attributes and sample sizes her team would accept, and I wrote them into the plan."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The process owners were the heart of the work: Beth Calloway in accounts payable, Jorge Salinas in procurement, Aiko Tanaka in treasury, Samira Qureshi for joint ventures, Derek Owusu in commercial, Liam Doherty for capital projects, Rosa Delgado in supply chain, Claire Foster in HR. Each one owned risks. Each one was busy. The best approach was a single twenty-minute walkthrough in their own office, with a one-page summary afterwards, not a workshop in a conference room."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The hardest relationship was Viktor Hale, the Basis Lead. Every finding in change management, privileged access and operations touched his team. In week two he told me, quietly, that he would give us whatever we asked for but he did not expect to enjoy it. I took that as an invitation. I made Mei and Ivan his working partners, not his auditors. We asked his team to show us how the landscape really worked, in their own words, and we wrote it down before we judged it. When the findings came, Viktor had already seen most of the evidence and had already started to fix it."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT I decided that nobody would learn about a finding in a report. Every finding was spoken first, to the person who owned it, with the evidence in front of both of us. It cost hours. It saved the project."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED Name the one stakeholder who can stop the project without saying so. Make them your partner before the first finding."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Which stakeholder would you meet in person before a written finding goes out?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you involve a defensive technical owner without lowering your standards?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 4 — The Landscape: What We Found in Seven Weeks", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 4 — The Landscape: What We Found in Seven Weeks"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The first two gates were about facts, not controls. By 6 February we had to have every system and interface listed and validated; by 20 February, every process mapped to its systems and its financial statement accounts. This is the work nobody finds exciting and everything later depends on."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W01 started with the landscape diagram Viktor's team gave us, which showed eight systems. We finished with eleven. The differences were instructive. A Fiori front-end server was not on the diagram; we asked whether it posted transactions, and the answer was yes, for invoice entry and journals, so it came into scope. A sandbox existed that nobody had mentioned; we checked the transport route display and confirmed it had no path to quality or production, and we excluded it, with the screenshot as evidence. A training client was in the quality system; we confirmed it used masked data and excluded it. A cloud integration tenant carried every bank file and partner statement; the provider had a SOC 1 Type II report, which we obtained and later mapped to our own controls. And the database layer, the HANA database underneath the SAP system, had a SYSTEM user and a handful of administrators who could change financial tables without ever logging into SAP."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W02, the interfaces, was where the project's best early finding came from. We asked process owners what came into SAP from outside and what went out, and we asked each one how they would know if it failed. For the payment file sent to the banks, the answer was a pause and then: \"I suppose treasury would notice if the bank complained.\" We checked the file directory. Four accounts payable users had write access to it. A payment file is a list of who gets paid and how much; anyone who could edit it could redirect money. That was in week three, long before any test. It became risk R-P2P-006."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Other interfaces told similar stories. The daily production volumes from the field allocation system fed revenue directly, and nobody reconciled what arrived to what was sent. The ChaRM connector between Solution Manager and production logged on with a user that held SAP_ALL. A custom upload program, ZFI_JE_UPLOAD, let twelve finance users load journals in bulk with no validation and no error log."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W03 settled the shape of the work: fourteen business processes plus four technical areas for access, change, operations and the database. We mapped thirteen financial statement line items to those processes. Payroll was the one process with no clear scope; Claire Foster confirmed in week three that payroll runs externally and SAP only receives the posting journal. Instead of guessing, we had marked it Outstanding until she said so, and it went in as a small, well-scoped interface."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "We passed gate G1 on 6 February and G2 on 20 February. I did not feel any pride. I felt relief, because the scope was now written down and signed."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT I refused to start risk work until every system was either validated with evidence or flagged with an open question. The pressure to move on was strong in week four. I held the line because every risk, control and test later would point back to a system ID, and an unvalidated system is a hole in the foundation."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED A landscape diagram is a claim, not a fact. Validate every row. Ask \"how would you know if it failed?\" of every interface. The answers are the risk register."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Which system on a client's diagram would you want to validate first, and with what evidence?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What would you ask a payment-file owner to find out if the file can be altered?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 5 — Risks, Controls and the Argument About \"Control Areas\"", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 5 — Risks, Controls and the Argument About \"Control Areas\""}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "By March we turned to the risk register, the risk and control matrix, and the segregation-of-duties rules. This is where the foundation either becomes a controls project or stays a list of technical settings."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "We wrote thirty-six risks, thirty-two of them relevant to SOX. Every risk followed the same sentence: there is a risk that something happens because of a cause, resulting in an impact. Writing them that way forced the process owners to say what could go wrong in words a tester could check. Beth Calloway in accounts payable had told me she had \"no risks\", only controls. After an hour we had seven, including the one she had been worrying about for years, vendor bank-detail changes made to divert payments."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Then came the argument I had expected. The client's existing control documentation listed what it called control areas: User Access, Change Management, Segregation of Duties. Internal Audit wanted to keep them as the unit of testing. I said no. An area is not a control. A control is one person or system doing one thing, at a stated frequency, producing evidence. I asked each owner to describe their control in five parts: who, what, when, how, and what evidence results. Forty-seven controls came out of that exercise, thirty-eight of them key controls. Some owners found out that what they thought was a control was actually a hope."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The matrix also taught us something I now put on the first slide of every kickoff: each control belongs to one category, not three. A three-way match is an automated application control. The control that stops someone changing the match tolerances is a change-management general control. SOX relevance is a flag that decides whether we test it, not another category. Understanding that let us build the reliance links: for every automated control and every report, we recorded which general controls it depended on. There were sixty-one such links."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The segregation-of-duties work belonged to Lerato and Tunde. We turned each risk into functions, transactions and authorisation objects: twenty functions and sixteen conflict rules, including the oil-and-gas-specific ones for joint venture equity changes and billing. We found four critical rules that could not be mitigated and had to be removed. Starting from the TIB baseline ruleset and adding the client's custom transactions got us to an approved version 1.0 on 27 March, gate G4."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "By 10 April, gate G5, every one of the forty-seven controls had a named owner, a performer, an evidence owner and a backup. That list of names was the quiet achievement of the project. When we later asked for evidence, we never had to ask who to ask."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT When Internal Audit asked to keep control areas as the unit of testing, I offered a trade: keep the areas as a way to group, but test controls. They accepted because I showed them one control from their own documentation that nobody could describe in five parts."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED A risk you cannot test is not a risk statement; a control you cannot evidence is not a control. The reliance links between general controls and automated controls are the most valuable thing in the matrix."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you explain the difference between a control area and a control to a process owner?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Which stakeholder would you ask to approve the SoD ruleset, and why?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 6 — The Evidence Chase", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 6 — The Evidence Chase"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Evidence is where good plans go to die. By June we had 95 requests to manage and a client team that was also running a quarter-end close. Olivia Grant ran the register like an air-traffic controller: every request had a control, a period, an owner, a due date and a status, and nothing was marked received until it had an evidence ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "We organised requests in waves. Wave one asked for configuration and scoping evidence, twenty-two requests. Wave two asked for the complete populations, such as every SAP user, every leaver, every transport imported to production, every Firefighter session: twenty-eight requests. Wave three, twenty-five requests, asked for supporting evidence, but only for the items we had selected. Asking for a sample before you have a population is the quickest way to be accused of cherry-picking."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Nadia Haddad's completeness and accuracy checks caught three things that would have embarrassed us. The first extract of SAP users left out locked accounts, which would have hidden exactly the leavers we were looking for. The first export of transports covered sixty days instead of five months. And in the Firefighter log, three sessions appeared in the system security log but not in GRC, which meant someone had used an emergency ID outside the tool. We sent each extract back, explained the gap, and did not sample until the result was Pass."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "These were the moments that built trust with the client's Basis team. We never said \"your extract was wrong\". We said \"the parameters we captured show a filter on active users; can we rerun with all user types?\" Viktor's analysts started checking their own extracts before sending them."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "In the end we filed 109 evidence items and ran 45 checks of populations. Every conclusion we later wrote could be traced to an evidence ID, a request, and a control."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT I made it a rule that no sample is drawn from a population that has not passed its check. A manager asked me to relax it to save a week. I told him I would give him the week back later, and we gave it back by not having a single disputed sample."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED Population first, sample second, evidence third. Nadia's completeness checks saved us more time than any other single step."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Which populations would you check first on a similar project?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you tell a client their extract is incomplete without blaming them?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 7 — Testing: General Controls First", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 7 — Testing: General Controls First"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "We finalised the test plan on 22 May. For each of the thirty-eight key controls there were two tests: a walkthrough of how the control is designed and a test of whether it operated. Seventy-six tests in all, each with written attributes derived from the control description, a sample size taken from the guide, a tester and a different reviewer."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The one planning decision I fought for was the order. General controls first, automated controls second. The reason is the reliance link. A three-way match that passes a test of one tells you the tolerances are right today. To say they were right all year, you need the change-management and access controls around them to have worked, or you need other evidence that nobody touched the configuration. Sofia's team needed that answer. So the access and change tests ran from 15 June to 10 July, and the automated-control tests from 13 July."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Each tester followed the same seven moves: read the control, walk it through, secure the population, select the sample, request the evidence, test every attribute, and conclude. I made Samuel, Elena and Kofi practise on a dummy control before the real tests, then insisted on a short peer review at the end of every workpaper. Not because they were weak, but because the first workpaper from any tester is always the one with a gap."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The easiest-looking tests were the most revealing. For the leavers test, we started from the HR report, not from the SAP lock list. Starting from SAP would only have shown us the users who were removed. For the transports test we started from what production received, not the list of tickets. For the access review we tested the listing the reviewers saw before we tested their decisions. In each case the start point was the system of record, because that is the only place a bypass shows up."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "By 31 July every operating test was done. We had a pile of exceptions and no idea yet what they meant."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT I put the general controls ahead of the automated controls and kept the schedule even when the finance pod complained that they were idle for three weeks. Their idle weeks became their review weeks, and the reliance conclusions were all the stronger."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED Start every test from the system of record, not from the process that is supposed to produce the record. Two tests per key control: design first. If the design is wrong, operating effectiveness is academic."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you explain the order of tests to a finance team waiting to start?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What would be your system of record for a leavers test, a change test and a payment test?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 8 — What the Tests Found", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 8 — What the Tests Found"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The numbers were uncomfortable. Of the thirty-eight key controls, we had exceptions on twenty-three operating tests, two of them ineffective, and four design failures. Behind those numbers were stories."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Privileged access was the first. Nine dialog users, people who log on every day, held SAP_ALL, the profile that bypasses every authorisation check. Four generic IDs were active. The person who owned the monthly review had left in January and nobody replaced them. Tunde asked the most useful question of the project: \"What have these users done?\" Ivan pulled the transaction statistics and the change documents for each of the nine for the whole period. Display transactions, mostly. Two had changed document header texts. None had touched configuration or master data. That analysis would later decide whether the finding was a material weakness or a significant deficiency."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Fourteen role assignments had been made directly in the user maintenance transactions by Basis users, outside the GRC workflow. On two dates, production had been opened for changes without a ticket, and in the first window two Z-tables were changed. The pattern pointed to one origin: the 2025 upgrade, when the team had needed fast access, never withdrew it."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "In the payment process, three weaknesses lined up. Sixty-one users had unmitigated critical segregation conflicts, including the ability to create a vendor and pay it. Six of twenty-five vendor bank-detail changes had no call-back to the vendor. Four AP users could write to the payment file directory. Any one would have been a finding. Together they described an open door."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Elsewhere the exceptions were smaller but telling. Six of twenty-five leavers were locked late, because the HR report reached Security weekly. A custom report built for the purchase-order exception review silently excluded orders marked for deletion, which meant thirty-one same-user purchase and receipt exceptions worth USD 210,000 were invisible to the reviewer. Eighteen EDI order messages sat in error for over a month, USD 84,000 of unbilled sales. And several reviews were late or thin: journals, bank reconciliations, JV statements, a BW consolidation load."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "There was also good news, and I made sure the team and the client heard it. The access review, the backup process, the direct-change restrictions, the integration provider's SOC 1 report and seven of the key automated controls tested effective. The three-way match, posting-period control, depreciation configuration and journal-release workflow were sound. We had found a landscape with serious but concentrated weaknesses, not a landscape in disrepair."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT I presented the good results first at the steering committee. Not to soften what followed, but because Eleanor needed to know what could be relied on before she heard what could not."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED Always ask \"what have these users done?\". Usage analysis is what turns a scary configuration finding into a measured one. Report what is working. The client needs to know what to protect."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Which of these findings would you take to the CIO in person first, and why?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you analyse the nine SAP_ALL users to decide how serious the finding is?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 9 — The Hard Week: Deciding What It Meant", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 9 — The Hard Week: Deciding What It Meant"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3 August was the hardest day of the project. We had thirty deficiencies and twenty-seven failed tests, and the next ten days decided how the client and the auditors would see them."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A deficiency is not an exception. Every exception goes through a path: is it real, is it design or operating, does it exceed the tolerated number in the sample, what could go wrong, what else compensates, and does it connect to others. We ran each one past that path with the pod leads, and discarded none. Then Carlos and I did the part nobody enjoys: grouping."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "DEF-001, the nine SAP_ALL users, looked on its own like a moderate finding. But it shared a cause, a team and a time with the direct role assignments, the two production openings, the late Firefighter reviews and the missing role-owner approvals. They all pointed at the same event: the upgrade. Grouped, they described a team that could bypass access and change controls at will. We rated the group a significant deficiency. The deciding factor in not rating it a material weakness was the evidence: full-period change logs for the automated-control tables showed no unauthorised change, and usage analysis for the privileged users showed no misuse."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The second group was the payment process: unmitigated critical conflicts, weak bank-change verification, an unprotected payment file. Compensating controls existed but two of them were themselves deficient. We rated it a significant deficiency too, and before we did, Fatima and Kofi examined every vendor bank change and every payment run for the period. They found nothing improper. That substantive review is why I could tell the CFO the exposure was theoretical, not actual."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The conversation with Eleanor was short. I did not bring slides. I brought the coverage matrix and two sheets of paper, one for each group. I said what we found, what we did to measure the impact, what we recommended, and what the auditors were likely to think. She asked one question: \"Can this be fixed before year end?\" I said yes for twenty-one of the thirty, and told her honestly which nine would need longer and why. She did not argue."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The reliance decisions came next. For each of the twelve automated controls, the two report controls and the review controls that depend on reports, we asked three questions. Could the configuration have changed without authorisation? Could the control have been overridden? If so, could we show with other evidence that it did not happen? Of sixty-one dependency links, eleven were fully supported because the general control around them was effective, forty-nine were supported with documented additional procedures, and one, the payment-file integrity control, was not relied on for the period. Sofia's team accepted our reasoning and tested the configuration themselves only for the nine tables we had flagged."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT I decided to group the findings before I presented any of them. Presented individually, thirty findings would have made Corvane look like it had no controls. Presented as seven groups with causes, they looked like what they were: a few concentrated failures with identifiable origins, and a long tail of small ones."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED Do the follow-up work before the severity discussion. The usage analysis and the substantive review changed the answer from \"possible material weakness\" to \"significant deficiency, no loss\". Group by cause. A client can fix a cause; they cannot fix thirty items."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What follow-up evidence would you gather before rating a privileged-access finding?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you decide whether three weaknesses on one fraud scenario are one finding or three?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 10 — Remediation: Turning Findings into Fixes", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 10 — Remediation: Turning Findings into Fixes"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A finding that is not fixed is only a report. I wanted every deficiency to have an owner, a date and a fix that addressed the cause, not just the instance. Removing SAP_ALL from nine users fixes nine users. Preventing Basis from assigning roles without a request, and alerting when production opens, fixes the cause."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "We wrote the remediation plans with the owners, not for them. Tunde sat with Neil Abernathy, the IT Security Manager, and agreed that SAP_ALL would exist only on Firefighter IDs, that Basis would lose user and role maintenance rights, and that GRC would alert on any direct assignment. Mei and Viktor agreed that opening production would trigger an automatic alert to the CIO and to me. Aiko in treasury restricted the payment directory to the integration service account and Yuki, the integration lead, added a hash check that alerted if the file the bank received differed from the file SAP created."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Some fixes were technical, some were behavioural. The late Firefighter reviews were fixed with a reminder at day three and an escalation at day five. The missing bank-change call-backs were fixed by removing the \"urgent\" path altogether. The HR leavers lag disappeared when Claire Foster agreed to send a daily feed into GRC that locks the account automatically. The purchase-order report was corrected and moved under change control, with a checklist for every report used in a key control."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I insisted on three rules. Every remediation date fell before the retest, never after. Every owner got a one-page plain-language guide from Ruth describing what they were agreeing to do. And I told the steering committee that a deficiency would be closed only when a retest showed the new control operating, not when someone told me it was done."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The remediation window ran from August to September. We had underestimated one thing: how many occurrences a control needs before a retest is meaningful. A weekly control needs about five weeks. A monthly control needs two month-ends. A quarterly control needs two quarters. We worked back from the retest dates and moved the implementation dates for weekly and daily controls forward by a week, and for the rest we agreed in advance with the auditors that a short operating period would leave the deficiency open."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT I let the retest calendar set the remediation deadlines, not the other way round. It made the plan harder to agree and made the results harder to argue with."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED Fix the cause, not the instance. Write the plan with the owner, in their language. Know how many occurrences each control needs before you promise a retest date."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you decide which fixes to agree first?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you explain to a CFO why a monthly control cannot be retested after three weeks?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 11 — Retest, Results and the Nine That Stayed Open", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 11 — Retest, Results and the Nine That Stayed Open"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The retests ran from 7 to 25 September. By the end, nineteen retests were done and every one was effective, closing twenty-one deficiencies, including both significant-deficiency groups."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The results are in the workbooks, but here they are in plain terms. Nine dialog users with SAP_ALL became none: SAP_ALL now exists only on Firefighter IDs. Fourteen direct role assignments in five months became zero among 188 assignments since remediation. Sixty-one users with unmitigated critical conflicts became none. Six late leaver locks out of twenty-five became none out of twenty-five. Six bank changes without a call-back out of twenty-five became none. Four people with write access to the payment file directory became one service account, and the hash alert caught a test alteration within minutes. Eighteen aged EDI order errors became none. Two unapproved openings of production became none."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Nine deficiencies stayed open, all at the lowest severity and none material. Five were quarterly or monthly controls that need more operating cycles: the quarterly database administrator review and the monthly reviews for journals, bank reconciliations, JV statements and the BW load. One was the emergency-transport approval, which cannot be tested until there is an emergency. Two were design fixes still in progress: the redesigned volume reconciliation and the locked depletion input file. One was a single AUC settlement documentation gap that needs the next qualifying settlements. I told the steering committee exactly which retest would close each one, and on what date."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Thirty became nine. That is a seventy percent reduction. I kept repeating the denominator, because a number without a base invites argument. Thirty deficiencies were logged in the baseline cycle. Twenty-one were closed after a retest showed the new control operating. Nine remain, with owners and due dates between 27 November and 18 December. And the severity of what is left is a fraction of what we started with: two significant-deficiency groups became none, and there is no material weakness."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "There was a second result that mattered as much. The auditors relied on the automated controls. Sofia confirmed in writing that for the period, the three-way match, posting-period control, depreciation configuration, journal-release workflow and the others could be relied upon, supported by the general controls and the additional procedures we had documented. Her team's own testing of IT-dependent areas shrank accordingly."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I did not claim victory. The nine open deficiencies are real. But on 30 September 2026 the coverage dashboard read: every risk covered, every key control tested, every deficiency evaluated, every exception traced to a deficiency, and a conclusion of no material weakness and no significant deficiency."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT I gave the steering committee a one-page close-out with a table of what was fixed, a table of what was open and when each would close, and the thirty-to-nine headline with its base. I deliberately resisted rounding the story up."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED Always state the denominator. \"Seventy percent fewer\" needs \"of thirty, logged in the baseline cycle\". Say plainly what is still open and when it closes. Credibility is made in the last meeting."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "How would you present partial success to a board that wants a clean result?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What would you do about an open deficiency that depends on an event that may not happen soon?"}]}, {"title": "Chapter 12 — Handover and What I Would Do Differently", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Chapter 12 — Handover and What I Would Do Differently"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A controls project that ends when the consultants leave has not ended. In the last month we handed the workbooks to Priya's team and Ruth ran four half-day sessions for the control owners, one for each pod's area. We left a monthly dashboard that reads the same coverage checks, a quarterly calendar for the access review and the DBA review, and a plain list of the nine open items with their retest dates. Priya's deputy now owns the foundation workbooks. Martin's team uses them for its own audits."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I learned a great deal and some of it the hard way."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I would have asked for the HR leavers feed in the first month, not the fifth. A daily feed is a quick technical fix that would have prevented one of the deficiencies, and I knew the weekly report was a problem from the first walkthrough. I did not raise it because I wanted to be sure. I should have raised it and been wrong."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I would have scheduled the weekly-control fixes earlier. The nine open deficiencies are partly an honest outcome and partly a result of my own calendar. If I had pulled the weekly and monthly remediation dates forward by three weeks, at least two of them would have closed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I would have involved Internal Audit in the design of the remediation, not just the testing. Martin's team had ideas I only heard in the lessons-learned session."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "I would have taken the fifteen-person team to the client's site for one week together in the first month. The pods worked well, but the project was strongest in the week the whole team shared a room."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "And I would have said no more often. At least twice I accepted requests for extra testing that were interesting but outside the scope we had signed. Each time the cost was a week of one pod's time and no change in what the client knew."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "If you are the next lead, here is what I would tell you in one paragraph. Build the foundation before you test anything. Make the thread from landscape to conclusion traceable in both directions. Hold the line on the population before the sample. Test general controls first. Group findings by cause. Talk to people before you write to them. Fix causes, not instances. Do not close anything without a retest. Say what is still open. And care about the people on your team and your client's team, because in thirty-seven weeks they are the project."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEAD'S DECISION POINT The last decision I made was to leave the workbooks with the client and not take a copy for ourselves. A controls project that produces a dependency on its consultants has failed on its own terms."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WHAT I LEARNED The measure of the project is the state of the client when you leave, not the report you hand over."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Questions for the new lead"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "What would you say no to on a similar project?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Which two things from this story would you do differently in your first month?"}]}, {"title": "Appendix A — Timeline", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix A — Timeline"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Date (2026)", "Milestone", "Gate"], ["12 Jan", "Kickoff; steering committee signs the one-page scope", ""], ["6 Feb", "Systems and interfaces listed and validated", "G1"], ["20 Feb", "Processes mapped to systems and accounts", "G2"], ["6 Mar", "Risk register agreed with owners", "G3"], ["27 Mar", "Controls and SoD ruleset approved", "G4"], ["10 Apr", "All 47 owners confirmed; scoping workpaper issued", "G5"], ["Apr to 12 Jun", "95 evidence requests in four waves; populations pass IPE checks", "G6"], ["22 May", "Test plan finalised", "G7"], ["15 Jun to 31 Jul", "General control tests, then automated and review control tests", "G8"], ["3 to 14 Aug", "Deficiency evaluation, grouping and reliance decisions", "G9 (prep)"], ["17 Aug to 4 Sep", "Remediation implemented", ""], ["7 to 25 Sep", "19 retests completed, all effective", "G9"], ["30 Sep", "Close-out: 30 deficiencies logged, 21 closed, 9 open; no material weakness", ""]]}]}, {"title": "Corvane at a glance", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Corvane at a glance"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Measure", "Result"], ["Systems / interfaces / processes", "11 systems (9 in scope); 11 interfaces; 14 business processes plus 4 technical areas"], ["Risks / controls", "36 risks (32 SOX-relevant); 47 controls (38 key); 61 general-control dependency links"], ["Segregation of duties", "20 functions; 16 rules; 4 critical rules that cannot be mitigated"], ["Evidence", "95 requests; 109 evidence items; 45 population checks, all Pass"], ["Testing", "76 baseline tests; 25 samples (379 items); 19 retests completed, 9 planned"], ["Deficiencies", "30 logged; 21 closed after retest; 9 open (control-deficiency severity); 2 significant-deficiency groups closed; 0 material weaknesses"], ["Reliance on automated controls", "49 dependencies supported with additional procedures; 11 supported; 1 not relied on"]]}]}, {"title": "Appendix B — Before and After", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix B — Before and After"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Measure", "Baseline (Jan to May 2026)", "After remediation (to 18 Sep 2026)"], ["Deficiencies logged / open", "30", "9 (70 percent reduction)"], ["Significant-deficiency groups", "2", "0"], ["Dialog users holding SAP_ALL", "9", "0 (Firefighter IDs only)"], ["Users with unmitigated critical conflicts", "61", "0"], ["Role assignments outside GRC", "14", "0 of 188"], ["Leavers locked late", "6 of 25", "0 of 25"], ["Bank changes without call-back", "6 of 25", "0 of 25"], ["Users able to write to the payment file directory", "4", "1 (service account)"], ["Aged EDI order errors over 30 days", "18", "0"], ["Unapproved openings of production", "2", "0"]]}]}, {"title": "Where to find each artefact", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Where to find each artefact"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["What", "Workbook"], ["Systems, servers, transport routes", "W01"], ["Interfaces and RFC users", "W02"], ["Processes and financial statement accounts", "W03"], ["The 36 risks", "W04"], ["The 47 controls and 61 reliance links", "W05"], ["SoD rules, sensitive access, mitigations", "W06"], ["Owners of every control", "W07"], ["The 95 evidence requests", "W08"], ["The 109 evidence items and 45 population checks", "W09"], ["The 76 tests and 28 retests", "W10"], ["Workpapers (six worked examples)", "W11"], ["The 25 plus 12 samples and their items", "W12"], ["The 30 deficiencies and 7 groups", "W13"], ["Coverage dashboard and conclusion", "W14"]]}]}], "word_count": 5828, "id": "corvane-story-controls-lead-narrative", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/Corvane_Story_Controls_Lead_Narrative.docx", "filename": "Corvane_Story_Controls_Lead_Narrative.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "Corvane Weekly Activity Plan — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Corvane Weekly Activity Plan | Corvane Energy Group"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Converts the Corvane Story into discrete activities grouped by week for the 38 calendar weeks from 12 January to 30 September 2026. Each activity has an owner, supporters, client stakeholders, the workbook where it is recorded and the output that shows it is done. Week_Summary gives the focus and gate of each week; Pod_Activity shows how work moves between the pods."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Filter Weekly_Plan by Week to see what the team does that week, or by Owner or Pod to follow one person or pod across the project."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Read Week_Summary for the phase, gate and focus of each week."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Read Pod_Activity to see when each pod is busy and when it is free to help another."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. To reuse on a new project, change the start date in Setup, replace the activities and re-read Checks."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). Weekly_Plan holds one discrete activity per row, grouped by project week; Week_Summary, Team and Pod_Activity read it."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every week has at least one discrete activity with an owner and an output."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Gates G1 to G9 each fall in a stated week."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every owner is a member of the team."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The Corvane Story, chapters 1 to 12; Controls Lead Playbook sections 2, 3 and 5."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Week", "Project week, 1 to 38", "Number 1 to 38", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Activity ID", "Auto number within the week", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Week commencing", "Monday of the week", "Formula from the project start date", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Phase", "Phase of the week", "Formula from Week_Summary", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Gate / milestone", "Gate or milestone falling in the week", "Formula from Week_Summary", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Activity", "One discrete activity, started and finished in the week", "Verb first; one deliverable", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Owner", "Person accountable for the activity", "Person in the Team tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Pod", "Owner pod", "Formula from Team", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Supported by", "Others who help", "First names", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Client stakeholder(s)", "Client people involved", "Names", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Workbook / artefact", "Where the work is recorded", "Workbook and key", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Output (done when)", "Evidence the activity is finished", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Story chapter", "Chapter of the Corvane Story", "Formula from Week_Summary", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Week", "Project week", "1 to 38", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Week commencing", "Monday of the week", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Phase", "Phase of the project", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Gate / milestone", "Gate or milestone in the week", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Focus of the week", "What the week is for", "One line", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Story chapter", "Chapter of the Corvane Story", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Activities", "Count of activities in the week", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Steering this week?", "Fortnightly steering on the Friday of odd weeks", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Team", "Person", "Name", "Full name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Team", "Role", "Role on the project", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Team", "Pod", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 563, "id": "corvane-weekly-activity-plan-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/Corvane_Weekly_Activity_Plan.xlsx", "filename": "Corvane_Weekly_Activity_Plan.xlsx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Deployment Guide"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set (W01–W14)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "How to set up, connect, populate, check and sign off the fourteen workbooks on a controls engagement or training lab"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026  |  Companion to the five-part manual \"Building the SAP Controls Project Foundation\""}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training and engagement use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "1. Purpose and Scope", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "1. Purpose and Scope"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "This guide explains how to deploy the fourteen Excel workbooks that define the foundation of an SAP controls project: from the landscape (W01) to the conclusion (W14). It covers the package contents, how to set up an engagement folder, how the workbooks connect to each other, the order in which they are populated, how to read their built-in checks, who does what, and how to handle evidence. It is written for engagement managers, senior consultants and course instructors. The manual explains why each workbook exists and how to fill it; this guide explains how to run the set as a whole."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["THE ONE IDEA TO HOLD ON TO Every workbook carries a unique key, and every downstream workbook carries the keys of the rows it depends on. The workbooks do not link to each other through Excel links. Instead, the keys you need from upstream are pasted as values into the small Ref_ tabs of the downstream workbook. That keeps each file independent and portable, and it puts one disciplined step between every stage: the paste."]]}]}, {"title": "2. What Is in the Package", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "2. What Is in the Package"}]}, {"title": "2.1 Documents", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.1 Documents"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Document", "Content", "Use"], ["Manual Part 1", "Sessions 1–6: golden thread, W01–W05", "Design of the environment, risk and control workbooks"], ["Manual Part 2", "Sessions 7–16: W06–W14, population sequence, gates", "Design of the evidence, test and conclusion workbooks"], ["Manual Part 3A", "Sessions 17–24: testing access controls", "Workpaper guidance for access tests"], ["Manual Part 3B", "Sessions 25–34: change, operations, ITAC, reliance, IPE", "Workpaper guidance for the other ITGC domains and automated controls"], ["Manual Part 4", "Sessions 35–40: deficiency evaluation, remediation, reporting, trace exercise", "Evaluation, reporting and the final trainee exercise"], ["This guide", "Deployment and operation of the workbook set", "Rollout reference"]]}]}, {"title": "2.2 Workbooks", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.2 Workbooks"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "File", "Primary key", "Phase"], ["W01", "W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx", "SYS-ID", "Environment"], ["W02", "W02_Interface_Data_Flow_Inventory.xlsx", "INT-ID", "Environment"], ["W03", "W03_Module_Business_Process_Scope.xlsx", "BP-ID", "Environment"], ["W04", "W04_Risk_Register.xlsx", "RISK-ID", "Risk and control design"], ["W05", "W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx", "CTRL-ID", "Risk and control design"], ["W06", "W06_SoD_Sensitive_Access_Matrix.xlsx", "SOD-ID", "Risk and control design"], ["W07", "W07_Control_Evidence_Owner_Matrix.xlsx", "CTRL-ID", "Risk and control design"], ["W08", "W08_PBC_Evidence_Request_Register.xlsx", "PBC-ID", "Evidence"], ["W09", "W09_Evidence_Index_IPE_Log.xlsx", "EV-ID", "Evidence"], ["W10", "W10_Test_Plan.xlsx", "TEST-ID", "Test"], ["W11", "W11_Test_Workpapers.xlsx", "TEST-ID", "Test"], ["W12", "W12_Sample_Selection_Log.xlsx", "SMP-ID", "Test"], ["W13", "W13_Deficiency_Remediation_Log.xlsx", "DEF-ID", "Conclude"], ["W14", "W14_Scoping_Workpaper_Coverage_Dashboard.xlsx", "—", "Conclude"]]}]}, {"title": "2.3 What every workbook contains", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.3 What every workbook contains"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Instructions tab: purpose, how to use, colour legend, completion gate, and a column dictionary (definition and allowed values for every field)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Register tabs: yellow input cells with blue text, grey formula cells, dropdowns, frozen header, filter, and a worked ABC Manufacturing example in the first row(s)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Ref_ tabs: where you paste keys from upstream workbooks."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Checks tab: the workbook's completion gate as formulas (PASS, REVIEW, INFO, plus HOLD and SCOPING where relevant)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Hidden Lists tab: the dropdown values. Visible, editable lists exist where users are expected to maintain them (Control_Areas in W05, Sample_Guide in W10, Templates in W11)."}]}, {"title": "3. Setting Up an Engagement", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3. Setting Up an Engagement"}]}, {"title": "3.1 Folder structure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.1 Folder structure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Create one folder per engagement, with the following subfolders. Keep the workbooks together so that every team member opens the same version."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Folder", "Contents"], ["00_Admin", "Engagement letter, scope statement, contact list, this guide"], ["01_Foundation_Workbooks", "W01–W14, one file each, plus an Archive subfolder for superseded versions"], ["02_Evidence_Raw", "Original extracts and documents exactly as received, read-only. Never edited."], ["03_Evidence_Working", "Copies used by testers, screenshots with annotations"], ["04_Workpapers", "One workpaper per test (copy of the W11 Workpaper tab) if kept as separate files"], ["05_Reports", "Draft and final reports, management responses"]]}]}, {"title": "3.2 Naming and versioning", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.2 Naming and versioning"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Keep the file names exactly as supplied so that references in the manual and in instructions remain valid."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Version by saving a dated copy into the Archive subfolder at each gate (for example W05_G4_2026-10-14.xlsx). The live file keeps the standard name."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Evidence files follow the W09 convention: EV-ID_SYS_description_date.ext (for example EV-019_ECP_STMS_import_history_2026-10-01.csv)."}]}, {"title": "3.3 Excel settings", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.3 Excel settings"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Use a current desktop version of Excel. Keep automatic calculation on. The checks rely on formulas that recalculate as you type."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Do not sort the register tabs by dragging a selection. Use the filter buttons; sorting only part of a table breaks the row formulas."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Do not delete the grey formula columns. If a formula column is damaged, copy the formula down from the row above."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Optional: protect the sheets with the grey columns locked and the yellow cells unlocked, so formula cells cannot be overwritten. This is not applied in the templates so that they stay easy to adapt."}]}, {"title": "4. How the Workbooks Connect", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "4. How the Workbooks Connect"}]}, {"title": "4.1 The paste rule", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.1 The paste rule"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Each downstream workbook has one or more Ref_ tabs with a header describing exactly what to paste. The rule is simple: copy the named columns from the upstream workbook and paste them into the Ref_ tab as values only (Paste Special > Values), starting at cell A2. Do not paste formulas, and do not paste the header row. The yellow cells hold ABC Manufacturing sample values that you overwrite."}]}, {"title": "4.2 Paste map", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.2 Paste map"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Workbook", "Ref_ tab", "Columns to paste", "Copied from"], ["W02", "Ref_SYS", "SYS-ID", "W01 System_Inventory"], ["W04", "Ref_BP", "BP-ID", "W03 BP_Index (formula column; copy and paste values)"], ["W05", "Ref_RISK", "RISK-ID, SOX relevant?", "W04 Risk_Register"], ["W06", "Ref_RISK", "RISK-ID, Risk category", "W04 Risk_Register"], ["W06", "Ref_CTRL", "CTRL-ID", "W05 Control_Register"], ["W07", "Ref_CTRL", "CTRL-ID, Control title, Control owner, Expected evidence, Key control?, Risk owner", "W05 Control_Register (risk owner from W04)"], ["W08", "Ref_CTRL", "CTRL-ID, Control title, Evidence owner, Expected evidence, Key control?", "W05 Control_Register; evidence owner from W07"], ["W09", "Ref_PBC", "PBC-ID, Period from, Period to, Evidence type", "W08 PBC_Register"], ["W10", "Ref_CTRL", "CTRL-ID, Control title, Category, Frequency, Key?, SOX in scope?", "W05 Control_Register"], ["W10", "Ref_EV", "EV-ID, IPE result, Record count", "W09 Evidence_Index"], ["W11", "Ref_TEST", "TEST-ID", "W10 Test_Plan"], ["W12", "Ref_TEST", "TEST-ID, Population EV-ID, IPE result, Population size, Planned sample size", "W10 Test_Plan and W09"], ["W13", "Ref_TEST", "TEST-ID, CTRL-ID, RISK-ID, BP-ID, Result", "W10 Test_Plan (risk and BP from W05/W04)"], ["W14", "Ref_Risk_Control", "RISK-ID, SOX?, Key controls mapped, Worst TOD, Worst TOE", "W05 Coverage_Check and W10"], ["W14", "Ref_Controls", "CTRL-ID, Category, Key?", "W05 Control_Register"], ["W14", "Ref_Tests", "TEST-ID, CTRL-ID, Type, Result, Status, IPE result (or n/a), Sample logged (Y/N/n/a), Category", "W10, W09, W12, W05"], ["W14", "Ref_Deficiencies", "DEF-ID, TEST-ID, CTRL-ID, RISK-ID, Severity, Group, Status", "W13 Deficiency_Log"], ["W14", "Ref_Reliance", "Dependent CTRL-ID, ITGC CTRL-ID, ITGC result, Reliance decision", "W05 ITGC_Dependency"]]}]}, {"title": "4.3 Keeping references in sync", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.3 Keeping references in sync"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Re-paste at every gate. Upstream data changes (a new control, a changed owner). Before reading a downstream Checks tab, re-paste the Ref_ tab. A stale Ref_ tab is the most common cause of confusing results."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Keys never change. If a row leaves scope, set In scope = N and keep the key. Renumbering breaks every link downstream."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Create new items at source. A new risk or control is added to W04 or W05 first and then re-pasted downstream. It is never created only in a workpaper."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Capacity. Registers hold 200 rows (W12 Items holds 1,000); Ref_ tabs hold 200 keys. See section 9 for extending them."}]}, {"title": "5. Reading the Built-In Checks", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "5. Reading the Built-In Checks"}]}, {"title": "5.1 Row checks", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.1 Row checks"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Most registers have a Row check or Check column (grey). It tests completeness, not correctness: for example, \"Evidence ref needed\" or \"Use the risk statement formula\". A row passes when it reads OK (or begins with OK, such as \"OK (test pending)\"). Filter the column to anything that is not OK and work through the list."}]}, {"title": "5.2 The Checks tab", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.2 The Checks tab"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Status", "Meaning", "Action"], ["PASS", "The count is zero (or positive, where a minimum is required)", "None"], ["REVIEW", "A gate condition is not yet met", "Follow the \"What to do\" column and re-check"], ["INFO", "A count for information (for example, rows not yet validated)", "Judge whether it matters at this stage"], ["HOLD", "Appears in row checks: the population has not passed IPE", "Do not sample until IPE = Pass"], ["SCOPING", "W14 only: testing-stage check not yet applicable", "Switch Stage to Testing when fieldwork starts"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The overall status at the top of each Checks tab reads ALL CHECKS PASS or REVIEW ITEMS: n. A workbook is ready for its gate when it reads ALL CHECKS PASS and every INFO line has been read and accepted."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["EXPECT REVIEW ON A TEMPLATE WITH SAMPLE DATA A new template holds one worked example against several reference rows, so some checks show REVIEW by design (for example, nine SOX risks with no key control in W05). They clear as the real records are entered. If a check still shows REVIEW after entry, it is telling you something true about the engagement."]]}]}, {"title": "6. Rollout Sequence and Gates", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "6. Rollout Sequence and Gates"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Follow the sequence below. Each stage has a gate: do not start the next stage until the gate is passed. The gates are the same as in Session 16 of the manual."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Stage", "Workbooks", "Gate", "Read before passing the gate"], ["1. Understand the environment", "W01, W02", "G1", "W01 and W02 Checks: all financially relevant systems and interfaces listed; validated or flagged; PRD transport route recorded"], ["2. Define process scope", "W03", "G2", "W03 Checks: every significant FSLI mapped; technical areas BP-TECH-01 to 03 present; unspecified processes Outstanding, not guessed"], ["3. Identify risks", "W04", "G3", "W04 Checks: no BP-ID without a risk; SOX risks flagged; owners agreed"], ["4. Design the control set", "W05, W06", "G4", "W05 Coverage_Check: no SOX risk without a key control; ITGC dependencies recorded. W06 Checks: every SoD risk has a rule; ruleset approved"], ["5. Confirm people", "W07", "G5", "W07 Checks: owners confirmed or escalated. Issue W14 in scoping form: \"No conclusion at scoping stage\""], ["6. Request and receive evidence", "W08, W09", "G6", "W09 Checks: populations received; every IPE result Pass"], ["7. Plan the tests", "W10", "G7", "W10 Checks: TOD and TOE for every key control; ITGC tests scheduled first"], ["8. Sample and test", "W12, W11", "G8", "W12 and W11 Checks: samples logged; workpapers reviewed; exceptions carry a DEF-ID or rationale"], ["9. Evaluate and conclude", "W13, W14", "G9", "W13 Checks and W14 Checks with Stage = Testing: all coverage checks pass; conclusion signed"]]}]}, {"title": "6.1 Scoping deliverable versus testing deliverable", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "6.1 Scoping deliverable versus testing deliverable"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Stages 1 to 5, plus the wave 1 and 2 evidence requests, make up the scope of work. At gate G5, W14 is issued with Stage = Scoping. Stages 6 to 9 are the testing phase that the scope of work plans and controls. Keep the G5 versions of W01–W08 and W14 in the Archive folder as the formal scope record."}]}, {"title": "6.2 Iteration", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "6.2 Iteration"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The sequence is not strictly one-way. A walkthrough in stage 3 may reveal an interface missing from W02, or a test may show a control works differently from its RCM description. Update the source workbook, keep the keys, re-paste downstream, and let the checks show the effect. Never patch a downstream workbook to cover an upstream gap."}]}, {"title": "7. Roles and Responsibilities", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "7. Roles and Responsibilities"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Role", "Responsible for", "Workbooks"], ["Engagement manager", "Scope, gates, severity approval, final conclusion; owns W14", "W14; reviews all"], ["Senior consultant", "Risk and control design, SoD ruleset, reliance assessment, deficiency evaluation", "W04, W05, W06, W13"], ["Tester / consultant", "Evidence requests, indexing, IPE checks, sampling, workpapers", "W08, W09, W11, W12"], ["Reviewer", "Independent review of workpapers and results; must differ from the tester", "W10, W11"], ["Client coordinator", "Single point of contact; tracks requests and dependencies with the client", "W07, W08, W14"], ["Basis / IT lead (client)", "System facts, extracts, transport routes, interface mechanics", "W01, W02, W09"], ["Process, risk and control owners (client)", "Confirm processes, risks, controls, owners, evidence", "W03, W04, W05, W07"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "In a training lab, the instructor plays the client coordinator and the engagement manager; trainees take the tester and senior consultant roles, working in pairs so that each has a reviewer."}]}, {"title": "8. Evidence Handling and Confidentiality", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "8. Evidence Handling and Confidentiality"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Raw originals are read-only. Store each file as received in 02_Evidence_Raw and record Original preserved = Y in W09. Work only on copies."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Extracts contain personal and sensitive data (user lists, payroll, vendor bank details). Store them only in the engagement folder, restrict folder access to the engagement team, and do not send them by unprotected email."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Do not include personal data in reports. User IDs belong in workpapers; reports refer to roles and counts."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Record how every extract was produced. W09 asks who ran it, when and how. Where possible, watch the extract being run, or capture the selection parameters in a screenshot with the system and date visible."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Retain per policy. Agree the retention and secure deletion of evidence with the client at the start of the engagement."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Client and ISP names. Use the real client names in engagement documents. Fictitious names are used only in the training manual's ABC Manufacturing case."}]}, {"title": "9. Customising the Templates", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "9. Customising the Templates"}]}, {"title": "9.1 Extending capacity", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.1 Extending capacity"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "More rows in a register: copy the last data row (including formulas, formatting and data validation) and paste it below. Then extend the ranges used by the Checks tab by editing the row limit in its formulas (the registers use rows 5 to 204)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "More keys in a Ref_ tab: the Ref_ tabs and their lookups cover rows 2 to 201. For more than 200 keys, split the engagement into two sets of workbooks (for example, by system) rather than extending ranges everywhere."}]}, {"title": "9.2 Editing lists and guides", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.2 Editing lists and guides"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Control_Areas (W05) and Templates (W11) are visible lists: add or rename entries."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Sample_Guide (W10): edit the low and high sample sizes to match the engagement firm or the external auditor's methodology. The manual's values are typical practice, not a standard."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Dropdown lists elsewhere are on the hidden Lists tab. Unhide it, edit the values, and keep the list items in a single contiguous column."}]}, {"title": "9.3 Adding columns", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.3 Adding columns"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Add new columns to the right of the last column (after the Row check column), never in the middle. Inserting a column shifts the letters that the row checks and the Checks tab depend on. If you must insert a column, update every formula that refers to the columns to its right."}]}, {"title": "9.4 Adding a second landscape or entity", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.4 Adding a second landscape or entity"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Use a separate set of fourteen workbooks for a separate legal entity or a separate SAP landscape. Combine results in W14 by pasting both sets into its Ref_ tabs with distinct key prefixes (for example, SYS-A-01 and SYS-B-01)."}]}, {"title": "10. Troubleshooting", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "10. Troubleshooting"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Symptom", "Likely cause", "Fix"], ["Lookup column is blank", "Key not in the Ref_ tab, or Ref_ tab is stale", "Re-paste the Ref_ tab as values; check for trailing spaces in the key"], ["Row check says \"not in W0x\"", "The upstream key does not exist or was renamed", "Add the item at source and re-paste; never retype keys"], ["Dropdown rejects a value", "Value not on the list; or list has been edited", "Pick from the list, or add the value to the list"], ["Dropdown warns but accepts", "Key lookups use warning-only validation", "Check that the key really exists upstream"], ["Counts look too low", "Rows pasted below row 204, or filter left on", "Clear filters; keep data within the register range"], ["#N/A or #VALUE! after a paste", "Formulas pasted into a Ref_ tab, or headers pasted into data rows", "Paste values only, from cell A2, without headers"], ["W10 sample \"outside guide\"", "Sample size outside Sample_Guide low and high", "Justify and record, or change the guide to match methodology"], ["W14 conclusion \"Not yet supportable\"", "Coverage checks open at Testing stage", "Open the Checks tab and clear each REVIEW"], ["Status shows HOLD", "Population IPE is not Pass", "Complete or repeat the IPE check in W09 before sampling"], ["File is slow", "Many volatile TODAY() formulas and large ranges", "Set calculation to manual while pasting, then recalculate (F9)"]]}]}, {"title": "11. Pilot Checklist and Sign-Off", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "11. Pilot Checklist and Sign-Off"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Before using the set on a live engagement or in a live cohort, run a pilot with the ABC Manufacturing data and tick each item."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Check", "Done"], ["1", "All fourteen files open without a repair prompt; calculation is automatic", ""], ["2", "Every Instructions tab is read; the column dictionary matches the manual", ""], ["3", "The example rows are overwritten with a real or training record in each register", ""], ["4", "Every Ref_ tab is pasted as values from the correct upstream workbook (section 4.2)", ""], ["5", "Every Checks tab has been read; every REVIEW is explained or cleared", ""], ["6", "W01 and W02: all PRD systems and financially relevant interfaces listed; transport route recorded", ""], ["7", "W04 and W05: every SOX risk has a key control; every ITAC/IPE/ITDM control lists dependent ITGCs", ""], ["8", "W06: every SoD risk has a rule; mitigations are real controls in W05", ""], ["9", "W09: every population has an IPE result of Pass before sampling", ""], ["10", "W10: TOD and TOE exist for every key control; ITGC tests are scheduled first", ""], ["11", "W12 and W11: sample items agree to the sample log; workpapers reviewed by a different person", ""], ["12", "W13 and W14: exceptions carry a DEF-ID or rationale; Stage is set correctly; the trace exercise (manual Session 39) can be completed end to end", ""], ["13", "Archive copies saved at each gate; folder access restricted to the engagement team", ""]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Role", "Name", "Signature", "Date"], ["Engagement manager / instructor", "", "", ""], ["Reviewer", "", "", ""]]}]}, {"title": "Appendix A. Workbook Quick Reference", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix A. Workbook Quick Reference"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Visible tabs", "Ref_ tabs needed", "Gate"], ["W01", "System_Inventory, Server_Detail, Transport_Routes, Open_Items", "None", "G1"], ["W02", "Interface_Register, RFC_Service_Users, Data_Flow_Map, Open_Items", "Ref_SYS", "G1"], ["W03", "Process_Scope, Technical_Areas, FSLI_Map, Open_Items, BP_Index", "None", "G2"], ["W04", "Risk_Register, Coverage_By_BP", "Ref_BP", "G3"], ["W05", "Control_Register, Risk_Control_Map, ITGC_Dependency, Coverage_Check, Control_Areas", "Ref_RISK", "G4"], ["W06", "Functions, Function_Auth, SoD_Rules, Sensitive_Access, Mitigations, Ruleset_Version", "Ref_RISK, Ref_CTRL", "G4"], ["W07", "Owner_Matrix, Open_Items", "Ref_CTRL", "G5"], ["W08", "PBC_Register", "Ref_CTRL", "G6"], ["W09", "Evidence_Index, IPE_Log", "Ref_PBC", "G6"], ["W10", "Test_Plan, Sample_Guide", "Ref_CTRL, Ref_EV", "G7"], ["W11", "WP_Register, Workpaper, Templates", "Ref_TEST", "G8"], ["W12", "Sample_Log, Items", "Ref_TEST", "G8"], ["W13", "Deficiency_Log, Groups", "Ref_TEST", "G9"], ["W14", "Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding, Dependencies, Engagement_Summary, Coverage_Matrix", "Ref_Risk_Control, Ref_Controls, Ref_Tests, Ref_Deficiencies, Ref_Reliance", "G5, G9"]]}]}], "word_count": 1441, "id": "tib-sap-controls-foundation-deployment-guide", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Deployment_Guide.docx", "filename": "TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Deployment_Guide.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Building the SAP Controls Project Foundation"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From Landscape to Every SOX, ITGC and ITAC Test: The Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Training Manual  |  Part 1 of 4: Sessions 1–6 (Golden Thread, W01–W05)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Running case: ABC Manufacturing  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "Session 1 — The Foundation and the Golden Thread", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 1 — The Foundation and the Golden Thread"}]}, {"title": "1.1 What this manual teaches", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.1 What this manual teaches"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A controls project does not begin with testing. It begins with a set of linked Excel workbooks that define, without ambiguity, what is being tested, why, against which risk, using which evidence, and by whom. If that foundation is weak, every test that follows is weak: samples are pulled from the wrong system, controls are tested that address no risk, and evidence cannot be traced back to scope when a reviewer or external auditor asks."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "This manual walks a trainee through the full foundation, one workbook at a time. For each workbook it explains what the workbook is for, how it is structured, every data field and its allowed values, where the data comes from, how it is populated, and how it links to the workbooks before and after it. By the end of the series, a trainee should be able to start from a single SAP server and follow the thread to every test step performed for SOX, ITGC and ITAC purposes, and back again."}]}, {"title": "1.2 The fourteen workbooks", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.2 The fourteen workbooks"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The Lab 1 scoping draft used six workbooks. This manual expands that set to fourteen so the thread continues past scoping into risk, control, test and conclusion. The workbooks are grouped into five phases."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Phase", "#", "Workbook", "Primary key", "Answers the question"], ["1. Environment", "W01", "Landscape & System Inventory", "SYS-ID", "What SAP systems, clients and servers exist, and which are in scope?"], ["", "W02", "Interface & Data Flow Inventory", "INT-ID", "How does financially relevant data move into, out of and between systems?"], ["", "W03", "Module & Business Process Scope", "BP-ID", "Which business processes run in which modules, on which systems?"], ["2. Risk & control design", "W04", "Risk Register", "RISK-ID", "What could go wrong in each process or technical area, and who owns that risk?"], ["", "W05", "Risk & Control Matrix (RCM)", "CTRL-ID", "Which specific control addresses each risk, and how is it classified?"], ["", "W06", "SoD & Sensitive Access Rule Matrix", "SOD-ID", "Which combinations of access are conflicting or sensitive?"], ["", "W07", "Control & Evidence Owner Matrix", "CTRL-ID", "Who operates each control and who can supply the evidence?"], ["3. Evidence", "W08", "PBC / Evidence Request Register", "PBC-ID", "What evidence is requested, from whom, for which period and which control?"], ["", "W09", "Evidence Index & IPE Log", "EV-ID", "What was received, is it complete and accurate, and where is it filed?"], ["4. Test", "W10", "Test Plan / Testing Program", "TEST-ID", "How, when and by whom will each control be tested (design and operating effectiveness)?"], ["", "W11", "Test Workpapers by control area", "TEST-ID", "What exactly was done, on which items, with what result?"], ["", "W12", "Sample Selection Log", "SMP-ID", "Which items were selected from which population, and how?"], ["5. Conclude", "W13", "Deficiency & Remediation Log", "DEF-ID", "What failed, how severe is it, and how is it being fixed?"], ["", "W14", "Scoping Workpaper & Coverage Dashboard", "—", "Is every risk covered, every key control tested, every gap visible?"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1 (this document) covers Session 1 and workbooks W01–W05. Part 2 covers W06–W14 and the population sequence. Parts 3A and 3B cover testing by control area. Part 4 covers deficiency evaluation and the end-to-end trace exercise."}]}, {"title": "1.3 The golden thread", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.3 The golden thread"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every workbook carries a unique primary key, and every downstream workbook carries the keys of the rows it depends on. This is the golden thread. It is what makes a controls project auditable."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "SYS → INT → BP → RISK → CTRL → OWNER → PBC → EV → TEST → SMP → DEF → CONCLUSION"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Read it left to right to plan: start from what exists and work toward what must be tested. Read it right to left to defend: start from any test conclusion and prove which evidence, control, risk, process and system it rests on."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Identifier conventions"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Key", "Pattern", "Example", "Meaning"], ["SYS-ID", "SYS-nn", "SYS-03", "Production S/4HANA client 300"], ["INT-ID", "INT-nn", "INT-02", "Bank statement inbound interface"], ["BP-ID", "BP-<process>-nn", "BP-P2P-02", "Procure-to-Pay: invoice processing"], ["RISK-ID", "R-<process>-nnn", "R-P2P-003", "Invoice paid without valid PO/GR"], ["CTRL-ID", "C-<category>-nnn", "C-ITAC-004   C-ITGC-AC-002", "Three-way match configuration   Termination of access"], ["PBC-ID", "PBC-nnn", "PBC-014", "Terminated users list for the period"], ["EV-ID", "EV-nnn", "EV-031", "Received HR leavers report, indexed"], ["TEST-ID", "<CTRL-ID>-TOD / -TOE", "C-ITGC-AC-002-TOE", "Operating-effectiveness test of control C-ITGC-AC-002"], ["DEF-ID", "DEF-nnn", "DEF-004", "Deficiency raised against a test"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — THE THREAD IS THE PRODUCT A reviewer does not judge a controls project by the number of tests performed. They judge it by whether any single conclusion can be traced, in both directions, without gaps. A test that cannot be traced to a risk is wasted effort. A risk that cannot be traced to a test is an unreported exposure."]]}]}, {"title": "1.4 How the records relate", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.4 How the records relate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The relationships between workbooks are not all one-to-one. Trainees must understand the cardinality, because it decides how rows are built."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "One system supports many processes; one process can run on several systems. W03 therefore lists the supporting SYS-IDs on each process row."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "One process has many risks. Each risk row in W04 carries exactly one BP-ID (or a technical-area BP-ID such as BP-TECH-01)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Risks and controls are many-to-many. One control (for example, the quarterly user access review) can mitigate several risks, and one risk can need more than one control. W05 handles this with a separate Risk-to-Control mapping tab."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Every control is tested twice in principle: once for design (TOD, also called walkthrough) and once for operating effectiveness (TOE). W10 creates both test rows for each in-scope key control."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "One control can draw on several PBC requests, and one PBC request (for example, the full user master extract) can serve several controls."}]}, {"title": "1.5 Control categories and how SOX scope applies", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.5 Control categories and how SOX scope applies"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A common misunderstanding is that each control is tested \"for ITGC, for ITAC and for SOX\". That is not how scoping works. Each control is classified into one category. SOX relevance is a separate flag on the control that decides whether formal design and operating-effectiveness testing is required for the financial statement audit."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Category", "What it covers", "SAP examples"], ["ITGC — Access to Programs & Data (APD)", "Who can get into the system and what they can do", "User provisioning, termination, periodic user access review, privileged and generic IDs, Firefighter/emergency access, SoD, password and logon parameters"], ["ITGC — Program Change (PC)", "How changes reach production", "Transport approval and migration (STMS), segregation of developer and migrator, client and system change settings (SCC4, SE06), direct changes in production, debug-and-replace"], ["ITGC — Computer Operations (CO)", "Whether the system runs reliably", "Batch job scheduling and monitoring (SM37), interface monitoring, backup and restore, incident management"], ["ITGC — Program Development (PD)", "New implementations and major upgrades", "Data migration reconciliation, go-live approval, testing sign-off (applies only when a project is in the audit period)"], ["ITAC — IT Application Control", "Automated controls inside SAP configuration", "Three-way match and tolerance limits, duplicate invoice check, posting period control (OB52), credit limit check, field status groups, automatic account determination"], ["IPE — Information Produced by the Entity", "System reports relied on by a control or the auditor", "Vendor master change report, aged debt report, user listings used in access reviews"], ["ITDM / Manual BPC", "Business-process controls performed by people, sometimes using system output", "Review of GR/IR clearing, bank reconciliation review, journal entry approval"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "The reliance link"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "ITGCs are not tested for their own sake. They are tested because automated controls and system reports can only be trusted if the environment that runs them is controlled. If a developer can change the three-way match configuration in production without approval, a clean test of the three-way match proves nothing about the rest of the year. This is why every ITAC, IPE and ITDM row in the RCM records its dependent ITGCs, and why ITGC results are evaluated before reliance is placed on automated controls."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — ITGCs PROTECT ITACs An ITAC that passes testing is reliable only for the period during which the related ITGCs (especially Program Change and APD over configuration) were also effective. A failed ITGC does not automatically fail the ITAC, but it removes the basis for a single \"test of one\" and forces additional work. Session 6 shows how this is recorded."]]}]}, {"title": "1.6 Rules that apply to every workbook", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.6 Rules that apply to every workbook"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Never invent. If the source does not specify a value, enter TBC and record the open question. A guessed value contaminates every downstream link."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Separate known from unconfirmed. Every workbook has a status or validation column for this purpose."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "No effectiveness conclusion during scoping. A missing owner, a missing report or an unvalidated system is a dependency, not a control failure."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Keys are never reused or renumbered. If a row is removed from scope, set In Scope = N and keep the key."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Preserve raw evidence. Indexed copies are worked on; originals are never overwritten."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "One fact, one home. A value such as a system owner lives in one workbook; others refer to it by key rather than retyping it."}]}, {"title": "Session 2 — W01 Landscape & System Inventory", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 2 — W01 Landscape & System Inventory"}]}, {"title": "2.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W01 records every SAP system, client and supporting component that holds financial data or processes business transactions, and states for each one whether it is in scope and whether its existence and configuration have actually been validated. It is the root of the golden thread: every other workbook ultimately points back to a SYS-ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The workbook exists to stop scoping from resting on a landscape diagram alone. Diagrams are often out of date, omit sandbox or legacy clients, and do not show where production data has been copied. W01 reconciles what the client says exists with what the consultant can confirm."}]}, {"title": "2.2 Workbook structure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.2 Workbook structure"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Content"], ["Instructions", "Purpose, column definitions, dropdown lists, version history"], ["System_Inventory", "One row per system/client combination (main register)"], ["Server_Detail", "One row per application server, database host or web dispatcher, linked by SYS-ID"], ["Transport_Routes", "The STMS route DEV → QAS → PRD for each landscape, used later for Program Change testing"], ["Open_Items", "Unresolved landscape questions, owner and target date"], ["Lists", "Hidden dropdown values (environment, component type, status)"]]}]}, {"title": "2.3 Data fields — System_Inventory tab", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.3 Data fields — System_Inventory tab"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["SYS-ID", "Unique key for the system/client", "SYS-nn", "Unique; never reused"], ["SID", "SAP system identifier", "Three characters, e.g. ECP", "Must match System > Status"], ["Client", "Logical client number", "Three digits, e.g. 300", "Must exist in SCC4"], ["Environment", "Landscape tier", "DEV / QAS / PRD / SBX / TRN", "Dropdown"], ["Product & release", "Installed SAP product", "e.g. S/4HANA 2022 FPS02; GRC AC 12.0 SP22", "From System > Status or SAP license data"], ["Component type", "Technical role", "ABAP back end / Fiori front-end server / GRC / BW / PI-PO or CPI / HANA DB / SolMan", "Dropdown"], ["Hosting model", "Where it runs", "On-premise / RISE with SAP / Hyperscaler (IaaS) / Other", "Drives which ITGCs the client vs provider performs"], ["Database & OS", "Underlying platform", "e.g. HANA 2.0 SPS07 / SLES 15", "From DB02 / Basis"], ["Primary purpose", "What business use it supports", "Free text, one line", "—"], ["Financially relevant?", "Holds or processes data that reaches the financial statements", "Y / N", "If N, Scope rationale must explain"], ["In scope?", "Included in this engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "PRD financially relevant = Y unless justified"], ["Scope rationale", "Reason for inclusion or exclusion", "Free text", "Mandatory if In scope = N"], ["System owner", "Accountable owner (usually Basis/IT lead)", "Name / role", "TBC allowed"], ["Validation method", "How the row was confirmed", "System > Status / SCC4 / SM51 / client inventory / interview", "At least one method"], ["Validation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Validated / Documented only / To confirm", "Validated requires an Evidence Ref"], ["Evidence ref", "Screenshot or document supporting the row", "EV-ID or filename", "Mandatory if Validated"], ["Open question", "Anything still to confirm", "Free text", "Mirror into Open_Items tab"]]}]}, {"title": "2.4 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.4 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SYS-ID", "SID / Client", "Env", "Component", "In scope?", "Validation", "Note"], ["SYS-01", "ECD / 100", "DEV", "S/4HANA ABAP", "Y", "System > Status — Validated", "In scope for Program Change only (source of transports)"], ["SYS-02", "ECQ / 200", "QAS", "S/4HANA ABAP", "Y", "Client inventory — Documented only", "Test client for transport approval evidence"], ["SYS-03", "ECP / 300", "PRD", "S/4HANA ABAP", "Y", "System > Status, SCC4 — Validated", "Primary financial system: FI/CO, MM, SD"], ["SYS-04", "GRP / 001", "PRD", "GRC Access Control 12.0", "Y", "Client inventory — To confirm", "Hosts ARA, ARM, EAM (Firefighter); confirm SP level"], ["SYS-05", "FEP / 100", "PRD", "Fiori front-end server", "TBC", "Documented only", "Confirm whether Fiori apps post financial transactions"], ["SYS-06", "HDP", "PRD", "HANA DB (for ECP)", "Y", "To confirm", "Database-level privileged users in scope for APD"]]}]}, {"title": "2.5 How W01 is populated", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.5 How W01 is populated"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Collect the client view. Request the landscape diagram, system inventory, hosting contract summary and the list of SAP products licensed. Enter every listed component with Validation status = Documented only."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Validate directly where access allows. For each accessible system run System > Status (SID, client, release), SCC4 (clients and their change settings), SM51 (application servers) and, through Basis, DB02 (database). Capture screenshots with the date and system visible. Update the row to Validated and enter the evidence reference."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Capture transport routes. Ask Basis for the STMS transport route display. Record the route in the Transport_Routes tab. This is the starting point for every Program Change test later."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Decide scope. Mark every financially relevant production system In scope = Y. Mark DEV and QAS in scope where they are needed as evidence sources (transport origin, approvals). Exclude sandboxes and training clients with a written rationale."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Record what is still open. Any row that is Documented only or To confirm generates an Open_Items entry with an owner and a target date."}]}, {"title": "2.6 Common errors", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.6 Common errors"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Listing systems from the diagram only and marking them validated without evidence."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Recording the SID but not the client; ECP/300 and ECP/400 can hold entirely different data and access populations."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Forgetting the database layer. HANA and OS-level privileged users bypass SAP application security and must be scoped for APD."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Omitting GRC and Fiori because they do not post transactions. GRC is where Firefighter and access request evidence lives."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Ignoring the hosting model. Under RISE or a managed service, some ITGCs are performed by the provider and are covered by a SOC 1 report rather than direct testing."}]}, {"title": "2.7 Completion gate", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.7 Completion gate"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W01 IS COMPLETE WHEN Every known component is listed with SID, client and environment. Every row has In scope = Y/N with a rationale for N. Every Validated row has an evidence reference; every other row has an open question. The PRD transport route is recorded. Downstream workbooks can now reference SYS-IDs."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 3 — W02 Interface & Data Flow Inventory", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 3 — W02 Interface & Data Flow Inventory"}]}, {"title": "3.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Financial data does not only enter SAP through people typing transactions. Bank statements, payroll postings, customer orders and master data often arrive through interfaces. If an interface drops, duplicates or alters records, the financial statements are wrong even though every SAP user control worked perfectly. W02 identifies each financially relevant interface, how it works, how errors are caught and who owns it."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W02 also captures the technical plumbing that matters for access: RFC destinations with stored credentials and the system or service users behind them are themselves privileged access paths."}]}, {"title": "3.2 Workbook structure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.2 Workbook structure"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Content"], ["Interface_Register", "One row per interface (main register)"], ["Data_Flow_Map", "Simple source → target view per business process, used to check nothing is missed"], ["RFC_Service_Users", "RFC destinations and the technical users they log on with"], ["Open_Items", "Unresolved interface questions"]]}]}, {"title": "3.3 Data fields — Interface_Register tab", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.3 Data fields — Interface_Register tab"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["INT-ID", "Unique key", "INT-nn", "Unique"], ["Interface name", "Business name of the interface", "e.g. Bank statement inbound", "—"], ["Source system", "Where data originates", "SYS-ID or external system name", "External systems named, not invented"], ["Target system", "Where data lands", "SYS-ID or external name", "At least one side must be an in-scope SYS-ID"], ["Direction", "Flow direction", "Inbound / Outbound / Bidirectional", "Dropdown"], ["Technology", "How data moves", "IDoc / RFC / BAPI / OData / SFTP file / PI-PO / CPI / Manual upload (LSMW, LTMC)", "Dropdown"], ["Data object", "What is transferred", "e.g. MT940 statements, payroll journal, ORDERS05", "—"], ["Related BP-ID", "Process the data feeds", "BP-ID", "Back-filled after W03"], ["Frequency & volume", "How often and how much", "e.g. Daily, ~400 lines", "—"], ["Financially relevant?", "Data reaches the financial statements", "Y / N", "If Y, In scope normally Y"], ["Error monitoring", "How failures are detected", "WE02/WE05, BD87, SM58, SXMB_MONI, CPI monitor, email alert", "\"None\" is a valid, important answer"], ["Reconciliation control?", "Is there a completeness/accuracy check between source and target", "Y / N / TBC; describe", "Feeds risk in W04"], ["Interface owner", "Who is accountable", "Name / role", "TBC allowed"], ["RFC / service user", "Technical user used", "User ID", "Cross-ref RFC_Service_Users tab"], ["In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Rationale if N"], ["Evidence ref", "Supporting document", "EV-ID / filename", "—"], ["Open question", "Still to confirm", "Free text", "—"]]}]}, {"title": "3.4 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.4 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["INT-ID", "Interface", "Source → Target", "Technology", "Fin. rel.", "Monitoring", "Note"], ["INT-01", "Bank statement inbound", "Bank (SWIFT) → SYS-03", "SFTP file (MT940)", "Y", "FF_5 log reviewed daily — TBC", "Feeds cash and bank reconciliation"], ["INT-02", "Payroll journal", "External payroll provider → SYS-03", "SFTP file / BAPI", "Y", "TBC", "HR process not confirmed in source; mark for confirmation"], ["INT-03", "EDI customer orders", "Customer EDI → SYS-03", "IDoc ORDERS05", "Y", "WE02 / BD87 by SD support", "Drives revenue completeness"], ["INT-04", "GRC to ECP connector", "SYS-04 → SYS-03", "RFC", "N (but access-relevant)", "SM59 / GRC connector test", "RFC user must not be a dialog user with SAP_ALL"], ["INT-05", "Vendor payment file", "SYS-03 → Bank", "SFTP file (DMEE / pain.001)", "Y", "TBC", "File could be altered after creation; high-risk"]]}]}, {"title": "3.5 How W02 is populated", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.5 How W02 is populated"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Ask by process, not by technology. For each in-scope process ask: what data comes in from outside SAP, and what leaves? Business owners know the flows; Basis knows the mechanics."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Confirm the mechanism. With Basis or integration support, review partner profiles (WE20), RFC destinations (SM59), PI/PO or CPI integration lists and scheduled file jobs (SM37). Record the technology and the technical user."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Ask how failures are detected. For each interface, ask who looks at errors, how often and what evidence exists. Record the honest answer, including \"nobody\"."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Flag financially relevant interfaces. Anything posting to the GL, creating payables or receivables, or transmitting payments is financially relevant."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Back-fill BP-IDs once W03 is built, so each interface points to the process it supports."}]}, {"title": "3.6 Common errors", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.6 Common errors"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Skipping outbound files. The payment file leaving SAP is one of the highest fraud risks in the landscape."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Treating RFC connections as \"infrastructure only\". Stored credentials in SM59 and over-privileged RFC users are access risks."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Recording monitoring as \"yes\" without identifying who, how often and what evidence."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Ignoring manual mass uploads (LSMW, LTMC, custom upload programs), which behave like interfaces without the controls."}]}, {"title": "3.7 Completion gate", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.7 Completion gate"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W02 IS COMPLETE WHEN Every financially relevant inbound and outbound flow is listed. Every interface has a technology, a monitoring answer and an owner (or TBC). RFC/service users are listed and cross-referenced. Interfaces with no monitoring or reconciliation are clearly visible for risk assessment in W04."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 4 — W03 Module & Business Process Scope", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 4 — W03 Module & Business Process Scope"}]}, {"title": "4.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W03 separates the SAP module from the business process, and ties each process to the system it runs on and the financial statement areas it affects. FI/CO, MM, SD, HCM and Basis/Security are modules or functional areas. Procure-to-Pay, Order-to-Cash, Record-to-Report and Inventory are business processes. A process often crosses modules: Procure-to-Pay uses MM for purchasing and goods receipt and FI for invoice posting and payment."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The BP-ID created here is the anchor for every risk in W04. A risk without a BP-ID has no home."}]}, {"title": "4.2 Workbook structure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.2 Workbook structure"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Content"], ["Process_Scope", "One row per sub-process (main register)"], ["FSLI_Map", "Significant financial statement line items and accounts mapped to processes"], ["Technical_Areas", "Basis/Security technical areas treated as processes for ITGC purposes"], ["Open_Items", "Processes or owners still to confirm"]]}]}, {"title": "4.3 Data fields — Process_Scope tab", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.3 Data fields — Process_Scope tab"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["BP-ID", "Unique key for the sub-process", "BP-<process>-nn, e.g. BP-P2P-02", "Unique"], ["Business process", "End-to-end process", "R2R / P2P / O2C / INV / H2R / TECH", "Dropdown"], ["Sub-process", "Specific activity within the process", "e.g. Vendor master maintenance", "—"], ["Module(s)", "SAP modules or functional areas used", "FI, CO, MM, SD, HCM, Basis/Security (multi-select)", "At least one"], ["Supporting SYS-ID(s)", "Systems the sub-process runs on", "SYS-ID list", "Must exist in W01"], ["Related INT-ID(s)", "Interfaces feeding the sub-process", "INT-ID list", "Must exist in W02"], ["Significant FSLI / accounts", "Financial statement areas affected", "e.g. Trade payables; Cash", "From FSLI_Map"], ["Significance", "Why it matters to the audit", "High / Medium / Low with reason", "Based on materiality and risk"], ["In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Rationale if N"], ["Process owner", "Accountable business owner", "Name / role", "TBC allowed"], ["Indicative transactions", "Key t-codes / Fiori apps used", "e.g. XK01/BP, ME21N, MIGO, MIRO, F110", "Used later for SoD and access testing"], ["Confirmation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Source-identified / Confirmed / Partially confirmed / Outstanding", "Dropdown"], ["Scoping note", "Reason for inclusion or open question", "Free text", "—"]]}]}, {"title": "4.4 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.4 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The Week 1 source identifies Finance, Procure-to-Pay, Inventory and Order-to-Cash as in-scope processes. HR is listed as a module but no HR business process is specified, so it is marked Outstanding rather than guessed."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["BP-ID", "Process / sub-process", "Modules", "SYS-IDs", "Key t-codes", "Status"], ["BP-R2R-01", "R2R — Manual journal entries", "FI", "SYS-03", "FB50, FB01, F-02", "Source-identified"], ["BP-R2R-02", "R2R — Period-end close", "FI, CO", "SYS-03", "OB52, FAGLGVTR", "Source-identified"], ["BP-P2P-01", "P2P — Vendor master maintenance", "MM, FI", "SYS-03", "BP / XK01, XK02", "Source-identified"], ["BP-P2P-02", "P2P — Purchasing, receipt and invoice", "MM, FI", "SYS-03", "ME21N, MIGO, MIRO", "Source-identified"], ["BP-P2P-03", "P2P — Vendor payment", "FI", "SYS-03, INT-05", "F110, FBZP", "Source-identified"], ["BP-INV-01", "Inventory — Movements and valuation", "MM", "SYS-03", "MIGO, MI07, MR21", "Source-identified"], ["BP-O2C-01", "O2C — Customer master and credit", "SD, FI", "SYS-03", "BP / XD02, FD32", "Source-identified"], ["BP-O2C-02", "O2C — Order, delivery and billing", "SD", "SYS-03, INT-03", "VA01, VL02N, VF01", "Source-identified"], ["BP-H2R-01", "HR — Payroll posting to FI", "HCM, FI", "INT-02", "TBC", "Outstanding"], ["BP-TECH-01", "Technical — User administration and access", "Basis/Security", "SYS-03, 04, 06", "SU01, PFCG, GRC ARM", "Confirmed"], ["BP-TECH-02", "Technical — Change management", "Basis", "SYS-01, 02, 03", "STMS, SE06, SCC4", "Confirmed"], ["BP-TECH-03", "Technical — Operations (jobs, interfaces)", "Basis", "SYS-03", "SM37, SM58, WE02", "Confirmed"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — WHY TECHNICAL AREAS GET A BP-ID ITGC risks (access, change, operations) are not owned by a business process; they cut across all of them. Giving them technical-area BP-IDs (BP-TECH-nn) lets ITGC risks follow exactly the same thread as business risks, so the RCM, test plan and coverage dashboard treat them consistently."]]}]}, {"title": "4.5 How W03 is populated", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.5 How W03 is populated"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Start from the financial statements. Obtain the significant accounts and FSLIs from the audit plan or management's SOX scoping. Enter them in FSLI_Map."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Map each significant account to the process that creates its balance. Trade payables comes from P2P; revenue and receivables from O2C; inventory from INV; every account is affected by R2R journals and close."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Break each process into sub-processes and assign a BP-ID to each. Use the client's own process narratives where they exist."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Record modules, systems, interfaces and indicative transactions. Confirm transactions with process owners or with a transaction usage report (ST03N / STAD) where available."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Add the technical areas BP-TECH-01 to BP-TECH-03 for every in-scope landscape."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 6 — Mark confirmation status honestly. Source-identified means a document says so; Confirmed means an owner has agreed it."}]}, {"title": "4.6 Common errors", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.6 Common errors"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Listing modules as if they were processes (\"FI is in scope\")."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Inventing a process the case does not specify, instead of marking it Outstanding."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Not linking interfaces, so the payment file or payroll journal falls outside every process."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Omitting the technical areas, which leaves ITGC risks with no anchor."}]}, {"title": "4.7 Completion gate", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.7 Completion gate"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W03 IS COMPLETE WHEN Every significant FSLI maps to at least one in-scope process. Every sub-process has a BP-ID, modules, SYS-IDs and a confirmation status. Technical areas BP-TECH-01 to 03 exist. Unspecified processes are marked Outstanding, not guessed."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 5 — W04 Risk Register", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 5 — W04 Risk Register"}]}, {"title": "5.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W04 states, for each in-scope process and technical area, what could go wrong that would cause a misstatement in the financial statements or a breach of the integrity of financial data. Each risk has a named risk owner: the person accountable for the outcome if the risk materialises. The risk register is a statement of exposure, not a finding. Nothing in W04 says a control has failed."}]}, {"title": "5.2 Risk owner versus control owner", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.2 Risk owner versus control owner"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "These are often confused. The risk owner is accountable for the business outcome, for example the Head of Accounts Payable owns the risk of paying a fictitious vendor. The control owner (recorded in W05 and W07) is accountable for performing a specific control, for example the AP supervisor who reviews vendor master changes. One risk owner may rely on several control owners. A risk owner without any control owner beneath them is an uncovered risk."}]}, {"title": "5.3 Writing a risk statement", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.3 Writing a risk statement"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Use a consistent structure so risks are comparable and testable:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RISK STATEMENT FORMULA There is a risk that  [event / condition]  because  [cause or access path]  resulting in  [financial impact or assertion affected]. Example: There is a risk that a fictitious vendor is created and paid  because  one user can both maintain vendor master data and execute the payment run,  resulting in  fraudulent payments and overstated expenses (Occurrence)."]]}]}, {"title": "5.4 Data fields", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.4 Data fields"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["RISK-ID", "Unique key", "R-<process>-nnn", "Unique"], ["BP-ID", "Process or technical area the risk belongs to", "BP-ID", "Must exist in W03"], ["SYS-ID / INT-ID", "System or interface where the risk arises", "SYS-ID / INT-ID", "Must exist in W01 / W02"], ["Risk category", "Type of risk", "ITGC-APD / ITGC-PC / ITGC-CO / ITGC-PD / ITAC / SoD / Business process / IPE / Interface", "Dropdown; guides control category in W05"], ["Risk statement", "What could go wrong", "Formula in 5.3", "Must not describe a finding"], ["Assertion(s)", "Financial statement assertions affected", "Occurrence, Completeness, Accuracy, Cut-off, Valuation, Existence, Rights & obligations, Presentation", "At least one for SOX-relevant risks"], ["Risk owner", "Accountable for the outcome", "Name / role", "TBC allowed"], ["Inherent likelihood", "Before controls", "1 Low / 2 Medium / 3 High", "—"], ["Inherent impact", "Before controls", "1 Low / 2 Medium / 3 High", "—"], ["Inherent rating", "Likelihood × impact", "1–9; High ≥ 6", "Formula"], ["SOX relevant?", "Could lead to a material misstatement", "Y / N", "Drives key-control requirement in W05"], ["Risk source", "Where the risk came from", "Client RCM / framework library / walkthrough / prior-year audit issue", "—"], ["Status", "Agreement status", "Draft / Agreed with owner / Dependency open", "No \"Failed\" value exists"], ["Notes", "Limitations or follow-ups", "Free text", "—"]]}]}, {"title": "5.5 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.5 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RISK-ID", "BP-ID", "Category", "Risk statement (abridged)", "Assertion", "Risk owner", "Rating"], ["R-P2P-001", "BP-P2P-01 / 03", "SoD", "A user can create a vendor and pay it, enabling fictitious vendor payments", "Occurrence", "Head of AP", "9 High"], ["R-P2P-002", "BP-P2P-02", "ITAC", "An invoice is posted and paid without a matching PO and goods receipt within tolerance", "Occurrence, Accuracy", "Head of AP", "6 High"], ["R-P2P-003", "BP-P2P-03", "Interface", "The payment file is altered between creation and transmission to the bank", "Occurrence", "Treasury Manager", "6 High"], ["R-R2R-001", "BP-R2R-02", "ITAC", "Postings are made to a closed accounting period", "Cut-off", "Financial Controller", "4 Medium"], ["R-TECH-001", "BP-TECH-01", "ITGC-APD", "Leavers retain active SAP access after termination", "Occurrence", "IT Security Manager", "6 High"], ["R-TECH-002", "BP-TECH-01", "ITGC-APD", "Emergency (Firefighter) access is used without approval or review", "Occurrence, Accuracy", "IT Security Manager", "6 High"], ["R-TECH-003", "BP-TECH-02", "ITGC-PC", "Unauthorised or untested changes reach production, including configuration changes to ITACs", "Accuracy", "Basis Lead", "9 High"], ["R-TECH-004", "BP-TECH-03", "ITGC-CO", "Critical batch jobs or interfaces fail without detection, so transactions are incomplete", "Completeness", "Basis Lead", "4 Medium"], ["R-O2C-001", "BP-O2C-02", "Interface", "EDI orders fail in IDoc processing and are not invoiced", "Completeness", "SD Process Owner", "4 Medium"]]}]}], "word_count": 2142, "id": "tib-sap-controls-foundation-manual-part1", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Manual_Part1.docx", "filename": "TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Manual_Part1.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Building the SAP Controls Project Foundation"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From Landscape to Every SOX, ITGC and ITAC Test: The Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Training Manual  |  Part 2 of 4: Sessions 7–16 (W06–W14 and the Population Sequence)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Running case: ABC Manufacturing  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Where Part 2 sits in the golden thread"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1 built SYS → INT → BP → RISK → CTRL (W01–W05). Part 2 completes the thread: the access rules that make SoD and sensitive-access risks testable (W06), the people behind each control (W07), the evidence layer (W08–W09), the test layer (W10–W12) and the conclusion layer (W13–W14). Session 16 then sets out the order in which all fourteen workbooks are populated and the checkpoints between them."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "SYS → INT → BP → RISK → CTRL → OWNER → PBC → EV → TEST → SMP → DEF → CONCLUSION"}]}, {"title": "Session 7 — W06 SoD & Sensitive Access Rule Matrix", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 7 — W06 SoD & Sensitive Access Rule Matrix"}]}, {"title": "7.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "7.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "An SoD risk in W04, such as \"a user can create a vendor and pay it\", cannot be tested until it is translated into SAP terms: which transactions and authorisation objects make up each side of the conflict. W06 does that translation. It defines every conflicting function pair and every sensitive (critical) access item in technical terms, so the access analysis in Part 3 can run them against the user population and return exact results."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Where the client uses SAP GRC Access Control, W06 mirrors the active ruleset (ARA). Where it does not, W06 is the manual ruleset the consultant will run against SUIM or table extracts (AGR_USERS, AGR_1251, USR02)."}]}, {"title": "7.2 Workbook structure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "7.2 Workbook structure"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Content"], ["Functions", "One row per business function (e.g. Vendor Master Maintenance) with its transactions"], ["Function_Auth", "Authorisation objects, fields and values that make each transaction executable (e.g. F_LFA1_BUK ACTVT 01/02)"], ["SoD_Rules", "One row per conflicting function pair, linked to RISK-ID"], ["Sensitive_Access", "Critical access that is risky on its own (SAP_ALL, SE16N edit, SCC4, SE06, PFCG in PRD, debug-change)"], ["Mitigations", "Approved mitigating controls for accepted conflicts, linked to CTRL-ID"], ["Ruleset_Version", "Version, source (GRC ARA export or manual), approval date"]]}]}, {"title": "7.3 Data fields — SoD_Rules tab", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "7.3 Data fields — SoD_Rules tab"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["SOD-ID", "Unique key for the conflict", "SOD-nnn", "Unique"], ["Function A / Function B", "The two conflicting functions", "From Functions tab", "Both must exist"], ["Conflict description", "Why the combination is dangerous", "Plain English", "Must match the W04 risk"], ["RISK-ID", "Risk the rule tests", "R-xxx-nnn", "Must exist in W04, category SoD"], ["BP-ID", "Process affected", "BP-ID", "From W03"], ["Risk level", "Severity of the conflict", "Critical / High / Medium", "Critical always reported"], ["Rule level", "How it is evaluated", "Transaction level / Permission level (auth objects)", "Permission level preferred"], ["Org-level scope", "Whether the conflict applies within the same company code / plant", "Cross-org / Same org", "Defines false-positive logic"], ["Mitigating CTRL-ID", "Control accepted for justified conflicts", "CTRL-ID or None", "Must exist in W05"], ["Rule owner", "Business owner who approved the rule", "Name / role", "—"], ["In scope?", "Run in this engagement", "Y / N", "Rationale if N"]]}]}, {"title": "7.4 Data fields — Sensitive_Access tab", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "7.4 Data fields — Sensitive_Access tab"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Example"], ["SENS-ID", "Unique key", "SENS-004"], ["Access item", "Transaction, profile or auth object value", "S_TABU_DIS ACTVT 02 with SE16N; SAP_ALL; S_DEVELOP ACTVT 02 (debug-change)"], ["Why sensitive", "Impact if held inappropriately", "Direct table changes bypass application controls"], ["RISK-ID", "Risk tested", "R-TECH-003"], ["Allowed holders", "Who may legitimately hold it", "Firefighter IDs only; none in PRD dialog users"], ["Related CTRL-ID", "Control that governs it", "C-ITGC-AC-004 (Firefighter)"]]}]}, {"title": "7.5 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "7.5 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SOD-ID", "Function A", "Function B", "RISK-ID", "Level", "Mitigation"], ["SOD-001", "Vendor master maintenance (BP, XK01, XK02)", "Payment run (F110)", "R-P2P-001", "Critical", "None — must be removed"], ["SOD-002", "Create purchase order (ME21N)", "Post goods receipt (MIGO)", "R-P2P-002", "High", "C-BPC-P2P-004 monthly PO/GR exception review"], ["SOD-003", "Post vendor invoice (MIRO, FB60)", "Vendor master maintenance", "R-P2P-001", "Critical", "None"], ["SOD-004", "Maintain customer credit limit (FD32/UKM)", "Create sales order (VA01)", "R-O2C-002", "High", "TBC with SD owner"], ["SOD-005", "Open/close posting periods (OB52)", "Post journal entries (FB50)", "R-R2R-001", "High", "C-BPC-R2R-002 period-close review"]]}]}, {"title": "7.6 How W06 is populated", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "7.6 How W06 is populated"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Obtain the client ruleset. Export the GRC ARA ruleset (functions, actions, permissions, risks) or the client's documented SoD matrix. If neither exists, start from the TIB baseline ruleset."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Map every W04 SoD risk to at least one rule, and every rule back to a W04 risk. Rules with no risk are noise; risks with no rule are untestable."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Add permission-level detail (authorisation object, field, value) so that users with a transaction but no real ability to act are not reported as conflicts."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Include custom transactions (Z-codes) that replicate standard functions. These are the most common gap in client rulesets."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Record approved mitigations and the sensitive-access list, then have the rule owner approve the version."}]}, {"title": "7.7 Common errors", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "7.7 Common errors"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Transaction-only rules that report thousands of false positives."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Missing custom Z-transactions and Fiori app equivalents."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Accepting a \"mitigation\" that is not a real, tested control in W05."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Treating sensitive access as SoD; SAP_ALL is a risk on its own, without a second function."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W06 IS COMPLETE WHEN Every SoD risk in W04 maps to at least one SOD-ID, and every rule maps to a risk. Rules carry permission-level detail and include custom transactions. Sensitive access items are listed with allowed holders. Mitigations point to real CTRL-IDs; the ruleset version is approved."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 8 — W07 Control & Evidence Owner Matrix", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 8 — W07 Control & Evidence Owner Matrix"}]}, {"title": "8.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "8.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W07 records, for every control in the RCM, who is accountable for it, who performs it day to day, who can provide the evidence and who the backup is. These are often different people. The Head of IT Security may own the access review control, a GRC administrator may run the review campaign, and a Basis analyst may be the only person who can extract the user listing. If the testing team does not know who provides each piece of evidence, the PBC process stalls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "In the Lab 1 draft this matrix was keyed by control area. In the full foundation it is keyed by CTRL-ID, so it lines up one-to-one with the RCM."}]}, {"title": "8.2 Data fields", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "8.2 Data fields"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["CTRL-ID", "Control from the RCM", "CTRL-ID", "Must exist in W05"], ["Control title", "Short name (lookup from W05)", "Formula", "Do not retype"], ["Risk owner", "Accountable for the risk (lookup from W04)", "Name / role", "—"], ["Control owner", "Accountable for the control operating", "Name / role", "Required or Outstanding"], ["Control performer", "Person who executes it day to day", "Name / role", "May equal owner"], ["Evidence owner", "Person or team who can produce the evidence", "Name / team", "Required or Outstanding"], ["Backup contact", "Alternate for leave or turnover", "Name / role", "Recommended"], ["Primary SYS-ID", "Where evidence resides", "SYS-ID", "From W01"], ["Expected evidence", "Lookup from W05", "Formula", "—"], ["Walkthrough contact", "Person who will demonstrate the control in TOD", "Name / role", "Usually the performer"], ["Owner status", "Confirmation state", "Confirmed / Outstanding", "Outstanding is not a failure"], ["Date confirmed", "When the owner agreed", "Date", "Required if Confirmed"], ["Follow-up", "What still needs resolving", "Free text", "—"]]}]}, {"title": "8.3 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "8.3 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CTRL-ID", "Control owner", "Performer", "Evidence owner", "Status"], ["C-ITGC-AC-002", "IT Security Manager", "Security Administrator", "HR Operations (leavers list); Basis (SU01 change docs)", "Confirmed"], ["C-ITGC-AC-003", "IT Security Manager", "Role owners (business)", "GRC Administrator (UAR campaign export)", "Confirmed"], ["C-ITGC-AC-004", "IT Security Manager", "Firefighter controllers", "GRC Administrator (EAM logs)", "Outstanding — controller list not provided"], ["C-ITGC-PC-001", "Basis Lead", "Change Manager / Basis", "Basis (STMS import history); ITSM admin (change tickets)", "Confirmed"], ["C-ITAC-004", "Head of AP", "System (automated)", "SAP functional support (OMR6 config, change log)", "Confirmed"], ["C-BPC-P2P-003", "Treasury Manager", "Treasury Analyst", "Treasury (bank confirmations, F110 logs)", "Outstanding"]]}]}, {"title": "8.4 How W07 is populated", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "8.4 How W07 is populated"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Start from the RCM, one row per CTRL-ID. Pre-fill the control owner from W05, then confirm each owner in a short meeting. For each control ask two separate questions: who makes sure this happens, and who can give us the evidence that it happened? Record the walkthrough contact for the design test. Mark anything unresolved as Outstanding with a follow-up; never record it as a control failure."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W07 IS COMPLETE WHEN Every in-scope CTRL-ID has a row. Control owner and evidence owner are identified or marked Outstanding. Walkthrough contacts are known for every key control. Outstanding owners are escalated in the W14 dependency list."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 9 — W08 PBC / Evidence Request Register", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 9 — W08 PBC / Evidence Request Register"}]}, {"title": "9.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "\"PBC\" means Provided By Client. W08 lists every piece of evidence requested, from whom, for which period, and for which control and test. Every request must answer a future testing question. If a request cannot be traced to a CTRL-ID and a planned test, it should not be made: unnecessary requests waste the client's time and damage the engagement relationship."}]}, {"title": "9.2 Two kinds of request", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.2 Two kinds of request"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Population requests — complete lists from which samples will be drawn (all users created in the period, all transports imported to PRD, all Firefighter sessions, all leavers from HR). These are also IPE and require completeness and accuracy checks in W09."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Sample evidence requests — the supporting documents for the specific items selected in W12 (the approval ticket for transport ECDK900123, the review sign-off for Firefighter session FF-0412). These are raised after sampling, as a second wave."}]}, {"title": "9.3 Data fields", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.3 Data fields"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["PBC-ID", "Unique key", "PBC-nnn", "Unique"], ["Request wave", "Timing of the request", "1 Scoping / 2 Population / 3 Sample / 4 Follow-up", "Dropdown"], ["CTRL-ID(s)", "Controls the evidence supports", "CTRL-ID list", "Must exist in W05"], ["TEST-ID(s)", "Tests that will use it", "TEST-ID list", "Back-filled when W10 is built"], ["BP-ID / SYS-ID", "Where the evidence applies", "Keys", "From W01/W03"], ["Requested evidence", "Exactly what is needed, including format and fields", "e.g. SM20/STMS import history PRD, all imports 01-Jan to 30-Sep, CSV with request no., date, user", "Specific enough to be unambiguous"], ["Period / as-of date", "Time the evidence must cover", "From–to or as-of date", "Must match test period"], ["Population or sample?", "Request type", "Population / Sample / Configuration / Policy", "Population triggers IPE check"], ["Evidence owner", "Who provides it (from W07)", "Name / team", "—"], ["Date requested / due", "Tracking dates", "Dates", "Due date agreed"], ["Priority", "Urgency", "High / Medium / Low", "High for populations"], ["Status", "Progress", "Requested / Partially received / Received / Rejected – incomplete / Closed", "Dropdown"], ["EV-ID(s)", "Evidence received, once indexed", "EV-ID list", "Required for Received"], ["Notes", "Completeness requirements or follow-up", "Free text", "—"]]}]}, {"title": "9.4 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.4 Worked example — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC-ID", "Wave", "Requested evidence", "CTRL-ID", "Owner", "Status"], ["PBC-010", "2", "All SAP users in ECP/300 (USR02 extract: user, type, valid from/to, lock status, last logon)", "C-ITGC-AC-001/002/003", "Basis", "Received"], ["PBC-011", "2", "HR leavers report for 01-Jan to 30-Sep with termination dates", "C-ITGC-AC-002", "HR Operations", "Requested"], ["PBC-014", "2", "GRC EAM Firefighter log report, all sessions in period", "C-ITGC-AC-004", "GRC Administrator", "Requested"], ["PBC-018", "2", "STMS import history to ECP, all transports in period", "C-ITGC-PC-001", "Basis", "Received"], ["PBC-019", "1", "SCC4 and SE06 settings for ECP with change logs", "C-ITGC-PC-002", "Basis", "Received"], ["PBC-024", "1", "OMR6 tolerance configuration and table change log (CDHDR/CDPOS or DBTABLOG)", "C-ITAC-004", "SAP functional support", "Requested"], ["PBC-040", "3", "Change tickets and test sign-off for 25 sampled transports (list from SMP-003)", "C-ITGC-PC-001", "Change Manager", "Not yet raised"]]}]}, {"title": "9.5 How W08 is populated", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "9.5 How W08 is populated"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Generate wave 1 and 2 requests from the RCM. For each in-scope key control, read the Expected evidence and Test approach columns and write the population and configuration requests needed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Consolidate. One USR02 extract serves several access controls; raise it once and list all CTRL-IDs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Make each request precise: system, client, period, fields, format. Vague requests produce unusable evidence."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Issue, then track daily during fieldwork. Update Status and add EV-IDs as items arrive and are indexed in W09."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Raise wave 3 sample requests only after W12 sampling is complete."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W08 IS COMPLETE WHEN Every request traces to at least one CTRL-ID (and TEST-ID once W10 exists). Every request states system, period and required fields. Population requests are flagged for IPE checks. Outstanding requests are visible with owner and due date."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 10 — W09 Evidence Index & IPE Log", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 10 — W09 Evidence Index & IPE Log"}]}, {"title": "10.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "10.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W09 is the filing system. Every item received is given an EV-ID, its source and extraction details are recorded, the original is preserved untouched, and population-type evidence is checked for completeness and accuracy before any sample is drawn from it. That last step is the IPE (Information Produced by the Entity) check. If a population is incomplete, every sample from it is unreliable, and so is every conclusion."}]}, {"title": "10.2 Data fields — Evidence_Index tab", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "10.2 Data fields — Evidence_Index tab"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["EV-ID", "Unique key", "EV-nnn", "Unique"], ["PBC-ID", "Request it answers", "PBC-ID", "Must exist in W08"], ["Filename", "Stored file name", "<EV-ID>_<SYS>_<description>_<date>.ext", "Naming convention enforced"], ["Description", "What the file contains", "Free text", "—"], ["Source SYS-ID", "System it was extracted from", "SYS-ID", "From W01"], ["Extraction method", "How it was produced", "t-code / report / table / GRC report / ITSM export / manual document", "—"], ["Extracted by / date", "Who ran it and when", "Name, date and time", "Observed or evidenced by screenshot"], ["Period covered", "Date range in the file", "From–to", "Must match PBC period"], ["Record count", "Rows in the population", "Number", "Used for completeness check"], ["Received date", "Date logged", "Date", "—"], ["Original preserved?", "Raw copy stored read-only", "Y / N", "Must be Y"], ["IPE required?", "Is it a population or report relied on", "Y / N", "Y for all populations"], ["IPE result", "Outcome of completeness & accuracy check", "Pass / Fail / Pending", "Pass required before sampling"], ["Used by TEST-ID(s)", "Tests relying on the file", "TEST-ID list", "Back-filled"]]}]}, {"title": "10.3 The IPE check", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "10.3 The IPE check"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "For each population, the IPE_Log tab records how completeness (nothing is missing) and accuracy (the data is correct) were established. Typical procedures are:"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Observe or evidence the extraction. The tester watches the report being run, or receives a screenshot showing the selection parameters (system, client, date range, no restrictive filters)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Reconcile record counts to an independent source, for example the user count in USR02 against SUIM, or the import count in STMS against the transport log."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Test parameters and logic for custom reports: confirm the selection logic does not exclude records (such as locked users or a company code)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Trace a few records both ways: from the system to the file (completeness) and from the file to the system (accuracy)."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE-ID", "EV-ID", "Population", "Completeness procedure", "Accuracy procedure", "Result"], ["IPE-001", "EV-012", "ECP users (USR02)", "Count agreed to SUIM RSUSR002 total; parameters screenshot shows no filters", "5 users traced file ↔ SU01", "Pass"], ["IPE-002", "EV-019", "PRD transport imports", "Count agreed to STMS import queue history; date range verified", "5 requests traced to SE01", "Pass"], ["IPE-003", "EV-021", "HR leavers", "Count reconciled to HR headcount movement report", "5 leavers traced to HR records", "Pending"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W09 IS COMPLETE WHEN Every received item has an EV-ID, standard filename and preserved original. Every population has an IPE check recorded. No sample is drawn from a population with IPE result other than Pass. W08 status is updated with EV-IDs."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 11 — W10 Test Plan / Testing Program", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 11 — W10 Test Plan / Testing Program"}]}, {"title": "11.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W10 converts the RCM into a schedule of tests. For every in-scope key control it creates two rows: a test of design (TOD) and a test of operating effectiveness (TOE). Each row states the test attributes, the procedure, the population, the sample size, the period, the tester, the reviewer and the planned dates. This is where the scope of work becomes a plan the team can execute and a manager can track."}]}, {"title": "11.2 Design versus operating effectiveness", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.2 Design versus operating effectiveness"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["", "Test of Design (TOD)", "Test of Operating Effectiveness (TOE)"], ["Question", "If performed as described, would this control address the risk?", "Did the control actually operate throughout the period?"], ["Method", "Walkthrough of one instance: inquiry plus observation or inspection", "Inspection or reperformance of a sample across the period"], ["Sample", "One transaction (a \"walkthrough\")", "Size driven by frequency and risk (11.4)"], ["Timing", "Before TOE; usually during planning", "Interim and/or roll-forward to period end"], ["If it fails", "Design deficiency; TOE is not performed", "Operating deficiency; evaluated in W13"]]}]}, {"title": "11.3 Data fields", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.3 Data fields"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["TEST-ID", "Unique key", "<CTRL-ID>-TOD or -TOE", "One TOD and one TOE per key control"], ["CTRL-ID", "Control being tested", "CTRL-ID", "From W05"], ["Control category", "Lookup from W05", "ITGC-APD / PC / CO / ITAC / IPE / BPC", "Drives workpaper template"], ["Test type", "TOD / TOE", "Dropdown", "—"], ["Test attributes", "The specific conditions each sample item must meet", "Lettered A, B, C...", "Each attribute traceable to control description"], ["Test procedure", "What the tester does", "Inquiry / Observation / Inspection / Reperformance with steps", "Inquiry alone is never sufficient"], ["Population EV-ID", "Population sampled from", "EV-ID", "IPE result must be Pass"], ["Population size", "Number of items", "Number", "From W09"], ["Sample size", "Items to test", "From sample guide (11.4)", "Justify deviations"], ["Test period", "Period covered", "From–to", "Matches PBC"], ["ITGC reliance needed?", "Whether dependent ITGCs must be effective", "Y / N", "Y for ITAC/IPE/ITDM"], ["Tester / reviewer", "Who performs and who reviews", "Names", "Different people"], ["Planned start / end", "Schedule", "Dates", "—"], ["Workpaper ref", "W11 workpaper", "WP filename", "—"], ["Status", "Progress", "Not started / In progress / Ready for review / Reviewed / Closed", "Dropdown"], ["Result", "Outcome", "Effective / Exception noted / Ineffective", "Only after review"]]}]}, {"title": "11.4 Typical sample size guide", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.4 Typical sample size guide"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The table below reflects common practice for SOX testing. Always apply the methodology of the engagement firm or the external auditor where one is specified."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control frequency", "Typical population", "Typical TOE sample"], ["Annual", "1", "1"], ["Quarterly", "4", "2"], ["Monthly", "12", "2–5"], ["Weekly", "52", "5–15"], ["Daily", "~250", "20–40"], ["Event-driven / multiple times per day", "More than 250", "25–60"], ["Automated (ITAC)", "—", "1 per configuration, provided dependent ITGCs are effective (baseline / benchmarking)"]]}]}, {"title": "11.5 Worked example — test attributes", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.5 Worked example — test attributes"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TEST-ID", "Attributes (each sample item must meet all)"], ["C-ITGC-AC-002-TOE", "A: Leaver appears in the HR leavers report   B: SAP user locked or end-dated (SU01 change history)   C: Lock date within two business days of termination date   D: No logon after termination date (USR02 last logon / SM20)"], ["C-ITGC-PC-001-TOE", "A: Change ticket exists and is approved before import   B: Evidence of testing in QAS with sign-off   C: Import to PRD performed by a user other than the developer   D: Transport contents agree to the approved change"], ["C-ITGC-AC-004-TOE", "A: Firefighter session was requested with a reason code   B: Request approved before use   C: Log (transactions, changes) reviewed by the controller   D: Review completed within five days and anomalies followed up"], ["C-ITAC-004-TOD/TOE", "A: OMR6 tolerances configured as designed for company code 1000   B: Reperform: post a test invoice outside tolerance in QAS and confirm payment block   C: Table change log shows no changes to tolerances in period, or changes followed C-ITGC-PC-001"]]}]}, {"title": "11.6 How W10 is populated", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.6 How W10 is populated"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Filter the RCM for SOX in scope = Y. For each control create the TOD and TOE rows. Derive the attributes directly from the five-part control description; each attribute tests one part of it. Set the sample size from the frequency. Mark ITGC reliance for ITAC, IPE and ITDM controls. Assign testers and reviewers, then schedule ITGC tests first, because their results determine how much reliance can be placed on automated controls."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — SCHEDULE ITGCs FIRST Testing the three-way match before Program Change testing is complete means the ITAC result may have to be reopened. Plan the access and change ITGCs in the first fieldwork window and the ITACs after them."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W10 IS COMPLETE WHEN Every SOX in-scope key control has a TOD and a TOE row. Every row has attributes derived from the control description. Sample sizes follow the guide or a justified deviation. ITGC tests are scheduled before dependent ITAC/IPE tests."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 12 — W11 Test Workpapers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 12 — W11 Test Workpapers"}]}, {"title": "12.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "12.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A workpaper is the record of what the tester actually did. It must allow an experienced reviewer who was not involved to understand the test, reperform it and reach the same conclusion. W11 is a set of templates, one per control area, all sharing the same structure so that every test across the project reads the same way."}]}, {"title": "12.2 Standard workpaper structure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "12.2 Standard workpaper structure"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Section", "Content"], ["Header", "TEST-ID, CTRL-ID, RISK-ID(s), control description, SYS-ID, period, tester, reviewer, dates"], ["Objective", "The question the test answers, in one sentence"], ["Population & IPE", "EV-ID, record count, IPE-ID and result"], ["Sample", "SMP-ID from W12 and selection method"], ["Procedure", "Numbered steps actually performed, including t-codes and tables used"], ["Attribute grid", "One row per sample item, one column per attribute, with tick / exception and EV-ID reference"], ["Exceptions", "Description, root cause from inquiry, and whether it is a deviation or a false positive"], ["Conclusion", "Effective / Exception noted / Ineffective, with rationale"], ["Review notes", "Reviewer questions and responses; sign-off"]]}]}, {"title": "12.3 Templates by control area", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "12.3 Templates by control area"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Parts 3A and 3B of this manual take each template in turn, with SAP navigation and worked tests. The set is:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Template", "Control area", "Typical SAP sources"], ["WP-AC-01", "User provisioning", "GRC ARM requests, SU01 change documents, USR02"], ["WP-AC-02", "Termination (leavers)", "HR leavers report, USR02 lock/validity, last logon"], ["WP-AC-03", "User access review", "GRC UAR campaign, review sign-offs, removal evidence"], ["WP-AC-04", "Privileged and generic IDs", "SUIM by profile (SAP_ALL, SAP_NEW), USR02 user types, DDIC/SAP* status"], ["WP-AC-05", "Emergency access (Firefighter)", "GRC EAM logs, request/approval, controller review"], ["WP-AC-06", "SoD and sensitive access", "GRC ARA user-level results, W06 ruleset, mitigation assignment"], ["WP-AC-07", "Password and logon parameters", "RZ11 / RSPARAM (login/min_password_lng etc.)"], ["WP-PC-01", "Transport management", "STMS import history, E070/E071, ITSM change tickets"], ["WP-PC-02", "System and client change settings", "SCC4, SE06, change logs"], ["WP-PC-03", "Direct changes / debug in production", "SM20 security audit log, S_DEVELOP holders"], ["WP-CO-01", "Batch job monitoring", "SM37, job owner, failure tickets"], ["WP-CO-02", "Interface monitoring", "WE02/WE05, BD87, SM58, SXMB_MONI, reconciliations"], ["WP-CO-03", "Backup and recovery", "Backup logs, restore test evidence (often SOC 1 for hosted)"], ["WP-ITAC-01", "Configuration controls", "SPRO paths (OMR6, OB52, credit control), table change logs"], ["WP-IPE-01", "Key reports", "Report logic, parameters, completeness and accuracy procedures"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W11 IS COMPLETE WHEN Each test has a completed workpaper in the standard structure. Every attribute result references an EV-ID. Exceptions are explained with root cause. The reviewer has signed off before the result is entered in W10."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 13 — W12 Sample Selection Log", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 13 — W12 Sample Selection Log"}]}, {"title": "13.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "13.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W12 records exactly which items were chosen from which population, by what method, and why. It allows a reviewer to confirm the sample was unbiased and covered the whole period, and it drives the wave 3 PBC requests for supporting evidence."}]}, {"title": "13.2 Data fields", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "13.2 Data fields"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["SMP-ID", "Unique key", "SMP-nnn", "Unique"], ["TEST-ID", "Test the sample is for", "TEST-ID", "From W10"], ["Population EV-ID", "Population used", "EV-ID", "IPE must be Pass"], ["Population size", "Total items", "Number", "Agrees to W09"], ["Sample size", "Items selected", "Number", "Agrees to W10"], ["Selection method", "How chosen", "Random (seeded) / Systematic / Haphazard / Targeted (risk-based) / All items", "Random preferred"], ["Seed / interval", "Parameters for reperformance", "Seed number or interval", "Required for random/systematic"], ["Period coverage", "Spread across the period", "Confirm items from each quarter/month", "Required"], ["Selected item IDs", "The items chosen", "User IDs, transport numbers, FF session IDs", "Listed on Items tab"], ["Wave 3 PBC-ID", "Request for supporting evidence", "PBC-ID", "Raised in W08"], ["Selected by / date", "Who and when", "Name, date", "—"]]}]}, {"title": "13.3 Worked example", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "13.3 Worked example"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "For C-ITGC-PC-001-TOE, the population EV-019 contains 312 transports imported to ECP between 01-Jan and 30-Sep (IPE-002 Pass). The event-driven guide gives a sample of 25. The tester uses Excel RAND with seed 4471, checks that every month is represented, adds two targeted items (emergency transports imported outside the change window), lists the 27 transport numbers on the Items tab and raises PBC-040 for the change tickets and test sign-offs."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W12 IS COMPLETE WHEN Every TOE has a sample logged with method and parameters. The sample covers the whole period. Targeted additions are explained. Wave 3 PBC requests are raised and cross-referenced."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 14 — W13 Deficiency & Remediation Log", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 14 — W13 Deficiency & Remediation Log"}]}, {"title": "14.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "14.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W13 records every test exception that is confirmed as a deficiency, evaluates its severity, links it back through the thread to the risk and process affected, and tracks remediation and retesting. An exception is not automatically a deficiency: it may be a false positive (for example, a \"conflict\" that the permission-level analysis shows cannot actually be executed) or it may be within the tolerated deviation rate for the sample."}]}, {"title": "14.2 Severity levels", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "14.2 Severity levels"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Level", "Meaning"], ["Control deficiency", "The control does not allow management or employees to prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis."], ["Significant deficiency", "Less severe than a material weakness, yet important enough to merit the attention of those responsible for oversight (audit committee)."], ["Material weakness", "A reasonable possibility that a material misstatement of the financial statements will not be prevented or detected on a timely basis."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Severity is judged on the magnitude of the potential misstatement and the likelihood it occurs, taking into account compensating controls. For ITGC deficiencies, the evaluation also considers which dependent ITAC, IPE and ITDM controls are affected (read from the W05 ITGC_Dependency tab)."}]}, {"title": "14.3 Data fields", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "14.3 Data fields"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Definition", "Format / allowed values", "Validation rule"], ["DEF-ID", "Unique key", "DEF-nnn", "Unique"], ["TEST-ID / CTRL-ID", "Where it was found", "Keys", "From W10/W05"], ["RISK-ID / BP-ID", "Risk and process exposed", "Keys", "Traced automatically"], ["Deficiency type", "Design or operating", "Design / Operating", "—"], ["Description", "Condition, criteria, cause, effect", "Four-part statement", "All parts present"], ["Exceptions", "Number of failed items out of sample", "e.g. 2 of 25", "From W11"], ["Dependent controls affected", "ITAC/IPE/ITDM relying on this ITGC", "CTRL-ID list", "From ITGC_Dependency"], ["Compensating controls", "Other controls reducing the exposure", "CTRL-ID list with test result", "Must be tested"], ["Severity", "Evaluated level", "Deficiency / Significant deficiency / Material weakness", "Reviewer approved"], ["Management response", "Owner's agreed action", "Free text", "—"], ["Remediation owner / due date", "Who fixes it, by when", "Name, date", "—"], ["Retest TEST-ID", "Test confirming the fix", "TEST-ID", "Remediated control must operate long enough to be tested"], ["Status", "Progress", "Open / Remediation in progress / Retest / Closed", "Dropdown"]]}]}, {"title": "14.4 Worked example", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "14.4 Worked example"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "DEF-004 — C-ITGC-AC-002-TOE found that 3 of 25 sampled leavers were locked between 6 and 14 business days after termination, and one (user JSMITH) logged on after the termination date. Condition: late removal. Criteria: removal within two business days. Cause: the HR leavers report is sent weekly, not daily. Effect: leavers retain the ability to post transactions. Dependent controls affected: C-ITGC-AC-003 is the compensating control (quarterly review) and was effective; the post-termination logon was investigated through SM20 and showed display-only activity. Severity: control deficiency. Remediation: daily HR feed into GRC with automatic lock; retest after 60 days of operation."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W13 IS COMPLETE WHEN Every confirmed exception has a DEF-ID traced to test, control, risk and process. Severity is evaluated, including impact on dependent controls and compensating controls. Management response, owner and due date are recorded. Retest is planned for every remediated deficiency."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 15 — W14 Scoping Workpaper & Coverage Dashboard", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 15 — W14 Scoping Workpaper & Coverage Dashboard"}]}, {"title": "15.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "15.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W14 is the master record and the reviewer's first stop. During scoping it summarises what is in scope, what is confirmed and what is outstanding (the role played by 06_Lab1_Scoping_Workpaper in the draft). As the project progresses it becomes the coverage dashboard: it proves that every risk has a control, every key control has been tested, and every deficiency has been evaluated."}]}, {"title": "15.2 Workbook structure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "15.2 Workbook structure"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Content"], ["Engagement_Summary", "Client, period, materiality basis, systems and processes in scope (lookups from W01 and W03)"], ["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Three-column view separating validated facts, unconfirmed items and outstanding requests"], ["Dependencies", "Access gaps, missing owners, missing evidence, IPE failures, each with owner and date"], ["Coverage_Matrix", "One row per SOX-relevant risk: mapped key controls, TOD result, TOE result, deficiencies, residual status"], ["ITGC_Reliance", "For each ITAC/IPE/ITDM control: dependent ITGCs and their results, and whether reliance is supported"], ["Progress", "Counts by status: PBC requests, tests, workpapers reviewed, deficiencies open"], ["Conclusion", "Scoping stage: \"No conclusion at scoping stage.\" Final stage: overall assessment by process and control category"]]}]}, {"title": "15.3 Coverage checks (formulas)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "15.3 Coverage checks (formulas)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Check", "Pass condition"], ["Risk coverage", "Every SOX-relevant RISK-ID has ≥ 1 key CTRL-ID in Risk_Control_Map"], ["Test coverage", "Every key CTRL-ID has a TOD and a TOE row in W10"], ["Evidence coverage", "Every TEST-ID has a population EV-ID with IPE = Pass"], ["Sample coverage", "Every TOE has an SMP-ID covering the full period"], ["Result coverage", "Every TEST-ID has a reviewed result"], ["Deficiency coverage", "Every Ineffective or Exception-noted test has a DEF-ID or a documented false-positive rationale"], ["Reliance coverage", "Every ITAC/IPE/ITDM shows all dependent ITGCs Effective, or has additional procedures recorded"]]}]}, {"title": "15.4 Worked example — Coverage_Matrix extract", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "15.4 Worked example — Coverage_Matrix extract"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RISK-ID", "Key controls", "TOD", "TOE", "DEF", "Status"], ["R-TECH-001", "C-ITGC-AC-001, -002, -003", "Effective", "Exception (AC-002)", "DEF-004", "Covered — deficiency, compensated"], ["R-TECH-003", "C-ITGC-PC-001, -002", "Effective", "Effective", "—", "Covered"], ["R-P2P-002", "C-ITAC-004", "Effective", "Effective (reliance supported)", "—", "Covered"], ["R-P2P-003", "C-BPC-P2P-003", "Pending", "Not started", "—", "Dependency: owner outstanding"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — W14 TELLS THE STORY A partner or audit committee member should be able to open W14 and, without opening any other workbook, see what was in scope, what was tested, what failed and what remains open. Every number on it is a formula over W01–W13, never a typed figure."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["W14 IS COMPLETE WHEN Scoping stage: in-scope items, known/unconfirmed/outstanding and dependencies are complete, and the conclusion reads \"No conclusion at scoping stage\". Testing stage: every coverage check passes or the gap is explained. All figures are formulas linked to the source workbooks."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 16 — The Population Sequence and Checkpoints", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 16 — The Population Sequence and Checkpoints"}]}, {"title": "16.1 Why order matters", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "16.1 Why order matters"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Each workbook depends on keys created in earlier ones. Populating out of order produces broken links, duplicate requests and tests against unconfirmed scope. The sequence below is the order a TIB engagement follows, with a checkpoint (gate) that must be passed before the next stage starts."}]}, {"title": "16.2 The sequence", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "16.2 The sequence"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Stage", "Workbooks", "Main inputs", "Who provides", "Gate to pass"], ["1. Understand the environment", "W01, W02", "Landscape diagram, system inventory, System > Status, SCC4, SM51, SM59, WE20, STMS routes", "Basis lead, integration team", "G1: all financially relevant systems and interfaces listed and validated or flagged"], ["2. Define process scope", "W03", "Significant accounts/FSLIs, process narratives, transaction usage", "Finance controller, process owners", "G2: every significant account maps to a process; technical areas added"], ["3. Identify risks", "W04", "Risk library, walkthrough interviews, prior audit issues, W01–W03 gaps", "Process owners, risk owners", "G3: every BP-ID has risks; SOX risks flagged; owners agreed"], ["4. Design the control set", "W05, W06", "Client RCM, policies, GRC ruleset, configuration", "Control owners, GRC administrator", "G4: every SOX risk has a key control; ITGC dependencies recorded; ruleset approved"], ["5. Confirm people", "W07", "Organisation charts, owner meetings", "Control and evidence owners", "G5: owners confirmed or escalated as dependencies"], ["6. Request and receive evidence", "W08, W09", "Population and configuration extracts", "Evidence owners", "G6: populations received and IPE Pass"], ["7. Plan the tests", "W10", "RCM, sample guide, team availability", "Engagement manager", "G7: TOD/TOE rows for all key controls; ITGCs scheduled first"], ["8. Sample and test", "W12, W11 (+ W08 wave 3)", "Populations, sample evidence", "Testers, evidence owners", "G8: workpapers complete and reviewed"], ["9. Evaluate and conclude", "W13, W14", "Test results, compensating controls, management responses", "Engagement manager, partner", "G9: all coverage checks pass; deficiencies evaluated; conclusion signed"]]}]}, {"title": "16.3 Scoping deliverable versus testing deliverable", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "16.3 Scoping deliverable versus testing deliverable"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Stages 1 to 5, plus wave 1 and 2 PBC requests, make up the scope of work (the deliverable from the Lab 1 scoping exercise). At the end of stage 5, W14 is issued in its scoping form with the conclusion \"No conclusion at scoping stage\". Stages 6 to 9 are the testing phase, which the scope of work plans and controls. The scope of work is therefore not a separate document; it is the state of W01–W08 and W14 at gate G5."}]}, {"title": "16.4 Iteration", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "16.4 Iteration"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The sequence is not strictly one-way. A walkthrough in stage 3 often reveals an interface missing from W02, or a test in stage 8 shows a control works differently from its RCM description. When this happens, update the source workbook, keep the existing keys, and let the links flow forward. Never patch a downstream workbook to cover an upstream gap."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FINAL TRACEABILITY TEST Pick any row in W13 or W14. A trainee should be able to follow it back: DEF → TEST → SMP → EV → PBC → OWNER → CTRL → RISK → BP → INT → SYS, and name the server, client and process owner it came from. If any link breaks, the foundation is not complete."]]}]}, {"title": "End of Part 2", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "End of Part 2"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Parts 1 and 2 together define the full Foundation Workbook Set and the order in which it is built. Parts 3A and 3B take each W11 workpaper template in turn (access, emergency access, SoD, change, operations, ITAC configuration and IPE) and walks through the SAP navigation, test steps and worked results for ABC Manufacturing. Part 4 covers deficiency evaluation in depth and the end-to-end trace exercise."}]}], "word_count": 2206, "id": "tib-sap-controls-foundation-manual-part2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Manual_Part2.docx", "filename": "TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Manual_Part2.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Building the SAP Controls Project Foundation"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From Landscape to Every SOX, ITGC and ITAC Test: The Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Training Manual  |  Part 3A of 4: Sessions 17–24 (Testing Access Controls)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Running case: ABC Manufacturing  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "Session 17 — How Part 3 Uses the Foundation", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 17 — How Part 3 Uses the Foundation"}]}, {"title": "17.1 From workbook to test", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "17.1 From workbook to test"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Parts 1 and 2 built the foundation. Part 3 uses it. Every test in this part starts from a row in the Test Plan (W10), which was generated from a key control in the RCM (W05), which responds to a risk in the Risk Register (W04), which belongs to a process or technical area in W03 running on a system in W01. Each session below names that thread at the top so the trainee never loses sight of why the test is being performed."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "SYS → BP → RISK → CTRL → TEST → POPULATION (EV, IPE) → SAMPLE (SMP) → ATTRIBUTES → RESULT → DEF"}]}, {"title": "17.2 The universal test cycle", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "17.2 The universal test cycle"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every access, change, operations and ITAC test in Parts 3A and 3B follows the same seven moves. Learn them once; the control areas only change the data sources."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Move", "What the tester does", "Workbook"], ["1. Read the control", "Re-read the five-part control description and the risk it addresses", "W05, W04"], ["2. Walk it through (TOD)", "Follow one instance end to end with the performer; confirm the design would address the risk", "W11 (TOD)"], ["3. Secure the population", "Obtain the full population for the period and complete the IPE check", "W08, W09"], ["4. Select the sample", "Select by the approved method, across the whole period", "W12"], ["5. Request sample evidence", "Raise wave 3 PBC requests for the selected items", "W08"], ["6. Test each attribute", "Complete the attribute grid; reference an EV-ID for every tick or exception", "W11"], ["7. Conclude and hand off", "Record the result in W10; raise DEF-IDs in W13; update reliance in W05 and W14", "W10, W13, W14"]]}]}, {"title": "17.3 Access controls in scope for Part 3A", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "17.3 Access controls in scope for Part 3A"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Session", "Template", "Control", "RISK-ID"], ["18", "WP-AC-01", "C-ITGC-AC-001 User provisioning", "R-TECH-001, R-P2P-001"], ["19", "WP-AC-02", "C-ITGC-AC-002 Termination of leavers", "R-TECH-001"], ["20", "WP-AC-03", "C-ITGC-AC-003 Periodic user access review", "R-TECH-001, R-P2P-001"], ["21", "WP-AC-04", "C-ITGC-AC-005 Privileged, generic and default IDs", "R-TECH-003, R-TECH-005"], ["22", "WP-AC-05", "C-ITGC-AC-004 Emergency access (Firefighter)", "R-TECH-002"], ["23", "WP-AC-06", "C-ITGC-SOD-001 SoD and sensitive access", "R-P2P-001, R-R2R-001"], ["24", "WP-AC-07", "C-ITGC-AC-006 Password and logon parameters", "R-TECH-005"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "C-ITGC-AC-005, C-ITGC-AC-006 and R-TECH-005 (\"unauthorised users gain access through privileged, default or weakly protected accounts\") are held at source in W04 and W05 (Part 1, Sessions 5 and 6, and the key register). Following Session 16.4, new keys are always created at source, never only in a workpaper."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — ACCESS TESTS ARE POPULATION TESTS Most access failures are found not in the sample but in the population: an active user with no HR record, a dialog user holding SAP_ALL, a Firefighter ID left unlocked. Always analyse the full population first, then test the sample. A clean sample over a dirty population is a wrong conclusion."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 18 — WP-AC-01 User Provisioning", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 18 — WP-AC-01 User Provisioning"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-AC-01"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-AC-001 — New and changed access is requested and approved by the line manager and role owner in GRC ARM, with SoD analysis, before provisioning to ECP/300"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-001; R-P2P-001"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-APD · IT-dependent manual · Event-driven"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 / SYS-04 → BP-TECH-01 → R-TECH-001 → C-ITGC-AC-001 → C-ITGC-AC-001-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "18.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "18.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Only access that a business owner has approved for a business reason should ever reach production. The control must ensure three things: approval happens before access is granted, the approver has the authority to approve that role, and what was granted matches what was approved. Where GRC is used, the request also passes an SoD check so conflicts are seen before they are created."}]}, {"title": "18.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "18.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["GRC ARM", "Access Management > Access Request (NWBC/Fiori); report: Access Request Details / Approver Delegation", "Request, approvers, approval timestamps, SoD result, provisioning log"], ["User change documents", "SUIM > Change Documents > For Users (RSUSR100N)", "Who created users / assigned roles, and when"], ["Role assignments", "Table AGR_USERS (via SE16 display or SUIM RSUSR002)", "Current roles per user with valid-from/to dates"], ["User master", "Table USR02 / SU01 display", "User type, creation date, creator (ANAME), validity"], ["Role ownership", "GRC Access Control > Master Data > Role (Business Role Management) or client role-owner list", "Who is authorised to approve each role"]]}]}, {"title": "18.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "18.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The population is every user created and every role assignment added in ECP/300 during the test period. Extract it from RSUSR100N (change documents for users, role assignments) with the period dates, or from the GRC request report filtered to provisioned requests. EV-031 contains 214 new role assignments across 61 users from 01-Jan to 30-Sep."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "IPE (IPE-004): screenshot of RSUSR100N selection parameters (client 300, full date range, no user filter); total count reconciled to GRC provisioning log count plus assignments made outside GRC; five assignments traced both ways between file and AGR_USERS. Any assignment made outside GRC, for example directly in PFCG or SU01 by Basis, is itself a potential exception and must be kept in the population, not filtered out."}]}, {"title": "18.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "18.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Walk through one new-joiner request with the GRC administrator: request created, manager approval, role owner approval, SoD analysis, automatic provisioning."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm the workflow cannot be bypassed: who can assign roles directly in SU01/PFCG in production (holders of S_USER_GRP and S_USER_AGR with change activity)?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm role owners are defined for every role in scope."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Conclude whether the design addresses R-TECH-001 and R-P2P-001."}]}, {"title": "18.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "18.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "A documented access request exists for the assignment", "GRC request ID"], ["B", "Approved by the line manager before provisioning", "Approval timestamp vs AGR_USERS / RSUSR100N date"], ["C", "Approved by the authorised role owner before provisioning", "Approver vs role-owner list"], ["D", "Roles provisioned agree to roles approved", "Request line items vs AGR_USERS"], ["E", "SoD analysis run; any conflict mitigated or rejected", "GRC risk analysis result; mitigation ID"]]}]}, {"title": "18.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "18.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Read C-ITGC-AC-001 and R-TECH-001 in W05/W04. Confirm IPE-004 = Pass."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Analyse the full population: flag assignments with no GRC request number and assignments made by Basis users directly. These are investigated regardless of the sample."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Select 25 assignments (event-driven guide) by seeded random selection across all three quarters; log as SMP-005."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Raise PBC-044 for GRC request records for the 25 items."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — For each item, complete attributes A–E. Compare timestamps to the minute where the system allows."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 6 — Document exceptions with cause from inquiry; record result in W10."}]}, {"title": "18.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "18.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Population analysis found 6 assignments without a GRC request, all made by Basis user BASIS_ADM via SU01 on 14-Mar."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "User", "Role", "A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "Note"], ["1", "KOFORI", "Z_AP_INVOICE_CLERK", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "EV-052"], ["2", "MPATEL", "Z_MM_BUYER", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "EV-053"], ["7", "TBROWN", "Z_FI_GL_ACCOUNTANT", "✔", "✔", "✗", "✔", "✔", "Role owner approval missing: approved by line manager only"], ["…", "…", "…", "", "", "", "", "", "Items 3–6, 8–25 all attributes met"], ["P", "6 items", "Various (BASIS_ADM)", "✗", "✗", "✗", "n/a", "✗", "Population exception: direct SU01 assignment, no request"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Exception noted. One sample deviation (item 7) and six population exceptions. Inquiry shows the six assignments were an urgent month-end fix without a later retrospective approval. These are raised together as DEF-006 and evaluated in W13, including whether any of the six users performed transactions with the access (STAD / change documents)."}]}, {"title": "18.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "18.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A missing approval is a deviation even if the access looks appropriate; the control is about approval, not suitability."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Approval after provisioning is a deviation. Record the gap in days."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Population exceptions (bypass of the workflow) usually weigh more than sample exceptions, because they show the control can be avoided."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Check whether the users holding direct-assignment rights (S_USER_GRP/S_USER_AGR) is itself a W06 sensitive-access item."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — TEST THE BYPASS, NOT JUST THE WORKFLOW GRC workflows almost always work as configured. The real risk is the path around them: Basis or security staff assigning roles directly in production. The population analysis in step 2 is what finds it."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 19 — WP-AC-02 Termination of Leavers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 19 — WP-AC-02 Termination of Leavers"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-AC-02"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-AC-002 — The IT Security Administrator locks and end-dates SAP users within two business days of the HR termination date"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-001"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-APD · Manual · Event-driven"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 → BP-TECH-01 → R-TECH-001 → C-ITGC-AC-002 → C-ITGC-AC-002-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "19.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "19.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A leaver who keeps an active SAP account can still post or approve transactions, either personally or through someone who knows the password. The control must remove the ability to log on promptly, and the test must show not only that accounts were locked but that no one used them after the termination date."}]}, {"title": "19.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "19.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["HR leavers report", "HR system (SuccessFactors / HCM PA40 actions / external HR)", "Authoritative list of terminations and dates"], ["User master", "USR02 fields UFLAG (lock), GLTGB (valid to), TRDAT/LTIME (last logon)", "Lock status, end date, last logon"], ["Lock history", "SUIM RSUSR100N change documents (lock/unlock events)", "Date and time the user was locked and by whom"], ["Logon activity", "SM20 / RSAU_READ_LOG (security audit log); STAD", "Logons and transactions after termination"]]}]}, {"title": "19.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "19.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The population is the HR leavers list for the period, not the SAP lock list. Starting from SAP would only show the users that were removed and miss those that were not. EV-021 contains 48 leavers from 01-Jan to 30-Sep. IPE-003: count reconciled to the HR headcount movement report; five leavers traced to HR personnel records."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Before sampling, match all 48 leavers to USR02 using the HR employee ID to SAP user mapping (often USR21 / the user's personnel number). Any leaver whose account is still unlocked and valid at the extraction date is a population exception."}]}, {"title": "19.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "19.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Walk through one termination: HR action, notification to IT Security, lock in SU01/GRC, confirmation."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm how IT Security is notified (automatic feed, weekly report, email) and what happens to leavers with no SAP account."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm the mapping between HR employee and SAP user is maintained."}]}, {"title": "19.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "19.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Leaver appears on the HR leavers report with termination date", "EV-021"], ["B", "SAP user is locked or end-dated", "USR02 UFLAG / GLTGB"], ["C", "Lock or end date is within two business days of termination", "RSUSR100N change document date"], ["D", "No logon after the termination date", "USR02 TRDAT; SM20 if needed"]]}]}, {"title": "19.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "19.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Confirm IPE-003 = Pass and agree the user-mapping approach with Basis."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Match all 48 leavers to USR02; flag unlocked accounts and logons after termination for the whole population."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Select 25 leavers across the period (SMP-006)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Obtain RSUSR100N lock events for the 25 users (PBC-045)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Complete attributes A–D; calculate business days between termination and lock."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 6 — Investigate any post-termination logon through SM20/STAD to see what was done."}]}, {"title": "19.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "19.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "User", "Term. date", "Lock date", "A", "B", "C", "D", "Note"], ["1", "RJONES", "15-Jan", "16-Jan", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "EV-061"], ["4", "JSMITH", "07-Mar", "25-Mar", "✔", "✔", "✗", "✗", "14 business days; logon on 10-Mar (display only, SM20)"], ["11", "AKHAN", "30-May", "09-Jun", "✔", "✔", "✗", "✔", "6 business days"], ["19", "LGARCIA", "22-Aug", "01-Sep", "✔", "✔", "✗", "✔", "6 business days"], ["…", "…", "…", "…", "", "", "", "", "Remaining items met all attributes"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Exception noted, 3 of 25. Cause: the HR leavers report is sent weekly rather than daily. This becomes DEF-004 (worked in Part 2, Session 14.4). Compensating control C-ITGC-AC-003 (quarterly review) was tested effective."}]}, {"title": "19.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "19.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Late removal without use is a deviation in timeliness; late removal with use raises severity and needs activity analysis."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A leaver with no SAP account is not an exception, but keep the evidence of the match."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Contractors and agency staff often fall outside the HR report; ask how they are terminated and test them separately if material."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — START FROM HR, NOT FROM SAP The population for a leaver test always comes from the authoritative source of terminations. A list of locked SAP users can only ever show the control working."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 20 — WP-AC-03 Periodic User Access Review", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 20 — WP-AC-03 Periodic User Access Review"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-AC-03"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-AC-003 — Quarterly, role owners review all users and their roles in ECP/300 through GRC UAR; inappropriate access is removed within 10 business days"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-001; R-P2P-001"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-APD · IT-dependent manual · Quarterly"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 → BP-TECH-01 → R-TECH-001 → C-ITGC-AC-003 → C-ITGC-AC-003-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "20.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "20.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Provisioning and termination controls deal with individual events; the access review catches what they miss: role creep, transfers, and accounts that should have been removed. The control is only as good as the list it reviews and the care of the reviewer, so the test focuses on completeness of the review population, precision of review, and follow-through on removals."}]}, {"title": "20.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "20.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["GRC UAR", "Access Control > Access Request > User Access Review; reports: UAR Review Status, Request status", "Campaign scope, reviewers, decisions, timestamps"], ["IPE: review listing", "The user/role listing generated for the campaign", "What the reviewer actually saw"], ["Current access", "AGR_USERS, USR02 at campaign generation date", "Independent population to test completeness"], ["Removal evidence", "GRC removal requests; RSUSR100N role removal dates", "Whether rejected access was removed"]]}]}, {"title": "20.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "20.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "There are two populations. First, the reviews themselves: 3 quarterly campaigns in the period (Q1–Q3), each with role-owner review packages. Second, within each sampled campaign, the user/role lines reviewed. Quarterly frequency gives a sample of 2 campaigns."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "IPE is critical here and is tested under C-IPE-001 and recorded in the dependency tab: the campaign listing must contain every active user and role assignment at the generation date. Reconcile the campaign line count to AGR_USERS for active users at that date."}]}, {"title": "20.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "20.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Walk through one campaign: generation, distribution to reviewers, decisions, escalation, closure."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm reviewers are the role owners (or line managers) defined in W07, not the users themselves."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm reviewers see enough information (role description, sensitive access, SoD flags) to make a judgement."}]}, {"title": "20.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "20.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Campaign listing is complete (reconciles to AGR_USERS at generation date)", "IPE reconciliation"], ["B", "Every package reviewed by an authorised reviewer", "UAR status report"], ["C", "Review completed within the defined window", "Decision timestamps"], ["D", "Reviewer evidence shows a real review (removals, comments or explicit confirmation of sensitive access)", "Review decisions"], ["E", "Rejected access removed within 10 business days", "RSUSR100N removal dates"]]}]}, {"title": "20.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "20.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Select Q1 and Q3 campaigns (SMP-007)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — For each, reconcile the listing to AGR_USERS at the generation date (attribute A)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Confirm every package was completed by an authorised reviewer (B, C)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Select 10 reviewer packages and inspect decisions; challenge any package where every line was approved in seconds (D)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — For every rejection in the sampled campaigns, trace the removal (E)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 6 — Cross-check: users found as exceptions in WP-AC-01 and WP-AC-02 — did the review catch them?"}]}, {"title": "20.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "20.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Campaign", "A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "Note"], ["Q1", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "4,812 lines reconciled; 37 rejections, all removed within 10 days"], ["Q3", "✔", "✔", "✗", "✔", "✔", "2 packages closed 9 days late after escalation; within tolerance per policy? — confirm with owner"], ["Cross-check", "", "", "", "", "", "JSMITH (WP-AC-02) was removed in the Q1 review — supports compensating-control conclusion"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Effective, pending confirmation of the late-completion tolerance with the control owner. The cross-check is what lets W13 treat C-ITGC-AC-003 as compensating for DEF-004."}]}, {"title": "20.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "20.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A review based on an incomplete listing fails attribute A even if every reviewer did their job."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "\"Rubber-stamp\" reviews (every line approved, no comments, completed in seconds) are a design and operating concern; discuss with the reviewer and document."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Rejected-but-not-removed access is a high-impact exception: the control detected the problem and then failed to fix it."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — THE LIST IS PART OF THE CONTROL A user access review relies on IPE. If the listing omits a user group (for example, users with roles assigned through composite roles or reference users), those users were never reviewed. Always test the listing before the review."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 21 — WP-AC-04 Privileged, Generic and Default IDs", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 21 — WP-AC-04 Privileged, Generic and Default IDs"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-AC-04"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-AC-005 — Privileged authorisations (SAP_ALL, SAP_NEW, user admin, table change, debug-change) and generic/default IDs are restricted to approved users; default IDs are locked or secured; Basis reviews the list monthly"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-003; R-TECH-005"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-APD · IT-dependent manual · Monthly"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 / SYS-06 → BP-TECH-01 → R-TECH-005 → C-ITGC-AC-005 → C-ITGC-AC-005-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "21.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "21.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Privileged access overrides every other control. A dialog user with SAP_ALL can create a vendor, pay it, and change the configuration that would have blocked it. Default IDs such as SAP* and DDIC are known to attackers. Generic IDs remove accountability. The control must keep this access small, approved and monitored, and the test examines both the current state and the monthly review over the period."}]}, {"title": "21.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "21.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["Users by profile", "SUIM > Users by Complex Selection (RSUSR002), profile SAP_ALL / SAP_NEW", "Users holding super-user profiles"], ["Users by authorisation", "RSUSR002 with authorisation values (W06 Sensitive_Access list)", "Holders of S_USER_GRP, S_TABU_DIS 02, S_DEVELOP 02 etc."], ["Default users", "RSUSR003 (status of SAP*, DDIC, EARLYWATCH, TMSADM in all clients)", "Lock and password status"], ["User types", "USR02 USTYP (A dialog, B system, C communication, S service, L reference)", "Identifies technical IDs used for dialog logon"], ["Profile parameter", "login/no_automatic_user_sapstar via RSPARAM", "Prevents hard-coded SAP* logon"], ["Database", "HANA: users with system privileges (via DBA cockpit / SQL by Basis)", "Privileged access below the SAP layer (SYS-06)"]]}]}, {"title": "21.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "21.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "This test combines a point-in-time analysis of the full user population (EV-012, IPE-001 Pass) with a test of the monthly review. The review population is 9 monthly reviews (Jan–Sep); the monthly guide gives a sample of 2–5, here 3 (SMP-008)."}]}, {"title": "21.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "21.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Inspect the privileged-access policy and the approved holder list."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Walk through one monthly review: who generates the list, who reviews, what is done with unapproved holders."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Run RSUSR003 in every PRD client, including 000 and 066 if present."}]}, {"title": "21.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "21.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Every holder of SAP_ALL / SAP_NEW / W06 sensitive items is on the approved list with a business reason", "RSUSR002 vs approved list"], ["B", "No dialog (type A) generic IDs without named ownership", "USR02, owner list"], ["C", "Default users locked or with changed passwords in all clients", "RSUSR003"], ["D", "Monthly review performed, evidenced and acted upon (sampled months)", "Review sign-off, removal evidence"]]}]}, {"title": "21.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "21.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Run the population analyses (A–C) on EV-012 and RSUSR003 output."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Extend to HANA (SYS-06) using the Basis-provided list of database users with system privileges."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — For the 3 sampled months, inspect the review evidence and follow up actions (D)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Map every unapproved holder to W06 SENS-IDs and to the risks they create."}]}, {"title": "21.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "21.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Check", "Result", "Note"], ["A — SAP_ALL holders", "2 found", "BASIS_ADM (approved, Basis lead) and FI_SUPPORT1 (not approved; dialog user)"], ["A — S_TABU_DIS 02 + SE16N", "4 found", "All approved FF IDs — expected per W06 SENS-004"], ["B — generic dialog IDs", "1 found", "TRAINING01 in client 300, active, last logon 02-Jul"], ["C — default users", "Pass", "SAP* locked, DDIC password changed in all clients"], ["D — monthly reviews", "2 of 3 evidenced", "June review not evidenced"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Ineffective. FI_SUPPORT1 holding SAP_ALL as a dialog user, an active generic ID in production, and a missing review month together form DEF-007. Because this control is a dependency for C-ITAC-004 and C-ITAC-005, W05 ITGC_Dependency and W14 ITGC_Reliance are updated, and additional procedures are required before relying on those ITACs (Part 3B)."}]}, {"title": "21.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "21.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Privileged access held by an unapproved dialog user is a significant exception even if never used; then test usage (SM20, STAD, change documents) to see whether it was."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Approved privileged access with no monitoring is a design gap, usually solved by moving it to Firefighter."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Always report the result to the Part 3B ITAC tests; this is the reliance link in action."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — ONE PRIVILEGED USER CAN UNDO THE AUDIT Privileged-access results flow into every ITAC and IPE that relies on this system. A single unapproved SAP_ALL holder means configuration may have been changed outside the change process, so reliance on automated controls must be re-examined."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 22 — WP-AC-05 Emergency Access (Firefighter)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 22 — WP-AC-05 Emergency Access (Firefighter)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-AC-05"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-AC-004 — Emergency access through GRC EAM Firefighter IDs is requested with a reason, approved before use, logged, and every log is reviewed by the assigned controller within five business days"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-002"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-APD · IT-dependent manual · Event-driven"], ["Thread", "SYS-04 → SYS-03 → BP-TECH-01 → R-TECH-002 → C-ITGC-AC-004 → C-ITGC-AC-004-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "22.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "22.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Firefighter IDs carry broad, sensitive access for urgent fixes. The control makes that access safe: it is borrowed only when needed, with a reason, by an authorised user, every action is logged, and an independent controller reviews what was done. The test checks the IDs themselves, the sessions, and the reviews."}]}, {"title": "22.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "22.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["EAM master data", "GRC > Emergency Access Management > Firefighter IDs, Owners, Controllers, Firefighters", "Who can use which FF ID; who reviews"], ["FF ID roles", "AGR_USERS for each FF ID in ECP", "What each FF ID can do"], ["Session logs", "GRC EAM reports: Firefighter Log Summary, Consolidated Log Report, Transaction Log and Session Details", "Every session, reason, transactions, change documents"], ["Log review workflow", "GRC EAM log review requests / controller sign-off", "Timing and evidence of review"], ["FF ID status", "USR02 for FF IDs (should be locked when not checked out, depending on configuration)", "Prevents direct logon"]]}]}, {"title": "22.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "22.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Population: every Firefighter session in the period from the Firefighter Log Summary (EV-034, 186 sessions). IPE-005: count reconciled to the Consolidated Log Report and to SM20 logons by FF IDs; five sessions traced both ways. A session in SM20 that is missing from the GRC log means the ID was used outside EAM and is a population exception."}]}, {"title": "22.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "22.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Inspect EAM configuration: FF IDs, assigned roles, owners, controllers, and whether log review workflow is active."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Walk through one session: request, approval, logon, actions, log, controller review."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm a controller cannot review their own sessions and that firefighters cannot be their own controller."}]}, {"title": "22.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "22.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Session has a reason code and description that matches the activity", "Log summary"], ["B", "Use was approved before the session (pre-assigned and authorised, or approved request)", "EAM request / assignment"], ["C", "Log reviewed by the assigned controller", "Log review workflow"], ["D", "Review completed within five business days", "Review timestamp vs session end"], ["E", "Activity was consistent with the stated reason; anomalies followed up", "Transaction log vs reason"]]}]}, {"title": "22.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "22.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Population analysis: compare SM20 logons by FF IDs with GRC sessions; confirm FF IDs are not used for direct logon."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Inspect FF ID role content against W06: are SoD-conflicting or SAP_ALL roles assigned, and is that approved?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Select 25 sessions (SMP-009), weighted to include sessions with change activity."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Complete A–E; for E, compare transactions executed to the reason given."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Record the result in W10 and update W14."}]}, {"title": "22.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "22.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Session", "FF ID", "A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "Note"], ["1", "FF-0102", "FF_FI_01", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "Period-end fix; FB02 only"], ["9", "FF-0287", "FF_BASIS_01", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✗", "✔", "Reviewed after 12 days"], ["14", "FF-0412", "FF_FI_01", "✗", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✗", "Reason \"month-end\"; activity included XK02 vendor bank change, not followed up"], ["…", "…", "…", "", "", "", "", "", "Remaining items met all attributes"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Exception noted. Item 14 is the more serious: a vendor bank-detail change under a generic reason, reviewed and approved without query. It is raised as DEF-008 and the vendor change is referred to the P2P team to confirm the payment was legitimate. This is the thread working in both directions: an ITGC test produced a question for a business-process risk (R-P2P-001)."}]}, {"title": "22.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "22.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A late review is a timeliness deviation; a review that fails to question unusual activity is a precision deviation and is weighed more heavily."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Sessions outside EAM (SM20 logons not in GRC logs) mean the control can be bypassed and are always significant."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Check that FF IDs are not used for routine work; high session counts for the same task suggest a missing standard role."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — REVIEW THE ACTIVITY, NOT THE SIGNATURE A controller sign-off proves the log was opened, not that it was understood. Attribute E, comparing the transactions to the stated reason, is where Firefighter tests find their real exceptions."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 23 — WP-AC-06 Segregation of Duties and Sensitive Access", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 23 — WP-AC-06 Segregation of Duties and Sensitive Access"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-AC-06"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-SOD-001 — GRC ARA risk analysis runs at provisioning and monthly at user level against the approved ruleset; conflicts are removed or assigned an approved mitigating control"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-P2P-001; R-R2R-001"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-APD · IT-dependent manual · Event-driven and monthly"], ["Thread", "SYS-04 → SYS-03 → BP-P2P-01/03 → R-P2P-001 → SOD-001 → C-ITGC-SOD-001 → C-ITGC-SOD-001-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "23.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "23.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "SoD prevents one person from controlling a transaction end to end. The W06 ruleset defines which combinations are not allowed. The control must detect every conflict that exists, and for each one either remove it or apply a mitigating control that is real and tested. The test reruns the analysis independently, then checks how each conflict was handled."}]}, {"title": "23.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "23.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["GRC ARA", "Access Risk Analysis > User Level, Permission level, Rule set = approved version (W06)", "Users with conflicts by SOD-ID"], ["Mitigation assignments", "GRC > Mitigated Users; mitigating control master data", "Which users have which mitigations, validity dates"], ["Without GRC", "RSUSR008_009_NEW (critical combinations) using W06 rules; or table analysis on AGR_USERS + AGR_1251", "Independent conflict analysis"], ["Usage", "STAD / ST03N; change documents", "Whether conflicting access was actually used"]]}]}, {"title": "23.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "23.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Population: the full user population at period end (EV-012) run against the approved W06 ruleset, plus the monthly ARA results for the period. The tester either runs ARA themselves with the client (observed) or reruns the rules independently from AGR_USERS and AGR_1251 (EV-013). The independent run is the IPE test of the client's ARA report."}]}, {"title": "23.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "23.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm the ruleset in GRC agrees to the W06 approved version (functions, actions, permissions, custom Z-transactions)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Walk through one provisioning request flagged with a conflict: was it rejected or mitigated, and by whom?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Inspect the mitigating controls in GRC: each must exist in W05 with an owner and test."}]}, {"title": "23.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "23.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Independent analysis agrees to client ARA results (no unreported conflicts)", "EV-013 vs ARA output"], ["B", "Each conflict is either removed or has an approved, valid mitigation", "Mitigation assignment, dates"], ["C", "Mitigating control exists in W05 and was tested effective", "W05 / W10 result"], ["D", "Critical conflicts (SOD-001, SOD-003) have no unmitigated holders", "ARA output"], ["E", "Where unmitigated, usage of the conflicting access is analysed", "STAD / change documents"]]}]}, {"title": "23.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "23.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Run the independent analysis for SOD-001 to SOD-005 at permission level, applying the org-level logic from W06."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Reconcile to the client's ARA output; explain every difference (A)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — For each conflicting user, test B–D; select 25 if the conflict population is large (SMP-010)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — For unmitigated conflicts, analyse usage over the period (E)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Feed results to W13 and to the business-process risk owners."}]}, {"title": "23.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "23.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SOD-ID", "Users with conflict", "Mitigated", "Unmitigated", "Note"], ["SOD-001", "3", "0", "3", "FI_SUPPORT1 (via SAP_ALL), and two AP supervisors; one AP supervisor used both XK02 and F110 in the period"], ["SOD-002", "11", "11", "0", "Mitigation C-BPC-P2P-004 tested effective"], ["SOD-003", "2", "0", "2", "Includes FI_SUPPORT1"], ["SOD-005", "1", "1", "0", "Mitigated by C-BPC-R2R-002"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Ineffective for SOD-001 and SOD-003. The AP supervisor's use of both vendor maintenance and payment is referred for a payment review over the period. Raised as DEF-009. FI_SUPPORT1 links this finding to DEF-007 (WP-AC-04); W13 evaluates them together because they share a root cause."}]}, {"title": "23.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "23.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A conflict reported by transaction but not executable at permission level is a false positive; document the rationale and keep it in W11."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A mitigation that is assigned but untested, expired, or not in W05 does not mitigate."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Unmitigated critical conflicts with actual usage require analysis of the transactions performed, not just a statement that access existed."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — RERUN, DO NOT RECEIVE The client's ARA report is IPE. If the ruleset is incomplete or the report is filtered, it will under-report conflicts. Independent rerun of the rules against AGR_USERS and AGR_1251 is what gives the result its authority."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 24 — WP-AC-07 Password and Logon Parameters", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 24 — WP-AC-07 Password and Logon Parameters"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-AC-07"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-AC-006 — SAP logon and password parameters in ECP are set in line with the ABC password policy on all application servers, and changes follow change management"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-005"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-APD · Automated (configuration) · Continuous"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 → BP-TECH-01 → R-TECH-005 → C-ITGC-AC-006 → C-ITGC-AC-006-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "24.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "24.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "System parameters enforce the password policy automatically. Because it is configuration, the test is a test of one, with confirmation that the setting applied all period. Two traps catch trainees: parameters can differ between application servers, and Single Sign-On may mean SAP password settings are not the real control for most users."}]}, {"title": "24.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "24.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["Parameter values", "RZ11 (single parameter) or report RSPARAM on each instance; SM51 to switch servers", "Current values per application server"], ["Profile files", "RZ10 / instance and default profiles", "Configured (not just active) values"], ["Change history", "RZ10 profile versions; SM21 system log for dynamic changes", "Whether values changed in the period"], ["Authentication method", "SNC / SSO configuration (Basis); USR02 password status fields", "Whether users log on by password or SSO"]]}]}, {"title": "24.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "24.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "No sample. The population is the set of in-scope parameters on every application server in ECP (EV-015, RSPARAM output per server listed in W01 Server_Detail). Confirm that the server list agrees to SM51."}]}, {"title": "24.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "24.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Obtain the password policy and map each requirement to its parameter."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm whether SSO is used and for which user groups; if so, also obtain evidence of the directory (e.g. Active Directory) policy."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm how profile changes are controlled (link to C-ITGC-PC-001)."}]}, {"title": "24.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "24.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "login/min_password_lng meets policy", "RSPARAM"], ["B", "login/password_expiration_time meets policy", "RSPARAM"], ["C", "login/fails_to_user_lock meets policy", "RSPARAM"], ["D", "login/no_automatic_user_sapstar = 1", "RSPARAM"], ["E", "rdisp/gui_auto_logout meets policy", "RSPARAM"], ["F", "Values identical on all application servers and unchanged in period (or changed through change management)", "RSPARAM per server; RZ10 history"]]}]}, {"title": "24.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "24.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — List all application servers from SM51 and confirm against W01."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Run RSPARAM on each server, or have Basis do so while observed; capture parameters A–E."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Compare to the policy and to each other server."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Inspect RZ10 profile version history for the period."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Conclude; where SSO is used, note that the directory policy is the effective control and test it or rely on another team's work."}]}, {"title": "24.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "24.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Parameter", "Policy", "Server 1", "Server 2", "Result"], ["login/min_password_lng", "≥ 8", "8", "8", "✔"], ["login/password_expiration_time", "≤ 90", "90", "90", "✔"], ["login/fails_to_user_lock", "≤ 5", "5", "10", "✗ Server 2 differs"], ["login/no_automatic_user_sapstar", "1", "1", "1", "✔"], ["rdisp/gui_auto_logout", "≤ 1800", "1800", "1800", "✔"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Exception noted. The second application server allows 10 failed attempts because its instance profile overrides the default profile. Raised as DEF-010 (low severity, with change-management cause referred to Part 3B)."}]}, {"title": "24.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "24.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Always test every application server; an instance profile can override the default profile."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A value that meets policy today but changed during the period requires testing for the whole period."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "If SSO is used for all dialog users, password parameters are a lower-weight control; say so in the conclusion."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — CONFIGURATION IS TESTED ONCE, BUT EVERYWHERE Automated controls need only a test of one, but \"one\" means one test of every place the configuration lives, plus evidence it did not change. That second part depends on Program Change ITGCs, the subject of Part 3B."]]}]}, {"title": "End of Part 3A", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "End of Part 3A"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 3A tested the seven access control areas and raised DEF-006 to DEF-010, two of which (DEF-007 and DEF-009) affect reliance on automated controls. Part 3B continues with Program Change (transports, system and client settings, direct changes), Computer Operations (batch jobs, interfaces, backup), ITAC configuration testing with the reliance assessment, and IPE testing of key reports."}]}], "word_count": 3175, "id": "tib-sap-controls-foundation-manual-part3a", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Manual_Part3A.docx", "filename": "TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Manual_Part3A.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Building the SAP Controls Project Foundation"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From Landscape to Every SOX, ITGC and ITAC Test: The Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Training Manual  |  Part 3B of 4: Sessions 25–34 (Change, Operations, ITAC, Reliance and IPE)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Running case: ABC Manufacturing  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "Session 25 — Where Part 3B Picks Up", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 25 — Where Part 3B Picks Up"}]}, {"title": "25.1 The story so far", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "25.1 The story so far"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 3A tested access. Two of its results matter for everything that follows: DEF-007 (an unapproved dialog user, FI_SUPPORT1, holding SAP_ALL) and DEF-009 (unmitigated vendor-maintenance and payment conflicts). Both mean someone could have changed data or configuration outside the normal controls. Part 3B tests the other ITGC domains, then brings all ITGC results together to decide whether the automated controls (ITACs) and system reports (IPE) can be relied on."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Session", "Template", "Control", "RISK-ID"], ["26", "WP-PC-01", "C-ITGC-PC-001 Transport management", "R-TECH-003"], ["27", "WP-PC-02", "C-ITGC-PC-002 System and client change settings", "R-TECH-003"], ["28", "WP-PC-03", "C-ITGC-PC-003 Direct changes and debugging in production", "R-TECH-003"], ["29", "WP-CO-01", "C-ITGC-CO-001 Batch job monitoring", "R-TECH-004"], ["30", "WP-CO-02", "C-ITGC-CO-002 Interface monitoring and reconciliation", "R-TECH-004, R-O2C-001, R-P2P-003"], ["31", "WP-CO-03", "C-ITGC-CO-003 Backup and recovery", "R-TECH-006"], ["32", "WP-ITAC-01", "C-ITAC-004 Three-way match; C-ITAC-005 Posting periods", "R-P2P-002, R-R2R-001"], ["33", "—", "ITGC reliance assessment", "All ITAC / IPE / ITDM"], ["34", "WP-IPE-01", "C-IPE-001 Key reports used in controls", "Per dependent control"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "C-ITGC-PC-003, C-ITGC-CO-002 (interfaces, separate from batch jobs in C-ITGC-CO-001), C-ITGC-CO-003, C-IPE-001 and R-TECH-006 (\"financial data cannot be recovered after failure or corruption\") are held at source in W04 and W05 (Part 1, Sessions 5 and 6, and the key register)."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — CHANGE CONTROLS ARE THE GUARD ON CONFIGURATION An ITAC is only a setting in a table. Program Change ITGCs decide whether that setting could have moved during the year. Read every Part 3B change result with the question: could the three-way match or posting-period configuration have been altered without anyone knowing?"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 26 — WP-PC-01 Transport Management", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 26 — WP-PC-01 Transport Management"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-PC-01"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-PC-001 — Every transport imported to ECP has an approved change ticket, evidence of testing and business sign-off in QAS, and is imported by Basis, not the developer; emergency changes are approved retrospectively within two business days"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-003"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-PC · IT-dependent manual · Event-driven"], ["Thread", "SYS-01 → SYS-02 → SYS-03 → BP-TECH-02 → R-TECH-003 → C-ITGC-PC-001 → C-ITGC-PC-001-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "26.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "26.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Transports are how programs and configuration reach production. The control must ensure that every change entering ECP was requested, approved, tested and moved by someone independent of the person who built it. The test starts from what actually arrived in production, never from the change tickets, because a ticket list cannot show a change that bypassed it."}]}, {"title": "26.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "26.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["Import history", "STMS > Import Overview > ECP > Import History; table TPALOG", "Every transport imported, date, time, return code, importing user"], ["Request header", "SE01 / table E070 (TRKORR, AS4USER owner, AS4DATE, TRSTATUS)", "Who created and released the request"], ["Request content", "Table E071 / E071K (objects and table keys)", "What changed: programs, configuration tables (e.g. T169G, T001B)"], ["Change tickets", "ITSM tool (ServiceNow / Jira) or SAP Solution Manager ChaRM", "Approval, testing evidence, sign-off"], ["Import rights", "RSUSR002: S_TRANSPRT, S_CTS_ADMI in ECP", "Who can import; are any developers among them"], ["Transport routes", "W01 Transport_Routes tab (STMS route display)", "Confirms the DEV → QAS → PRD path"]]}]}, {"title": "26.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "26.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The population is every transport imported to ECP/300 in the period from TPALOG / STMS import history (EV-019, 312 transports; IPE-002 Pass). Do not use the ITSM change list: it can only show changes that went through ITSM."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Before sampling, analyse the full population: (1) match every transport to a ticket number (often in the request description); (2) compare E070 owner to the importing user; (3) flag imports with no prior import to QAS; (4) flag transports containing ITAC configuration tables (E071K for T169G, T001B, credit control tables), which are always selected."}]}, {"title": "26.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "26.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Walk through one normal and one emergency change from ticket to import."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Inspect the import-rights list: confirm developers cannot import to production."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm the transport route forces QAS before PRD (no direct DEV → PRD route)."}]}, {"title": "26.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "26.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Approved change ticket exists before import to PRD", "Ticket approval timestamp vs TPALOG"], ["B", "Evidence of testing in QAS and business sign-off", "Ticket attachments; QAS import date"], ["C", "Imported by a user other than the request owner", "E070 AS4USER vs TPALOG user"], ["D", "Transport content agrees to the approved change", "E071 objects vs ticket description"], ["E", "Emergency changes approved retrospectively within two business days", "Ticket history"]]}]}, {"title": "26.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "26.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Run the four population analyses above on EV-019."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Select 25 by seeded random selection (SMP-003), plus all transports touching ITAC tables (here 2), giving 27."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Raise PBC-040 for tickets and test evidence."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Complete A–E for each item."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Record the list of ITAC-table transports for Session 32."}]}, {"title": "26.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "26.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["#", "Transport", "Owner / importer", "A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "Note"], ["1", "ECDK900114", "DEV_KUMAR / TMSADM (Basis)", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "n/a", "Program fix"], ["8", "ECDK900187", "DEV_LEE / BASIS_ADM", "✔", "✗", "✔", "✔", "n/a", "No QAS sign-off attached"], ["26", "ECDK900201", "FUNC_AP1 / BASIS_ADM", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "n/a", "Targeted: T169G tolerance change, approved"], ["27", "ECDK900233", "FUNC_FI2 / BASIS_ADM", "✔", "✔", "✔", "✔", "n/a", "Targeted: T001B variant change, approved"], ["…", "…", "…", "", "", "", "", "", "Remaining items met all attributes"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Population result: all 312 transports have ticket references; no developer imported to production. Sample result: Exception noted, 1 of 27 (missing test sign-off), raised as DEF-011, low severity. Both ITAC-table transports were approved and tested, which is direct evidence for Session 32."}]}, {"title": "26.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "26.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A missing ticket for a transport is a population exception and is always significant: the change bypassed the process."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Developer importing their own change is a segregation failure; check the importing user against the developer list, not just the request owner."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Transports containing configuration for ITACs feed the reliance assessment whatever the result."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — START FROM PRODUCTION The change population comes from what production received (TPALOG), not from what the change process recorded. Only that direction can find a change that skipped the process."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 27 — WP-PC-02 System and Client Change Settings", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 27 — WP-PC-02 System and Client Change Settings"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-PC-02"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-PC-002 — ECP is set to \"Not modifiable\" (SE06) and client 300 to \"No changes allowed\" for client-specific objects and no changes to cross-client objects (SCC4); any opening is approved, time-limited and logged; table logging is active"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-003"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-PC · Automated (configuration) with manual opening process · Continuous"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 → BP-TECH-02 → R-TECH-003 → C-ITGC-PC-002 → C-ITGC-PC-002-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "27.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "27.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "If production is open for changes, configuration and repository objects can be changed directly, without a transport, so C-ITGC-PC-001 sees nothing. These settings keep production closed. The test confirms they are closed today, finds every time they were opened during the period, and checks that table logging is on, because Session 32 depends on those logs."}]}, {"title": "27.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "27.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["System change option", "SE06 > System Change Option; Display Log", "Global setting and history of openings"], ["Client settings", "SCC4 > client 300 details; change documents for table T000 (via SCU3 if logged)", "Client role, change and transport settings, history"], ["Table logging", "Profile parameter rec/client (RSPARAM)", "Whether table changes in client 300 are logged in DBTABLOG"], ["Table change logs", "SCU3 / DBTABLOG", "Evidence of direct configuration changes"], ["Opening rights", "RSUSR002: S_ADMI_FCD, S_CTS_ADMI, S_TABU_CLI / SCC4 and SE06 rights", "Who can open the system"]]}]}, {"title": "27.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "27.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Configuration is tested once on every relevant setting. The openings are a population: every SE06 and SCC4 change in the period from the logs (EV-022: 3 openings). All are tested."}]}, {"title": "27.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "27.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Inspect the procedure for opening production (approval, duration, who closes it)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm who holds the rights to change SE06 and SCC4."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm rec/client includes 300 (or ALL) so table logging is active."}]}, {"title": "27.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "27.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "SE06 global setting = Not modifiable", "SE06 screenshot"], ["B", "SCC4 client 300: No changes allowed; no changes to repository and cross-client customising; production role", "SCC4 screenshot"], ["C", "rec/client includes 300 on all servers", "RSPARAM per server"], ["D", "Every opening had prior approval and a ticket", "Ticket vs log timestamp"], ["E", "Every opening was closed within the approved window and changes made were reviewed", "SE06/SCC4 log; DBTABLOG for the window"]]}]}, {"title": "27.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "27.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Capture current settings (A, B) with date and system visible."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Run RSPARAM on each server for rec/client (C)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Extract SE06 and SCC4 change history for the period; list every opening."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Test D and E for all openings; for each window, list table changes made in DBTABLOG."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Pass the logging result and any open windows to Session 32."}]}, {"title": "27.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "27.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Result", "Note"], ["A — SE06", "✔", "Not modifiable"], ["B — SCC4 client 300", "✔", "No changes allowed; production"], ["C — rec/client", "✔", "ALL on both servers"], ["Opening 1 (12-Feb, 2 h)", "✔", "Approved ticket CHG-1022; support pack"], ["Opening 2 (14-Mar, 6 h)", "✗", "No ticket; opened by BASIS_ADM; same day as direct SU01 assignments (DEF-006)"], ["Opening 3 (19-Jul, 1 h)", "✔", "Approved ticket CHG-1188"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Exception noted. The 14-Mar opening was unapproved. DBTABLOG for the window shows changes to two Z-tables but none to T169G or T001B. Raised as DEF-012 and linked to DEF-006 because the same user and date are involved. W13 will consider whether these share a root cause."}]}, {"title": "27.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "27.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "An unapproved opening is significant even if no changes are found, because it shows the control can be bypassed."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "If rec/client is off, there is no evidence of direct table changes; that alone can prevent reliance on ITACs."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Always analyse what changed during an opening; a closed system today says nothing about the period."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — THE LOGS ARE PART OF THE CONTROL Table logging (rec/client) is what allows the tester to prove that ITAC configuration did not change during the year. Check it here, early, because Session 32 cannot be completed without it."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 28 — WP-PC-03 Direct Changes and Debugging in Production", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 28 — WP-PC-03 Direct Changes and Debugging in Production"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-PC-03"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-PC-003 — Debug-with-change and direct table editing in ECP are restricted to Firefighter IDs; the Security Audit Log records these events and Basis reviews them monthly"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-003"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-PC · IT-dependent manual · Monthly"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 → BP-TECH-02 → R-TECH-003 → C-ITGC-PC-003 → C-ITGC-PC-003-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "28.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "28.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Even with production closed, a user with the right authorisation can change data in memory through the debugger or edit tables directly. These changes leave no transport and often no change document. The control restricts the ability and monitors its use."}]}, {"title": "28.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "28.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["Debug-change rights", "RSUSR002: S_DEVELOP with OBJTYPE = DEBUG, ACTVT = 02", "Who can change values in the debugger"], ["Table edit rights", "RSUSR002: SE16N with &SAP_EDIT, S_TABU_DIS / S_TABU_NAM ACTVT 02", "Who can edit tables directly"], ["Audit log config", "RSAU_CONFIG (formerly SM19)", "Whether debug and critical events are recorded for all users"], ["Audit log", "RSAU_READ_LOG (formerly SM20)", "Debugger change events and critical transactions"], ["SE16N edit log", "Tables SE16N_CD_KEY / SE16N_CD_DATA", "Direct edits made with SE16N"]]}]}, {"title": "28.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "28.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Two parts. Rights: the full user population (EV-012). Events: all debug-change and SE16N edit events for the period from the audit log and SE16N log (EV-024: 7 events). Monthly reviews: 9; sample 3 (SMP-011)."}]}, {"title": "28.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "28.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm the Security Audit Log is active with filters covering all users and clients for the relevant events."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm the log retention covers the whole period."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Walk through one monthly review."}]}, {"title": "28.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "28.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Only FF IDs hold debug-change and table-edit rights", "RSUSR002"], ["B", "Audit log active and filters cover required events for all users", "RSAU_CONFIG"], ["C", "Each event was performed under Firefighter with a valid reason", "Event vs WP-AC-05 sessions"], ["D", "Monthly review performed and evidenced", "Review sign-off"]]}]}, {"title": "28.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "28.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Run the rights analysis (A). Reuse W06 SENS items."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Inspect audit log configuration and retention (B)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Match every event to a Firefighter session tested in WP-AC-05 (C)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Test the three sampled monthly reviews (D)."}]}, {"title": "28.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "28.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Check", "Result", "Note"], ["A — rights", "✗", "FI_SUPPORT1 holds S_DEVELOP DEBUG 02 through SAP_ALL (same as DEF-007)"], ["B — audit log", "✔", "Active, all clients, retention 13 months"], ["C — events (7)", "✔", "All 7 under FF IDs with reasons; none by FI_SUPPORT1"], ["D — reviews (3)", "✔", "Evidenced"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Exception noted on A only; no new deficiency raised because it is part of DEF-007. The fact that the audit log shows no debug-change events by FI_SUPPORT1 is recorded as evidence for Session 32."}]}, {"title": "28.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "28.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Rights without use are still an exception against attribute A; usage analysis decides severity."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "If the audit log is not active for all users, debug changes cannot be detected; treat this as a design gap."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Link findings to existing DEF-IDs rather than raising duplicates for the same root cause."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — ONE FINDING, MANY TESTS FI_SUPPORT1 appears in WP-AC-04, WP-AC-06 and WP-PC-03. The workbook set lets the tester record it once (DEF-007) and reference it everywhere, so the client sees one problem with its full impact."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 29 — WP-CO-01 Batch Job Monitoring", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 29 — WP-CO-01 Batch Job Monitoring"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-CO-01"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-CO-001 — Critical financial batch jobs are scheduled under an approved technical user, monitored daily by Basis, and every failure is ticketed and resolved"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-004"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-CO · Manual · Daily"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 → BP-TECH-03 → R-TECH-004 → C-ITGC-CO-001 → C-ITGC-CO-001-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "29.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "29.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Many financial processes run in batch: payment runs, GR/IR clearing, depreciation, interface postings. If a critical job fails and nobody notices, transactions are incomplete. Jobs also run under a step user whose authorisations apply, so a job can be a route to privileged access."}]}, {"title": "29.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "29.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["Job overview", "SM37; tables TBTCO (header, status, scheduler) and TBTCP (steps, step user)", "Job runs, statuses, users"], ["Critical job list", "Client document; agreed with process owners", "Which jobs are financially relevant"], ["Job change rights", "RSUSR002: S_BTCH_ADM, S_BTCH_JOB, S_BTCH_NAM", "Who can schedule or change jobs, or run as other users"], ["Failure tickets", "ITSM", "Response to cancelled jobs"]]}]}, {"title": "29.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "29.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Daily monitoring over ~190 working days in the period; daily guide gives 25 days (SMP-012). Separately, all cancelled runs (status A) of critical jobs in the period from TBTCO (EV-026: 14) are tested in full."}]}, {"title": "29.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "29.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Inspect the critical job list and confirm it covers jobs for in-scope processes (F110 payment run, GR/IR, interface postings)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Walk through one day of monitoring and one failure ticket."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Inspect step users for critical jobs: technical user type B, not a dialog user; authorisations appropriate."}]}, {"title": "29.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "29.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Monitoring performed and evidenced on the sampled day", "Monitoring checklist / alert log"], ["B", "Every cancelled critical job ticketed", "TBTCO vs ITSM"], ["C", "Failure resolved and rerun with completeness confirmed", "Ticket, rerun in SM37"], ["D", "Critical jobs run under approved technical users", "TBTCP step user, USR02 type"]]}]}, {"title": "29.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "29.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Confirm the critical job list with process owners."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Test A for 25 sampled days."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Test B and C for all 14 cancelled runs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Test D for all critical jobs."}]}, {"title": "29.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "29.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Check", "Result", "Note"], ["A — 25 days", "✔", "All evidenced via daily SM37 checklist"], ["B/C — 14 failures", "✗", "13 ticketed and rerun; 1 GR/IR clearing failure on 30-Jun not ticketed, rerun 4 days later"], ["D — step users", "✗", "F110 scheduled under dialog user AP_SUPER1, who is also in DEF-009"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Exception noted. Untracked failure (DEF-013, low) and the payment run scheduled under a dialog user who already has an unmitigated SoD conflict. The latter is added to DEF-009 as additional impact."}]}, {"title": "29.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "29.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A job running under a dialog or privileged user is an access issue as well as an operations issue."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A failure that was rerun late may affect period-end cut-off; inform the R2R team."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "If no critical job list exists, it is a design gap; agree one before testing operating effectiveness."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — JOBS CARRY THEIR USER'S ACCESS A batch job has every authorisation of its step user. Reviewing step users connects Computer Operations back to the access tests in Part 3A."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 30 — WP-CO-02 Interface Monitoring and Reconciliation", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 30 — WP-CO-02 Interface Monitoring and Reconciliation"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-CO-02"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-CO-002 — Errors on financially relevant interfaces (INT-01 to INT-05) are monitored daily and resolved; inbound and outbound files are reconciled by count and value; the payment file is protected from change after creation"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-004; R-O2C-001; R-P2P-003"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-CO · Manual / ITDM · Daily"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 → INT-01…05 → BP-P2P-03 / BP-O2C-02 → R-P2P-003, R-O2C-001 → C-ITGC-CO-002 → C-ITGC-CO-002-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "30.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "30.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W02 identified the interfaces that carry financial data. This control makes sure every record arrives once, complete and unchanged, and that errors are corrected. The payment file (INT-05) needs particular care: after SAP creates it, it sits in a directory until transmitted, and anyone who can edit it can redirect payments."}]}, {"title": "30.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "30.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["IDoc status", "WE02 / WE05 (status 51 error, 53 posted); BD87 reprocess", "Inbound IDoc errors, e.g. INT-03 EDI orders"], ["tRFC / qRFC", "SM58; SMQ1 / SMQ2", "Failed RFC calls between systems"], ["Middleware", "SXMB_MONI (PI/PO) or CPI message monitor", "Message failures outside SAP ERP"], ["Bank statements", "FF_5 import log; FEBAN", "INT-01 completeness"], ["Payment file", "F110 log, DMEE format tree, file directory (AL11), OS directory permissions", "INT-05 file creation and protection"], ["Reconciliations", "Client reconciliation evidence", "Count and value agreement source ↔ target"]]}]}, {"title": "30.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "30.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Daily monitoring over the period: 25 days sampled (SMP-013). Reconciliations for INT-05 per payment run: 38 runs, sample 25. All IDoc errors in status 51 older than five days at period end (EV-028)."}]}, {"title": "30.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "30.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Walk through one day's monitoring for each interface."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "For INT-05, trace one payment run from F110 to bank confirmation; inspect who has write access to the payment file directory at OS and AL11 level."}]}, {"title": "30.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "30.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Daily monitoring evidenced for each in-scope interface", "Checklist / alert log"], ["B", "Errors resolved or escalated within the agreed time", "WE02 / ticket"], ["C", "Payment file total agrees to F110 total and to bank confirmation", "Reconciliation evidence"], ["D", "Only the transmission service account can write to the payment directory", "OS permission listing"]]}]}, {"title": "30.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "30.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Test A and B for 25 sampled days."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Test C for 25 payment runs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Test D once, with the infrastructure team."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Review aged IDoc errors at period end for unposted financial transactions."}]}, {"title": "30.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "30.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Check", "Result", "Note"], ["A/B — 25 days", "✔", "Monitoring evidenced"], ["C — 25 payment runs", "✔", "All totals agreed to bank confirmation"], ["D — payment directory", "✗", "Four AP users have write access to the directory"], ["Aged IDocs", "✗", "18 ORDERS05 IDocs in status 51 for 30+ days (INT-03), value not yet quantified"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Exception noted. Directory write access (DEF-014, high, linked to R-P2P-003) and aged EDI errors that may mean revenue is unbilled (DEF-015, referred to O2C team to quantify)."}]}, {"title": "30.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "30.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "File-level access is outside SAP authorisations; check it with infrastructure, or the payment risk is not covered."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Aged interface errors are a completeness issue for the financial statements, not only an IT matter."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "If no reconciliation exists, the control relies entirely on monitoring; assess whether that is enough."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — THE INTERFACE INVENTORY PAYS OFF Without W02, the payment directory would never have been examined. The inventory built in Session 3 is what turned an infrastructure detail into a tested control."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 31 — WP-CO-03 Backup and Recovery", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 31 — WP-CO-03 Backup and Recovery"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-CO-03"], ["Control tested", "C-ITGC-CO-003 — ECP database and logs are backed up daily, failures are resolved, and a restore is tested at least annually"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-TECH-006"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITGC-CO · Automated with manual monitoring · Daily / Annual"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 / SYS-06 → BP-TECH-03 → R-TECH-006 → C-ITGC-CO-003 → C-ITGC-CO-003-TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "31.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "31.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "If financial data is lost or corrupted it must be recoverable to a known point. Many external auditors treat backup as lower risk for SOX than access and change, but it remains in scope as an ITGC. Under a hosted or RISE model, the provider usually performs this control, and the tester relies on its SOC 1 report."}]}, {"title": "31.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "31.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["Backup logs", "DBA Cockpit (DBACOCKPIT) / HANA backup catalog", "Daily backup success or failure"], ["Restore test", "Infrastructure test records", "Evidence data can actually be restored"], ["SOC 1 report", "Provider SOC 1 Type II and bridge letter", "Provider-operated controls; complementary user entity controls (CUECs)"]]}]}, {"title": "31.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "31.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Daily backups: 25 days sampled (SMP-014). Restore test: the most recent test in or near the period. Where hosted, the SOC 1 replaces direct testing of the provider's controls."}]}, {"title": "31.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "31.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm backup schedule, retention and failure alerting."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "If hosted (W01 Hosting model), obtain the SOC 1, confirm period coverage, read exceptions, and map CUECs to ABC controls."}]}, {"title": "31.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "31.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Backup completed successfully on sampled day, or failure resolved", "Backup catalog"], ["B", "Restore test performed within 12 months and successful", "Test record"], ["C", "If SOC 1 relied on: period covered, exceptions assessed, CUECs in place", "SOC 1, bridge letter"]]}]}, {"title": "31.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "31.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Test A for 25 days."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Inspect B."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — If applicable, complete the SOC 1 review in C and record any CUECs in W05 as ABC controls."}]}, {"title": "31.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "31.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Check", "Result", "Note"], ["A — 25 days", "✔", "All successful"], ["B — restore test", "✔", "Performed 11-Apr, successful"], ["C — SOC 1", "n/a", "ECP is on-premise (W01)"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Effective."}]}, {"title": "31.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "31.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A SOC 1 that does not cover the full period needs a bridge letter."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "CUECs listed in the SOC 1 (for example, the client reviewing provider access) are the client's controls and must be tested by the client team."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — HOSTING CHANGES WHO OPERATES, NOT WHO IS ACCOUNTABLE When the provider runs a control, ABC still owns the risk. The SOC 1 review and CUEC mapping is how that accountability is demonstrated."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 32 — WP-ITAC-01 Configuration Controls", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 32 — WP-ITAC-01 Configuration Controls"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-ITAC-01"], ["Control tested", "C-ITAC-004 — Invoices outside OMR6 tolerances are automatically blocked for payment; only authorised AP supervisors can release blocks (MRBR). C-ITAC-005 — Posting periods in OB52 are restricted and only authorised users can change them"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "R-P2P-002; R-R2R-001"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITAC · Automated · Continuous"], ["Thread", "SYS-03 → BP-P2P-02 / BP-R2R-02 → R-P2P-002, R-R2R-001 → C-ITAC-004, C-ITAC-005 → TOD/TOE"]]}]}, {"title": "32.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "32.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "An ITAC is tested by confirming the configuration is designed correctly, proving it works (test of one), and establishing that it could not have changed unnoticed during the period. The third part is answered by the ITGCs, which is why this session follows them."}]}, {"title": "32.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "32.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["Tolerance configuration", "OMR6 (table T169G), tolerance keys e.g. PP price variance, DQ quantity, BD small differences", "Configured limits per company code"], ["GR-based invoice verification", "PO item flag / vendor master (WEBRE)", "Whether invoices must match goods receipts"], ["Blocked invoices", "RBKP payment block (ZLSPR = R); MRBR release", "Evidence the block happens; who releases"], ["Posting periods", "OB52 (table T001B), period variant", "Open periods by account type"], ["Change history", "SCU3 / DBTABLOG for T169G, T001B; E071K transports (Session 26)", "Whether configuration changed in the period"], ["Release / change rights", "RSUSR002: MRBR, F_REBZ_BUK; table maintenance for T001B", "Who can override or change the ITAC"]]}]}, {"title": "32.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "32.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Configuration: one test of each setting for company code 1000. Change history: all changes to T169G and T001B in the period (EV-029). Release rights: full user population."}]}, {"title": "32.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "32.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Inspect OMR6 values against the documented policy; inspect OB52 against the close calendar."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Confirm GR-based invoice verification is mandatory for relevant PO types."}]}, {"title": "32.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "32.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "OMR6 tolerances agree to policy (TOD)", "OMR6 screenshot"], ["B", "Reperform: invoice outside tolerance is blocked (TOE, test of one)", "Test posting in QAS, after confirming QAS T169G equals PRD; or inspect a real PRD invoice with ZLSPR = R"], ["C", "Only authorised users can release blocks (MRBR)", "RSUSR002"], ["D", "OB52 shows only the current period open; only authorised users can change it", "OB52, RSUSR002"], ["E", "Every change to T169G and T001B in the period was transported and approved, or explained", "DBTABLOG, E071K, Session 26"]]}]}, {"title": "32.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "32.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Capture configuration (A, D)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — Reperform the block (B); if using QAS, first compare T169G QAS to PRD (table compare, SCU0) to prove they are the same."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Run release and change rights (C, D)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 4 — Compare DBTABLOG changes to transports and tickets (E)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 5 — Hand results to Session 33 for the reliance decision."}]}, {"title": "32.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "32.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Check", "Result", "Note"], ["A — OMR6", "✔", "PP ±5% / max USD 500; agrees to policy"], ["B — reperformance", "✔", "PRD invoice 5105600871 blocked R; QAS reperformance also blocked"], ["C — MRBR release", "✗", "FI_SUPPORT1 can release (SAP_ALL)"], ["D — OB52", "✔", "Only current period open; 2 authorised users plus FI_SUPPORT1"], ["E — change history", "✔", "2 changes, both by transport ECDK900201 / 900233, approved (Session 26)"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: TOD and TOE effective, subject to the reliance assessment in Session 33 because FI_SUPPORT1 (DEF-007) could release blocks and change periods."}]}, {"title": "32.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "32.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "QAS reperformance only counts if QAS configuration is shown to equal PRD."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "An ITAC that can be overridden (MRBR release, period opening) is only as strong as the access to the override."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "In later years a benchmarking (baseline) approach can be used if change ITGCs remain effective and the configuration is unchanged."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — TEST OF ONE, PLUS PROOF OF NO CHANGE One successful test shows the ITAC works today. The change history and the ITGC results show it worked all year. Both are needed."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 33 — The ITGC Reliance Assessment", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 33 — The ITGC Reliance Assessment"}]}, {"title": "33.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "33.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "This session brings the ITGC results together and decides, for every ITAC, IPE and ITDM control, whether reliance is supported. It is recorded in the W05 ITGC_Dependency tab and summarised in W14 ITGC_Reliance. It is the point where the golden thread from Part 1, Section 1.5 is closed."}]}, {"title": "33.2 ITGC results summary", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "33.2 ITGC results summary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC", "Result", "Deficiency"], ["C-ITGC-AC-001 Provisioning", "Exception noted", "DEF-006"], ["C-ITGC-AC-002 Leavers", "Exception noted", "DEF-004"], ["C-ITGC-AC-003 Access review", "Effective", "—"], ["C-ITGC-AC-004 Firefighter", "Exception noted", "DEF-008"], ["C-ITGC-AC-005 Privileged access", "Ineffective", "DEF-007"], ["C-ITGC-SOD-001 SoD", "Ineffective", "DEF-009"], ["C-ITGC-PC-001 Transports", "Exception noted (low)", "DEF-011"], ["C-ITGC-PC-002 System settings", "Exception noted", "DEF-012"], ["C-ITGC-PC-003 Debug / direct change", "Exception (part of DEF-007)", "—"], ["C-ITGC-CO-001 Batch jobs", "Exception noted", "DEF-013"], ["C-ITGC-CO-002 Interfaces", "Exception noted", "DEF-014, DEF-015"], ["C-ITGC-CO-003 Backup", "Effective", "—"]]}]}, {"title": "33.3 Decision logic", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "33.3 Decision logic"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "For each dependent control ask three questions:"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Could the control's configuration or logic have changed without authorisation? (Program Change ITGCs, system settings, privileged access.)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Could the control have been overridden? (Access to release, unblock, open or change.)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "If yes to either, do additional procedures show it did not happen? (Table change logs for the full period, usage analysis of the risky users, audit log.)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "If additional procedures give sufficient evidence, reliance is supported with the procedures documented. If not, reliance is not supported and the team must test the business outcome directly (substantive testing of transactions) or rely on other controls."}]}, {"title": "33.4 Worked assessment — C-ITAC-004 and C-ITAC-005", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "33.4 Worked assessment — C-ITAC-004 and C-ITAC-005"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Evidence", "Answer"], ["Change without authorisation?", "Unapproved SE06 opening (DEF-012) and FI_SUPPORT1 SAP_ALL (DEF-007)", "Possible"], ["Additional procedure 1", "DBTABLOG for T169G and T001B for the full period (rec/client active, WP-PC-02): only the two approved transports", "No unauthorised change"], ["Additional procedure 2", "STAD / change documents for FI_SUPPORT1: no use of OMR6, OB52, MRBR in the period", "No override by the risky user"], ["Additional procedure 3", "Audit log: no debug-change by FI_SUPPORT1 (WP-PC-03)", "No hidden change"], ["Override by other users?", "MRBR releases in the period: 142, all by the two authorised AP supervisors", "No inappropriate override"], ["Conclusion", "—", "Reliance supported, with additional procedures documented"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "If table logging had been inactive, procedure 1 would have been impossible and reliance on both ITACs would not have been supported. That is why Session 27 checks rec/client so early."}]}, {"title": "33.5 Other dependent controls", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "33.5 Other dependent controls"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control", "Dependent ITGC issues", "Decision"], ["C-BPC-P2P-003 Payment file reconciliation", "DEF-014 directory write access; DEF-009 F110 under conflicting user", "Not supported until directory access is removed; substantive check of vendor bank changes and payments to new vendors required"], ["C-ITGC-AC-003 review listing (IPE)", "DEF-007 (could alter tables)", "Supported: listing reconciled independently to AGR_USERS (IPE-check)"], ["C-BPC-P2P-004 PO/GR exception review", "Relies on report IPE (Session 34)", "Pending IPE test"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — DEFICIENT ITGCs DO NOT AUTOMATICALLY FAIL ITACs They remove the presumption of reliance. Evidence from logs and usage analysis can restore it. Documenting that reasoning is one of the most valuable things a controls consultant does, and it is only possible when the foundation workbooks link every control to its dependencies."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 34 — WP-IPE-01 Key Reports", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 34 — WP-IPE-01 Key Reports"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Template", "WP-IPE-01"], ["Control tested", "C-IPE-001 — Reports used in key controls are complete and accurate: standard reports are run with correct parameters; custom reports have tested logic and are under change control"], ["Risk(s) addressed", "Inherited from each dependent control"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "IPE · Automated report with manual parameters · Each use"], ["Thread", "Report → dependent CTRL-ID (e.g. C-BPC-P2P-004) → its RISK-ID → BP-ID → SYS-03"]]}]}, {"title": "34.1 What the control must achieve", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "34.1 What the control must achieve"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "When a control reviewer looks at a report, the control is only as good as the report. If the report omits records, the reviewer cannot see them. IPE testing confirms that each key report gives a complete and accurate picture for each time it is used."}]}, {"title": "34.2 SAP navigation and data sources", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "34.2 SAP navigation and data sources"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source", "Navigation / object", "What it gives the tester"], ["Report register", "W05 \"IPE used\" column, consolidated to an IPE list", "Which reports support which controls"], ["Standard reports", "e.g. vendor master change display (S_ALR_87010039 / RFKABL00), GR/IR list (MB5S), aged payables", "SAP-delivered logic"], ["Custom reports", "SE38 / SE80 source; TADIR / transport history", "Custom logic and its change history"], ["Parameters", "Screenshot or variant (SE38 > Variants)", "Selections used each time the report is run"]]}]}, {"title": "34.3 Population and IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "34.3 Population and IPE"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Population: every report in the IPE list (here 6). For each report, the instances when it was used in the sampled control tests."}]}, {"title": "34.4 Test of design (walkthrough)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "34.4 Test of design (walkthrough)"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Classify each report: standard, standard with custom variant, or custom."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "For custom reports, review the logic with a developer and confirm it is under C-ITGC-PC-001."}]}, {"title": "34.5 Test attributes (TOE)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "34.5 Test attributes (TOE)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Attr.", "Each sample item must show", "Evidence"], ["A", "Report classified and its use in each control identified", "IPE list"], ["B", "Parameters used were appropriate (company code, period, no filters)", "Parameter evidence"], ["C", "Completeness: count/total reconciled to an independent source", "Reconciliation"], ["D", "Accuracy: sample of records traced to source data", "Trace"], ["E", "Custom report logic reviewed and unchanged in period, or changes approved", "Code review; transport history"]]}]}, {"title": "34.6 Step-by-step procedure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "34.6 Step-by-step procedure"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 1 — Build the IPE list from W05."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 2 — For each report, test A–E, reusing completeness and accuracy work from W09 where it is the same extract."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Step 3 — Feed results to Session 33 for any control waiting on IPE."}]}, {"title": "34.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "34.7 Worked result — ABC Manufacturing"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Report", "Type", "B", "C", "D", "E", "Note"], ["ZMM_POGR_EXCEPT (PO/GR exceptions)", "Custom", "✔", "✗", "✔", "✔", "Excludes POs with deletion flag; 31 exceptions not shown to reviewer"], ["Vendor master changes", "Standard", "✔", "✔", "✔", "n/a", "Used by C-BPC-P2P-001"], ["GR/IR list MB5S", "Standard", "✔", "✔", "✔", "n/a", "—"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Result: Exception noted for the custom PO/GR report (DEF-016). The dependent control C-BPC-P2P-004, which is also the SoD mitigation for SOD-002 in W06, is therefore not supported and the mitigation status in W06 changes. The thread has carried a report logic error through to an access conclusion."}]}, {"title": "34.8 Evaluating exceptions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "34.8 Evaluating exceptions"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A report used by a mitigating control that fails IPE means the mitigation does not work; update W06 and W14."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Standard SAP reports still need parameter testing on every use."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Do not accept a reviewer's report screenshot without seeing the parameters used."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — IPE CONNECTS EVERYTHING A report error found here changed a business-process control, an SoD mitigation and an access conclusion. Only a fully linked workbook set makes that visible."]]}]}, {"title": "End of Part 3B", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "End of Part 3B"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Parts 3A and 3B tested every control area in scope and raised DEF-004 and DEF-006 to DEF-016. Part 4 evaluates these deficiencies in full, including aggregation of related deficiencies (DEF-006, DEF-007, DEF-012 share a user and a date), compensating controls and severity, and closes with the end-to-end trace exercise from SYS-03 to every conclusion."}]}], "word_count": 2822, "id": "tib-sap-controls-foundation-manual-part3b", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Manual_Part3B.docx", "filename": "TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Manual_Part3B.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Building the SAP Controls Project Foundation"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "From Landscape to Every SOX, ITGC and ITAC Test: The Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Training Manual  |  Part 4 of 4: Sessions 35–40 (Evaluate, Remediate, Report, Trace)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Running case: ABC Manufacturing  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "Session 35 — Evaluating Deficiencies", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 35 — Evaluating Deficiencies"}]}, {"title": "35.1 From exception to deficiency", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "35.1 From exception to deficiency"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A test exception is a fact: a sample item did not meet an attribute. A deficiency is a judgement: the control, as designed or as it operated, does not allow management to prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis. Between the two sits evaluation, recorded in W13. Every exception follows the same path."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Step", "Question", "Outcome"], ["1. Validate", "Is the exception real? (False positive, wrong population, evidence later provided?)", "Discard with rationale, or confirm"], ["2. Classify", "Is it a design or operating failure?", "Design: control would not work even if performed. Operating: control exists but was not performed as designed"], ["3. Tolerate?", "Does the number of deviations exceed the tolerable rate for the sample?", "Commonly 0 deviations tolerated for samples up to 25; 1 may be tolerated for larger samples with extended testing"], ["4. Describe", "Condition, criteria, cause, effect", "Four-part statement in W13"], ["5. Assess impact", "What could go wrong, how much, how likely, which dependent controls are affected?", "Magnitude and likelihood"], ["6. Compensate", "Do other tested, effective controls reduce the exposure?", "Compensating controls recorded with TEST-ID"], ["7. Aggregate", "Do several deficiencies share a cause, process or account?", "Group and evaluate together"], ["8. Conclude severity", "Deficiency, significant deficiency or material weakness", "Reviewer approval"]]}]}, {"title": "35.2 The four-part deficiency statement", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "35.2 The four-part deficiency statement"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CONDITION — CRITERIA — CAUSE — EFFECT Condition:  what was found (facts, numbers, dates). Criteria:  what should have happened (the control description in W05, the policy). Cause:  why it happened (from inquiry with the control owner). Effect:  what could go wrong as a result, linked to the RISK-ID and assertion."]]}]}, {"title": "35.3 Severity factors", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "35.3 Severity factors"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Factor", "Points toward lower severity", "Points toward higher severity"], ["Magnitude", "Accounts affected are below materiality; low transaction value", "Material accounts; high-value transactions (payments, revenue)"], ["Likelihood", "Isolated, explained, detected by another control", "Systemic, repeated, unexplained, bypass possible"], ["Fraud susceptibility", "Error-only risk", "Misappropriation possible (vendor creation, payment, bank details)"], ["Dependent controls", "No ITAC/IPE relies on it, or reliance restored by additional procedures", "Reliance on ITACs/IPE cannot be restored"], ["Compensating controls", "Tested, effective, precise enough to detect a material misstatement", "Absent, untested, or too imprecise"], ["Actual occurrence", "No evidence the risk materialised", "Evidence of misuse or misstatement"]]}]}, {"title": "35.4 Evaluating ITGC deficiencies", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "35.4 Evaluating ITGC deficiencies"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "An ITGC deficiency rarely causes a misstatement directly. Its severity is driven by what it does to the controls and reports that depend on it. The evaluation therefore always reads the W05 ITGC_Dependency tab and the Session 33 reliance decision:"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "If reliance on all dependent controls was restored by additional procedures, the ITGC deficiency is normally a deficiency or, if pervasive, a significant deficiency."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "If reliance could not be restored and no other controls cover the risk, the ITGC deficiency takes on the severity of the business-process exposure it leaves open, and can contribute to a material weakness."}]}, {"title": "35.5 Materiality for the running case", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "35.5 Materiality for the running case"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "For ABC Manufacturing, the external auditor has set overall materiality at USD 2.5 million and a clearly trivial threshold of USD 125,000. These figures are used in Session 36 to judge magnitude."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — JUDGE THE GROUP, NOT JUST THE ITEM Two or three modest deficiencies that share a root cause can together be significant. The workbook links (same user, same date, same process) are what make aggregation possible. Evaluate each DEF, then evaluate the groups."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 36 — Worked Evaluation — ABC Manufacturing", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 36 — Worked Evaluation — ABC Manufacturing"}]}, {"title": "36.1 The deficiency register", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "36.1 The deficiency register"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["DEF-ID", "Control", "Condition (abridged)", "Type", "Initial view"], ["DEF-004", "C-ITGC-AC-002", "3 of 25 leavers removed late; one display-only logon after termination", "Operating", "Deficiency"], ["DEF-006", "C-ITGC-AC-001", "6 role assignments made directly in SU01 by BASIS_ADM on 14-Mar, no request; 1 sample missing role-owner approval", "Operating", "To aggregate"], ["DEF-007", "C-ITGC-AC-005", "FI_SUPPORT1 (dialog) holds SAP_ALL; generic ID TRAINING01 active; June review missing", "Operating", "To aggregate"], ["DEF-008", "C-ITGC-AC-004", "FF review late in 1 case; vendor bank change under generic reason not challenged", "Operating", "Deficiency"], ["DEF-009", "C-ITGC-SOD-001", "Unmitigated SOD-001/003 conflicts; AP_SUPER1 used XK02 and F110; F110 job scheduled under AP_SUPER1", "Operating", "To aggregate"], ["DEF-010", "C-ITGC-AC-006", "fails_to_user_lock = 10 on one server", "Operating", "Deficiency"], ["DEF-011", "C-ITGC-PC-001", "1 of 27 transports without QAS sign-off", "Operating", "Deficiency"], ["DEF-012", "C-ITGC-PC-002", "Unapproved SE06 opening on 14-Mar by BASIS_ADM; 2 Z-tables changed", "Operating", "To aggregate"], ["DEF-013", "C-ITGC-CO-001", "1 GR/IR clearing failure not ticketed; rerun after 4 days", "Operating", "Deficiency"], ["DEF-014", "C-ITGC-CO-002", "Four AP users can write to payment file directory", "Design", "To aggregate"], ["DEF-015", "C-ITGC-CO-002", "18 EDI order IDocs in error 30+ days", "Operating", "Quantify"], ["DEF-016", "C-IPE-001", "Custom PO/GR report excludes deletion-flagged POs; SoD mitigation C-BPC-P2P-004 not supported", "Design", "To aggregate"]]}]}, {"title": "36.2 Follow-up procedures before concluding", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "36.2 Follow-up procedures before concluding"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Procedure", "Result"], ["FI_SUPPORT1 origin", "RSUSR100N for 14-Mar", "FI_SUPPORT1 was one of the six direct assignments by BASIS_ADM (DEF-006), during the unapproved SE06 opening window (DEF-012)"], ["FI_SUPPORT1 usage", "STAD and change documents for the period", "Display transactions and two FB02 document header text changes; no configuration, master data or payment activity"], ["AP_SUPER1 dual use (DEF-009)", "Review of all vendor bank changes by AP_SUPER1 and subsequent payments", "14 bank changes, all supported by vendor letters and call-back evidence; no payment to unsupported details"], ["DEF-008 vendor bank change", "Vendor confirmation", "Legitimate; vendor confirmed new account"], ["DEF-015 aged IDocs", "Value the 18 orders", "USD 84,000 unbilled, below the USD 125,000 clearly trivial threshold; posted as a late adjustment"], ["DEF-016 report gap", "Rerun PO/GR exceptions with corrected logic", "31 additional exceptions totalling USD 210,000; all subsequently resolved, no misstatement at period end"]]}]}, {"title": "36.3 Aggregation", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "36.3 Aggregation"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Group", "DEFs", "Common factor", "Evaluated exposure"], ["A — Privileged access governance", "DEF-006, DEF-007, DEF-012", "Same user (BASIS_ADM), same date (14-Mar), emergency fix outside process; created FI_SUPPORT1 with SAP_ALL", "Change and access controls can be bypassed by Basis; mitigated by full-period table logs and usage analysis showing no misuse"], ["B — Payment process", "DEF-009, DEF-014, DEF-016", "All weaken controls over vendor creation, bank details and payment (R-P2P-001, R-P2P-003)", "Fraud-susceptible; reliance on C-BPC-P2P-003 not supported; substantive review found no misappropriation"], ["C — Access hygiene", "DEF-004, DEF-008, DEF-010", "Timeliness and precision of access controls", "Compensated by effective quarterly access review (C-ITGC-AC-003)"], ["D — Operations and change", "DEF-011, DEF-013, DEF-015", "Isolated operating lapses", "Low magnitude; DEF-015 quantified below clearly trivial"]]}]}, {"title": "36.4 Severity conclusions", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "36.4 Severity conclusions"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Group / DEF", "Severity", "Rationale"], ["Group A", "Significant deficiency", "Pervasive potential to bypass change and access controls by the Basis team; no evidence of misuse and ITAC reliance restored, so not a material weakness; important enough for audit committee attention"], ["Group B", "Significant deficiency", "Fraud-susceptible payment risk with three weaknesses together and no reliance on the payment reconciliation; substantive review showed no loss, and magnitude of a single undetected payment run is assessed as reasonably possible but not material"], ["Group C (each)", "Deficiency", "Compensating access review effective and precise enough to detect inappropriate access within a quarter"], ["DEF-011, 013, 015", "Deficiency", "Isolated, low magnitude"], ["Overall", "No material weakness identified", "All groups evaluated against materiality of USD 2.5m; reviewed by engagement manager and partner"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "These conclusions are illustrative for training. In practice, severity is agreed with the external auditor and supported by documented judgement in W13."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — FOLLOW-UP CHANGES THE ANSWER Before follow-up, Group A looked like a possible material weakness: a SAP_ALL user created in an unapproved window. Usage analysis, logs and the ITAC reliance work (Session 33) turned that into a well-evidenced significant deficiency. The evaluation is only as good as the evidence trail behind it."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 37 — Remediation and Retesting", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 37 — Remediation and Retesting"}]}, {"title": "37.1 Remediation plan", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "37.1 Remediation plan"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Each deficiency, or each aggregated group, has one remediation plan in W13, owned by the control owner and agreed with management. A good plan fixes the cause, not just the instance: removing FI_SUPPORT1's SAP_ALL fixes the instance; preventing Basis from assigning roles and opening production without approval fixes the cause."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Group", "Remediation actions", "Owner", "Due"], ["A", "Remove SAP_ALL from all dialog users; move emergency Basis work to Firefighter; require ticket and approval for SE06/SCC4 openings with automatic alert; lock TRAINING01", "Basis Lead / IT Security Manager", "30-Nov"], ["B", "Remove SOD-001/003 conflicts; reschedule F110 under a technical user; restrict payment directory to the transmission account; correct ZMM_POGR_EXCEPT logic and transport through change management", "Head of AP / Treasury / Basis", "15-Dec"], ["C", "Daily HR leavers feed to GRC with auto-lock; FF reason codes made specific with mandatory controller query of master-data changes; align instance profile parameter", "IT Security Manager", "31-Dec"], ["D", "Ticket all critical job failures; aged IDoc alert after 3 days; enforce QAS sign-off attachment in ITSM", "Basis Lead / SD Process Owner", "31-Dec"]]}]}, {"title": "37.2 Minimum operating period before retest", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "37.2 Minimum operating period before retest"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A remediated control must operate long enough to produce a meaningful sample. Typical minimums are shown below; the external auditor's methodology prevails."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Frequency", "Typical minimum operation", "Typical retest sample"], ["Annual", "One occurrence", "1"], ["Quarterly", "Two quarters", "2"], ["Monthly", "Two months", "2"], ["Weekly", "Five weeks", "5"], ["Daily", "20–30 days", "15–25"], ["Event-driven", "Until enough occurrences exist (often 30 days or more)", "15–25"], ["Automated / configuration", "Once implemented, with change controls effective", "1, plus change history since implementation"]]}]}, {"title": "37.3 Retest workflow", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "37.3 Retest workflow"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Create a new TEST-ID in W10 (for example C-ITGC-AC-005-RT1), linked to the DEF-ID."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Request the post-remediation population in W08 and complete the IPE check in W09."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Sample (W12) from the post-remediation period only."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Complete the workpaper (W11). If effective, set DEF status to Closed with the retest reference; if not, keep it open and update severity."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Update W14 so the coverage matrix shows both the original result and the remediated position at period end."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — A FIX IS NOT A CLOSE A deficiency closes only when the retest shows the remediated control operating. Until then, W14 must show it as open, however confident management is in the fix."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 38 — Reporting the Results", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 38 — Reporting the Results"}]}, {"title": "38.1 W14 at completion", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "38.1 W14 at completion"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "At the end of testing W14 replaces \"No conclusion at scoping stage\" with the overall conclusion. Every figure is a formula over W01–W13: systems and processes in scope, risks covered, controls tested, results by ITGC domain and control category, deficiencies by severity and status, and the reliance decisions from Session 33."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Coverage measure", "ABC result"], ["SOX-relevant risks with ≥ 1 key control", "100%"], ["Key controls with TOD and TOE complete", "100%"], ["Populations with IPE Pass", "100% (IPE-003 resolved before sampling)"], ["Tests reviewed", "100%"], ["Deficiencies", "12 individual; 2 significant deficiency groups; 0 material weaknesses"], ["ITAC reliance", "Supported with additional procedures (C-ITAC-004, C-ITAC-005)"], ["Controls not relied on", "C-BPC-P2P-003, C-BPC-P2P-004 (substantive procedures performed)"]]}]}, {"title": "38.2 Report structure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "38.2 Report structure"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Section", "Content", "Source"], ["1. Scope", "Systems, processes, period, approach", "W01, W03, W14"], ["2. Summary of results", "Coverage measures and overall conclusion", "W14"], ["3. Significant deficiencies", "One page per group: condition, criteria, cause, effect, severity rationale, management response", "W13"], ["4. Other deficiencies", "Table with owner and due date", "W13"], ["5. Reliance on automated controls", "ITGC results and reliance decisions", "W05 ITGC_Dependency, W14"], ["6. Appendices", "Control list, test results, traceability matrix", "W05, W10, W14"]]}]}, {"title": "38.3 Writing a finding", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "38.3 Writing a finding"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["EXAMPLE FINDING — GROUP A (SIGNIFICANT DEFICIENCY) Condition:  On 14 March, a Basis administrator opened the production system for changes without an approved ticket and directly assigned roles to six users, including SAP_ALL to the dialog user FI_SUPPORT1, which remained in place until September. The June privileged-access review was not performed. Criteria:  Production is to remain closed except for approved, time-limited openings (C-ITGC-PC-002); all access is to be requested and approved through GRC (C-ITGC-AC-001); privileged access is restricted to approved users and reviewed monthly (C-ITGC-AC-005). Cause:  An urgent month-end fix was performed outside the change and access processes, and no retrospective approval or review took place. Effect:  The Basis team could bypass change and access controls, creating a risk of unauthorised changes to financial data and configuration (R-TECH-003, R-TECH-005). Table change logs and user activity analysis for the full period found no unauthorised changes to financial configuration and no misuse of the access. Management response:  Agreed. Actions and dates per remediation plan Group A."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A finding uses plain language, states facts with numbers and dates, cites the control (not the tester's opinion) as criteria, and says what was done to assess the impact. It never names individuals in a report circulated beyond the control owners; user IDs are given in the workpaper, not the report."}]}, {"title": "Session 39 — The End-to-End Trace Exercise", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 39 — The End-to-End Trace Exercise"}]}, {"title": "39.1 Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "39.1 Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "This is the final test of the foundation. A trainee who can complete these traces without help understands how a controls project is built and why every workbook exists. Each trace is shown forward (planning direction) and must also be completed backward (defending direction)."}]}, {"title": "39.2 Trace 1 — From a server to a conclusion (forward)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "39.2 Trace 1 — From a server to a conclusion (forward)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Step", "Workbook", "Key", "What it says"], ["1", "W01", "SYS-03", "ECP client 300, PRD S/4HANA, financially relevant, validated by System > Status"], ["2", "W02", "INT-05", "Vendor payment file from SYS-03 to the bank, DMEE / pain.001"], ["3", "W03", "BP-P2P-03", "P2P vendor payment, FI, transactions F110 / FBZP"], ["4", "W04", "R-P2P-001", "Fictitious vendor created and paid; Occurrence; risk owner Head of AP"], ["5", "W05", "C-ITGC-SOD-001; C-BPC-P2P-003", "SoD analysis at provisioning and monthly; payment file reconciliation"], ["6", "W06", "SOD-001", "Vendor maintenance vs payment run; Critical"], ["7", "W07", "—", "Control owner IT Security Manager; evidence owner GRC Administrator"], ["8", "W08 / W09", "PBC-010, EV-012, IPE-001", "User master extract, IPE Pass"], ["9", "W10", "C-ITGC-SOD-001-TOE", "Attributes A–E, independent rerun"], ["10", "W12", "SMP-010", "Conflict population sampled"], ["11", "W11", "WP-AC-06", "SOD-001: 3 unmitigated users; AP_SUPER1 used both functions"], ["12", "W13", "DEF-009 → Group B", "Significant deficiency after aggregation"], ["13", "W14", "Coverage_Matrix", "R-P2P-001 covered; deficiency; substantive review performed; no material weakness"]]}]}, {"title": "39.3 Trace 2 — From a deficiency back to the server (backward)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "39.3 Trace 2 — From a deficiency back to the server (backward)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Step", "Key", "Answer"], ["Deficiency", "DEF-016", "Custom PO/GR report excludes deletion-flagged POs"], ["Test", "C-IPE-001 (WP-IPE-01)", "IPE completeness attribute C failed"], ["Dependent control", "C-BPC-P2P-004", "Monthly PO/GR exception review; also SoD mitigation for SOD-002"], ["Access rule", "SOD-002 (W06)", "Create PO vs post goods receipt; 11 mitigated users now unmitigated"], ["Risk", "R-P2P-002", "Invoice posted and paid without valid PO and GR"], ["Process", "BP-P2P-02", "Purchasing, receipt and invoice; MM, FI"], ["System", "SYS-03", "ECP / 300, production"]]}]}, {"title": "39.4 Trace 3 — How one ITGC result reaches an ITAC", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "39.4 Trace 3 — How one ITGC result reaches an ITAC"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Step", "Key", "Answer"], ["ITAC", "C-ITAC-004", "Three-way match tolerances (OMR6 / T169G)"], ["Dependent ITGCs (W05)", "C-ITGC-PC-001, C-ITGC-PC-002, C-ITGC-AC-003 (and AC-005 through privileged access)", "Change and access controls over configuration"], ["ITGC results", "DEF-007, DEF-012", "Possible bypass of change control"], ["Additional procedures (Session 33)", "DBTABLOG full period; FI_SUPPORT1 usage; audit log", "No unauthorised change to T169G"], ["Reliance decision (W14)", "Supported with additional procedures", "C-ITAC-004 relied on for the full period"]]}]}, {"title": "39.5 Trainee exercises", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "39.5 Trainee exercises"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Complete each trace in both directions using the ABC Manufacturing workbooks. Write the key at each step and one sentence explaining the link."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Exercise 1. Start from INT-03 (EDI customer orders). Trace forward to the final W14 status. Which deficiency is reached and how was it quantified?"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Exercise 2. Start from DEF-004. Trace backward to SYS-03, then explain why it was rated a deficiency and not a significant deficiency."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Exercise 3. Start from C-ITAC-005 (posting periods). List its dependent ITGCs and explain what would have happened to reliance if rec/client had been inactive."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Exercise 4. BP-H2R-01 (payroll posting) is still Outstanding in W03. List every workbook that would need new rows if HR is confirmed in scope, and the first key you would create in each."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Exercise 5. A reviewer asks: \"Which tests prove that no one changed the three-way match configuration during the year?\" Answer with TEST-IDs and EV-IDs only."}]}, {"title": "39.6 Answer guide (for instructors)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "39.6 Answer guide (for instructors)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ex.", "Key points expected"], ["1", "INT-03 → BP-O2C-02 → R-O2C-001 → C-ITGC-CO-002 → TEST → DEF-015; 18 IDocs valued at USD 84,000, below clearly trivial, posted as late adjustment; W14 shows Deficiency, Group D"], ["2", "DEF-004 → C-ITGC-AC-002-TOE → C-ITGC-AC-002 → R-TECH-001 → BP-TECH-01 → SYS-03; compensating C-ITGC-AC-003 effective and precise; post-termination logon was display only"], ["3", "C-ITGC-PC-001, C-ITGC-PC-002, C-ITGC-AC-003; without table logging, no evidence that T001B did not change outside transports, so reliance not supported and substantive cut-off testing required"], ["4", "W03 confirm BP-H2R-01; W02 INT-02 in scope; W04 new R-H2R-nnn; W05 new C-xxx; W06 HR SoD rules if HCM in SAP; W07 owners; W08 PBC; then W09–W14 as testing proceeds"], ["5", "C-ITGC-PC-001-TOE (EV-019, transports ECDK900201/900233), C-ITGC-PC-002-TOE (EV-022, rec/client), C-ITAC-004-TOE (EV-029 DBTABLOG), C-ITGC-PC-003-TOE (EV-024 audit log)"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 40 — Course Summary", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 40 — Course Summary"}]}, {"title": "40.1 The complete map", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "40.1 The complete map"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "SYS → INT → BP → RISK → CTRL → OWNER → PBC → EV → TEST → SMP → DEF → CONCLUSION"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Phase", "Workbooks", "Sessions", "Key output"], ["Environment", "W01 Landscape, W02 Interfaces, W03 Process Scope", "2–4", "What exists, what is in scope"], ["Risk & control design", "W04 Risks, W05 RCM, W06 SoD rules, W07 Owners", "5–8", "What could go wrong and which control answers it"], ["Evidence", "W08 PBC, W09 Evidence & IPE", "9–10", "Complete, accurate populations"], ["Test", "W10 Test Plan, W11 Workpapers, W12 Samples", "11–13, 17–34", "TOD and TOE for every key control, ITGC before ITAC"], ["Conclude", "W13 Deficiencies, W14 Dashboard", "14–15, 35–39", "Evaluated, aggregated, reported, remediated"]]}]}, {"title": "40.2 Ten principles to take away", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "40.2 Ten principles to take away"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The thread is the product: every conclusion traces back to a server, and every server traces forward to a conclusion."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Modules are not processes; processes are not controls; control areas are not controls."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Risks are written before controls, and controls before evidence requests."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Never invent scope; mark it TBC and follow it up."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Populations come from the system of record: production for changes, HR for leavers, the full user master for access."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Test the population before the sample."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Every report relied on is IPE and is tested for completeness and accuracy."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "ITGCs are tested first because ITACs and IPE rely on them."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A failed ITGC removes the presumption of reliance; evidence can restore it."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Evaluate groups, not just items, and close a deficiency only on a successful retest."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FINAL WORD A trainee who completes this course should be able to take any SAP client, build the fourteen workbooks from nothing, and defend every test to a reviewer. That is what it means to build the foundation upon which a controls project is built."]]}]}], "word_count": 1027, "id": "tib-sap-controls-foundation-manual-part4", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Manual_Part4.docx", "filename": "TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Manual_Part4.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Video Outline"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Building the SAP Controls Project Foundation: 40 Sessions"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show for each session video  |  40 sessions  |  about 7 h 34 min of content"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026  |  Companion to the five-part manual and the 14-workbook set"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "1. Series Plan and Production Standards", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "1. Series Plan and Production Standards"}]}, {"title": "1.1 Structure", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.1 Structure"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Block", "Videos", "Running time"], ["Part 1 — Environment, Risk and Control Design", "6 sessions", "80 min"], ["Part 2 — Evidence, Test and Conclusion Workbooks", "10 sessions", "105 min"], ["Part 3A — Testing Access Controls", "8 sessions", "96 min"], ["Part 3B — Change, Operations, ITAC, Reliance, IPE", "10 sessions", "107 min"], ["Part 4 — Evaluate, Remediate, Report, Trace", "6 sessions", "66 min"], ["Series trailer and five part intros", "6 clips", "about 6 min (1 min each)"], ["Total (sessions only)", "40 sessions", "454 min (7 h 34 min)"]]}]}, {"title": "1.2 Format for every session video", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.2 Format for every session video"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Length: 6 to 16 minutes, as listed. One idea per video."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Opening (0:00 to 0:30): the golden-thread bar with the current stage highlighted, a one-line objective, and the manual or workbook reference."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Body: three or four timed segments. Alternate a short concept slide with a live screen recording of the workbook or SAP screenshots."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Recap (last 45 seconds): the single takeaway sentence on screen, the Checks tab status, and the link to the next session."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Running case: ABC Manufacturing throughout. Show the actual keys on screen (SYS-03, R-P2P-001, C-ITAC-004, DEF-007) so viewers can follow the thread."}]}, {"title": "1.3 Visual standards", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.3 Visual standards"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Use TIB colours (navy and teal) and the same slide master. Callouts follow the manual: Core Insight boxes appear as a teal side-bar."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Zoom the workbook to 125% on screen recordings; highlight the cursor and the cell being discussed. Keep examples in the first row so the screen shows the sample rows."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Never show real client data. SAP screenshots must use the ABC training system or be redacted."}]}, {"title": "1.4 Per-session timing rule", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.4 Per-session timing rule"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Each block below shows timed segments calculated from the running time: a 30-second opening, equal body segments, and a 45-second recap. Adjust live segments (demos) to fit, but keep the opening and recap as written."}]}, {"title": "1.5 File naming", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.5 File naming"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Name each video S##_Short_Title (for example S19_WP-AC-02_Leavers) and keep the script and slides in the same folder. Keep the numbering identical to the manual sessions."}]}, {"title": "2. Series Trailer and Part Intros", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "2. Series Trailer and Part Intros"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Clip", "Length", "Content"], ["Series trailer", "1:30", "The problem (a project with no foundation), the golden thread bar, the 14 workbooks, the promise: follow any test from landscape to conclusion"], ["Part 1 intro", "1:00", "Environment, risk and control design: W01–W05"], ["Part 2 intro", "1:00", "Evidence, test and conclusion workbooks and the nine gates: W06–W14"], ["Part 3A intro", "1:00", "Testing access controls: seven workpapers"], ["Part 3B intro", "1:00", "Change, operations, ITAC, reliance and IPE"], ["Part 4 intro", "1:00", "Evaluating, remediating, reporting and the trace exercise"]]}]}, {"title": "3.1 Part 1 — Environment, Risk and Control Design", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3.1 Part 1 — Environment, Risk and Control Design"}]}, {"title": "Session 1: The Foundation and the Golden Thread (12 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 1: The Foundation and the Golden Thread (12 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Explain why a controls project starts with linked workbooks, and name the twelve links of the golden thread."], ["Visuals and demo", "Animated thread bar building link by link; table of 14 workbooks; ITAC-depends-on-ITGC diagram"], ["Key takeaway", "Every conclusion must trace back to a server, and every server forward to a conclusion."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 1, Session 1)"], ["0:30", "Why controls projects fail at the foundation, not at testing"], ["3:11", "The 14 workbooks in five phases"], ["5:52", "The golden thread SYS to CONCLUSION, read forwards and backwards"], ["8:33", "Categories: ITGC domains, ITAC, IPE, ITDM; why ITGCs protect ITACs"], ["11:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 2: W01 — Landscape & System Inventory (14 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 2: W01 — Landscape & System Inventory (14 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Build W01 from a landscape diagram and validate it with system evidence."], ["Visuals and demo", "Screen recording of W01; SAP screenshots (System > Status, SCC4); STMS route diagram"], ["Key takeaway", "Validated needs evidence; every other row needs an open question."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 1, Session 2; W01)"], ["0:30", "Why diagrams are not enough: Documented only vs Validated"], ["3:41", "Fields and dropdowns walk-through"], ["6:52", "Demo: ABC rows SYS-01 to SYS-06; System > Status, SCC4, SM51 evidence"], ["10:03", "Transport routes and the QAS bypass check; Checks tab"], ["13:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 3: W02 — Interface & Data Flow Inventory (12 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 3: W02 — Interface & Data Flow Inventory (12 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Identify financially relevant interfaces and the risks hidden in unmonitored ones."], ["Visuals and demo", "Data-flow map of ABC; W02 demo; RFC user table with a SAP_ALL flag"], ["Key takeaway", "The honest answer \"no monitoring\" is the most valuable cell in the register."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 1, Session 3; W02)"], ["0:30", "Data enters SAP without users: bank files, EDI, payroll"], ["3:11", "Ask by process, not technology; monitoring and reconciliation answers"], ["5:52", "Demo: INT-05 payment file, RFC and service users"], ["8:33", "Risk flag formula and Data_Flow_Map"], ["11:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 4: W03 — Module & Business Process Scope (13 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 4: W03 — Module & Business Process Scope (13 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Separate module, process and control; create BP-IDs including technical areas."], ["Visuals and demo", "Module vs process diagram; FSLI to process map; W03 demo"], ["Key takeaway", "Technical areas get BP-TECH IDs so ITGC risks follow the same thread as business risks."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 1, Session 4; W03)"], ["0:30", "Modules are not processes: P2P crosses MM and FI"], ["3:26", "From significant accounts to processes to sub-processes"], ["6:22", "Demo: Process_Scope, Technical_Areas, FSLI_Map, BP_Index"], ["9:18", "Outstanding vs guessed: the HR example"], ["12:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 5: W04 — Risk Register (13 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 5: W04 — Risk Register (13 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Write testable risk statements and agree risk owners."], ["Visuals and demo", "Formula banner \"There is a risk that … because … resulting in …\"; W04 demo; before/after risk statements"], ["Key takeaway", "A risk that cannot be tested is not a risk statement."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 1, Session 5; W04)"], ["0:30", "Risk versus finding: what goes wrong, not what has gone wrong"], ["3:26", "The risk statement formula; risk owner versus control owner"], ["6:22", "Demo: R-P2P-001 and R-TECH-003; rating and band; Coverage_By_BP"], ["9:18", "Common errors: generic risks, findings disguised as risks"], ["12:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 6: W05 — Risk & Control Matrix (16 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 6: W05 — Risk & Control Matrix (16 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Define controls with five-part descriptions, classify them and record ITGC dependencies."], ["Visuals and demo", "RCM demo; five-part description highlight; dependency diagram"], ["Key takeaway", "The RCM is the centre: if it is not in the RCM, it is not tested."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 1, Session 6; W05)"], ["0:30", "Control area versus control; why each row is one control"], ["4:11", "Five-part description: who, what, when, how, evidence"], ["7:52", "Many-to-many Risk_Control_Map; Coverage_Check for design gaps"], ["11:33", "ITGC_Dependency: why C-ITAC-004 depends on three ITGCs"], ["15:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "3.2 Part 2 — Evidence, Test and Conclusion Workbooks", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3.2 Part 2 — Evidence, Test and Conclusion Workbooks"}]}, {"title": "Session 7: W06 — SoD & Sensitive Access Rule Matrix (12 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 7: W06 — SoD & Sensitive Access Rule Matrix (12 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Translate SoD risks into functions, permissions and rules."], ["Visuals and demo", "Function pair diagram (vendor maintenance vs payment run); W06 demo"], ["Key takeaway", "A rule with no risk is noise; a risk with no rule is untestable."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 2, Session 7; W06)"], ["0:30", "From a risk statement to transactions and auth objects"], ["3:11", "Functions, permissions, SoD_Rules, Sensitive_Access"], ["5:52", "Mitigations must be real controls; ruleset approval"], ["8:33", "Custom Z-transactions: the common gap"], ["11:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 8: W07 — Control & Evidence Owner Matrix (8 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 8: W07 — Control & Evidence Owner Matrix (8 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Capture owners, performers and evidence owners separately."], ["Visuals and demo", "W07 demo; RACI-style graphic"], ["Key takeaway", "Ask two questions: who makes sure this happens, and who can give us the evidence?"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 2, Session 8; W07)"], ["0:30", "Three different people: owner, performer, evidence owner"], ["2:45", "Lookups from W05 and W04; confirming owners"], ["5:00", "Outstanding is a dependency, not a failure"], ["7:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 9: W08 — PBC / Evidence Request Register (10 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 9: W08 — PBC / Evidence Request Register (10 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Raise precise, traceable evidence requests in waves."], ["Visuals and demo", "Good versus bad request side by side; W08 demo"], ["Key takeaway", "If a request cannot be traced to a control and a test, do not make it."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 2, Session 9; W08)"], ["0:30", "Population versus sample requests; four waves"], ["2:41", "Writing a precise request: system, client, period, fields, format"], ["4:52", "Demo: PBC-018 STMS import history; overdue tracking"], ["7:03", "Every request traces to a control and a test"], ["9:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 10: W09 — Evidence Index & IPE Log (11 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 10: W09 — Evidence Index & IPE Log (11 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Index evidence and perform completeness and accuracy checks before sampling."], ["Visuals and demo", "IPE flow diagram; W09 demo; parameter screenshot example"], ["Key takeaway", "No sample from a population that has not passed IPE."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 2, Session 10; W09)"], ["0:30", "The filing system: EV-ID, filename convention, original preserved"], ["2:56", "IPE: completeness and accuracy; reconcile to an independent source"], ["5:22", "Demo: IPE-001 and IPE-002; HOLD status"], ["7:48", "What happens when IPE fails"], ["10:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 11: W10 — Test Plan / Testing Program (13 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 11: W10 — Test Plan / Testing Program (13 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Create TOD and TOE rows with attributes, samples and a sensible schedule."], ["Visuals and demo", "TOD vs TOE table; attribute derivation animation; W10 demo"], ["Key takeaway", "Test attributes come straight from the five-part control description."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 2, Session 11; W10)"], ["0:30", "Design versus operating effectiveness"], ["3:26", "Deriving attributes from the control description"], ["6:22", "Sample_Guide and justifying deviations"], ["9:18", "Schedule ITGCs first; the scheduling check"], ["12:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 12: W11 — Test Workpapers (10 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 12: W11 — Test Workpapers (10 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Complete a workpaper that a reviewer can reperform."], ["Visuals and demo", "Workpaper tab walk-through; template list"], ["Key takeaway", "A good workpaper lets a stranger reach the same conclusion."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 2, Session 12; W11)"], ["0:30", "Standard structure: header to conclusion"], ["2:41", "The attribute grid and EV-ID references"], ["4:52", "Demo: transport test with one exception"], ["7:03", "The template list and WP_Register"], ["9:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 13: W12 — Sample Selection Log (9 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 13: W12 — Sample Selection Log (9 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Select samples that are unbiased, covered across the period and reperformable."], ["Visuals and demo", "Seeded random demo in Excel; quarter coverage bars; W12 demo"], ["Key takeaway", "Record the seed so the selection can be reperformed."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 2, Session 13; W12)"], ["0:30", "Random seeded versus targeted selection"], ["3:05", "Items tab, quarter coverage, wave 3 requests"], ["5:40", "Demo: SMP-003 with 25 random and 2 targeted items"], ["8:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 14: W13 — Deficiency & Remediation Log (10 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 14: W13 — Deficiency & Remediation Log (10 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Turn exceptions into evaluated, grouped deficiencies with remediation and retest."], ["Visuals and demo", "Four-part statement graphic; Groups tab demo"], ["Key takeaway", "A deficiency closes only after a successful retest."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 2, Session 14; W13)"], ["0:30", "Exception versus deficiency; validating first"], ["2:41", "Condition, criteria, cause, effect"], ["4:52", "Groups and compensating controls"], ["7:03", "Remediation, retest, closure rule"], ["9:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 15: W14 — Scoping Workpaper & Coverage Dashboard (10 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 15: W14 — Scoping Workpaper & Coverage Dashboard (10 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Use W14 as the scoping record and the final coverage dashboard."], ["Visuals and demo", "W14 demo with Stage toggled; coverage matrix"], ["Key takeaway", "No figure on the dashboard is typed; all come from the workbooks."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 2, Session 15; W14)"], ["0:30", "Known, unconfirmed, outstanding"], ["2:41", "Dependencies versus failures"], ["4:52", "Stage switch: Scoping and Testing"], ["7:03", "Coverage matrix and the seven checks"], ["9:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 16: The Population Sequence and Checkpoints (12 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 16: The Population Sequence and Checkpoints (12 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Order the workbooks and pass the nine gates."], ["Visuals and demo", "Gate roadmap graphic; sequence table"], ["Key takeaway", "Never patch a downstream workbook to cover an upstream gap."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 2, Session 16; Deployment Guide §6)"], ["0:30", "Why order matters"], ["3:11", "Nine stages and gates G1 to G9"], ["5:52", "Scoping deliverable versus testing deliverable"], ["8:33", "Iteration without breaking keys"], ["11:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "3.3 Part 3A — Testing Access Controls", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3.3 Part 3A — Testing Access Controls"}]}, {"title": "Session 17: How Part 3 Uses the Foundation (9 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 17: How Part 3 Uses the Foundation (9 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Apply the universal seven-step test cycle."], ["Visuals and demo", "Seven-step cycle graphic"], ["Key takeaway", "Most access failures are found in the population, not the sample."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3A, Session 17)"], ["0:30", "From workbook rows to tests"], ["3:05", "Seven moves: read, walk through, secure population, sample, request, test, conclude"], ["5:40", "Access controls in scope; population before sample"], ["8:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 18: WP-AC-01 User Provisioning (13 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 18: WP-AC-01 User Provisioning (13 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test approval before provisioning and find the bypass."], ["Visuals and demo", "GRC request screenshots; attribute grid demo"], ["Key takeaway", "Test the bypass, not just the workflow."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3A, Session 18)"], ["0:30", "What the control must achieve"], ["3:26", "Navigation: GRC ARM, RSUSR100N, AGR_USERS, USR02"], ["6:22", "Population analysis: direct SU01 assignments"], ["9:18", "Attribute grid and the six population exceptions"], ["12:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 19: WP-AC-02 Termination of Leavers (12 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 19: WP-AC-02 Termination of Leavers (12 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test timely removal starting from the HR leavers population."], ["Visuals and demo", "HR to SAP matching diagram; grid demo"], ["Key takeaway", "A list of locked users can only show the control working."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3A, Session 19)"], ["0:30", "Start from HR, not from SAP"], ["3:11", "Matching leavers to USR02; lock dates from RSUSR100N"], ["5:52", "Business-day calculation; post-termination logon"], ["8:33", "Worked result: DEF-004"], ["11:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 20: WP-AC-03 Periodic User Access Review (13 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 20: WP-AC-03 Periodic User Access Review (13 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test the review listing, the review and the removals."], ["Visuals and demo", "UAR campaign screenshots; reconciliation demo"], ["Key takeaway", "A review on an incomplete list fails even if every reviewer did their job."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3A, Session 20)"], ["0:30", "The list is part of the control: reconcile the listing"], ["3:26", "Authorised reviewers, timing, rubber-stamping"], ["6:22", "Removals traced to RSUSR100N"], ["9:18", "Cross-check to leaver exceptions"], ["12:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 21: WP-AC-04 Privileged, Generic and Default IDs (13 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 21: WP-AC-04 Privileged, Generic and Default IDs (13 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Find privileged access and link results to ITAC reliance."], ["Visuals and demo", "RSUSR002 / RSUSR003 screenshots; reliance arrow graphic"], ["Key takeaway", "One privileged user can undo the audit."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3A, Session 21)"], ["0:30", "SAP_ALL, SAP_NEW, user types, default IDs"], ["3:26", "Navigation: RSUSR002, RSUSR003, RSPARAM, HANA users"], ["6:22", "Worked result: FI_SUPPORT1 and TRAINING01"], ["9:18", "Reliance link to Part 3B"], ["12:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 22: WP-AC-05 Emergency Access (Firefighter) (13 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 22: WP-AC-05 Emergency Access (Firefighter) (13 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test Firefighter sessions, approvals and reviews."], ["Visuals and demo", "EAM log screenshots; session grid"], ["Key takeaway", "Review the activity, not the signature."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3A, Session 22)"], ["0:30", "What makes Firefighter access safe"], ["3:26", "GRC EAM logs; SM20 comparison for outside-EAM use"], ["6:22", "Attributes A to E; activity versus reason"], ["9:18", "Worked result: vendor bank change"], ["12:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 23: WP-AC-06 Segregation of Duties and Sensitive Access (14 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 23: WP-AC-06 Segregation of Duties and Sensitive Access (14 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Rerun the SoD rules independently and test mitigations."], ["Visuals and demo", "ARA screenshots; independent rerun demo"], ["Key takeaway", "The independent rerun gives the result its authority."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3A, Session 23)"], ["0:30", "Rerun, do not receive: ARA is IPE"], ["3:41", "Permission-level analysis; false positives"], ["6:52", "Mitigations: real, valid and tested"], ["10:03", "Worked result: SOD-001 and SOD-003"], ["13:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 24: WP-AC-07 Password and Logon Parameters (9 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 24: WP-AC-07 Password and Logon Parameters (9 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test configuration on every application server."], ["Visuals and demo", "RSPARAM screenshot; server comparison table"], ["Key takeaway", "Configuration is tested once, but everywhere."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3A, Session 24)"], ["0:30", "Parameters and policy mapping"], ["2:26", "Every server: RSPARAM, SM51"], ["4:22", "SSO caveat"], ["6:18", "Worked result: fails_to_user_lock"], ["8:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "3.4 Part 3B — Change, Operations, ITAC, Reliance, IPE", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3.4 Part 3B — Change, Operations, ITAC, Reliance, IPE"}]}, {"title": "Session 25: Where Part 3B Picks Up (7 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 25: Where Part 3B Picks Up (7 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Carry Part 3A results into change, operations and ITAC testing."], ["Visuals and demo", "Session roadmap; reliance question graphic"], ["Key takeaway", "Read every change result with one question: could the configuration have moved unnoticed?"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3B, Session 25)"], ["0:30", "DEF-007 and DEF-009 as reliance risks"], ["2:25", "Map of Part 3B sessions"], ["4:20", "Keys added at source"], ["6:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 26: WP-PC-01 Transport Management (14 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 26: WP-PC-01 Transport Management (14 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test transports starting from production."], ["Visuals and demo", "STMS and TPALOG screenshots; grid demo"], ["Key takeaway", "The change population comes from what production received."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3B, Session 26)"], ["0:30", "Start from production: TPALOG, not tickets"], ["3:41", "Four population analyses before sampling"], ["6:52", "Attributes A to E; ITAC-table transports always selected"], ["10:03", "Worked result: DEF-011"], ["13:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 27: WP-PC-02 System and Client Change Settings (11 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 27: WP-PC-02 System and Client Change Settings (11 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test SE06, SCC4, logging and every opening."], ["Visuals and demo", "SE06 / SCC4 screenshots; timeline of the 14 March opening"], ["Key takeaway", "Table logging is what lets you prove configuration did not change."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3B, Session 27)"], ["0:30", "Why an open system defeats transport control"], ["2:56", "Navigation: SE06, SCC4, rec/client, DBTABLOG"], ["5:22", "Every opening tested; worked result DEF-012"], ["7:48", "Link to DEF-006"], ["10:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 28: WP-PC-03 Direct Changes and Debugging (9 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 28: WP-PC-03 Direct Changes and Debugging (9 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test debug-change, table edits and the audit log."], ["Visuals and demo", "Audit log screenshots; rights vs events table"], ["Key takeaway", "One finding, many tests: record once, reference everywhere."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3B, Session 28)"], ["0:30", "Rights versus use"], ["3:05", "Navigation: RSUSR002, RSAU_CONFIG, RSAU_READ_LOG, SE16N log"], ["5:40", "Matching events to Firefighter sessions"], ["8:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 29: WP-CO-01 Batch Job Monitoring (10 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 29: WP-CO-01 Batch Job Monitoring (10 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test critical job monitoring and step users."], ["Visuals and demo", "SM37 screenshot; job step-user table"], ["Key takeaway", "A job has every authorisation of its step user."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3B, Session 29)"], ["0:30", "Critical job list; SM37 and TBTCO"], ["3:25", "Failures and reruns"], ["6:20", "Step users: jobs carry their user's access"], ["9:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 30: WP-CO-02 Interface Monitoring and Reconciliation (12 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 30: WP-CO-02 Interface Monitoring and Reconciliation (12 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test monitoring, reconciliation and payment-file protection."], ["Visuals and demo", "Interface flow with controls; AL11 / OS permission example"], ["Key takeaway", "The interface inventory is what turned a directory permission into a tested control."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3B, Session 30)"], ["0:30", "Interface monitoring tools"], ["3:11", "Reconciliation: counts and totals"], ["5:52", "Payment directory write access"], ["8:33", "Aged IDoc errors"], ["11:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 31: WP-CO-03 Backup and Recovery (7 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 31: WP-CO-03 Backup and Recovery (7 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test backups and rely on a SOC 1 properly."], ["Visuals and demo", "Backup catalog screenshot; SOC 1 checklist"], ["Key takeaway", "Hosting changes who operates the control, not who is accountable."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3B, Session 31)"], ["0:30", "Backup logs and restore test"], ["3:22", "SOC 1 review: period, exceptions, CUECs"], ["6:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 32: WP-ITAC-01 Configuration Controls (13 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 32: WP-ITAC-01 Configuration Controls (13 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test ITACs and prove no unnoticed change."], ["Visuals and demo", "OMR6 / OB52 screenshots; test invoice block demo"], ["Key takeaway", "One test shows it works today; logs and ITGCs show it worked all year."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3B, Session 32)"], ["0:30", "Test of one plus proof of no change"], ["3:26", "OMR6 and OB52 configuration; reperformance"], ["6:22", "Override access: MRBR and period changes"], ["9:18", "QAS reperformance only if QAS equals PRD"], ["12:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 33: The ITGC Reliance Assessment (14 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 33: The ITGC Reliance Assessment (14 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Decide reliance for each dependent control."], ["Visuals and demo", "Decision tree; reliance tab in W05 and W14"], ["Key takeaway", "Deficient ITGCs remove the presumption of reliance; evidence can restore it."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3B, Session 33)"], ["0:30", "ITGC results summary"], ["3:41", "Three decision questions"], ["6:52", "Worked assessment: C-ITAC-004 and C-ITAC-005"], ["10:03", "Controls where reliance is not supported"], ["13:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 34: WP-IPE-01 Key Reports (10 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 34: WP-IPE-01 Key Reports (10 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Test completeness and accuracy of reports used in controls."], ["Visuals and demo", "Report logic example; IPE chain graphic"], ["Key takeaway", "IPE connects everything: one report error changed an access conclusion."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 3B, Session 34)"], ["0:30", "IPE list from W05"], ["2:41", "Parameters, completeness, accuracy, custom logic"], ["4:52", "Worked result: ZMM_POGR_EXCEPT"], ["7:03", "Impact on a SoD mitigation"], ["9:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "3.5 Part 4 — Evaluate, Remediate, Report, Trace", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3.5 Part 4 — Evaluate, Remediate, Report, Trace"}]}, {"title": "Session 35: Evaluating Deficiencies (12 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 35: Evaluating Deficiencies (12 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Evaluate exceptions to a defensible severity."], ["Visuals and demo", "Eight-step flow; severity factor table"], ["Key takeaway", "Judge the group, not just the item."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 4, Session 35)"], ["0:30", "Eight-step path from exception to severity"], ["3:11", "Condition, criteria, cause, effect"], ["5:52", "Severity factors; evaluating ITGC deficiencies"], ["8:33", "Materiality for the running case"], ["11:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 36: Worked Evaluation — ABC Manufacturing (14 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 36: Worked Evaluation — ABC Manufacturing (14 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Evaluate and aggregate the full deficiency register."], ["Visuals and demo", "Deficiency register; group diagram; W13 Groups demo"], ["Key takeaway", "Follow-up work changes the answer; the evidence trail makes the evaluation."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 4, Session 36)"], ["0:30", "The register and follow-up procedures"], ["3:41", "Aggregation into groups A to D"], ["6:52", "Severity conclusions"], ["10:03", "How follow-up changed Group A"], ["13:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 37: Remediation and Retesting (9 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 37: Remediation and Retesting (9 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Plan remediation and retest properly."], ["Visuals and demo", "Remediation plan table; retest timeline"], ["Key takeaway", "A fix is not a close."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 4, Session 37)"], ["0:30", "Fix the cause, not the instance"], ["4:22", "Minimum operating period; retest workflow"], ["8:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 38: Reporting the Results (10 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 38: Reporting the Results (10 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Present results and write a clear finding."], ["Visuals and demo", "Report outline; finding example with highlighted parts"], ["Key takeaway", "State facts with numbers and dates, cite the control as criteria, say how impact was assessed."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 4, Session 38)"], ["0:30", "W14 at completion; report structure"], ["3:25", "Writing a finding: Group A example"], ["6:20", "What not to put in a report"], ["9:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 39: The End-to-End Trace Exercise (15 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 39: The End-to-End Trace Exercise (15 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Trace any result from server to conclusion and back."], ["Visuals and demo", "Animated trace paths on the key register; exercise sheet"], ["Key takeaway", "If any link breaks, the foundation is not complete."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 4, Session 39)"], ["0:30", "Trace 1 forward, Trace 2 backward, Trace 3 ITGC to ITAC"], ["5:05", "Exercise briefing"], ["9:40", "Instructor answer guide (separate)"], ["14:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "Session 40: Course Summary (6 min)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 40: Course Summary (6 min)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Detail"], ["Objective", "Recap the map and the ten principles."], ["Visuals and demo", "Full golden-thread graphic; ten principles slide"], ["Key takeaway", "You can now build the foundation, and defend every test."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Run of show"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Time", "Segment"], ["0:00", "Opening: thread bar, objective, reference (Part 4, Session 40)"], ["0:30", "The complete map"], ["2:05", "Ten principles"], ["3:40", "What a trainee can now do"], ["5:15", "Recap: takeaway on screen, Checks tab status, link to next session"]]}]}, {"title": "4. Production Checklist", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "4. Production Checklist"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Step", "Done"], ["Script written from the session in the manual (use the objective, beats and takeaway above)", ""], ["Slides built on the TIB master with the thread bar", ""], ["Workbook demo rehearsed with the ABC example rows intact", ""], ["SAP screenshots checked: ABC training system only; nothing confidential", ""], ["Recorded at 1080p; cursor highlighted; zoom at 125%", ""], ["Captions reviewed; key terms spelled consistently (ITGC, ITAC, IPE, TOD, TOE, PBC)", ""], ["Video filed as S##_Short_Title with script and slides", ""], ["Linked from the matching manual session and the course page", ""]]}]}], "word_count": 856, "id": "tib-sap-controls-foundation-video-outline", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Video_Outline.docx", "filename": "TIB_SAP_Controls_Foundation_Video_Outline.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Using the Foundation Workbooks"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A Hands-On Prerequisite Course for the SAP Controls Project Workbook Set (W01–W14)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Training Manual  |  Part 1 of 3: Course Overview and Sessions 1–5 (Excel Skills, Workbook Anatomy and Reading the Checks)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Worked examples come from the completed Corvane Energy Group case file.  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "About This Course", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "About This Course"}]}, {"title": "Purpose", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The five-part manual \"Building the SAP Controls Project Foundation\" teaches the method of a controls project. It assumes you can already work comfortably in the 14 Excel workbooks that carry that method. This course gets you there. It teaches how the workbooks are built, how to enter and read data in them, how they check themselves, and how they connect to each other. It is a prerequisite: finish it before you start Session 1 of the manual."}]}, {"title": "Who it is for", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Who it is for"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "New consultants, analysts and testers joining a controls team, and new controls leads who want to understand the tools their team uses. You need only basic Excel: opening files, moving between tabs, typing in cells, and saving."}]}, {"title": "What you will be able to do", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "What you will be able to do"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Read any register in the set as a list of records, find a record, and filter to a group of records without damaging the table."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Enter data correctly: use dropdowns, type keys exactly, enter dates and numbers properly, and paste values without breaking formulas."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Read the formulas you meet (IF, COUNTIF, SUMPRODUCT, INDEX with MATCH) well enough to explain what a grey cell is doing."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Recognise the five kinds of tab and know which ones you type in."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Read the Checks tab and every row check, and know what to do about each message."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Use each of the 14 workbooks, and follow a key from one workbook to the next (Parts 2 and 3)."}]}, {"title": "How the course is organised", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "How the course is organised"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Part", "Sessions", "Topic", "Manual part"], ["A", "1–3", "Excel skills for controls work: reading registers, entering data, reading formulas", "This part"], ["B", "4–5", "Anatomy of a workbook and reading the Checks", "This part"], ["C", "6–10", "Using each workbook, W01 to W14, in five groups", "Part 2"], ["D", "11–12", "Connecting the workbooks and the trace exercise; final assessment", "Part 3"]]}]}, {"title": "How each session works", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "How each session works"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Element", "What it is"], ["Learning outcomes", "Three things you will be able to do"], ["Concept and example", "A short explanation followed by a real row, formula or message from the Corvane files"], ["TRY IT", "A hands-on exercise in the Corvane workbooks. Work in a copy, never in the original"], ["CHECK YOURSELF", "Three questions to test understanding"], ["Answer key", "At the end of each part. Try before you look"]]}]}, {"title": "Files you need", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Files you need"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Open the Corvane case file folder (Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project). The workbooks are in folders 01 to 05. Before you begin, copy the whole folder to a practice location. Every exercise assumes you are working on your own copy."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Session", "Open this file"], ["1", "02_Risk_and_Control_Design / W04_Risk_Register.xlsx; 01_Environment / W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx"], ["2", "W04_Risk_Register.xlsx; 01_Environment / W03_Module_Business_Process_Scope.xlsx"], ["3", "W04_Risk_Register.xlsx; W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx; W07_Control_Evidence_Owner_Matrix.xlsx; W01"], ["4", "W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx (and the tab lists in Appendix B)"], ["5", "W09_Evidence_Index_IPE_Log.xlsx; the blank W04 template in 06_Reference_Manuals / Blank_Workbook_Templates"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["A NOTE ON THE EXAMPLE COMPANY Corvane Energy Group is a fictitious oil and gas company. Every name, key and number is invented but consistent across the files, so you can follow one item from the landscape to the conclusion."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 1 — Reading a Register", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 1 — Reading a Register"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Identify the title, header row, key column and data rows of any register. Use filters and frozen panes to find records safely. Read one row as a complete record in words."]]}]}, {"title": "1.1 What a register is", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.1 What a register is"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every workbook in the set is built around one or more registers. A register is a table in which each row is one thing (a risk, a control, a test, a deficiency) and each column is one fact about it (its owner, its category, its status). The first column is almost always the key: a unique code that identifies the row, such as R-P2P-006 for a risk or DEF-007 for a deficiency. Other workbooks refer to the row by that key, never by its description."}]}, {"title": "1.2 The layout is the same everywhere", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.2 The layout is the same everywhere"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Row", "What is in it"], ["1", "The title of the register"], ["2", "A one-line note (in the completed Corvane files: how many records it holds)"], ["3", "Empty (the Checks tab uses it for the overall status)"], ["4", "The header row with the column names. Filter buttons sit here"], ["5 onward", "The data. One record per row. Registers hold about 200 rows (W12 Items holds 1,000)"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The key column and the header row stay on screen when you scroll, because the panes are frozen at row 5 and column B. You can always see which record and which column you are in."}]}, {"title": "1.3 Colours tell you what to do", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.3 Colours tell you what to do"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["What you see", "What it means", "What you do"], ["Yellow cell with blue text", "An input cell", "Type or select here"], ["Grey cell with black text", "A formula", "Never type here; copy the formula down if damaged"], ["Navy header", "An input column", "Fill it in"], ["Slate-grey header", "A formula column", "Read it only"]]}]}, {"title": "1.4 Filtering safely", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.4 Filtering safely"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Click the filter button on any header to show only some rows. Choose a value or tick several. A filtered table still contains all its rows; the hidden ones are simply out of view. Click the button again and choose Clear Filter to bring them back."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — NEVER SORT PART OF A TABLE If you select one column and sort it, only that column moves. The rest stay behind and every row now describes the wrong record. Sort only from a header filter button (Sort A to Z), which moves whole rows together, and never select part of the table first."]]}]}, {"title": "1.5 Reading a row as a record", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "1.5 Reading a row as a record"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A row is a sentence broken into cells. Read it left to right. Here is risk R-P2P-006 from W04 in the Corvane file:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Column", "Value in the row", "Read as"], ["RISK-ID", "R-P2P-006", "The key of this risk"], ["BP-ID", "BP-P2P-03", "It belongs to the vendor payment process"], ["SYS-ID / INT-ID", "INT-02", "The interface involved is the payment file to the banks"], ["Risk category", "Interface", "The type of risk"], ["Risk statement", "There is a risk that the payment file is altered between creation and bank transmission because file directory permissions and file integrity are not controlled, resulting in payments to unauthorised accounts.", "What could go wrong, why, and the effect"], ["Risk owner", "Treasury Manager", "The person accountable for the outcome"], ["Likelihood × impact", "2 × 3 = 6, band High", "How significant it is before controls"], ["SOX relevant?", "Y", "It could lead to a material misstatement"], ["Risk source / Status", "Walkthrough; Agreed with owner", "Where it came from and whether the owner has accepted it"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 1 Open a copy of W04_Risk_Register.xlsx and go to the Risk_Register tab. A.  How many risks are in the register?  B.  How many are SOX relevant?  C.  Filter the Risk category column to Interface. How many risks remain?  D.  Clear the filter, then filter Rating band to High and SOX relevant to Y. How many remain?  E.  How many risks have an inherent rating of 9? Now open W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx, System_Inventory tab.  F.  How many systems have In scope? = Y?  G.  Which two systems are not in scope, and where does the register say why?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What is the difference between a register and a workbook?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why must you never select a single column and sort it?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. A cell is grey with black text. May you type in it?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS A register is a table with one record per row and a key in the first column. Yellow and blue means type; grey means formula. Filter from the header buttons; never sort part of a table."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 2 — Entering Data and Pasting Values", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 2 — Entering Data and Pasting Values"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Use dropdowns and understand the two kinds of validation. Enter keys, dates and numbers so the checks accept them. Paste values only into Ref_ tabs, from the correct source cells."]]}]}, {"title": "2.1 Dropdowns", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.1 Dropdowns"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Many input cells carry a dropdown. Click the cell and a small arrow appears. The list comes from the hidden Lists tab or from another tab in the workbook. Dropdowns exist so that everyone spells a value the same way; the checks and counts depend on exact matches."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "There are two kinds, and the difference matters:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Kind", "What happens if you type something not on the list", "Where used", "Example in W04"], ["Stop", "Excel refuses it and shows \"Pick a value from the list\"", "Fixed lists of allowed values", "Risk category, Status, Likelihood, Impact"], ["Warning", "Excel warns, but lets you continue", "Keys that must exist in another workbook", "BP-ID (checked against Ref_BP)"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A warning is not permission. The row check beside the record tells you if the key is not found."}]}, {"title": "2.2 Keys, text, dates and numbers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.2 Keys, text, dates and numbers"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Keys: type them exactly as they appear in the source workbook, with the same capital letters, hyphens and numbers. R-P2P-006 and r-p2p-006 may look the same to you but a stray space or a missing hyphen breaks a lookup. Never renumber a key; if a row leaves scope, mark it out of scope and leave the key."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Dates: type a real date (for example 31-May-2026 or 2026-05-31) so Excel right-aligns it. A date typed as text, left-aligned, will not compare correctly in formulas such as days overdue."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Numbers: type a number without units or words. 25 is a number; \"25 items\" is text and a sample-size check will not read it."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Y and N: use the dropdown values exactly: Y, N (and TBC where offered)."}]}, {"title": "2.3 Paste values only", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.3 Paste values only"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Workbooks do not link to each other. Instead, the keys you need from an upstream workbook are pasted into a Ref_ tab in the downstream workbook. You must paste values only:"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Copy the cells from the source workbook."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Click the first cell of the target (A2 on a Ref_ tab; the headers are in row 1)."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Use Paste Special and choose Values (Ctrl+Alt+V, then V, then Enter)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Why values? A normal paste would carry formulas that point to the source file and break the moment the file moves, or it would bring formatting and validation that overwrite the target. A value is just the key, which is all the Ref_ tab needs."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — COPY ONLY THE ROWS THAT HAVE KEYS In W03, the BP_Index tab lists keys by formula. The 14 business keys sit in rows 5 to 18 and the four technical keys sit in rows 155 to 158, with blank formula cells between them. If you copy the whole column you also paste empty text into the Ref_ tab. Copy the two blocks separately and paste them one under the other."]]}]}, {"title": "2.4 Never overwrite a grey cell", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "2.4 Never overwrite a grey cell"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "If you type over a formula, the check or lookup in that cell stops working for that row. Repair it by copying the formula from the row above and pasting it into the damaged cell."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 2 Work in a copy of W04_Risk_Register.xlsx (do not save over the original). A.  In the Risk_Register tab, row 41 is the first empty row. In the Status column, type Maybe. What happens?  B.  In row 41 enter: RISK-ID R-TEST-001; BP-ID BP-XXX-99; Risk category SoD; Risk statement \"There is a risk that a test happens because of a cause, resulting in an effect.\" What does the BP-ID cell do when you press Enter? What does the Row check column show?  C.  Change the BP-ID to BP-P2P-01. What does the Row check show now? Now open W03_Module_Business_Process_Scope.xlsx.  D.  On BP_Index, copy A5:A18, go to W04 Ref_BP and paste values at A2. Copy A155:A158 and paste values at A16. How many BP-IDs does Ref_BP now hold? Open the Coverage_By_BP tab and note what appears in its first column."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What is the difference between a stop dropdown and a warning dropdown?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why do you paste values rather than ordinary cells into a Ref_ tab?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. You typed \"25 items\" in a sample size cell. Why is that a problem?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Dropdowns keep values consistent; stop refuses, warning only warns. Type keys exactly; use real dates and plain numbers. Paste values only, starting at A2, and copy only rows that hold keys."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 3 — The Formulas You Will Meet", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 3 — The Formulas You Will Meet"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Find and read a formula in a grey cell. Explain five functions: IF, COUNTIF, SUMPRODUCT, INDEX with MATCH, and IFERROR. Read a row check from its first condition to its last."]]}]}, {"title": "3.1 How to see a formula", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.1 How to see a formula"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Click a grey cell and read the formula bar above the sheet. Press F2 to edit in the cell and Esc to leave without changing anything. You do not need to write these formulas, only to read them well enough to explain what the cell is telling you."}]}, {"title": "3.2 Five functions in plain language", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.2 Five functions in plain language"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "IF: choose between answers"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "IF(test, answer if true, answer if false). In W04 the Rating band column turns the number in Inherent rating into a word:"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "=IF(J5=\"\",\"\",IF(J5>=6,\"High\",IF(J5>=3,\"Medium\",\"Low\")))"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Read it: if the rating is empty, show nothing; otherwise if it is 6 or more show High; otherwise if it is 3 or more show Medium; otherwise show Low. For R-TECH-008 the rating is 3, so the band is Medium."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The rating itself is a multiplication wrapped in an IF that waits until both inputs exist:"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "=IF(OR(H5=\"\",I5=\"\"),\"\",H5*I5)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "COUNTIF: count matching rows"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "COUNTIF(range, value) counts how many cells in a range equal a value. In W05 the Risks mapped column counts how many risk links point to this control:"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "=IF(A5=\"\",\"\",COUNTIF(Risk_Control_Map!$C$5:$C$204,A5))"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Read it: look in column C of the Risk_Control_Map tab, rows 5 to 204, and count the cells equal to this control's key. A result of 0 means the control addresses no risk, which the row check flags."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "INDEX with MATCH: look something up"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "MATCH finds which row holds a key; INDEX returns the value from another column of that row. W07 pulls the control title from its Ref_CTRL tab:"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "=IFERROR(INDEX(Ref_CTRL!$B$2:$B$201,MATCH(A5,Ref_CTRL!$A$2:$A$201,0)),\"\")"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Read it: find the row in Ref_CTRL column A where the key equals A5; return column B of that same row; and if nothing is found, show nothing (that is what IFERROR does). If a lookup cell is blank, check that the key exists in the Ref_ tab and was pasted as a value."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "SUMPRODUCT: count with several conditions"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The duplicate check on every Checks tab uses SUMPRODUCT to count rows whose key appears more than once:"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "=SUMPRODUCT((Risk_Register!A5:A204<>\"\")*(COUNTIF(Risk_Register!A5:A204,Risk_Register!A5:A204)>1))"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Read it: for each row, ask whether the key is not empty and whether it appears more than once; multiply the two answers (1 means yes, 0 means no) and add them up. The result should be 0."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TODAY: a date that moves"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Some cells, such as Days overdue in W08, use TODAY(). They change every day, so the numbers you see tomorrow may differ from today."}]}, {"title": "3.3 Reading a row check", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.3 Reading a row check"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A Row check cell is a chain of IF tests. Excel works through them in order and shows the message of the first test that fails. Here is the order for the W01 System_Inventory row check:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Order", "Condition tested", "Message shown if it fails"], ["1", "SID, client and environment are all filled", "SID, client, environment needed"], ["2", "If In scope is N, a rationale is given", "Rationale needed (not in scope)"], ["3", "If Validated, an evidence reference is given", "Evidence ref needed"], ["4", "If not Validated, an open question is given", "Open question needed"], ["5", "A financial PRD system is in scope", "PRD financial system should be in scope"], ["6", "All of the above passed", "OK"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Because the first failing test wins, fix the messages in the order they appear. Fixing the first often changes the message to the next."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 3 Open the Corvane workbooks and click the cells named. A.  In W04 Risk_Register, click J5 and K5. Read each formula. If likelihood is 1 and impact is 3, what are the rating and the band?  B.  In W05 Control_Register, click S5 and say in one sentence what it counts.  C.  In W07 Owner_Matrix, click B5 and say what it does when the key in A5 is not found in Ref_CTRL.  D.  In W01 System_Inventory row 7 (SYS-03), the row check reads OK. Which values in that row make it pass the fifth test?  E.  In a copy of W01, clear the Evidence ref in row 7. What does the row check now show?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What does IFERROR add to a lookup?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. In which order does a row check test its conditions, and why does it matter?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. A formula uses TODAY(). What does that tell you about its result?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Read a formula from the inside out; you need to read, not write. COUNTIF counts, INDEX with MATCH looks up, SUMPRODUCT counts with several conditions. A row check shows the first failing message; fix them in order."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 4 — Anatomy of a Workbook", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 4 — Anatomy of a Workbook"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Name the kinds of tab in the workbooks and say which you type in. Use the Instructions tab and its column dictionary. Find a hidden tab and know when to open it."]]}]}, {"title": "4.1 The kinds of tab", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.1 The kinds of tab"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab type", "Examples", "You type in it?", "Purpose"], ["Instructions", "Instructions", "No", "Purpose, how to use, colour legend, completion gate and the column dictionary"], ["Register tabs", "Risk_Register, Control_Register, Test_Plan", "Yes", "The records; yellow input cells and grey formula cells"], ["Reference tabs (Ref_)", "Ref_BP, Ref_CTRL, Ref_TEST", "Paste values only", "Keys pasted from the upstream workbook for lookups and dropdowns"], ["Helper tabs", "Coverage_By_BP, BP_Index, Coverage_Check, Pod activity", "No", "Formulas that summarise or combine records"], ["Editable lists", "Control_Areas, Sample_Guide, Templates", "Yes, deliberately", "Lists the engagement may edit"], ["Lists (hidden)", "Lists", "Only to edit dropdown values", "The values behind the dropdowns"], ["Checks", "Checks", "No", "The completion gate as formulas"]]}]}, {"title": "4.2 A tour of W04", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.2 A tour of W04"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The Risk Register has six tabs. Walk through them in order:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Type", "What you do there"], ["Instructions", "Instructions", "Read the purpose, steps and column dictionary once"], ["Risk_Register", "Register", "Enter and read the 36 risks"], ["Coverage_By_BP", "Helper", "See whether each process has at least one risk; read only"], ["Ref_BP", "Reference", "Paste the BP-IDs from W03; this feeds the BP-ID dropdown and the row check"], ["Lists", "Hidden", "Holds the values for Risk category, Status, Likelihood and so on"], ["Checks", "Checks", "Read the gate result before you say the workbook is ready"]]}]}, {"title": "4.3 The Instructions tab", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.3 The Instructions tab"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every workbook opens on its Instructions tab. It has the same sections each time: Purpose, How to use this workbook, Colour legend, Completion gate, Manual reference, and a Column dictionary. The dictionary lists every field of every register with its definition and its rule, and says whether the column is an input or a formula. Where a column takes a dropdown the rule says so, and often names the values; the full list of values is in the dropdown itself and on the hidden Lists tab. When you are unsure what a column wants, look it up there before you ask."}]}, {"title": "4.4 Hidden tabs", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "4.4 Hidden tabs"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The Lists tab is hidden so that it does not distract. To see it, right-click any tab, choose Unhide and pick Lists. Open it only to add or change a dropdown value. Keep each list in one unbroken column, with no gaps, because the dropdown reads a fixed range. Re-hide the tab when you finish."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — KNOW WHICH TABS YOU TYPE IN In every workbook, you type in the register tabs, you paste values into the Ref_ tabs, and you read everything else. If you are unsure, look for the yellow cells: where there are none, there is nothing to type."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 4 Open W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx. A.  List every visible tab.  B.  For each, say whether it is a register, a reference tab, a helper tab, an editable list or the Checks tab.  C.  On the Instructions tab, find the dictionary entry for the Control category field and read its definition and rule. Then click a cell in that column of Control_Register and open the dropdown. What values does it offer?  D.  Unhide the Lists tab. Which list holds the control categories? Re-hide it.  E.  Which tab would you look at to see whether every SOX-relevant risk has a key control?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Name three tab types and say whether you may type in each."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Where would you look to find what a column in a register is supposed to contain?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why must a dropdown list have no gaps?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Instructions, register, Ref_, helper, editable list, Lists, Checks: seven kinds of tab. Type in registers, paste values into Ref_ tabs, read the rest. The column dictionary is the answer to \"what does this column want?\"."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 5 — Reading the Checks", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 5 — Reading the Checks"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Tell a row check from the Checks tab and say what each is for. Read the five statuses and act on each. Explain why a fresh template can show REVIEW and a finished file shows ALL CHECKS PASS."]]}]}, {"title": "5.1 Two levels of checking", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.1 Two levels of checking"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Each workbook checks itself at two levels. The Row check (or Check) column beside each record tests that one record is complete and its keys exist. The Checks tab tests the whole workbook against its completion gate, for example that no key is duplicated, that every process has a risk, and that nothing is sampled from a population that failed its IPE check. Fix row checks first; most Checks tab lines depend on them."}]}, {"title": "5.2 The five statuses", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.2 The five statuses"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Status", "Meaning", "What you do"], ["PASS", "The count is zero where zero is required, or above zero where a minimum is required", "Nothing"], ["REVIEW", "A gate condition is not yet met", "Read the \"What to do if REVIEW\" column on the Checks tab, fix, and look again"], ["INFO", "A count shown for your information", "Judge whether the number matters at this stage"], ["HOLD", "Appears in row checks: the population has not passed IPE", "Do not sample; complete the check in W09"], ["SCOPING", "W14 only: a testing-stage check that does not yet apply", "Switch Stage to Testing when fieldwork starts"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The overall result sits in cell B3 of the Checks tab. It reads ALL CHECKS PASS or REVIEW ITEMS: n, where n is the number of REVIEW lines. Say a workbook is ready for its gate only when it reads ALL CHECKS PASS and you have read every INFO line."}]}, {"title": "5.3 Common row-check messages", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.3 Common row-check messages"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Message", "Workbook", "What it means", "What to do"], ["Evidence ref needed", "W01", "Row says Validated but gives no evidence ID", "Add the EV-ID or downgrade the status"], ["BP-ID not in W03", "W04", "The process key was not found in Ref_BP", "Fix the key, or add the process to W03 and re-paste"], ["Use the risk statement formula", "W04", "Statement lacks \"There is a risk that\"", "Rewrite as there is a risk that … because … resulting in …"], ["Dependent ITGCs needed", "W05", "An ITAC, report or review control lists no ITGC dependency", "Record the ITGCs it relies on"], ["EV-ID needed once received", "W08", "Status is Received but no evidence ID is recorded", "Index the evidence in W09 and enter the ID"], ["HOLD: IPE not Pass - do not sample", "W09, W12", "Population has not passed its completeness check", "Complete the IPE log; then sample"], ["Sample outside guide: justify", "W10", "Sample size is outside the Sample_Guide range", "Change it or record a reason"], ["Closed requires a retest TEST-ID", "W13", "A deficiency is Closed with no retest recorded", "Add the retest TEST-ID or reopen the deficiency"]]}]}, {"title": "5.4 A fresh template can show REVIEW; a finished file does not", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "5.4 A fresh template can show REVIEW; a finished file does not"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Open the blank W04 template and its Checks tab shows REVIEW on two lines: \"In-scope BP-IDs with no risk\" (the template lists the 12 sample processes but holds only one risk) and \"Technical-area risks recorded\" (none yet). That is correct: gate conditions are not met until real records are entered. Not every blank template shows REVIEW, because some hold example rows that already satisfy their checks; the point is that REVIEW means a gate condition is genuinely unmet. In the completed Corvane files every Checks tab reads ALL CHECKS PASS."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CORE INSIGHT — CHECKS MEASURE COMPLETENESS, NOT CORRECTNESS A row can read OK and still be wrong. The checks prove that a key exists, a field is filled and a rule is met. They cannot prove that the owner is the right owner or that a control description is true. A passing workbook is ready for review, not finished with thinking."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 5 Open W09_Evidence_Index_IPE_Log.xlsx (Corvane) and read its Checks tab. Then open the blank W04 in 06_Reference_Manuals / Blank_Workbook_Templates and read its Checks tab. A.  What does cell B3 say in each?  B.  Which lines show REVIEW in the blank W04?  C.  In the Corvane W09, how many evidence items are indexed, and how many populations have an IPE result that is not Pass? D.  Imagine this partial Checks tab. Say what you would do for each REVIEW line:"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Check", "Count", "Status"], ["Evidence items indexed", "109", "INFO"], ["Duplicate EV-IDs", "0", "PASS"], ["Evidence rows needing attention (incl. IPE HOLD)", "3", "REVIEW"], ["Populations whose IPE result is not Pass", "2", "REVIEW"], ["Populations with no IPE_Log row", "1", "REVIEW"], ["Originals not preserved", "0", "PASS"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What is the difference between a row check and the Checks tab?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What does HOLD mean and what must you not do while it shows?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why can a blank template show REVIEW?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Fix row checks first, then the Checks tab. PASS, REVIEW, INFO, HOLD and SCOPING each have one action. The checks prove completeness, not correctness."]]}]}, {"title": "Answer Key to Part 1", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Answer Key to Part 1"}]}, {"title": "Session 1", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 1"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["A", "36 risks"], ["B", "32 are SOX relevant (the four that are not: R-O2C-001, R-PM-001, R-PS-001, R-H2R-001)"], ["C", "8 risks (R-R2R-002, R-P2P-005, R-P2P-006, R-O2C-002, R-O2C-004, R-JVA-002, R-H2R-001, R-TRE-001)"], ["D", "23 risks; every High-band risk is SOX relevant"], ["E", "7 risks with rating 9 (R-P2P-001, R-P2P-002, R-O2C-002, R-TECH-001, R-TECH-002, R-TECH-004, R-TECH-005)"], ["F", "9 systems are in scope"], ["G", "SYS-10 (sandbox) and SYS-11 (training client); the reason is in the Scope rationale column"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) A register is one table of records; a workbook is a file that holds one or more registers plus the instructions, reference and checks tabs. (2) Only that column would move and every row would describe the wrong record. (3) No. Grey with black text means a formula."}]}, {"title": "Session 2", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 2"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["A", "Excel refuses the entry and shows \"Pick a value from the list\": Status is a stop-type dropdown"], ["B", "The BP-ID cell shows a warning that you may accept. The Row check reads \"BP-ID not in W03\""], ["C", "The Row check reads OK"], ["D", "Ref_BP holds 18 BP-IDs (14 business plus 4 technical). Coverage_By_BP lists those 18 keys in its first column, one per row"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) Stop refuses a value not on the list; warning allows it after a prompt, and the row check then flags the key if it is not found. (2) A normal paste brings formulas that point to the source file and formatting that overwrites the target. (3) It is text, not a number, so the sample-size check and any arithmetic cannot read it."}]}, {"title": "Session 3", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 3"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["A", "Rating = 1 × 3 = 3; band Medium"], ["B", "S5 counts how many rows in the Risk_Control_Map have this control as their CTRL-ID, that is, how many risks it is linked to"], ["C", "It shows nothing (an empty cell), because IFERROR returns an empty string when the lookup fails"], ["D", "Financially relevant is Y, Environment is PRD and In scope is Y, so the PRD financial system is in scope"], ["E", "The Row check shows \"Evidence ref needed\", because the status is Validated but the evidence reference is empty"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) IFERROR replaces an error, such as a key not found, with a harmless result such as an empty cell. (2) In the order written, because the first failing test shows its message and later ones are hidden until you fix it. (3) The result changes with the date, so numbers can differ from day to day."}]}, {"title": "Session 4", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 4"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["A", "Instructions, Control_Register, Risk_Control_Map, ITGC_Dependency, Coverage_Check, Control_Areas, Ref_RISK, Checks (Lists is hidden)"], ["B", "Instructions: instructions. Control_Register, Risk_Control_Map, ITGC_Dependency: registers. Coverage_Check: helper. Control_Areas: editable list. Ref_RISK: reference tab. Checks: checks"], ["C", "The dictionary says: Classification; one value only; type Input. The dropdown offers ITGC-APD, ITGC-PC, ITGC-CO, ITGC-PD, ITAC, IPE, ITDM and Manual BPC"], ["D", "The list headed Cat holds the control categories"], ["E", "Coverage_Check, which shows each risk with the number of key controls mapped"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) For example: register (type), reference tab (paste values), Checks (read only). (2) In the column dictionary on the Instructions tab, then in the dropdown itself. (3) The dropdown reads a fixed range of cells, so a gap would leave blank entries or cut the list short."}]}, {"title": "Session 5", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 5"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["A", "Corvane W09: ALL CHECKS PASS. Blank W04: REVIEW ITEMS: 2"], ["B", "\"In-scope BP-IDs with no risk\" (11 processes have no risk yet) and \"Technical-area risks recorded\" (0)"], ["C", "109 evidence items indexed; 0 populations with an IPE result other than Pass"], ["D", "Rows needing attention: filter the Row check column to anything not OK and fix each message. IPE not Pass: do not sample; complete the IPE_Log check and set the result. No IPE_Log row: add a row recording the completeness and accuracy work"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) The row check tests one record; the Checks tab tests the whole workbook against its gate. (2) A population has not passed IPE: do not draw a sample from it. (3) A template holds only a sample row, so some gate conditions, such as every process having a risk, are not yet met; they clear as real records are entered. A finished file with all conditions met reads ALL CHECKS PASS."}]}, {"title": "Appendix A — Glossary for Part 1", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix A — Glossary for Part 1"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Term", "Meaning"], ["Register", "A table with one record per row and a key in the first column"], ["Key", "A unique code, such as R-P2P-006, that identifies a record and links it to other workbooks"], ["Input cell", "A yellow cell with blue text where you type or select"], ["Formula cell", "A grey cell with black text that calculates or looks up"], ["Dropdown (data validation)", "A list of allowed values in a cell; stop refuses others, warning allows them after a prompt"], ["Ref_ tab", "A tab where keys from an upstream workbook are pasted as values"], ["Row check", "A formula column that tests one record for completeness"], ["Checks tab", "The workbook's completion gate expressed as formulas"], ["PASS / REVIEW / INFO / HOLD / SCOPING", "The five check statuses"], ["Paste values", "Pasting only the contents of cells, without formulas or formatting"]]}]}, {"title": "Appendix B — Tabs in Each Workbook", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix B — Tabs in Each Workbook"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Workbook", "Tabs (hidden Lists tab not shown)"], ["W01", "Instructions, System_Inventory, Server_Detail, Transport_Routes, Open_Items, Checks"], ["W02", "Instructions, Interface_Register, RFC_Service_Users, Data_Flow_Map, Open_Items, Ref_SYS, Checks"], ["W03", "Instructions, Process_Scope, Technical_Areas, FSLI_Map, Open_Items, BP_Index, Checks"], ["W04", "Instructions, Risk_Register, Coverage_By_BP, Ref_BP, Checks"], ["W05", "Instructions, Control_Register, Risk_Control_Map, ITGC_Dependency, Coverage_Check, Control_Areas, Ref_RISK, Checks"], ["W06", "Instructions, Functions, Function_Auth, SoD_Rules, Sensitive_Access, Mitigations, Ruleset_Version, Ref_RISK, Ref_CTRL, Checks"], ["W07", "Instructions, Owner_Matrix, Open_Items, Ref_CTRL, Checks"], ["W08", "Instructions, PBC_Register, Ref_CTRL, Checks"], ["W09", "Instructions, Evidence_Index, IPE_Log, Ref_PBC, Checks"], ["W10", "Instructions, Test_Plan, Sample_Guide, Ref_CTRL, Ref_EV, Checks"], ["W11", "Instructions, WP_Register, Workpaper, Templates, six worked workpaper tabs (Corvane file), Ref_TEST, Checks"], ["W12", "Instructions, Sample_Log, Items, Ref_TEST, Checks"], ["W13", "Instructions, Deficiency_Log, Groups, Ref_TEST, Checks"], ["W14", "Instructions, Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding, Dependencies, Engagement_Summary, Coverage_Matrix, Ref_Risk_Control, Ref_Controls, Ref_Tests, Ref_Deficiencies, Ref_Reliance, Checks"]]}]}], "word_count": 2404, "id": "tib-workbook-primer-part1-sessions-1-5", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/TIB_Workbook_Primer_Part1_Sessions_1-5.docx", "filename": "TIB_Workbook_Primer_Part1_Sessions_1-5.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Using the Foundation Workbooks"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A Hands-On Prerequisite Course for the SAP Controls Project Workbook Set (W01–W14)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Training Manual  |  Part 2 of 3: Sessions 6–10 (Using Each Workbook, W01 to W14)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Worked examples come from the completed Corvane Energy Group case file.  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "How to Use Part 2", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "How to Use Part 2"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1 taught you the skills and the anatomy that all 14 workbooks share. Part 2 takes the workbooks one by one, in five groups. For every workbook you get a one-page card: what it is for, what you type, what it looks up, what you paste into it, which checks to watch, a real row from the Corvane file, and the mistakes people make. Each session ends with a hands-on exercise and three questions. Answers are in the key at the back."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Session", "Workbooks", "Theme"], ["6", "W01, W02, W03", "Build the environment: systems, interfaces, processes"], ["7", "W04, W05", "Risks and controls"], ["8", "W06, W07, W08", "Segregation of duties, owners and evidence requests"], ["9", "W09, W10, W11", "Evidence, the test plan and workpapers"], ["10", "W12, W13, W14", "Samples, deficiencies and the dashboard"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["HOW EVERY CARD IS LAID OUT Purpose:  one line.  You type:  the input columns.  Looks up:  grey columns that fill themselves.  You paste:  the Ref_ tabs and where the values come from.  Watch:  the Checks lines that matter most. Then a real example and the common mistakes."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["BEFORE YOU START Work in a copy of the Corvane folder. Each exercise names the file and the tab. Do not save over the originals."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 6 — Build the Environment: W01, W02 and W03", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 6 — Build the Environment: W01, W02 and W03"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Say what each of W01, W02 and W03 records and what depends on it. Enter a system, an interface and a process correctly. Read the lookups and flags that feed W04."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "These three workbooks hold facts, not judgements. W01 says what systems exist, W02 how data moves between them and the outside world, W03 which business processes run on them. Everything later points back to a key from one of these three."}]}, {"title": "W01 Landscape and System Inventory", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W01 Landscape and System Inventory"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Records every SAP system and client, whether it is in scope, and whether it has been validated with evidence"], ["You type", "System_Inventory: key, SID, client, environment, product, component, hosting, purpose, financially relevant, in scope, rationale, owner, validation method and status, evidence reference, open question. Also Server_Detail, Transport_Routes and Open_Items"], ["Looks up", "Row check; SID in Server_Detail; environments and the DEV-to-PRD bypass flag in Transport_Routes"], ["You paste", "Nothing: W01 is the root of the chain"], ["Watch", "Validated rows with no evidence reference; routes that bypass QAS; servers whose SYS-ID is not in System_Inventory"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: SYS-05, the Fiori front-end server"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["SYS-ID / SID / client", "SYS-05 / FEP / 100"], ["Environment", "PRD"], ["Product and component", "SAP Fiori front-end server 2023; Fiori front-end server"], ["Purpose", "Fiori launchpad for invoice entry, journal posting and approvals"], ["Financially relevant? / In scope?", "Y / Y"], ["Validation", "System > Status; app catalogue extract; status Validated; evidence EV-005"], ["Row check", "OK"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Marking a row Validated with no evidence reference. Recording the SID but not the client. Forgetting the database and integration layers."}]}, {"title": "W02 Interface and Data Flow Inventory", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W02 Interface and Data Flow Inventory"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Records each financially relevant interface, how errors are caught, and the technical users behind them"], ["You type", "Interface_Register (name, source, target, direction, technology, data object, process, monitoring, reconciliation, owner, technical user, scope, evidence, open question); RFC_Service_Users; Data_Flow_Map; Open_Items"], ["Looks up", "Risk flag (\"Carry to W04 as risk\" when a financial interface has weak or unknown monitoring or reconciliation); Row check; interface name in RFC_Service_Users; coverage in Data_Flow_Map"], ["You paste", "Ref_SYS: the SYS-IDs from W01 System_Inventory, column A"], ["Watch", "Financially relevant interfaces flagged for W04; RFC or service users flagged as sensitive; data flows with no covering interface"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: INT-11, the journal upload program"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["INT-ID / name", "INT-11 / Journal upload program ZFI_JE_UPLOAD"], ["Source to target", "Excel files (finance users) to SYS-03; inbound"], ["Technology / data", "Manual upload (LSMW/LTMC); mass journal lines"], ["Process", "BP-R2R-01; month-end, about 60 files"], ["Error monitoring / reconciliation", "None / N"], ["Owner / technical user", "Financial Controller / dialog users"], ["Risk flag", "Carry to W04 as risk (it became risk R-R2R-002)"], ["Row check", "OK"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Skipping outbound files such as the payment file. Treating RFC destinations as infrastructure only. Writing \"monitored\" without saying who looks, how often and what evidence exists."}]}, {"title": "W03 Module and Business Process Scope", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W03 Module and Business Process Scope"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Separates module from business process and ties each process to its systems, interfaces and financial statement accounts"], ["You type", "Process_Scope (business sub-processes), Technical_Areas (BP-TECH rows), FSLI_Map (significant accounts mapped to processes), Open_Items"], ["Looks up", "Row check and FSLI check; BP_Index combines business and technical keys for W04"], ["You paste", "Nothing"], ["Watch", "Significant FSLIs not mapped to a process; in-scope rows with no supporting system; technical areas present"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: BP-O2C-02 and its account FSLI-09"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["BP-ID / process", "BP-O2C-02 / O2C"], ["Sub-process", "Crude and product sales: order, delivery, billing, revenue"], ["Modules / systems / interfaces", "SD, FI / SYS-03 / INT-03, INT-05"], ["Accounts / significance", "Revenue; trade receivables / High"], ["Owner / transactions", "Commercial Manager / VA01, VL01N, VF01, VF04, VKM1"], ["Confirmation", "Confirmed"], ["FSLI-09 maps to", "Revenue (crude, products, tariffs): BP-O2C-02, BP-JVA-01; assertions Occurrence, Accuracy, Cut-off"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Writing \"FI is in scope\": a module is not a process. Guessing a process instead of marking it Outstanding. Leaving out the technical areas for access, change and operations."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 6 Work in copies of W01, W02 and W03. A.  In W01 Server_Detail, how many rows belong to SYS-03, and how many are application servers?  B.  In W01 Transport_Routes, what do the From env and To env columns show for each route, and what does the bypass column say?  C.  In W02 Interface_Register, filter Risk flag to \"Carry to W04 as risk\". How many interfaces? Which one has error monitoring set to None?  D.  In W02 RFC_Service_Users, which row has a Review flag other than OK, and why?  E.  In W03, how many business sub-processes and how many technical areas are there, and how many accounts are in FSLI_Map?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why is W01 called the root of the chain?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What does the Risk flag in W02 tell you to do?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What is the difference between a module and a business process?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS W01, W02 and W03 hold facts; later workbooks point back to their keys. A flagged interface becomes a risk in W04. Technical areas get their own rows so access, change and operations risks have a home."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 7 — Risks and Controls: W04 and W05", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 7 — Risks and Controls: W04 and W05"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Enter a risk in the standard formula and a control in five parts. Read the coverage views that show gaps. Read a dependency row and the reliance decision."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W04 states what could go wrong; W05 states what answers it. The two are linked through the Risk_Control_Map, and W05 also records which general controls each automated control relies on."}]}, {"title": "W04 Risk Register", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W04 Risk Register"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "States what could go wrong in each process and technical area, who owns the risk, and how significant it is"], ["You type", "Risk_Register: key, BP-ID, system or interface, category, risk statement, assertions, owner, likelihood, impact, SOX flag, source, status, notes"], ["Looks up", "Inherent rating (likelihood times impact), Rating band, Row check; Coverage_By_BP counts risks per process"], ["You paste", "Ref_BP: the BP-IDs from W03 (copy the business block and the technical block separately)"], ["Watch", "In-scope BP-IDs with no risk; technical-area risks recorded; rows needing attention"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: R-R2R-002"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["RISK-ID / BP-ID", "R-R2R-002 / BP-R2R-01"], ["Interface / category", "INT-11 / Interface"], ["Statement", "There is a risk that mass journal uploads are incomplete or inaccurate because the custom upload program ZFI_JE_UPLOAD has no validation or monitoring, resulting in misposted or missing journal lines."], ["Owner", "Financial Controller"], ["Likelihood × impact", "3 × 2 = 6, band High"], ["SOX / source / status", "Y / Walkthrough / Agreed with owner"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Writing a finding as a risk (\"leavers are not removed\"). A statement that does not use \"There is a risk that … because … resulting in …\". Naming the control owner as the risk owner by default."}]}, {"title": "W05 Risk and Control Matrix", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W05 Risk and Control Matrix"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Defines the specific control that answers each risk, classifies it, and records the general controls it relies on"], ["You type", "Control_Register (key, title, five-part description, area, category, type, nature, frequency, key and SOX flags, owner, system, SAP mechanism, dependent ITGCs, IPE used, expected evidence, test approach); Risk_Control_Map; ITGC_Dependency"], ["Looks up", "Risks mapped, Row check; lookups in the map; dependency category; Coverage_Check shows each risk with key controls mapped"], ["You paste", "Ref_RISK: RISK-ID and SOX relevant flag from W04 (columns A and L, pasted as two columns); Control_Areas is an editable list"], ["Watch", "SOX risks with no key control (design gap); ITAC, report and review controls with no dependency row; dependency rows needing attention"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: C-ITAC-001 and its reliance links"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["CTRL-ID / title", "C-ITAC-001 / Three-way match blocks out-of-tolerance invoices"], ["Category / nature / frequency", "ITAC / Automated / Continuous; key control Y"], ["Owner / system / mechanism", "Head of AP / SYS-03 / OMR6 and T169G; MRBR; RBKP"], ["Risks mapped", "1 (R-P2P-003, coverage Full)"], ["Expected evidence", "OMR6 screenshot, T169G change log, blocked invoice sample"], ["Dependency D-01", "C-ITGC-PC-001: Exception noted; Supported with additional procedures"], ["Dependency D-02", "C-ITGC-PC-002: Exception noted; Supported with additional procedures"], ["Dependency D-03", "C-ITGC-AC-005: Ineffective; Supported with additional procedures"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Recording a control area (\"User access\") instead of a control. A description with no evidence (\"access is managed appropriately\"). Marking reliance as Supported when the general control failed and no extra procedures are recorded."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 7 Work in copies of W04 and W05. A.  In W04 Coverage_By_BP, which process has the most risks and how many?  B.  How many risks are in each rating band?  C.  In W05 Control_Register, how many controls are there in each category, and how many are key?  D.  In Risk_Control_Map, how many links are there, and which controls answer risk R-P2P-006?  E.  In ITGC_Dependency, which row has the reliance decision Not supported, and why?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What does the Risk_Control_Map do that a single \"risk\" column in the control register cannot?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why does an automated control need dependency rows?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does \"SOX risk with no key control\" mean?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS W04 is what could go wrong; W05 is what answers it. One control, one category; the map links many risks to many controls. Dependency rows connect automated controls to the general controls they rely on."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 8 — Rules, Owners and Requests: W06, W07 and W08", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 8 — Rules, Owners and Requests: W06, W07 and W08"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Read an SoD rule and its mitigation. Record who owns, performs and evidences each control. Write and track an evidence request."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W06 turns SoD risks into testable rules. W07 names the people behind each control. W08 asks those people for the evidence the tests will need."}]}, {"title": "W06 SoD and Sensitive Access Matrix", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W06 SoD and Sensitive Access Matrix"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Translates SoD and sensitive-access risks into functions, permissions and rules, and records approved mitigations"], ["You type", "Functions, Function_Auth, SoD_Rules, Sensitive_Access, Mitigations, Ruleset_Version"], ["Looks up", "Function names in permissions and rules; Row checks, including mitigation expiry and test result"], ["You paste", "Ref_RISK (RISK-ID and category from W04) and Ref_CTRL (CTRL-IDs from W05)"], ["Watch", "SoD risks in W04 with no rule; mitigation rows needing attention; critical rules with no mitigation (these conflicts must be removed)"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: SOD-001 and mitigation MIT-03"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["SOD-001", "FN-01 Vendor master maintenance against FN-02 Payment run and release"], ["Conflict", "A user can create or change a vendor and run the payment, enabling fictitious vendor payments"], ["Risk / process / level", "R-P2P-001 / BP-P2P-03 / Critical; permission level; cross-org"], ["Mitigation / owner", "None (the conflict must be removed) / Head of AP"], ["MIT-03", "C-BPC-P2P-003 mitigates SOD-005 for 4 users, valid 1 April to 31 December 2026; tested effective: Y"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Writing transaction-only rules that report false conflicts. Naming a mitigating control that is not in W05. Missing custom Z-transactions and Fiori equivalents."}]}, {"title": "W07 Control and Evidence Owner Matrix", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W07 Control and Evidence Owner Matrix"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Records, for every control, who owns it, who performs it, who can supply evidence and who is the backup"], ["You type", "Owner_Matrix: key, control owner, performer, evidence owner, backup, system, walkthrough contact, owner status, date confirmed, follow-up"], ["Looks up", "Control title, key flag, risk owner and expected evidence from Ref_CTRL; Row check"], ["You paste", "Ref_CTRL: six columns from W05 (key, title, control owner, expected evidence, key flag) plus the risk owner from W04"], ["Watch", "W05 controls with no owner row; owners still Outstanding; key controls whose owner is not confirmed"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: C-BPC-P2P-003"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["Control", "Payment file integrity and reconciliation (key control)"], ["Risk owner / control owner", "Treasury Manager / Treasury Manager"], ["Performer / evidence owner / backup", "Treasury Analyst / Treasury Manager / Treasury Analyst"], ["System / walkthrough contact", "SYS-03 / Treasury Analyst"], ["Owner status / date", "Confirmed / 9 April 2026"], ["Expected evidence (looked up)", "Hash alert, reconciliation, directory permission listing"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Treating owner and performer as the same person by default. Marking an owner Confirmed with no date. Leaving the walkthrough contact blank on a key control."}]}, {"title": "W08 PBC Evidence Request Register", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W08 PBC Evidence Request Register"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Lists every piece of evidence requested, from whom, for which period and for which control and test"], ["You type", "PBC_Register: key, wave, control, other controls, tests, system or process, requested evidence, period from and to, evidence type, dates, priority, status, evidence IDs, notes"], ["Looks up", "Evidence owner (from W07 through Ref_CTRL); IPE needed (Y for populations); days overdue; Row check"], ["You paste", "Ref_CTRL: key, title, evidence owner, expected evidence and key flag"], ["Watch", "Key controls with no request; overdue requests; row issues; population requests that need IPE"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: PBC-032, the transport population"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["PBC-ID / wave", "PBC-032 / 2 Population"], ["Control / test", "C-ITGC-PC-001 / C-ITGC-PC-001-TOE"], ["Requested evidence", "All transports imported to CEP/300 (TPALOG and STMS import history)"], ["Period / type", "1 Jan to 31 May 2026 / Population"], ["Owner (looked up) / priority", "ITSM Administrator / High"], ["Dates / status", "Requested 3 Jun; due 16 Jun; Closed"], ["Evidence / IPE needed", "EV-046 / Y"], ["Note", "Date range corrected after the first extract covered 60 days only"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Asking for sample evidence before the population is received. A request with no period, system or format. Marking Received with no evidence ID."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 8 Work in copies of W06, W07 and W08. A.  In W06 SoD_Rules, how many rules have Risk level Critical, and what is their mitigation?  B.  In W06 Mitigations, how many mitigations are there, and which control mitigates SOD-005?  C.  In W07, how many controls have owner status Confirmed, and on what date were the last owners confirmed?  D.  In W08, how many requests are in each wave?  E.  How many requests show IPE needed = Y?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What must happen to a critical conflict that has no mitigation?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What is the difference between a control owner and an evidence owner?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Why does W08 ask for populations before samples?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS W06 rules must trace to W04 risks and W05 mitigations. W07 gives every control four named people. W08 requests populations first, with a period, a system and a format."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 9 — Evidence, Test Plan and Workpapers: W09, W10 and W11", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 9 — Evidence, Test Plan and Workpapers: W09, W10 and W11"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Index evidence and record the completeness check. Read a test row and its lookups. Read a workpaper and its result."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Evidence arrives in W09, where it is proven complete. W10 turns each key control into a design test and an operating test. W11 records exactly what was done."}]}, {"title": "W09 Evidence Index and IPE Log", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W09 Evidence Index and IPE Log"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "The filing system: every item received gets an ID, and every population is proven complete and accurate before sampling"], ["You type", "Evidence_Index: key, PBC-ID, file name, description, source, method, who, dates, period covered, record count, preserved, IPE result, tests; IPE_Log: key, evidence ID, completeness and accuracy procedures, source count, records traced, tester, date, result"], ["Looks up", "Requested period and IPE required from W08; population description and count in the IPE log; count difference; Row check"], ["You paste", "Ref_PBC: PBC-ID, period from, period to, evidence type from W08"], ["Watch", "Populations whose IPE result is not Pass; populations with no IPE log row; originals not preserved"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: EV-046 and IPE-010"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["EV-046", "EV-046_TPALOG_import_history_2026-06-17.csv; 418 records; original preserved; IPE required Y; result Pass"], ["Request / period", "PBC-032 / 1 Jan to 31 May 2026"], ["IPE-010 completeness", "Parameters captured; count agreed to STMS import queue history (418 against 418, difference 0)"], ["IPE-010 accuracy", "5 records traced from file to system and back"], ["IPE-010 result", "Pass, tested by N. Haddad on 18 June 2026"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Sampling before the IPE result is Pass. A file name that does not start with the evidence ID. An extract whose period does not cover the request."}]}, {"title": "W10 Test Plan", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W10 Test Plan"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Turns each key control into a test of design (TOD) and a test of operating effectiveness (TOE), with attributes, population, sample size, people and dates"], ["You type", "Test_Plan: key, control, test type, attributes, procedure, population evidence, sample size, period, tester, reviewer, planned dates, workpaper reference, status, result"], ["Looks up", "Control title, category and frequency; IPE result and population size; guide sample range; ITGC reliance needed; Row check. Sample_Guide is an editable list"], ["You paste", "Ref_CTRL (key, title, category, frequency, key flag, SOX flag) and Ref_EV (evidence ID, IPE result, record count)"], ["Watch", "Key controls with no TOD or TOE; HOLD (population not Pass); dependent tests starting before the last ITGC test ends; samples outside the guide"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: C-ITGC-PC-001-TOE"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["Control (looked up)", "Transports approved, tested and imported independently; ITGC-PC; Event-driven"], ["Population", "EV-046; IPE result Pass; 418 records"], ["Sample", "Guide 25 to 60; planned 25"], ["Reliance needed?", "N (it is an ITGC, not a dependent control)"], ["People / dates", "Tester E. Rossi; reviewer M. Lin; 22 to 26 June 2026"], ["Workpaper / result", "WP-PC-01_C-ITGC-PC-001-TOE / Exception noted (status Closed)"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Writing attributes that do not come from the control description. Using the same person as tester and reviewer. Typing the sample size as text."}]}, {"title": "W11 Test Workpapers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W11 Test Workpapers"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Records what the tester did so a reviewer can reperform it; holds the standard Workpaper tab, the template list and a register"], ["You type", "WP_Register: reference, test, template, tester, items tested, exceptions, result, reviewer, date, deficiency IDs, status. Copy the Workpaper tab for each test and complete it"], ["Looks up", "Row check; on the Workpaper tab: item result, items tested, exceptions by attribute, suggested result"], ["You paste", "Ref_TEST: the TEST-IDs from W10"], ["Watch", "Exceptions with no DEF-ID or rationale; W10 tests with no workpaper"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: the transports workpaper"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["Register row", "WP-PC-01_C-ITGC-PC-001-TOE; 27 items tested; 5 with exceptions; Exception noted; reviewed by M. Lin on 29 June 2026"], ["Deficiencies", "DEF-013; DEF-027"], ["Workpaper tab", "27 items tested; 5 items with exception; exceptions by attribute: B 2, E 3"], ["Suggested result", "Exception noted: evaluate in W13 (Session 35)"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Ticking an attribute with no evidence reference. Entering a result before the reviewer has signed off. Using a template that is not on the Templates list."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 9 Work in copies of W09, W10 and W11. A.  In W09 Evidence_Index, find EV-046. What is its file name, and what are its record count and IPE result?  B.  How many rows are in W09 IPE_Log?  C.  In W10 Test_Plan, how many tests are there, how many TOE rows have the result Ineffective, and how many rows have the status Not started?  D.  For C-ITGC-PC-001-TOE, read the looked-up columns: category, frequency, guide sample, ITGC reliance needed.  E.  Open the W11 tab WP_PC01_transports_TOE. How many items were tested, how many had exceptions, and which attributes did they fail?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What must be true of a population before a sample is drawn from it?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why does an ITGC row show reliance needed = N while an ITAC row shows Y?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does the suggested result on a workpaper tab do, and who sets the final result?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS W09 proves populations complete and accurate; only Pass allows sampling. W10 gives every key control a design test and an operating test. W11 records the work; the reviewer sets the final result."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 10 — Samples, Deficiencies and the Dashboard: W12, W13 and W14", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 10 — Samples, Deficiencies and the Dashboard: W12, W13 and W14"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Log a sample so it can be reperformed. Record and group a deficiency and follow it to closure. Read the dashboard and its conclusion."]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "W12 records which items were chosen. W13 turns exceptions into evaluated, grouped deficiencies. W14 reads everything and tells the story on one screen."}]}, {"title": "W12 Sample Selection Log", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W12 Sample Selection Log"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Records which items were selected from which population, how, and why, so a reviewer can confirm and reperform the selection"], ["You type", "Sample_Log: key, test, method, seed, targeted additions, coverage confirmation, request ID, who, date, rationale; Items: one row per item with date and basis"], ["Looks up", "Population, IPE result and sizes from Ref_TEST; items selected; items per quarter; quarters covered; Row check"], ["You paste", "Ref_TEST: test, population evidence, IPE result, population size, planned sample size"], ["Watch", "Tests needing a sample but with none logged; items selected not equal to planned plus targeted; HOLD"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: SMP-008"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["SMP-008", "C-ITGC-PC-001-TOE; population EV-046 (418); IPE Pass"], ["Planned / selected", "25 planned plus 2 targeted = 27 items selected"], ["Method / seed", "Random (seeded); seed 4259"], ["Quarter spread", "17 items in Q1 and 10 in Q2; 2 quarters covered"], ["Request / selector", "PBC-058 / E. Rossi, 24 June 2026"], ["Rationale", "Two targeted transports touching ITAC tables added to the random sample"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: No seed or interval recorded. Selected items not listed on the Items tab. Targeted additions with no rationale."}]}, {"title": "W13 Deficiency and Remediation Log", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W13 Deficiency and Remediation Log"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "Records each confirmed exception as a deficiency, evaluates severity, groups related deficiencies, and tracks remediation and retest"], ["You type", "Deficiency_Log: key, test, type, condition, criteria, cause, effect, exceptions, dependent and compensating controls, severity, group, response, owner, due date, retest, status; Groups: factor, exposure, severity, rationale"], ["Looks up", "Control, risk and process from Ref_TEST; per group the number of deficiencies and the highest individual severity; Row check"], ["You paste", "Ref_TEST: test, control, risk, process, result from W10"], ["Watch", "Tests with an exception and no DEF-ID; Closed with no retest ID; a group rated below its highest individual severity"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: DEF-007 and group GR-B"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["DEF-007", "From test C-BPC-P2P-003-TOD; control C-BPC-P2P-003; risk R-P2P-006; process BP-P2P-03"], ["Type / severity / group", "Design / Control deficiency / GR-B"], ["Retest / status", "C-BPC-P2P-003-RT1 / Closed"], ["GR-B Payment process", "4 deficiencies; highest individual severity Significant deficiency; group severity Significant deficiency"], ["Rationale", "Three weaknesses on one fraud scenario with the compensating controls also deficient; a misstatement is reasonably possible but not material"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Writing a finding as a risk statement. A severity rating with no compensating-control evidence. Closing a deficiency without a retest."}]}, {"title": "W14 Scoping Workpaper and Coverage Dashboard", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "W14 Scoping Workpaper and Coverage Dashboard"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "What to know"], ["Purpose", "The master record: a scoping summary at the start and a coverage dashboard at the end"], ["You type", "Engagement_Summary (client, systems, period, materiality, stage), Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding, Dependencies"], ["Looks up", "Coverage_Matrix per risk; results by category; deficiencies by severity; the conclusion in cell B16; coverage checks"], ["You paste", "Five Ref_ tabs: Ref_Risk_Control, Ref_Controls, Ref_Tests, Ref_Deficiencies, Ref_Reliance, from W04, W05, W10, W12 and W13"], ["Watch", "The seven coverage checks (SCOPING until the Stage is set to Testing); open dependencies"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Real example: the Corvane dashboard"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Value"], ["Stage / as-of date", "Testing / 30 September 2026"], ["Conclusion (B16)", "No material weakness or significant deficiency; control deficiencies identified"], ["Coverage_Matrix R-P2P-006", "Key controls mapped 2; worst TOD Ineffective; worst TOE Exception noted; 1 deficiency; Covered - deficiency (Control deficiency)"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Common mistakes: Typing figures that should be formulas. Forgetting to re-paste a Ref_ tab before reading the dashboard. Leaving the Stage on Scoping after testing starts."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 10 Work in copies of W12, W13 and W14. A.  In W12 Sample_Log, read SMP-008: how many items were planned, targeted and selected, and how many quarters are covered?  B.  How many samples are logged, and how many items are on the Items tab?  C.  In W13 Deficiency_Log, how many deficiencies have status Closed and how many Remediation in progress?  D.  In W13, what is the type and retest ID of DEF-007?  E.  In W14, what is the Stage and what does cell B16 say?  F.  In W14 Checks, how many of the seven numbered coverage checks pass?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why must a seed or interval be recorded?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What must a Closed deficiency have in W13?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does the Stage switch in W14 change?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS W12 makes selection reperformable. W13 records, groups and closes; a closure needs a retest. W14 only reads what you paste: refresh before you trust it."]]}]}, {"title": "Answer Key to Part 2", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Answer Key to Part 2"}]}, {"title": "Session 6", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 6"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["A", "5 rows belong to SYS-03 (cep-app01, 02, 03, ascs01 and wd01); 3 are application servers"], ["B", "Route 1 shows DEV to QAS; route 2 shows QAS to PRD; the bypass column reads OK for both"], ["C", "6 interfaces (INT-02, INT-03, INT-05, INT-06, INT-09, INT-11); INT-11 has monitoring None"], ["D", "RFC-02 (SMD_RFC): it holds SAP_ALL, so the flag reads \"SAP_ALL on RFC user: sensitive (W06)\""], ["E", "14 business sub-processes, 4 technical areas, 13 FSLIs"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) Every later key points back to a system, so everything starts there. (2) It tells you to carry the interface into W04 as a risk because monitoring or reconciliation is weak or unknown. (3) A module is part of SAP such as FI or MM; a business process is what the business does, such as vendor payment, and it may cross several modules."}]}, {"title": "Session 7", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 7"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["A", "BP-TECH-01, with 5 risks"], ["B", "High 23, Medium 13, Low 0"], ["C", "47 controls: ITAC 12, ITDM 12, ITGC-APD 8, Manual BPC 6, ITGC-CO 4, ITGC-PC 3, IPE 2; 38 are key"], ["D", "66 links; R-P2P-006 is answered by C-BPC-P2P-003 (Full) and C-ITGC-CO-002 (Partial)"], ["E", "D-46: C-BPC-P2P-003 relying on C-ITGC-CO-002. The ITGC had exceptions and the payment-file integrity control could not be relied on for January to May 2026; all bank changes and payment runs were examined substantively"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) It links many risks to many controls; one control can answer several risks and one risk can need several controls. (2) Automated controls are only reliable if the general controls over change and access were effective, or other evidence shows the configuration did not change. (3) A SOX-relevant risk has no key control mapped to it, which is a design gap."}]}, {"title": "Session 8", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 8"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["A", "4 critical rules (SOD-001, SOD-002, SOD-006, SOD-011); their mitigation is None because the conflict must be removed"], ["B", "12 mitigations; C-BPC-P2P-003 mitigates SOD-005 (MIT-03)"], ["C", "47 controls are Confirmed; the last owners were confirmed on 9 April 2026"], ["D", "22 in wave 1 (scoping and configuration), 28 in wave 2, 25 in wave 3, 20 in wave 4"], ["E", "45 requests need IPE (all the population requests)"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) It must be removed; it cannot be mitigated. (2) The control owner is accountable for the control operating; the evidence owner is the person or team who can produce the evidence. (3) A sample is only reliable if the population it is drawn from is complete, so the population must arrive first and pass its check."}]}, {"title": "Session 9", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 9"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["A", "EV-046_TPALOG_import_history_2026-06-17.csv; 418 records; IPE result Pass"], ["B", "45 rows"], ["C", "104 tests; 2 TOE rows are Ineffective (privileged access and SoD analysis); 9 rows have status Not started (the planned retests)"], ["D", "ITGC-PC; Event-driven; guide sample 25 to 60; reliance needed N"], ["E", "27 items tested; 5 with exceptions; attribute B failed on 2 items and attribute E on 3"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) It must have an IPE result of Pass. (2) Reliance applies to controls that depend on general controls (automated controls, reports and reviews); an ITGC is itself the control others rely on. (3) It counts items and exceptions and proposes a result; the reviewer sets the final result."}]}, {"title": "Session 10", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Session 10"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["A", "Planned 25, targeted 2, selected 27; 2 quarters covered (17 items in Q1 and 10 in Q2)"], ["B", "37 samples; 601 items"], ["C", "21 Closed and 9 Remediation in progress"], ["D", "Design; retest C-BPC-P2P-003-RT1"], ["E", "Stage is Testing; B16 reads \"No material weakness or significant deficiency; control deficiencies identified\""], ["F", "All seven numbered coverage checks pass"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: (1) So the selection can be reperformed and shown to be unbiased. (2) A retest TEST-ID showing the new control operating. (3) It switches the coverage checks between scoping, where tests do not yet exist, and testing, where every check must pass."}]}, {"title": "Appendix — Where Each Workbook Gets Its Pasted Keys", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix — Where Each Workbook Gets Its Pasted Keys"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Workbook", "Ref_ tab", "Source"], ["W02", "Ref_SYS", "W01 System_Inventory, column A"], ["W04", "Ref_BP", "W03 Process_Scope and Technical_Areas (BP-IDs)"], ["W05", "Ref_RISK", "W04 RISK-ID and SOX relevant"], ["W06", "Ref_RISK, Ref_CTRL", "W04 RISK-ID and category; W05 CTRL-IDs"], ["W07", "Ref_CTRL", "W05 key, title, owner, expected evidence, key flag; risk owner from W04"], ["W08", "Ref_CTRL", "W05 and W07 (evidence owner)"], ["W09", "Ref_PBC", "W08 PBC-ID, period, evidence type"], ["W10", "Ref_CTRL, Ref_EV", "W05; W09 evidence ID, IPE result, record count"], ["W11", "Ref_TEST", "W10 TEST-ID"], ["W12", "Ref_TEST", "W10 and W09"], ["W13", "Ref_TEST", "W10, with risk and process from W04 and W05"], ["W14", "Five Ref_ tabs", "W04, W05, W10, W12, W13"]]}]}], "word_count": 1395, "id": "tib-workbook-primer-part2-sessions-6-10", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/TIB_Workbook_Primer_Part2_Sessions_6-10.docx", "filename": "TIB_Workbook_Primer_Part2_Sessions_6-10.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Using the Foundation Workbooks"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A Hands-On Prerequisite Course for the SAP Controls Project Workbook Set (W01–W14)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Training Manual  |  Part 3 of 3: Sessions 11–12 (Connecting the Workbooks and the Trace Exercise), Final Assessment, Quick Reference and Glossary"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Worked examples come from the completed Corvane Energy Group case file.  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "Session 11 — Connecting the Workbooks", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 11 — Connecting the Workbooks"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Explain how a key travels from one workbook to the next. Refresh the Ref_ tabs in the right order and recognise a stale one. Find every place a control appears across the set."]]}]}, {"title": "11.1 The thread, workbook by workbook", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.1 The thread, workbook by workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every workbook creates one kind of key, and the next workbook refers to it. The thread runs from a system to a conclusion. Read the table from top to bottom as the order in which the keys are created, and from bottom to top as the order in which you defend a conclusion."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Key", "Created in", "First used in", "What it identifies"], ["SYS-ID", "W01", "W02, W03", "A system and client"], ["INT-ID", "W02", "W04", "An interface"], ["BP-ID", "W03", "W04", "A business process or technical area"], ["RISK-ID", "W04", "W05, W06", "A risk"], ["CTRL-ID", "W05", "W06, W07, W08", "A control"], ["SOD-ID", "W06", "W06 Mitigations", "A segregation-of-duties rule"], ["PBC-ID", "W08", "W09", "An evidence request"], ["EV-ID, IPE-ID", "W09", "W10, W12", "An evidence item and its completeness check"], ["TEST-ID", "W10", "W11, W12, W13", "A test (design, operating or retest)"], ["SMP-ID", "W12", "W11", "A sample"], ["DEF-ID", "W13", "W14", "A deficiency"]]}]}, {"title": "11.2 The paste map in working order", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.2 The paste map in working order"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The keys cross from one workbook to the next only when you paste them. Work in this order, so each workbook's Ref_ tabs are current before the next one needs them."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Step", "Paste into", "Copy from"], ["1", "W02 Ref_SYS", "W01 SYS-IDs"], ["2", "W04 Ref_BP", "W03 BP-IDs (business block, then technical block)"], ["3", "W05 Ref_RISK", "W04 RISK-ID and SOX relevant"], ["4", "W06 Ref_RISK and Ref_CTRL", "W04 RISK-ID and category; W05 CTRL-IDs"], ["5", "W07 Ref_CTRL", "W05 control details; risk owner from W04"], ["6", "W08 Ref_CTRL", "W05 and W07 (evidence owner)"], ["7", "W09 Ref_PBC", "W08 PBC-ID, period, type"], ["8", "W10 Ref_CTRL and Ref_EV", "W05; W09 evidence ID, IPE result, record count"], ["9", "W11, W12, W13 Ref_TEST", "W10 tests (with W09 and W04/W05 details for W12 and W13)"], ["10", "W14 five Ref_ tabs", "W04, W05, W10, W12, W13"]]}]}, {"title": "11.3 When to refresh", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.3 When to refresh"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "At every gate, before you read a Checks tab."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Whenever an upstream record is added or changed: a new control, a changed owner, a new test."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Never edit a Ref_ tab by hand to make a check pass. Fix the source workbook and paste again."}]}, {"title": "11.4 Spotting a stale Ref_ tab", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.4 Spotting a stale Ref_ tab"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["What you see", "Likely cause", "What to do"], ["A lookup column is blank", "The key is not in the Ref_ tab, or was pasted with a stray space", "Check the source for the key; paste values again"], ["Row check says \"CTRL-ID not in W05\"", "A control was added or renamed in W05 after the last paste", "Add the control in W05 and re-paste Ref_CTRL"], ["A dropdown shows an old list", "The Ref_ tab was not refreshed", "Re-paste and look again"], ["The Checks tab counts do not match the source", "Several Ref_ tabs are at different dates", "Refresh in the order of 11.2, then read the Checks"]]}]}, {"title": "11.5 One control across the whole set", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "11.5 One control across the whole set"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A control key shows up in many workbooks. This table follows C-BPC-P2P-003, payment file integrity and reconciliation, through the Corvane files."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Workbook", "Where C-BPC-P2P-003 appears"], ["W01 to W03", "Not as a control, but its system SYS-03, interface INT-02 and process BP-P2P-03 are there"], ["W04", "The risk it answers: R-P2P-006"], ["W05", "Control_Register; Risk_Control_Map link to R-P2P-006 (Full); three dependency rows (D-46, D-47, D-48)"], ["W06", "Mitigation MIT-03 for SOD-005"], ["W07", "Owner Treasury Manager; performer Treasury Analyst"], ["W08", "Requests PBC-043 (population), PBC-067 (sample), PBC-081 (retest)"], ["W09", "Evidence EV-057, EV-081 and EV-095; population check IPE-021"], ["W10", "Tests C-BPC-P2P-003-TOD, -TOE and -RT1"], ["W11", "Three register rows, for example WP-BPC-01_C-BPC-P2P-003-TOD"], ["W12", "Sample SMP-017 (25 payment runs)"], ["W13", "DEF-007, found by the design test; group GR-B"], ["W14", "Ref_Controls, Ref_Tests, Ref_Reliance, and through R-P2P-006 the Coverage_Matrix"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 11 Work in copies. A.  In W08, go to Ref_CTRL and delete the row for C-ITGC-PC-001. How many requests now show \"CTRL-ID not in W05\" and what happens to their Evidence owner column? Undo the deletion.  B.  List the workbooks in which the key C-BPC-P2P-003 appears.  C.  You are about to read the W14 dashboard. In what order do you refresh the Ref_ tabs of the other workbooks first?"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Why does the order of pasting matter?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. What should you do if a Ref_ tab shows an old list?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Name the keys created in W10, W12 and W13."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Each workbook creates one kind of key and the next refers to it. Refresh the Ref_ tabs in workbook order; read the Checks after. A single control key can be followed through ten workbooks."]]}]}, {"title": "Session 12 — The Trace Exercise", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Session 12 — The Trace Exercise"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["LEARNING OUTCOMES Follow a risk forward from a server to its tests and conclusion. Follow a deficiency backward to the system it came from. Trace an automated control to the general controls it relies on."]]}]}, {"title": "12.1 Why trace", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "12.1 Why trace"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A controls project is only as good as its traceability. A reviewer will pick any deficiency and ask where it came from, or pick any system and ask what was done about it. If you can answer either question in a few steps, the foundation is sound. The trace uses only keys: you move from one to the next by reading them off a row."}]}, {"title": "12.2 Worked forward trace: from SYS-03 to a conclusion", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "12.2 Worked forward trace: from SYS-03 to a conclusion"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Step", "Workbook", "What you read"], ["1", "W01", "SYS-03: CEP, client 300, production, the primary financial system"], ["2", "W02", "INT-02: vendor payment files outbound to the banks; monitoring Other documented; no reconciliation (N); flagged \"Carry to W04 as risk\""], ["3", "W03", "BP-P2P-03: vendor payment; owner Treasury Manager"], ["4", "W04", "R-P2P-006: the payment file could be altered between creation and the bank; owner Treasury Manager; High"], ["5", "W05", "Controls answering it: C-BPC-P2P-003 (Full) and C-ITGC-CO-002 (Partial)"], ["6", "W06", "C-BPC-P2P-003 also mitigates SOD-005 (MIT-03, 4 users)"], ["7", "W07", "Owner Treasury Manager; performer Treasury Analyst"], ["8", "W08", "PBC-043 (population), PBC-067 (sample), PBC-081 (retest)"], ["9", "W09", "EV-057 passed IPE-021 as a complete population; EV-081 and EV-095 hold the sample and retest evidence"], ["10", "W10", "C-BPC-P2P-003-TOD Ineffective; C-BPC-P2P-003-TOE Effective as designed; C-BPC-P2P-003-RT1 Effective"], ["11", "W12 and W11", "SMP-017: 25 payment runs; workpapers WP-BPC-01_C-BPC-P2P-003-TOD, -TOE and -RT1"], ["12", "W13", "DEF-007: design deficiency, control deficiency, group GR-B; closed after the retest"], ["13", "W14", "Coverage_Matrix R-P2P-006: 2 key controls mapped; worst TOD Ineffective; worst TOE Exception noted; 1 deficiency; Covered - deficiency"]]}]}, {"title": "12.3 Worked backward trace: from DEF-016 to a server", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "12.3 Worked backward trace: from DEF-016 to a server"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Step", "Workbook", "What you read"], ["1", "W13", "DEF-016: design deficiency; control deficiency; group GR-E; status Remediation in progress; due 11 December 2026; retest C-BPC-O2C-001-RT1"], ["2", "W10", "Found by C-BPC-O2C-001-TOD (Ineffective): the volume reconciliation was an uncontrolled spreadsheet with no independent review"], ["3", "W05", "Control C-BPC-O2C-001, daily volume load reconciliation; owner Commercial Manager; it answers R-O2C-002 (Full)"], ["4", "W04", "R-O2C-002: revenue incomplete or inaccurate because allocated production volumes are not completely or correctly loaded; process BP-O2C-02; interface INT-03"], ["5", "W02", "INT-03: field production volumes from the allocation system into SYS-03"], ["6", "W01", "SYS-03: CEP, client 300, production"]]}]}, {"title": "12.4 Worked reliance trace: from an automated control to the ITGCs", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "12.4 Worked reliance trace: from an automated control to the ITGCs"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "C-ITAC-006 (pricing procedure and condition maintenance locked) relies on three general controls. Its dependency rows in W05 and their reliance decisions are:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["General control", "Baseline result", "Reliance decision"], ["C-ITGC-PC-001 transports", "Exception noted", "Supported with additional procedures"], ["C-ITGC-PC-002 system and client settings", "Exception noted", "Supported with additional procedures"], ["C-ITGC-AC-003 quarterly access review", "Effective", "Supported"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The test of C-ITAC-006 found three unauthorised pricing changes (DEF-029, group GR-G). It was closed after the retest C-ITAC-006-RT1. The additional procedures that let the auditors rely on the control in spite of the change deficiencies were a full-period review of table change logs and of the transports that touch ITAC tables."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["TRY IT 12 Use the Corvane workbooks. A.  Trace DEF-004 backward to a server: give the test, control, risk, process, system and the servers behind it, and say its status.  B.  Trace C-ITAC-003 (posting periods) to the ITGCs it relies on: give each ITGC, its baseline result and its reliance decision, and the additional procedure recorded for each.  C.  Trace interface INT-03 forward to a deficiency: give the process, risk, controls, the test that failed and the deficiency."]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Check yourself"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What do you read from a row to move to the next workbook?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why does a backward trace end at a system?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What does a \"Supported with additional procedures\" reliance decision mean?"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["KEY POINTS Trace forward to plan; trace backward to defend. You move between workbooks by reading keys off a row. Reliance on an automated control is recorded against each general control it depends on."]]}]}, {"title": "Final Assessment", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Final Assessment"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The assessment tests the skills in this course. It has three sections and is worth 100 marks. The pass mark is 80. Use your own copy of the Corvane folder and give yourself 90 minutes."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Section", "Content", "Marks"], ["1 Knowledge", "15 short questions, 2 marks each", "30"], ["2 Practical", "4 tasks in the Corvane workbooks, 15 marks each", "60"], ["3 Trace", "One backward trace, 10 marks", "10"]]}]}, {"title": "Section 1: Knowledge (30 marks)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Section 1: Knowledge (30 marks)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. What does a yellow cell with blue text mean, and what does a grey cell with black text mean?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Why must you never select one column and sort it?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. What is the difference between a stop and a warning dropdown?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Why do you paste values rather than ordinary cells into a Ref_ tab?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. In plain words, what does COUNTIF do?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "6. A row check shows more than one possible problem. Which message appears?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "7. Name four kinds of tab and say which ones you type in."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "8. What is the difference between a row check and the Checks tab?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "9. What does HOLD mean?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "10. Why must a mitigating control named in W06 exist in W05?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "11. Why must a population have an IPE result of Pass before sampling?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "12. When must you refresh the Ref_ tabs?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "13. What does the Stage switch in W14 change?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "14. What must a deficiency have before it can be Closed in W13?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "15. Name the key created in W09 and the key created in W12."}]}, {"title": "Section 2: Practical (60 marks)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Section 2: Practical (60 marks)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Task", "Instructions", "Marks"], ["P1", "In a copy of W04, enter a new risk in the first empty row: RISK-ID R-TEST-002; BP-ID BP-P2P-02; category ITAC; statement \"There is a risk that a test happens because of a cause, resulting in an effect.\"; assertion Accuracy; owner Head of AP; likelihood 2; impact 2; SOX relevant Y; source Walkthrough; status Draft. State the rating, the band and the Row check.", "15"], ["P2", "In a copy of W07, delete the row for C-BPC-P2P-003 from Ref_CTRL. What happens in Owner_Matrix, and what does the Checks tab show? Restore it.", "15"], ["P3", "In W10 Test_Plan, filter the Result column. How many tests are Exception noted and how many are Ineffective? Of the Ineffective ones, which are operating tests and which are design tests?", "15"], ["P4", "In W13, list the open deficiencies (status Remediation in progress) with their groups and due dates.", "15"]]}]}, {"title": "Section 3: Trace (10 marks)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Section 3: Trace (10 marks)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Trace DEF-029 backward to a server. Give the test, control, risk, process and system, and say whether the deficiency is closed and how that was shown."}]}, {"title": "Assessment Answer Key and Marking Guide", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Assessment Answer Key and Marking Guide"}]}, {"title": "Section 1", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Section 1"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Q", "Answer", "Marks"], ["1", "Yellow with blue text is an input cell; grey with black text is a formula", "2"], ["2", "Only that column would move and every row would describe the wrong record", "2"], ["3", "Stop refuses a value not on the list; warning allows it after a prompt, and the row check then flags an unknown key", "2"], ["4", "Normal pasting brings formulas that point to the source file and formatting that overwrites the target", "2"], ["5", "It counts the cells in a range that equal a given value", "2"], ["6", "The message of the first failing test, in the order the tests are written", "2"], ["7", "Any four of: Instructions (read), register (type), Ref_ (paste values), helper (read), editable list (type), Lists (edit values), Checks (read)", "2"], ["8", "A row check tests one record; the Checks tab tests the workbook against its gate", "2"], ["9", "The population has not passed its IPE check; do not sample from it", "2"], ["10", "Because a mitigation must be a real, owned, tested control, and the row check flags a mitigation that is not in W05", "2"], ["11", "A sample drawn from an incomplete or inaccurate population is unreliable", "2"], ["12", "At every gate and whenever an upstream record changes, before reading the Checks tabs", "2"], ["13", "It switches the coverage checks between scoping (SCOPING shown) and testing (each must pass), and it feeds the conclusion", "2"], ["14", "A retest TEST-ID showing the remediated control operating", "2"], ["15", "EV-ID (and IPE-ID) in W09; SMP-ID in W12", "2"]]}]}, {"title": "Section 2", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Section 2"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Task", "Model answer", "Marking"], ["P1", "Rating 2 × 2 = 4; band Medium; Row check OK. The row is complete because SOX is Y and assertion and owner are filled, the BP-ID is in Ref_BP, and the statement uses the formula", "Entry correct 10; rating, band and check 5"], ["P2", "The Owner_Matrix row for C-BPC-P2P-003 shows \"CTRL-ID not in W05\" and its looked-up columns go blank; the Checks line \"Rows needing attention\" shows 1. Restoring the row clears it", "Row check 5; lookups 5; Checks and restore 5"], ["P3", "21 tests are Exception noted; 6 are Ineffective. The Ineffective operating tests are C-ITGC-AC-005-TOE and C-ITGC-SOD-001-TOE; the four Ineffective design tests are C-BPC-P2P-003-TOD, C-BPC-O2C-001-TOD, C-BPC-R2R-004-TOD and C-ITAC-012-TOD", "Counts 5; operating 5; design 5"], ["P4", "DEF-004 GR-F 18 Dec; DEF-016 GR-E 11 Dec; DEF-018 GR-E 11 Dec; DEF-021 GR-E 11 Dec; DEF-022 GR-E 11 Dec; DEF-023 GR-E 11 Dec; DEF-024 GR-E 11 Dec; DEF-025 GR-E 18 Dec; DEF-027 GR-D 27 Nov (2026)", "All nine 10; groups and dates 5"]]}]}, {"title": "Section 3", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Section 3"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Step", "Answer"], ["Test", "C-ITAC-006-TOE (Exception noted)"], ["Control", "C-ITAC-006: pricing procedure and condition maintenance locked; owner Commercial Manager"], ["Risk / process", "R-O2C-003, sales invoiced at unauthorised prices / BP-O2C-02"], ["System", "SYS-03 (CEP, client 300, production)"], ["Status and proof", "Closed; the retest C-ITAC-006-RT1 was Effective after the sales support role lost its pricing authorisation"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Marking: test 2, control 2, risk and process 2, system 2, status and proof 2. A pass is 80 marks or more."}]}, {"title": "Answer Key to Part 3 Exercises", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Answer Key to Part 3 Exercises"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Answer"], ["Session 11 A", "5 requests (the five whose primary control is C-ITGC-PC-001) show \"CTRL-ID not in W05\" and their Evidence owner column goes blank"], ["Session 11 B", "W05, W06, W07, W08, W09, W10, W11, W12, W13 and W14, and its risk R-P2P-006 is in W04"], ["Session 11 C", "Refresh in workbook order: W02, W04, W05, W06, W07, W08, W09, W10, W11, W12, W13, then paste the five W14 Ref_ tabs last"], ["Session 12 A", "DEF-004 from C-ITGC-AC-007-TOE (Exception noted); control C-ITGC-AC-007 (DBA access restricted and reviewed); risk R-TECH-009; process BP-TECH-04; system SYS-06 (HANA database), with servers cep-hdb01 (Houston) and cep-hdb02 (Dallas); status Remediation in progress, due 18 December 2026; retest C-ITGC-AC-007-RT1 planned"], ["Session 12 B", "C-ITGC-PC-001: Exception noted, supported with additional procedures (full-period transport population compared to approved tickets; ITAC-table transports inspected). C-ITGC-PC-002: Exception noted, supported with additional procedures (DBTABLOG change logs for the full period; opening windows analysed). C-ITGC-AC-005: Ineffective, supported with additional procedures (usage analysis of unapproved SAP_ALL holders; table change logs)"], ["Session 12 C", "INT-03 field production volumes; process BP-O2C-02; risk R-O2C-002; controls C-BPC-O2C-001 (Full) and C-ITGC-CO-002 (Partial); the failed test is C-BPC-O2C-001-TOD (Ineffective); the deficiency is DEF-016, open, due 11 December 2026"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Check yourself: S11 (1) Each workbook's Ref_ tabs must be current before the next workbook uses them. (2) Re-paste it from the source workbook. (3) TEST-ID, SMP-ID and DEF-ID. S12 (1) The keys: you read the key off a row and look for it in the next workbook. (2) Because a system is where every item starts; there is nothing earlier in the chain. (3) The general control had exceptions or failed, but other evidence recorded in the dependency row shows the configuration did not change, so the automated control can still be relied on."}]}, {"title": "Appendix A — Quick Reference Card", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix A — Quick Reference Card"}]}, {"title": "Colours and statuses", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Colours and statuses"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Meaning"], ["Yellow, blue text", "Input: type or select"], ["Grey, black text", "Formula: never type"], ["PASS", "Count is zero (or above zero where a minimum is needed)"], ["REVIEW", "A gate condition is not met: fix and re-check"], ["INFO", "A count to read and judge"], ["HOLD", "Population not Pass: do not sample"], ["SCOPING", "Testing-stage check not yet applicable (W14)"]]}]}, {"title": "Rules to remember", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Rules to remember"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Work in a copy. Never sort part of a table. Type keys exactly."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Paste values only, starting at A2 of a Ref_ tab. Copy only rows that hold keys."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Fix row checks first, in the order the messages appear; then read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "A Closed deficiency needs a retest. A population needs IPE Pass before sampling."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Refresh Ref_ tabs in workbook order before reading any Checks or the dashboard."}]}, {"title": "The 14 workbooks at a glance", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "The 14 workbooks at a glance"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Workbook", "Main key", "What it records"], ["W01", "SYS-ID", "Systems and clients"], ["W02", "INT-ID", "Interfaces and data flows"], ["W03", "BP-ID", "Processes, technical areas, accounts"], ["W04", "RISK-ID", "Risks"], ["W05", "CTRL-ID", "Controls, links and reliance"], ["W06", "SOD-ID", "SoD rules and mitigations"], ["W07", "CTRL-ID", "Owners and evidence owners"], ["W08", "PBC-ID", "Evidence requests"], ["W09", "EV-ID, IPE-ID", "Evidence and completeness checks"], ["W10", "TEST-ID", "Tests, samples sizes and dates"], ["W11", "TEST-ID", "Workpapers"], ["W12", "SMP-ID", "Samples and items"], ["W13", "DEF-ID", "Deficiencies, groups, remediation"], ["W14", "none", "Scoping, coverage and conclusion"]]}]}, {"title": "Appendix B — Glossary", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "Appendix B — Glossary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Term", "Meaning"], ["Assertion", "What a financial statement item claims, for example Occurrence, Completeness, Accuracy, Cut-off"], ["BP-ID", "Key for a business process or technical area (W03)"], ["Checks tab", "The workbook's completion gate expressed as formulas"], ["Control", "One person or system doing one thing, at a stated frequency, producing evidence (W05)"], ["Coverage", "Whether every risk has a control, every key control a test, every exception a deficiency"], ["Deficiency", "A confirmed failure of a control, evaluated for severity (W13)"], ["Evidence ID (EV-ID)", "Key of an item of evidence in W09"], ["FSLI", "Financial statement line item"], ["Gate", "A checkpoint (G1 to G9) between stages"], ["Golden thread", "The chain of keys from system to conclusion"], ["IPE", "Information produced by the entity: reports and populations that must be proven complete and accurate"], ["ITAC", "Automated application control, such as a three-way match"], ["ITDM", "A manual control that relies on system data or a report"], ["ITGC", "General control over access, change or operations"], ["Key", "A unique code that identifies a record and links it to other workbooks"], ["Mitigation", "An approved, tested control that accepts an SoD conflict"], ["PBC", "Provided by client: an evidence request (W08)"], ["Population", "The complete list from which a sample is drawn"], ["Ref_ tab", "A tab holding keys pasted as values from an upstream workbook"], ["Reliance", "Trusting an automated control or report because the general controls around it worked or other evidence shows no unauthorised change"], ["Retest", "A repeat test after remediation, needed to close a deficiency"], ["Row check", "A formula column that tests one record"], ["SoD", "Segregation of duties"], ["SOX", "Sarbanes-Oxley: the requirement for management to assess internal control over financial reporting"], ["TOD / TOE", "Test of design / test of operating effectiveness"], ["Trace", "Following keys forward or backward through the workbooks"]]}]}], "word_count": 1004, "id": "tib-workbook-primer-part3-sessions-11-12-assessment", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/06_Core_Materials/TIB_Workbook_Primer_Part3_Sessions_11-12_Assessment.docx", "filename": "TIB_Workbook_Primer_Part3_Sessions_11-12_Assessment.docx", "category": "Core Materials", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "W01 Landscape System Inventory — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W01 — Landscape & System Inventory | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records every SAP system, client and supporting component that holds financial data or processes business transactions, and states whether each is in scope and whether it has actually been validated. W01 is the root of the golden thread: every other workbook points back to a SYS-ID."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Enter the client-provided landscape in System_Inventory with Validation status = Documented only."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Validate directly (System > Status, SCC4, SM51, DB02). Upgrade the status to Validated and add the evidence ref."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Record application servers and hosts in Server_Detail, and the STMS route in Transport_Routes."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Decide scope and record a rationale for every exclusion."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Log every unresolved question in Open_Items. Finish by reading the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every known component is listed with SID, client and environment."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every row has In scope = Y/N with a rationale for N."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every Validated row has an evidence reference; every other row has an open question."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The PRD transport route is recorded."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 2 (W01)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "SYS-ID", "Unique key for the system/client", "SYS-nn; unique; never reused", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "SID", "SAP system identifier", "3 characters; must match System > Status", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Client", "Logical client number", "3 digits; must exist in SCC4", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Environment", "Landscape tier", "DEV / QAS / PRD / SBX / TRN", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Product & release", "Installed SAP product and release", "e.g. S/4HANA 2022 FPS02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Component type", "Technical role of the component", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Hosting model", "Where it runs; drives which ITGCs ABC vs the provider performs", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Database & OS", "Underlying platform", "From DB02 / Basis", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Primary purpose", "Business use the system supports", "One line", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Financially relevant?", "Holds or processes data reaching the financial statements", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "In scope?", "Included in this engagement", "Y / N / TBC; PRD financial system = Y unless justified", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Scope rationale", "Reason for inclusion or exclusion", "Mandatory if In scope = N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "System owner", "Accountable owner (usually Basis/IT lead)", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Validation method", "How the row was confirmed", "System > Status / SCC4 / SM51 / client inventory / interview", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Validation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Validated requires an evidence ref", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Evidence ref", "Screenshot or document supporting the row", "EV-ID or filename; mandatory if Validated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Open question", "Anything still to confirm", "Mirror into Open_Items", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Server ID", "Unique key for the server record", "SRV-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "SYS-ID", "System the server belongs to", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "SID (lookup)", "SID pulled from System_Inventory", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Host name", "Server host name", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Role", "Server role", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "OS", "Operating system and version", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Location / hosting", "Data centre, site or cloud region", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Owner", "Accountable infrastructure owner", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Validated via", "Evidence source", "SM51 / DB02 / inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Evidence ref", "Supporting evidence", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Route ID", "Unique route reference", "TR-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Landscape", "Landscape / transport group", "e.g. ECC-S4 landscape", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "From SYS-ID", "Source system", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "From env (lookup)", "Environment of source", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "To SYS-ID", "Target system", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "To env (lookup)", "Environment of target", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Route type", "STMS route type", "Consolidation / Delivery", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Direct DEV→PRD?", "Flags a route that bypasses QAS", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Evidence ref", "STMS route display screenshot", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 791, "id": "w01-landscape-system-inventory-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/01_Environment/W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx", "filename": "W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W02 Interface Data Flow Inventory — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W02 — Interface & Data Flow Inventory | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Identifies each financially relevant interface, how it works, how errors are caught and who owns it. Also captures RFC destinations and service users, which are privileged access paths in their own right."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste the in-scope SYS-IDs from W01 into the Ref_SYS tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Ask by process, not technology: what comes in from outside SAP, and what leaves? Enter each flow in Interface_Register."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Confirm mechanism and technical user with Basis (WE20, SM59, PI/PO or CPI lists, SM37 file jobs)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Record honestly how failures are detected, including \"None\"."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Complete RFC_Service_Users and Data_Flow_Map; back-fill BP-IDs after W03."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every financially relevant inbound and outbound flow is listed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every interface has a technology, a monitoring answer and an owner (or TBC)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "RFC / service users are listed and cross-referenced."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Interfaces with no monitoring or reconciliation are visible for W04."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 3 (W02)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "INT-ID", "Unique key", "INT-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Interface name", "Business name", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Source", "Where data originates", "SYS-ID or named external system", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Target", "Where data lands", "SYS-ID or named external system; at least one side must be an in-scope SYS-ID", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Direction", "Flow direction", "Inbound / Outbound / Bidirectional", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Technology", "How data moves", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Data object", "What is transferred", "e.g. MT940 statements, ORDERS05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "BP-ID", "Process the data feeds", "Back-fill after W03", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Frequency & volume", "How often and how much", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Financially relevant?", "Data reaches the financial statements", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Error monitoring", "How failures are detected", "\"None\" is a valid, important answer", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Reconciliation control?", "Completeness/accuracy check between source and target", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Interface owner", "Accountable person", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "RFC / service user", "Technical user used", "User ID; cross-ref RFC_Service_Users", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Evidence ref", "Supporting document", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Open question / rationale", "Still to confirm, or reason for exclusion", "Mandatory if In scope = N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Risk flag", "Whether the interface feeds a W04 risk", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "RFC ID", "Unique key", "RFC-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "INT-ID", "Interface using the connection", "Must exist in Interface_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Interface (lookup)", "Interface name", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "RFC destination / connection", "SM59 destination or connection name", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Technical user", "User the connection logs on with", "User ID", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "User type", "USR02 user type", "Dialog / System / Communication / Service", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Authorisation level", "Roles or profiles held", "e.g. SAP_ALL, narrow technical role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Stored credentials?", "Password stored in the destination", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Review flag", "Link to W06 sensitive access", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Flow ID", "Unique key", "FL-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "BP-ID", "Process the flow belongs to", "From W03", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Flow description", "What data moves and why", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Source", "Where it starts", "System or external", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Target", "Where it ends", "System or external", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "INT-ID", "Interface carrying it", "INT-ID, or N/A if entirely in-system", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Coverage", "Is the flow covered by the register", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 732, "id": "w02-interface-data-flow-inventory-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/01_Environment/W02_Interface_Data_Flow_Inventory.xlsx", "filename": "W02_Interface_Data_Flow_Inventory.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W03 Module Business Process Scope — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W03 — Module & Business Process Scope | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Separates the SAP module from the business process and ties each process to its systems, interfaces and the financial statement areas it affects. The BP-ID created here anchors every risk in W04."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Start from the financial statements: enter significant accounts in FSLI_Map."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Map each account to the process that creates its balance; break each process into sub-processes with a BP-ID in Process_Scope."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Record modules, SYS-IDs (W01), INT-IDs (W02), owners and indicative transactions."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Add the technical areas BP-TECH-01 to 03 in Technical_Areas."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Mark confirmation status honestly. Unspecified processes are Outstanding, never guessed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every significant FSLI maps to at least one in-scope process."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every sub-process has a BP-ID, modules, SYS-IDs and a confirmation status."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Technical areas BP-TECH-01 to 03 exist."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Unspecified processes are marked Outstanding."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 4 (W03)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "BP-ID", "Unique key for the sub-process", "BP-…; unique", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Business process", "End-to-end process", "R2R / P2P / O2C / INV / H2R / TECH", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Sub-process", "Specific activity within the process", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Module(s)", "SAP modules or functional areas used", "FI, CO, MM, SD, HCM, Basis/Security", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Supporting SYS-ID(s)", "Systems the sub-process runs on", "SYS-IDs from W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Related INT-ID(s)", "Interfaces feeding the sub-process", "INT-IDs from W02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Significant FSLI / accounts", "Financial statement areas affected", "From FSLI_Map", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Significance", "Why it matters to the audit", "High / Medium / Low", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Process owner", "Accountable business owner", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Indicative transactions", "Key t-codes / Fiori apps", "Used later for SoD and access testing", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Confirmation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Source-identified / Confirmed / Partially confirmed / Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Scoping note", "Reason for inclusion or open question", "Mandatory if not in scope or Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "BP-ID", "Unique key for the sub-process", "BP-TECH-…; unique", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Business process", "End-to-end process", "R2R / P2P / O2C / INV / H2R / TECH", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Sub-process", "Specific activity within the process", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Module(s)", "SAP modules or functional areas used", "FI, CO, MM, SD, HCM, Basis/Security", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Supporting SYS-ID(s)", "Systems the sub-process runs on", "SYS-IDs from W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Related INT-ID(s)", "Interfaces feeding the sub-process", "INT-IDs from W02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Significant FSLI / accounts", "Financial statement areas affected", "From FSLI_Map", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Significance", "Why it matters to the audit", "High / Medium / Low", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Process owner", "Accountable business owner", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Indicative transactions", "Key t-codes / Fiori apps", "Used later for SoD and access testing", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Confirmation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Source-identified / Confirmed / Partially confirmed / Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Scoping note", "Reason for inclusion or open question", "Mandatory if not in scope or Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "FSLI ID", "Unique key", "FSLI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "FSLI / account", "Significant line item or account", "From audit plan", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Significant?", "Above scoping threshold", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Primary process", "Process that creates the balance", "R2R / P2P / O2C / INV / H2R", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Mapped BP-ID(s)", "Sub-processes that feed the account", "BP-IDs from Process_Scope, comma separated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Assertions", "Relevant assertions", "Existence, Completeness, Accuracy, …", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Check", "Is the account mapped to a process", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 770, "id": "w03-module-business-process-scope-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/01_Environment/W03_Module_Business_Process_Scope.xlsx", "filename": "W03_Module_Business_Process_Scope.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W04 Risk Register — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W04 — Risk Register | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "States, for each in-scope process and technical area, what could go wrong, who owns the risk and how significant it is. A risk is an exposure, never a finding: nothing here says a control has failed."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste the BP-IDs from W03 (BP_Index tab, values only) into the Ref_BP tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Start from the TIB risk library or the client RCM; tailor to each process and technical area."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Write each risk with the formula: There is a risk that … because … resulting in …"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Assign category, assertions, owner and likelihood/impact. Mark SOX-relevant risks."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Agree owners with the business and read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every in-scope BP-ID, including technical areas, has at least one risk."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every risk follows the formula, has a category, assertions and a risk owner (or TBC)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "SOX-relevant risks are flagged."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "No risk statement contains an effectiveness conclusion."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 5 (W04)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "RISK-ID", "Unique key", "R-<process>-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "BP-ID", "Process or technical area the risk belongs to", "Must exist in W03 (Ref_BP)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "SYS-ID / INT-ID", "System or interface where the risk arises", "Keys from W01 / W02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk category", "Type of risk; guides control category in W05", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk statement", "What could go wrong", "\"There is a risk that … because … resulting in …\"; never a finding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Assertion(s)", "Financial statement assertions affected", "Occurrence, Completeness, Accuracy, Cut-off, Valuation, Existence, Rights & obligations, Presentation", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk owner", "Accountable for the outcome", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent likelihood", "Before controls", "1 Low / 2 Medium / 3 High", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent impact", "Before controls", "1 Low / 2 Medium / 3 High", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent rating", "Likelihood × impact", "Formula; 1–9", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Rating band", "High ≥ 6, Medium 3–5, Low < 3", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "SOX relevant?", "Could lead to a material misstatement", "Y / N; drives key-control requirement in W05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk source", "Where the risk came from", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Status", "Agreement status", "No \"Failed\" value exists", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Notes", "Limitations or follow-ups", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 466, "id": "w04-risk-register-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/02_Risk_and_Control_Design/W04_Risk_Register.xlsx", "filename": "W04_Risk_Register.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W05 Risk Control Matrix — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W05 — Risk & Control Matrix (RCM) | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The centre of the foundation. Defines the specific control that answers each risk, classifies it, names its owner, and records the SAP mechanism, expected evidence and test approach. Everything in the Test phase (W10 to W12) is generated from RCM rows."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste RISK-IDs and SOX flags from W04 into the Ref_RISK tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. For each risk, record what actually prevents or detects it. Write each control in the five-part form in Control_Register."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Link controls to risks in Risk_Control_Map (one row per pair; the register is many-to-many)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. For every ITAC, IPE and ITDM control, list the ITGCs it relies on in ITGC_Dependency."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Read Coverage_Check: any SOX risk without a key control is a design gap."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every risk in W04 maps to at least one control; every SOX-relevant risk maps to at least one key control."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every control has a five-part description, single category, nature, frequency and owner."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every ITAC, IPE and ITDM control lists its dependent ITGCs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Expected evidence is defined for every control, ready to drive W08."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 6 (W05); Part 3B, Session 33 (reliance)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "CTRL-ID", "Unique key", "C-<category>-nnn, e.g. C-ITGC-AC-002, C-ITAC-004", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control title", "Short name", "e.g. Timely removal of leavers", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control description", "Who does what, when, how, with what evidence", "Five-part description: who / what / when / how / evidence", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control area", "Grouping", "From Control_Areas tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control category", "Classification", "One value only", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Type", "When it acts", "Preventive / Detective", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Nature", "How it operates", "Must agree with category; ITAC = Automated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Frequency", "How often it operates; drives sample size in W10", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Key control?", "Relied on to address a SOX risk", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SOX in scope?", "Requires formal TOD and TOE", "Y if key control for a SOX-relevant risk", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control owner", "Person or role performing the control", "Name / role; carried into W07", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SYS-ID", "Where the control operates", "From W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SAP mechanism", "Where the control lives in SAP", "t-code, config path, table, GRC component", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Dependent ITGCs", "ITGCs this control relies on", "CTRL-ID list; mandatory for ITAC, IPE, ITDM", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "IPE used", "Reports the control relies on", "Each IPE needs completeness & accuracy testing", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Expected evidence", "What the control produces", "Feeds W08 PBC requests", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Test approach", "Planned procedure", "Inquiry / Observation / Inspection / Reperformance; TOD + TOE", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Planned session", "Where it is tested in the course", "Part 3 session reference", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Risks mapped", "Number of risks linked in Risk_Control_Map", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Map ID", "Unique key for the link", "M-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "RISK-ID", "Risk from W04", "Must exist in Ref_RISK", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "CTRL-ID", "Control from Control_Register", "Must exist in Control_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Coverage", "Does the control fully address the risk", "Full / Partial", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Rationale", "Why this control answers this risk", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Risk SOX? (lookup)", "SOX flag of the risk (W04)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Key control? (lookup)", "Key flag of the control", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dep ID", "Unique key", "D-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dependent CTRL-ID", "ITAC / IPE / ITDM control that relies on an ITGC", "Must exist in Control_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dependent category", "Category (lookup)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC CTRL-ID", "ITGC being relied on", "CTRL-ID beginning C-ITGC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC?", "Is the dependency really an ITGC", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC test result", "Result from W10 / W11", "Updated in Part 3 sessions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Additional procedures", "Logs, usage analysis, etc. used to restore reliance", "Free text; see Session 33", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Reliance decision", "Outcome for the dependent control", "Pending until ITGC tested", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 739, "id": "w05-risk-control-matrix-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/02_Risk_and_Control_Design/W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx", "filename": "W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W06 SoD Sensitive Access Matrix — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W06 — SoD & Sensitive Access Rule Matrix | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Translates each SoD and sensitive-access risk from W04 into technical terms: which transactions and authorisation objects make up each side of a conflict. The access analysis in Part 3A runs these rules against the user population."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste RISK-IDs and categories from W04 into Ref_RISK, and CTRL-IDs from W05 into Ref_CTRL."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Define business functions with their transactions (Functions) and permission detail (Function_Auth)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Define each conflict pair in SoD_Rules, linked to a W04 SoD risk. List critical single-item access in Sensitive_Access."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Record approved mitigations (must be real controls in W05) and the approved ruleset version."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Include custom Z-transactions and Fiori equivalents. Read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every SoD risk in W04 maps to at least one SOD-ID, and every rule maps to a risk."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Rules carry permission-level detail and include custom transactions."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Sensitive access items are listed with allowed holders."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Mitigations point to real CTRL-IDs; the ruleset version is approved."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 7 (W06)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Function ID", "Unique key", "FN-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Function name", "Business function", "e.g. Vendor master maintenance", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "BP-ID", "Process the function belongs to", "From W03", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Transactions (t-codes)", "Standard transactions", "e.g. BP, XK01, XK02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Fiori apps / custom Z-codes", "Equivalent apps and custom transactions", "Include Z-transactions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Description", "What the function allows", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Auth ID", "Unique key", "AU-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Function ID", "Function the permission belongs to", "Must exist in Functions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Function (lookup)", "Function name", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Transaction", "Transaction code", "t-code", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Auth object", "Authorisation object", "e.g. F_LFA1_BUK", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Field", "e.g. ACTVT", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Values", "Field values that enable the action", "e.g. 01, 02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "SOD-ID", "Unique key", "SOD-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Function A", "First function", "Must exist in Functions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Function B", "Second function", "Must exist in Functions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Function A (lookup)", "Name", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Function B (lookup)", "Name", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Conflict description", "Why the combination is dangerous", "Must match the W04 risk", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "RISK-ID", "Risk the rule tests", "Must exist in W04, category SoD", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "BP-ID", "Process affected", "From W03", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Risk level", "Severity", "Critical / High / Medium", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Rule level", "How it is evaluated; permission level preferred", "Transaction level / Permission level", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Org-level scope", "Does the conflict apply across or within the same org unit", "Cross-org / Same org", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Mitigating CTRL-ID", "Accepted mitigation for justified conflicts", "CTRL-ID in W05, or None", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Rule owner", "Business owner who approved the rule", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "In scope?", "Run in this engagement", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "SENS-ID", "Unique key", "SENS-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "Access item", "Transaction, profile or auth object value", "e.g. SAP_ALL; S_TABU_DIS ACTVT 02 with SE16N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "Why sensitive", "Impact if held inappropriately", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "RISK-ID", "Risk tested", "From W04", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "Allowed holders", "Who may legitimately hold it", "e.g. Firefighter IDs only", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "Related CTRL-ID", "Control that governs it", "From W05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Mit ID", "Unique key", "MIT-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Mitigating CTRL-ID", "Control that mitigates", "Must exist in W05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "SOD-ID covered", "Rule the mitigation covers", "Must exist in SoD_Rules", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Mitigated users", "Users carrying the conflict", "Count or list", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Valid from", "Start date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Valid to", "Expiry date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Approver", "Who approved", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Control tested effective?", "Result from W10", "Y / N / Pending", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Version", "Ruleset version", "e.g. 1.0", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Source", "Where the ruleset came from", "GRC ARA export / manual / TIB baseline", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Approved by", "Business approver", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Approval date", "Date approved", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Custom Z-transactions included?", "Z-codes and Fiori equivalents covered", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Notes", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 773, "id": "w06-sod-sensitive-access-matrix-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/02_Risk_and_Control_Design/W06_SoD_Sensitive_Access_Matrix.xlsx", "filename": "W06_SoD_Sensitive_Access_Matrix.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W07 Control Evidence Owner Matrix — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W07 — Control & Evidence Owner Matrix | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records, for every control in the RCM, who is accountable for it, who performs it, who can provide the evidence and who the backup is. These are often different people. Keyed by CTRL-ID so it lines up one-to-one with W05."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste CTRL-ID, title, evidence expectation, key flag and risk owner from W05/W04 into Ref_CTRL."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Add one Owner_Matrix row per CTRL-ID. Pre-fill the control owner from W05."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Meet each owner. Ask two questions: who makes sure this happens, and who can give us the evidence?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Record walkthrough contacts for key controls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Mark unresolved items Outstanding with a follow-up, never as a control failure."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every in-scope CTRL-ID has a row."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Control owner and evidence owner are identified or marked Outstanding."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Walkthrough contacts are known for every key control."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Outstanding owners are escalated in the W14 dependency list."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 8 (W07)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "CTRL-ID", "Control from the RCM", "Must exist in W05 (Ref_CTRL)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Control title", "Lookup from W05", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Key control?", "Lookup from W05", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Risk owner", "Lookup from W04 via Ref_CTRL", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Control owner", "Accountable for the control operating", "Required, or mark Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Control performer", "Person who executes it day to day", "May equal owner", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Evidence owner", "Person or team who can produce the evidence", "Required, or mark Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Backup contact", "Alternate for leave or turnover", "Recommended", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Primary SYS-ID", "Where evidence resides", "From W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Expected evidence", "Lookup from W05", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Walkthrough contact", "Person who will demonstrate the control in TOD", "Usually the performer", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Owner status", "Confirmation state; Outstanding is not a failure", "Confirmed / Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Date confirmed", "When the owner agreed", "Date; required if Confirmed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Follow-up", "What still needs resolving", "Required if Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 497, "id": "w07-control-evidence-owner-matrix-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/02_Risk_and_Control_Design/W07_Control_Evidence_Owner_Matrix.xlsx", "filename": "W07_Control_Evidence_Owner_Matrix.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W08 PBC Evidence Request Register — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W08 — PBC / Evidence Request Register | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Lists every piece of evidence requested, from whom, for which period and for which control and test. Every request must answer a future testing question. If it cannot be traced to a CTRL-ID and a planned test, do not make it."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste CTRL-ID, title, evidence owner (W07), key flag (W05) into Ref_CTRL."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. For each in-scope key control, read Expected evidence and Test approach in W05 and write the wave 1 and 2 requests (configuration and populations)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Make each request precise: system, client, period, fields, format. Consolidate requests that serve several controls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Issue, then track daily. Update Status and add EV-IDs as items are indexed in W09."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Raise wave 3 sample requests only after W12 sampling is complete."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every request traces to at least one CTRL-ID (and TEST-ID once W10 exists)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every request states system, period and required fields."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Population requests are flagged for IPE checks."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Outstanding requests are visible with owner and due date."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 9 (W08)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "PBC-ID", "Unique key", "PBC-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Request wave", "Timing of the request", "1 Scoping / 2 Population / 3 Sample / 4 Follow-up", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Primary CTRL-ID", "Main control the evidence supports", "Must exist in W05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Other CTRL-ID(s)", "Other controls served by the same request", "CTRL-ID list", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "TEST-ID(s)", "Tests that will use it", "Back-filled when W10 is built; required for wave 3", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "BP-ID / SYS-ID", "Where the evidence applies", "Keys from W03 / W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Requested evidence", "Exactly what is needed: system, client, period, fields, format", "Specific enough to be unambiguous", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Period from", "Start of period or as-of date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Period to", "End of period", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Evidence type", "Request type; Population triggers an IPE check", "Population / Sample / Configuration / Policy", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Evidence owner", "From W07 (lookup via Ref_CTRL)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Date requested", "Tracking", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Due date", "Agreed due date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Priority", "Urgency; High for populations", "High / Medium / Low", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Status", "Progress", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "EV-ID(s)", "Evidence received, once indexed in W09", "Required once Received", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "IPE needed?", "Population requests need completeness and accuracy checks", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Days overdue", "Today minus due date while not received", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Notes", "Completeness requirements or follow-up", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 506, "id": "w08-pbc-evidence-request-register-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/03_Evidence/W08_PBC_Evidence_Request_Register.xlsx", "filename": "W08_PBC_Evidence_Request_Register.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W09 Evidence Index IPE Log — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W09 — Evidence Index & IPE Log | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The filing system. Every item received gets an EV-ID, its source and extraction details are recorded, the original is preserved, and population evidence is checked for completeness and accuracy before any sample is drawn."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste PBC-ID, period from, period to and evidence type from W08 into Ref_PBC."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Log every item received in Evidence_Index using the filename convention EV-ID_SYS_description_date.ext. Keep the raw original untouched."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. For each population, record the completeness and accuracy work in IPE_Log."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Set the IPE result. No sample may be drawn until the result is Pass."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Back-fill TEST-IDs once W10 exists. Update W08 status with EV-IDs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every received item has an EV-ID, standard filename and preserved original."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every population has an IPE check recorded."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "No sample is drawn from a population with an IPE result other than Pass."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W08 status is updated with EV-IDs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 10 (W09)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "EV-ID", "Unique key", "EV-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "PBC-ID", "Request it answers", "Must exist in W08 (Ref_PBC)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "PBC period from", "Requested period start (lookup)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "PBC period to", "Requested period end (lookup)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Filename", "Stored file name", "<EV-ID>_<SYS>_<description>_<date>.ext", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Description", "What the file contains", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Source SYS-ID", "System it was extracted from", "From W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Extraction method", "How it was produced", "t-code / report / table / GRC report / ITSM export / document", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Extracted by", "Who ran it", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Extraction date", "When it was run", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Period covered from", "Start date in the file", "Date; must cover PBC period", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Period covered to", "End date in the file", "Date; must cover PBC period", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Record count", "Rows in the population", "Number; used for completeness check", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Received date", "Date logged", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Original preserved?", "Raw copy stored read-only", "Must be Y", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "IPE required?", "Population requests need IPE checks", "Formula from W08 evidence type", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "IPE result", "Outcome of completeness & accuracy check", "Pass required before sampling", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Used by TEST-ID(s)", "Tests relying on the file", "Back-filled from W10", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "IPE-ID", "Unique key", "IPE-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "EV-ID", "Population being tested", "Must exist in Evidence_Index", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Population (lookup)", "Description from Evidence_Index", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Record count (lookup)", "Rows in the file", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Completeness procedure", "How completeness was established", "Observed extraction, parameter screenshot, no filters", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Reconciled to", "Independent source used", "e.g. SUIM total, STMS queue, HR headcount report", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Source count", "Count in the independent source", "Number", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Count difference", "File count minus source count", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Accuracy procedure", "How accuracy was tested", "Records traced both ways", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Records traced", "Number of records traced", "Number", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Parameters evidenced?", "Selection parameters captured", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Tester", "Who performed it", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Test date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Result", "Outcome", "Pass / Fail / Pending", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 607, "id": "w09-evidence-index-ipe-log-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/03_Evidence/W09_Evidence_Index_IPE_Log.xlsx", "filename": "W09_Evidence_Index_IPE_Log.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W10 Test Plan — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W10 — Test Plan / Testing Program | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Converts the RCM into a schedule of tests. Every in-scope key control gets a test of design (TOD) and a test of operating effectiveness (TOE) with attributes, procedure, population, sample size, period, tester, reviewer and dates."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste controls from W05 into Ref_CTRL, and evidence IDs with IPE result and record count from W09 into Ref_EV."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Filter W05 for SOX in scope = Y. Create a TOD and a TOE row for each key control; derive attributes from the control description."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Set sample size from the Sample_Guide (edit the guide to match engagement methodology). Mark ITGC reliance."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Assign testers and reviewers (different people). Schedule ITGC tests before dependent ITAC/IPE tests."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Update Status and Result only after review. Read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every SOX in-scope key control has a TOD and a TOE row."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every row has attributes derived from the control description."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Sample sizes follow the guide or a justified deviation."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "ITGC tests are scheduled before dependent ITAC/IPE tests."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 11 (W10)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "TEST-ID", "Unique key", "<CTRL-ID>-TOD or -TOE", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "CTRL-ID", "Control being tested", "From W05 (Ref_CTRL)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Control title", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Control category", "Lookup; drives workpaper template", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Frequency", "Lookup; drives sample size", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test type", "TOD or TOE", "One TOD and one TOE per key control", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test attributes", "Conditions each sample item must meet", "Lettered A, B, C…; derived from the control description", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test procedure", "What the tester does", "Inquiry / Observation / Inspection / Reperformance; inquiry alone is never enough", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Population EV-ID", "Population sampled from (TOE)", "IPE must be Pass", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "IPE result", "Lookup from W09", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Population size", "Lookup from W09", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Guide sample", "Typical range for the frequency", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Sample size", "Items to test", "Number; justify deviations from the guide", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test period from", "Start of period", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test period to", "End of period", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "ITGC reliance needed?", "Dependent controls need effective ITGCs", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Tester", "Who performs", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Reviewer", "Who reviews; must differ from tester", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Planned start", "Schedule", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Planned end", "Schedule", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Workpaper ref", "W11 workpaper", "File name or WP ref", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Status", "Progress", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Result", "Outcome (only after review)", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 509, "id": "w10-test-plan-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/04_Testing/W10_Test_Plan.xlsx", "filename": "W10_Test_Plan.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W11 Test Workpapers — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W11 — Test Workpapers | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A workpaper records what the tester actually did, so a reviewer who was not involved can understand, reperform and reach the same conclusion. This file holds the standard workpaper structure, the list of 15 templates by control area, and a register of workpapers."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste TEST-IDs from W10 into Ref_TEST."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. For each test, copy the Workpaper tab (right-click > Move or Copy) and rename it with the TEST-ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Complete header, population/IPE, sample, procedure and attribute grid. Every Met or Exception needs an EV-ID reference."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Describe exceptions and root cause. The suggested result is a formula; the reviewer sets the final result."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Log each workpaper in WP_Register. Raise DEF-IDs in W13."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Each test has a completed workpaper in the standard structure."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every attribute result references an EV-ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Exceptions are explained with root cause."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The reviewer has signed off before the result is entered in W10."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 12 (W11); Parts 3A and 3B (templates)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "WP ref", "Workpaper reference", "e.g. WP-PC-01_<TEST-ID>", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "TEST-ID", "Test the workpaper supports", "Must exist in W10 (Ref_TEST)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Template", "Standard template used", "From Templates tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Tester", "Who performed", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Items tested", "From the Workpaper tab", "Number", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Items with exception", "From the Workpaper tab", "Number", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Result", "Outcome (after review)", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Reviewed by", "Reviewer", "Name; different from tester", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Review date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "DEF-ID(s) / rationale", "Deficiency raised, or false-positive rationale", "Required if Exception noted / Ineffective", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Status", "Progress", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 371, "id": "w11-test-workpapers-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/04_Testing/W11_Test_Workpapers.xlsx", "filename": "W11_Test_Workpapers.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W12 Sample Selection Log — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W12 — Sample Selection Log | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records exactly which items were chosen from which population, by what method and why. It lets a reviewer confirm the sample was unbiased and spread across the whole period, and it drives the wave 3 PBC requests."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste TEST-ID, population EV-ID, IPE result, population size and planned sample size from W10/W09 into Ref_TEST."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Create one Sample_Log row per test. Selection is only allowed from a population whose IPE result is Pass."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. List every selected item on the Items tab with its date and basis (Random / Systematic / Targeted / All items)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Record the seed or interval so the selection can be reperformed. Explain targeted additions."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Raise the wave 3 PBC request in W08 and enter its ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every TOE has a sample logged with method and parameters."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The sample covers the whole period."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Targeted additions are explained."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Wave 3 PBC requests are raised and cross-referenced."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 13 (W12)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "SMP-ID", "Unique key", "SMP-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "TEST-ID", "Test the sample is for", "From W10 (Ref_TEST)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Population EV-ID", "Lookup from W10", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "IPE result", "Lookup from W09", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Population size", "Lookup from W09", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Planned sample size", "Lookup from W10", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Items selected", "Count of items listed on the Items tab", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Selection method", "How chosen", "Random (seeded) preferred", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Seed / interval", "Parameter for reperformance", "Required for random / systematic", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Targeted additions", "Risk-based items added to the planned sample", "Number; explain in rationale", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Q1 items", "Items dated in Q1", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Q2 items", "Items dated in Q2", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Q3 items", "Items dated in Q3", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Q4 items", "Items dated in Q4", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Quarters covered", "Quarters with at least one item", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Period coverage confirmed?", "Sample spread across the whole period", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Wave 3 PBC-ID", "Request for supporting evidence", "Raised in W08", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Selected by", "Who selected", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Selection date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Rationale for targeted items / deviation", "Why items were added or the count differs", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "SMP-ID", "Sample the item belongs to", "Must exist in Sample_Log", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Item ID", "The item selected", "User ID, transport number, FF session ID, invoice no.", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Item date", "Date of the item (for period coverage)", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Selection basis", "Why selected", "Random / Systematic / Targeted / All items", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Quarter", "Calendar quarter of the item date", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Wave 3 evidence received?", "Supporting evidence for this item received", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 559, "id": "w12-sample-selection-log-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/04_Testing/W12_Sample_Selection_Log.xlsx", "filename": "W12_Sample_Selection_Log.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W13 Deficiency Remediation Log — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W13 — Deficiency & Remediation Log | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records every confirmed test exception, evaluates its severity (condition, criteria, cause, effect), links it back through the thread to the risk and process, groups related deficiencies, and tracks remediation and retest."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste TEST-ID, CTRL-ID, RISK-ID, BP-ID and the W10 result into Ref_TEST."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Validate each exception first: false positive, wrong population or later evidence? Only confirmed exceptions get a DEF-ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Write the four-part statement, record compensating controls and dependent controls affected, and set severity."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Aggregate related deficiencies in Groups (same user, date, process or cause) and evaluate the group."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Record management response, owner and due date; add the retest TEST-ID. A deficiency closes only after a successful retest."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every confirmed exception has a DEF-ID traced to test, control, risk and process."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Severity is evaluated, including impact on dependent controls and compensating controls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Management response, owner and due date are recorded."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Retest is planned for every remediated deficiency."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 4, Sessions 35–37 (W13)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "DEF-ID", "Unique key", "DEF-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "TEST-ID", "Test where it was found", "From W10 (Ref_TEST)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "CTRL-ID", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "RISK-ID", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "BP-ID", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Deficiency type", "Design or operating", "Design / Operating", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Condition", "What was found (facts, numbers, dates)", "Four-part statement", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Criteria", "What should have happened", "Control description or policy", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Cause", "Why it happened (from inquiry)", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Effect", "What could go wrong; link to risk and assertion", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Exceptions", "Failed items out of sample", "e.g. 3 of 25", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Dependent controls affected", "ITAC / IPE / ITDM relying on this ITGC", "From W05 ITGC_Dependency", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Compensating controls", "Other controls reducing the exposure", "CTRL-ID list", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Compensating tested effective?", "Result of testing the compensating control", "Y / N / Pending", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Severity", "Evaluated level", "Control deficiency / Significant deficiency / Material weakness", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Group ID", "Aggregation group", "From Groups tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Management response", "Owner's agreed action", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Remediation owner", "Who fixes it", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Due date", "Remediation due", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Retest TEST-ID", "Test confirming the fix", "e.g. <CTRL-ID>-RT1", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Status", "Progress", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Group ID", "Unique key", "GR-x", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Group name", "Short name", "e.g. Privileged access governance", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Common factor", "What links the deficiencies", "Same user, date, process, root cause", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Evaluated exposure", "Combined exposure after compensating controls and follow-up", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Group severity", "Severity of the group", "Reviewer approved", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Rationale", "Why this severity", "Magnitude, likelihood, compensating controls, reliance", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "DEFs in group", "Count", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Highest individual severity", "Highest rating among its DEFs", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 567, "id": "w13-deficiency-remediation-log-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/05_Conclusion/W13_Deficiency_Remediation_Log.xlsx", "filename": "W13_Deficiency_Remediation_Log.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W14 Scoping Workpaper Coverage Dashboard — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W14 — Scoping Workpaper & Coverage Dashboard | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The master record and a reviewer's first stop. At scoping stage it summarises what is in scope, what is confirmed, what is unconfirmed and which dependencies must be resolved. At testing stage it becomes the coverage dashboard: every risk covered, every key control tested, every deficiency evaluated."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Complete Engagement_Summary (client, systems, processes, period, materiality, stakeholders) and set Stage = Scoping."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Log known, unconfirmed and outstanding items, and access/evidence dependencies."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Paste the summary rows from W04/W05 (Ref_Risk_Control, Ref_Controls, Ref_Reliance), W10/W12 (Ref_Tests) and W13 (Ref_Deficiencies). No figure on the dashboard is typed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. At scoping stage the conclusion reads \"No conclusion at scoping stage\" and coverage checks 2–7 show SCOPING. Switch Stage to Testing when fieldwork starts."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Read the Checks tab and Coverage_Matrix before issuing any report."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Scoping stage: in-scope items, known/unconfirmed/outstanding and dependencies are complete, and the conclusion reads \"No conclusion at scoping stage\"."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Testing stage: every coverage check passes or the gap is explained."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "All figures are formulas linked to the pasted source data."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Sessions 15–16 (W14); Part 4, Session 38 (reporting)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Item ID", "Unique key", "KU-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Category", "Area", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Item", "The fact, unconfirmed matter or outstanding item", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Status", "Known = verified; Unconfirmed = reported but not verified; Outstanding = still to obtain", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Source", "Workbook and key", "e.g. W01 SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Owner", "Person to resolve", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Next step", "Action to resolve", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Dep ID", "Unique key", "DEP-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Type", "Kind of dependency", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Description", "What must be resolved before testing", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Source key", "Workbook and key", "e.g. W07 C-ITGC-AC-004", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Owner", "Person to resolve", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Target date", "Needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Blocks", "Gate or test it blocks", "e.g. G5; WP-AC-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Days overdue", "Today minus target while not closed", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 468, "id": "w14-scoping-workpaper-coverage-dashboard-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/05_Conclusion/W14_Scoping_Workpaper_Coverage_Dashboard.xlsx", "filename": "W14_Scoping_Workpaper_Coverage_Dashboard.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W01 Landscape System Inventory — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W01 — Landscape & System Inventory"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records every SAP system, client and supporting component that holds financial data or processes business transactions, and states whether each is in scope and whether it has actually been validated. W01 is the root of the golden thread: every other workbook points back to a SYS-ID."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Enter the client-provided landscape in System_Inventory with Validation status = Documented only."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Validate directly (System > Status, SCC4, SM51, DB02). Upgrade the status to Validated and add the evidence ref."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Record application servers and hosts in Server_Detail, and the STMS route in Transport_Routes."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Decide scope and record a rationale for every exclusion."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Log every unresolved question in Open_Items. Finish by reading the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every known component is listed with SID, client and environment."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every row has In scope = Y/N with a rationale for N."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every Validated row has an evidence reference; every other row has an open question."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The PRD transport route is recorded."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 2 (W01)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "SYS-ID", "Unique key for the system/client", "SYS-nn; unique; never reused", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "SID", "SAP system identifier", "3 characters; must match System > Status", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Client", "Logical client number", "3 digits; must exist in SCC4", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Environment", "Landscape tier", "DEV / QAS / PRD / SBX / TRN", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Product & release", "Installed SAP product and release", "e.g. S/4HANA 2022 FPS02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Component type", "Technical role of the component", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Hosting model", "Where it runs; drives which ITGCs ABC vs the provider performs", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Database & OS", "Underlying platform", "From DB02 / Basis", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Primary purpose", "Business use the system supports", "One line", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Financially relevant?", "Holds or processes data reaching the financial statements", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "In scope?", "Included in this engagement", "Y / N / TBC; PRD financial system = Y unless justified", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Scope rationale", "Reason for inclusion or exclusion", "Mandatory if In scope = N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "System owner", "Accountable owner (usually Basis/IT lead)", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Validation method", "How the row was confirmed", "System > Status / SCC4 / SM51 / client inventory / interview", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Validation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Validated requires an evidence ref", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Evidence ref", "Screenshot or document supporting the row", "EV-ID or filename; mandatory if Validated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Open question", "Anything still to confirm", "Mirror into Open_Items", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Server ID", "Unique key for the server record", "SRV-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "SYS-ID", "System the server belongs to", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "SID (lookup)", "SID pulled from System_Inventory", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Host name", "Server host name", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Role", "Server role", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "OS", "Operating system and version", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Location / hosting", "Data centre, site or cloud region", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Owner", "Accountable infrastructure owner", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Validated via", "Evidence source", "SM51 / DB02 / inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Evidence ref", "Supporting evidence", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Route ID", "Unique route reference", "TR-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Landscape", "Landscape / transport group", "e.g. ECC-S4 landscape", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "From SYS-ID", "Source system", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "From env (lookup)", "Environment of source", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "To SYS-ID", "Target system", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "To env (lookup)", "Environment of target", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Route type", "STMS route type", "Consolidation / Delivery", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Direct DEV→PRD?", "Flags a route that bypasses QAS", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Evidence ref", "STMS route display screenshot", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 789, "id": "w01-landscape-system-inventory-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx", "filename": "W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W02 Interface Data Flow Inventory — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W02 — Interface & Data Flow Inventory"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Identifies each financially relevant interface, how it works, how errors are caught and who owns it. Also captures RFC destinations and service users, which are privileged access paths in their own right."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste the in-scope SYS-IDs from W01 into the Ref_SYS tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Ask by process, not technology: what comes in from outside SAP, and what leaves? Enter each flow in Interface_Register."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Confirm mechanism and technical user with Basis (WE20, SM59, PI/PO or CPI lists, SM37 file jobs)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Record honestly how failures are detected, including \"None\"."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Complete RFC_Service_Users and Data_Flow_Map; back-fill BP-IDs after W03."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every financially relevant inbound and outbound flow is listed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every interface has a technology, a monitoring answer and an owner (or TBC)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "RFC / service users are listed and cross-referenced."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Interfaces with no monitoring or reconciliation are visible for W04."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 3 (W02)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "INT-ID", "Unique key", "INT-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Interface name", "Business name", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Source", "Where data originates", "SYS-ID or named external system", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Target", "Where data lands", "SYS-ID or named external system; at least one side must be an in-scope SYS-ID", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Direction", "Flow direction", "Inbound / Outbound / Bidirectional", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Technology", "How data moves", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Data object", "What is transferred", "e.g. MT940 statements, ORDERS05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "BP-ID", "Process the data feeds", "Back-fill after W03", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Frequency & volume", "How often and how much", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Financially relevant?", "Data reaches the financial statements", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Error monitoring", "How failures are detected", "\"None\" is a valid, important answer", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Reconciliation control?", "Completeness/accuracy check between source and target", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Interface owner", "Accountable person", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "RFC / service user", "Technical user used", "User ID; cross-ref RFC_Service_Users", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Evidence ref", "Supporting document", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Open question / rationale", "Still to confirm, or reason for exclusion", "Mandatory if In scope = N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Risk flag", "Whether the interface feeds a W04 risk", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "RFC ID", "Unique key", "RFC-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "INT-ID", "Interface using the connection", "Must exist in Interface_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Interface (lookup)", "Interface name", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "RFC destination / connection", "SM59 destination or connection name", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Technical user", "User the connection logs on with", "User ID", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "User type", "USR02 user type", "Dialog / System / Communication / Service", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Authorisation level", "Roles or profiles held", "e.g. SAP_ALL, narrow technical role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Stored credentials?", "Password stored in the destination", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Review flag", "Link to W06 sensitive access", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Flow ID", "Unique key", "FL-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "BP-ID", "Process the flow belongs to", "From W03", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Flow description", "What data moves and why", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Source", "Where it starts", "System or external", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Target", "Where it ends", "System or external", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "INT-ID", "Interface carrying it", "INT-ID, or N/A if entirely in-system", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Coverage", "Is the flow covered by the register", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 730, "id": "w02-interface-data-flow-inventory-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W02_Interface_Data_Flow_Inventory.xlsx", "filename": "W02_Interface_Data_Flow_Inventory.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W03 Module Business Process Scope — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W03 — Module & Business Process Scope"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Separates the SAP module from the business process and ties each process to its systems, interfaces and the financial statement areas it affects. The BP-ID created here anchors every risk in W04."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Start from the financial statements: enter significant accounts in FSLI_Map."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Map each account to the process that creates its balance; break each process into sub-processes with a BP-ID in Process_Scope."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Record modules, SYS-IDs (W01), INT-IDs (W02), owners and indicative transactions."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Add the technical areas BP-TECH-01 to 03 in Technical_Areas."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Mark confirmation status honestly. Unspecified processes are Outstanding, never guessed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every significant FSLI maps to at least one in-scope process."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every sub-process has a BP-ID, modules, SYS-IDs and a confirmation status."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Technical areas BP-TECH-01 to 03 exist."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Unspecified processes are marked Outstanding."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 4 (W03)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "BP-ID", "Unique key for the sub-process", "BP-…; unique", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Business process", "End-to-end process", "R2R / P2P / O2C / INV / H2R / TECH", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Sub-process", "Specific activity within the process", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Module(s)", "SAP modules or functional areas used", "FI, CO, MM, SD, HCM, Basis/Security", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Supporting SYS-ID(s)", "Systems the sub-process runs on", "SYS-IDs from W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Related INT-ID(s)", "Interfaces feeding the sub-process", "INT-IDs from W02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Significant FSLI / accounts", "Financial statement areas affected", "From FSLI_Map", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Significance", "Why it matters to the audit", "High / Medium / Low", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Process owner", "Accountable business owner", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Indicative transactions", "Key t-codes / Fiori apps", "Used later for SoD and access testing", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Confirmation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Source-identified / Confirmed / Partially confirmed / Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Scoping note", "Reason for inclusion or open question", "Mandatory if not in scope or Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "BP-ID", "Unique key for the sub-process", "BP-TECH-…; unique", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Business process", "End-to-end process", "R2R / P2P / O2C / INV / H2R / TECH", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Sub-process", "Specific activity within the process", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Module(s)", "SAP modules or functional areas used", "FI, CO, MM, SD, HCM, Basis/Security", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Supporting SYS-ID(s)", "Systems the sub-process runs on", "SYS-IDs from W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Related INT-ID(s)", "Interfaces feeding the sub-process", "INT-IDs from W02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Significant FSLI / accounts", "Financial statement areas affected", "From FSLI_Map", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Significance", "Why it matters to the audit", "High / Medium / Low", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Process owner", "Accountable business owner", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Indicative transactions", "Key t-codes / Fiori apps", "Used later for SoD and access testing", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Confirmation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Source-identified / Confirmed / Partially confirmed / Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Scoping note", "Reason for inclusion or open question", "Mandatory if not in scope or Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "FSLI ID", "Unique key", "FSLI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "FSLI / account", "Significant line item or account", "From audit plan", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Significant?", "Above scoping threshold", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Primary process", "Process that creates the balance", "R2R / P2P / O2C / INV / H2R", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Mapped BP-ID(s)", "Sub-processes that feed the account", "BP-IDs from Process_Scope, comma separated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Assertions", "Relevant assertions", "Existence, Completeness, Accuracy, …", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Check", "Is the account mapped to a process", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 768, "id": "w03-module-business-process-scope-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W03_Module_Business_Process_Scope.xlsx", "filename": "W03_Module_Business_Process_Scope.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W04 Risk Register — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W04 — Risk Register"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "States, for each in-scope process and technical area, what could go wrong, who owns the risk and how significant it is. A risk is an exposure, never a finding: nothing here says a control has failed."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste the BP-IDs from W03 (BP_Index tab, values only) into the Ref_BP tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Start from the TIB risk library or the client RCM; tailor to each process and technical area."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Write each risk with the formula: There is a risk that … because … resulting in …"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Assign category, assertions, owner and likelihood/impact. Mark SOX-relevant risks."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Agree owners with the business and read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every in-scope BP-ID, including technical areas, has at least one risk."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every risk follows the formula, has a category, assertions and a risk owner (or TBC)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "SOX-relevant risks are flagged."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "No risk statement contains an effectiveness conclusion."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 5 (W04)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "RISK-ID", "Unique key", "R-<process>-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "BP-ID", "Process or technical area the risk belongs to", "Must exist in W03 (Ref_BP)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "SYS-ID / INT-ID", "System or interface where the risk arises", "Keys from W01 / W02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk category", "Type of risk; guides control category in W05", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk statement", "What could go wrong", "\"There is a risk that … because … resulting in …\"; never a finding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Assertion(s)", "Financial statement assertions affected", "Occurrence, Completeness, Accuracy, Cut-off, Valuation, Existence, Rights & obligations, Presentation", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk owner", "Accountable for the outcome", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent likelihood", "Before controls", "1 Low / 2 Medium / 3 High", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent impact", "Before controls", "1 Low / 2 Medium / 3 High", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent rating", "Likelihood × impact", "Formula; 1–9", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Rating band", "High ≥ 6, Medium 3–5, Low < 3", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "SOX relevant?", "Could lead to a material misstatement", "Y / N; drives key-control requirement in W05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk source", "Where the risk came from", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Status", "Agreement status", "No \"Failed\" value exists", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Notes", "Limitations or follow-ups", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 464, "id": "w04-risk-register-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W04_Risk_Register.xlsx", "filename": "W04_Risk_Register.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W05 Risk Control Matrix — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W05 — Risk & Control Matrix (RCM)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The centre of the foundation. Defines the specific control that answers each risk, classifies it, names its owner, and records the SAP mechanism, expected evidence and test approach. Everything in the Test phase (W10 to W12) is generated from RCM rows."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste RISK-IDs and SOX flags from W04 into the Ref_RISK tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. For each risk, record what actually prevents or detects it. Write each control in the five-part form in Control_Register."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Link controls to risks in Risk_Control_Map (one row per pair; the register is many-to-many)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. For every ITAC, IPE and ITDM control, list the ITGCs it relies on in ITGC_Dependency."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Read Coverage_Check: any SOX risk without a key control is a design gap."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every risk in W04 maps to at least one control; every SOX-relevant risk maps to at least one key control."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every control has a five-part description, single category, nature, frequency and owner."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every ITAC, IPE and ITDM control lists its dependent ITGCs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Expected evidence is defined for every control, ready to drive W08."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 6 (W05); Part 3B, Session 33 (reliance)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "CTRL-ID", "Unique key", "C-<category>-nnn, e.g. C-ITGC-AC-002, C-ITAC-004", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control title", "Short name", "e.g. Timely removal of leavers", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control description", "Who does what, when, how, with what evidence", "Five-part description: who / what / when / how / evidence", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control area", "Grouping", "From Control_Areas tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control category", "Classification", "One value only", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Type", "When it acts", "Preventive / Detective", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Nature", "How it operates", "Must agree with category; ITAC = Automated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Frequency", "How often it operates; drives sample size in W10", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Key control?", "Relied on to address a SOX risk", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SOX in scope?", "Requires formal TOD and TOE", "Y if key control for a SOX-relevant risk", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control owner", "Person or role performing the control", "Name / role; carried into W07", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SYS-ID", "Where the control operates", "From W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SAP mechanism", "Where the control lives in SAP", "t-code, config path, table, GRC component", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Dependent ITGCs", "ITGCs this control relies on", "CTRL-ID list; mandatory for ITAC, IPE, ITDM", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "IPE used", "Reports the control relies on", "Each IPE needs completeness & accuracy testing", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Expected evidence", "What the control produces", "Feeds W08 PBC requests", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Test approach", "Planned procedure", "Inquiry / Observation / Inspection / Reperformance; TOD + TOE", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Planned session", "Where it is tested in the course", "Part 3 session reference", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Risks mapped", "Number of risks linked in Risk_Control_Map", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Map ID", "Unique key for the link", "M-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "RISK-ID", "Risk from W04", "Must exist in Ref_RISK", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "CTRL-ID", "Control from Control_Register", "Must exist in Control_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Coverage", "Does the control fully address the risk", "Full / Partial", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Rationale", "Why this control answers this risk", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Risk SOX? (lookup)", "SOX flag of the risk (W04)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Key control? (lookup)", "Key flag of the control", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dep ID", "Unique key", "D-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dependent CTRL-ID", "ITAC / IPE / ITDM control that relies on an ITGC", "Must exist in Control_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dependent category", "Category (lookup)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC CTRL-ID", "ITGC being relied on", "CTRL-ID beginning C-ITGC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC?", "Is the dependency really an ITGC", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC test result", "Result from W10 / W11", "Updated in Part 3 sessions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Additional procedures", "Logs, usage analysis, etc. used to restore reliance", "Free text; see Session 33", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Reliance decision", "Outcome for the dependent control", "Pending until ITGC tested", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 737, "id": "w05-risk-control-matrix-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx", "filename": "W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W06 SoD Sensitive Access Matrix — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W06 — SoD & Sensitive Access Rule Matrix"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Translates each SoD and sensitive-access risk from W04 into technical terms: which transactions and authorisation objects make up each side of a conflict. The access analysis in Part 3A runs these rules against the user population."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste RISK-IDs and categories from W04 into Ref_RISK, and CTRL-IDs from W05 into Ref_CTRL."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Define business functions with their transactions (Functions) and permission detail (Function_Auth)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Define each conflict pair in SoD_Rules, linked to a W04 SoD risk. List critical single-item access in Sensitive_Access."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Record approved mitigations (must be real controls in W05) and the approved ruleset version."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Include custom Z-transactions and Fiori equivalents. Read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every SoD risk in W04 maps to at least one SOD-ID, and every rule maps to a risk."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Rules carry permission-level detail and include custom transactions."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Sensitive access items are listed with allowed holders."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Mitigations point to real CTRL-IDs; the ruleset version is approved."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 7 (W06)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Function ID", "Unique key", "FN-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Function name", "Business function", "e.g. Vendor master maintenance", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "BP-ID", "Process the function belongs to", "From W03", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Transactions (t-codes)", "Standard transactions", "e.g. BP, XK01, XK02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Fiori apps / custom Z-codes", "Equivalent apps and custom transactions", "Include Z-transactions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Description", "What the function allows", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Functions", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Auth ID", "Unique key", "AU-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Function ID", "Function the permission belongs to", "Must exist in Functions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Function (lookup)", "Function name", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Transaction", "Transaction code", "t-code", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Auth object", "Authorisation object", "e.g. F_LFA1_BUK", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Field", "e.g. ACTVT", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Values", "Field values that enable the action", "e.g. 01, 02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Function_Auth", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "SOD-ID", "Unique key", "SOD-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Function A", "First function", "Must exist in Functions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Function B", "Second function", "Must exist in Functions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Function A (lookup)", "Name", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Function B (lookup)", "Name", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Conflict description", "Why the combination is dangerous", "Must match the W04 risk", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "RISK-ID", "Risk the rule tests", "Must exist in W04, category SoD", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "BP-ID", "Process affected", "From W03", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Risk level", "Severity", "Critical / High / Medium", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Rule level", "How it is evaluated; permission level preferred", "Transaction level / Permission level", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Org-level scope", "Does the conflict apply across or within the same org unit", "Cross-org / Same org", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Mitigating CTRL-ID", "Accepted mitigation for justified conflicts", "CTRL-ID in W05, or None", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Rule owner", "Business owner who approved the rule", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "In scope?", "Run in this engagement", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SoD_Rules", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "SENS-ID", "Unique key", "SENS-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "Access item", "Transaction, profile or auth object value", "e.g. SAP_ALL; S_TABU_DIS ACTVT 02 with SE16N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "Why sensitive", "Impact if held inappropriately", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "RISK-ID", "Risk tested", "From W04", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "Allowed holders", "Who may legitimately hold it", "e.g. Firefighter IDs only", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "Related CTRL-ID", "Control that governs it", "From W05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sensitive_Access", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Mit ID", "Unique key", "MIT-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Mitigating CTRL-ID", "Control that mitigates", "Must exist in W05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "SOD-ID covered", "Rule the mitigation covers", "Must exist in SoD_Rules", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Mitigated users", "Users carrying the conflict", "Count or list", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Valid from", "Start date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Valid to", "Expiry date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Approver", "Who approved", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Control tested effective?", "Result from W10", "Y / N / Pending", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Mitigations", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Version", "Ruleset version", "e.g. 1.0", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Source", "Where the ruleset came from", "GRC ARA export / manual / TIB baseline", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Approved by", "Business approver", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Approval date", "Date approved", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Custom Z-transactions included?", "Z-codes and Fiori equivalents covered", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Ruleset_Version", "Notes", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 771, "id": "w06-sod-sensitive-access-matrix-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W06_SoD_Sensitive_Access_Matrix.xlsx", "filename": "W06_SoD_Sensitive_Access_Matrix.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W07 Control Evidence Owner Matrix — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W07 — Control & Evidence Owner Matrix"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records, for every control in the RCM, who is accountable for it, who performs it, who can provide the evidence and who the backup is. These are often different people. Keyed by CTRL-ID so it lines up one-to-one with W05."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste CTRL-ID, title, evidence expectation, key flag and risk owner from W05/W04 into Ref_CTRL."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Add one Owner_Matrix row per CTRL-ID. Pre-fill the control owner from W05."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Meet each owner. Ask two questions: who makes sure this happens, and who can give us the evidence?"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Record walkthrough contacts for key controls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Mark unresolved items Outstanding with a follow-up, never as a control failure."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every in-scope CTRL-ID has a row."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Control owner and evidence owner are identified or marked Outstanding."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Walkthrough contacts are known for every key control."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Outstanding owners are escalated in the W14 dependency list."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 8 (W07)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "CTRL-ID", "Control from the RCM", "Must exist in W05 (Ref_CTRL)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Control title", "Lookup from W05", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Key control?", "Lookup from W05", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Risk owner", "Lookup from W04 via Ref_CTRL", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Control owner", "Accountable for the control operating", "Required, or mark Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Control performer", "Person who executes it day to day", "May equal owner", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Evidence owner", "Person or team who can produce the evidence", "Required, or mark Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Backup contact", "Alternate for leave or turnover", "Recommended", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Primary SYS-ID", "Where evidence resides", "From W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Expected evidence", "Lookup from W05", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Walkthrough contact", "Person who will demonstrate the control in TOD", "Usually the performer", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Owner status", "Confirmation state; Outstanding is not a failure", "Confirmed / Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Date confirmed", "When the owner agreed", "Date; required if Confirmed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Follow-up", "What still needs resolving", "Required if Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Owner_Matrix", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 495, "id": "w07-control-evidence-owner-matrix-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W07_Control_Evidence_Owner_Matrix.xlsx", "filename": "W07_Control_Evidence_Owner_Matrix.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W08 PBC Evidence Request Register — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W08 — PBC / Evidence Request Register"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Lists every piece of evidence requested, from whom, for which period and for which control and test. Every request must answer a future testing question. If it cannot be traced to a CTRL-ID and a planned test, do not make it."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste CTRL-ID, title, evidence owner (W07), key flag (W05) into Ref_CTRL."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. For each in-scope key control, read Expected evidence and Test approach in W05 and write the wave 1 and 2 requests (configuration and populations)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Make each request precise: system, client, period, fields, format. Consolidate requests that serve several controls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Issue, then track daily. Update Status and add EV-IDs as items are indexed in W09."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Raise wave 3 sample requests only after W12 sampling is complete."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every request traces to at least one CTRL-ID (and TEST-ID once W10 exists)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every request states system, period and required fields."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Population requests are flagged for IPE checks."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Outstanding requests are visible with owner and due date."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 9 (W08)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "PBC-ID", "Unique key", "PBC-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Request wave", "Timing of the request", "1 Scoping / 2 Population / 3 Sample / 4 Follow-up", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Primary CTRL-ID", "Main control the evidence supports", "Must exist in W05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Other CTRL-ID(s)", "Other controls served by the same request", "CTRL-ID list", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "TEST-ID(s)", "Tests that will use it", "Back-filled when W10 is built; required for wave 3", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "BP-ID / SYS-ID", "Where the evidence applies", "Keys from W03 / W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Requested evidence", "Exactly what is needed: system, client, period, fields, format", "Specific enough to be unambiguous", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Period from", "Start of period or as-of date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Period to", "End of period", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Evidence type", "Request type; Population triggers an IPE check", "Population / Sample / Configuration / Policy", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Evidence owner", "From W07 (lookup via Ref_CTRL)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Date requested", "Tracking", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Due date", "Agreed due date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Priority", "Urgency; High for populations", "High / Medium / Low", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Status", "Progress", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "EV-ID(s)", "Evidence received, once indexed in W09", "Required once Received", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "IPE needed?", "Population requests need completeness and accuracy checks", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Days overdue", "Today minus due date while not received", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Notes", "Completeness requirements or follow-up", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["PBC_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 504, "id": "w08-pbc-evidence-request-register-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W08_PBC_Evidence_Request_Register.xlsx", "filename": "W08_PBC_Evidence_Request_Register.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W09 Evidence Index IPE Log — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W09 — Evidence Index & IPE Log"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The filing system. Every item received gets an EV-ID, its source and extraction details are recorded, the original is preserved, and population evidence is checked for completeness and accuracy before any sample is drawn."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste PBC-ID, period from, period to and evidence type from W08 into Ref_PBC."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Log every item received in Evidence_Index using the filename convention EV-ID_SYS_description_date.ext. Keep the raw original untouched."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. For each population, record the completeness and accuracy work in IPE_Log."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Set the IPE result. No sample may be drawn until the result is Pass."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Back-fill TEST-IDs once W10 exists. Update W08 status with EV-IDs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every received item has an EV-ID, standard filename and preserved original."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every population has an IPE check recorded."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "No sample is drawn from a population with an IPE result other than Pass."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W08 status is updated with EV-IDs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 10 (W09)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "EV-ID", "Unique key", "EV-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "PBC-ID", "Request it answers", "Must exist in W08 (Ref_PBC)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "PBC period from", "Requested period start (lookup)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "PBC period to", "Requested period end (lookup)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Filename", "Stored file name", "<EV-ID>_<SYS>_<description>_<date>.ext", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Description", "What the file contains", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Source SYS-ID", "System it was extracted from", "From W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Extraction method", "How it was produced", "t-code / report / table / GRC report / ITSM export / document", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Extracted by", "Who ran it", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Extraction date", "When it was run", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Period covered from", "Start date in the file", "Date; must cover PBC period", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Period covered to", "End date in the file", "Date; must cover PBC period", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Record count", "Rows in the population", "Number; used for completeness check", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Received date", "Date logged", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Original preserved?", "Raw copy stored read-only", "Must be Y", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "IPE required?", "Population requests need IPE checks", "Formula from W08 evidence type", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "IPE result", "Outcome of completeness & accuracy check", "Pass required before sampling", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Used by TEST-ID(s)", "Tests relying on the file", "Back-filled from W10", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "IPE-ID", "Unique key", "IPE-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "EV-ID", "Population being tested", "Must exist in Evidence_Index", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Population (lookup)", "Description from Evidence_Index", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Record count (lookup)", "Rows in the file", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Completeness procedure", "How completeness was established", "Observed extraction, parameter screenshot, no filters", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Reconciled to", "Independent source used", "e.g. SUIM total, STMS queue, HR headcount report", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Source count", "Count in the independent source", "Number", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Count difference", "File count minus source count", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Accuracy procedure", "How accuracy was tested", "Records traced both ways", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Records traced", "Number of records traced", "Number", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Parameters evidenced?", "Selection parameters captured", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Tester", "Who performed it", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Test date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Result", "Outcome", "Pass / Fail / Pending", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 605, "id": "w09-evidence-index-ipe-log-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W09_Evidence_Index_IPE_Log.xlsx", "filename": "W09_Evidence_Index_IPE_Log.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W10 Test Plan — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W10 — Test Plan / Testing Program"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Converts the RCM into a schedule of tests. Every in-scope key control gets a test of design (TOD) and a test of operating effectiveness (TOE) with attributes, procedure, population, sample size, period, tester, reviewer and dates."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste controls from W05 into Ref_CTRL, and evidence IDs with IPE result and record count from W09 into Ref_EV."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Filter W05 for SOX in scope = Y. Create a TOD and a TOE row for each key control; derive attributes from the control description."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Set sample size from the Sample_Guide (edit the guide to match engagement methodology). Mark ITGC reliance."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Assign testers and reviewers (different people). Schedule ITGC tests before dependent ITAC/IPE tests."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Update Status and Result only after review. Read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every SOX in-scope key control has a TOD and a TOE row."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every row has attributes derived from the control description."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Sample sizes follow the guide or a justified deviation."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "ITGC tests are scheduled before dependent ITAC/IPE tests."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 11 (W10)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "TEST-ID", "Unique key", "<CTRL-ID>-TOD or -TOE", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "CTRL-ID", "Control being tested", "From W05 (Ref_CTRL)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Control title", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Control category", "Lookup; drives workpaper template", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Frequency", "Lookup; drives sample size", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test type", "TOD or TOE", "One TOD and one TOE per key control", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test attributes", "Conditions each sample item must meet", "Lettered A, B, C…; derived from the control description", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test procedure", "What the tester does", "Inquiry / Observation / Inspection / Reperformance; inquiry alone is never enough", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Population EV-ID", "Population sampled from (TOE)", "IPE must be Pass", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "IPE result", "Lookup from W09", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Population size", "Lookup from W09", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Guide sample", "Typical range for the frequency", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Sample size", "Items to test", "Number; justify deviations from the guide", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test period from", "Start of period", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test period to", "End of period", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "ITGC reliance needed?", "Dependent controls need effective ITGCs", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Tester", "Who performs", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Reviewer", "Who reviews; must differ from tester", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Planned start", "Schedule", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Planned end", "Schedule", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Workpaper ref", "W11 workpaper", "File name or WP ref", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Status", "Progress", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Result", "Outcome (only after review)", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 507, "id": "w10-test-plan-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W10_Test_Plan.xlsx", "filename": "W10_Test_Plan.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W11 Test Workpapers — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W11 — Test Workpapers"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A workpaper records what the tester actually did, so a reviewer who was not involved can understand, reperform and reach the same conclusion. This file holds the standard workpaper structure, the list of 15 templates by control area, and a register of workpapers."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste TEST-IDs from W10 into Ref_TEST."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. For each test, copy the Workpaper tab (right-click > Move or Copy) and rename it with the TEST-ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Complete header, population/IPE, sample, procedure and attribute grid. Every Met or Exception needs an EV-ID reference."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Describe exceptions and root cause. The suggested result is a formula; the reviewer sets the final result."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Log each workpaper in WP_Register. Raise DEF-IDs in W13."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Each test has a completed workpaper in the standard structure."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every attribute result references an EV-ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Exceptions are explained with root cause."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The reviewer has signed off before the result is entered in W10."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 12 (W11); Parts 3A and 3B (templates)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "WP ref", "Workpaper reference", "e.g. WP-PC-01_<TEST-ID>", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "TEST-ID", "Test the workpaper supports", "Must exist in W10 (Ref_TEST)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Template", "Standard template used", "From Templates tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Tester", "Who performed", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Items tested", "From the Workpaper tab", "Number", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Items with exception", "From the Workpaper tab", "Number", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Result", "Outcome (after review)", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Reviewed by", "Reviewer", "Name; different from tester", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Review date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "DEF-ID(s) / rationale", "Deficiency raised, or false-positive rationale", "Required if Exception noted / Ineffective", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Status", "Progress", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["WP_Register", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 369, "id": "w11-test-workpapers-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W11_Test_Workpapers.xlsx", "filename": "W11_Test_Workpapers.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W12 Sample Selection Log — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W12 — Sample Selection Log"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records exactly which items were chosen from which population, by what method and why. It lets a reviewer confirm the sample was unbiased and spread across the whole period, and it drives the wave 3 PBC requests."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste TEST-ID, population EV-ID, IPE result, population size and planned sample size from W10/W09 into Ref_TEST."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Create one Sample_Log row per test. Selection is only allowed from a population whose IPE result is Pass."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. List every selected item on the Items tab with its date and basis (Random / Systematic / Targeted / All items)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Record the seed or interval so the selection can be reperformed. Explain targeted additions."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Raise the wave 3 PBC request in W08 and enter its ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every TOE has a sample logged with method and parameters."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The sample covers the whole period."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Targeted additions are explained."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Wave 3 PBC requests are raised and cross-referenced."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 13 (W12)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "SMP-ID", "Unique key", "SMP-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "TEST-ID", "Test the sample is for", "From W10 (Ref_TEST)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Population EV-ID", "Lookup from W10", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "IPE result", "Lookup from W09", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Population size", "Lookup from W09", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Planned sample size", "Lookup from W10", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Items selected", "Count of items listed on the Items tab", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Selection method", "How chosen", "Random (seeded) preferred", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Seed / interval", "Parameter for reperformance", "Required for random / systematic", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Targeted additions", "Risk-based items added to the planned sample", "Number; explain in rationale", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Q1 items", "Items dated in Q1", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Q2 items", "Items dated in Q2", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Q3 items", "Items dated in Q3", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Q4 items", "Items dated in Q4", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Quarters covered", "Quarters with at least one item", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Period coverage confirmed?", "Sample spread across the whole period", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Wave 3 PBC-ID", "Request for supporting evidence", "Raised in W08", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Selected by", "Who selected", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Selection date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Rationale for targeted items / deviation", "Why items were added or the count differs", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Sample_Log", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "SMP-ID", "Sample the item belongs to", "Must exist in Sample_Log", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Item ID", "The item selected", "User ID, transport number, FF session ID, invoice no.", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Item date", "Date of the item (for period coverage)", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Selection basis", "Why selected", "Random / Systematic / Targeted / All items", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Quarter", "Calendar quarter of the item date", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Wave 3 evidence received?", "Supporting evidence for this item received", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Items", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 557, "id": "w12-sample-selection-log-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W12_Sample_Selection_Log.xlsx", "filename": "W12_Sample_Selection_Log.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W13 Deficiency Remediation Log — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W13 — Deficiency & Remediation Log"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records every confirmed test exception, evaluates its severity (condition, criteria, cause, effect), links it back through the thread to the risk and process, groups related deficiencies, and tracks remediation and retest."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste TEST-ID, CTRL-ID, RISK-ID, BP-ID and the W10 result into Ref_TEST."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Validate each exception first: false positive, wrong population or later evidence? Only confirmed exceptions get a DEF-ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Write the four-part statement, record compensating controls and dependent controls affected, and set severity."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Aggregate related deficiencies in Groups (same user, date, process or cause) and evaluate the group."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Record management response, owner and due date; add the retest TEST-ID. A deficiency closes only after a successful retest."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every confirmed exception has a DEF-ID traced to test, control, risk and process."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Severity is evaluated, including impact on dependent controls and compensating controls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Management response, owner and due date are recorded."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Retest is planned for every remediated deficiency."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 4, Sessions 35–37 (W13)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "DEF-ID", "Unique key", "DEF-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "TEST-ID", "Test where it was found", "From W10 (Ref_TEST)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "CTRL-ID", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "RISK-ID", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "BP-ID", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Deficiency type", "Design or operating", "Design / Operating", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Condition", "What was found (facts, numbers, dates)", "Four-part statement", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Criteria", "What should have happened", "Control description or policy", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Cause", "Why it happened (from inquiry)", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Effect", "What could go wrong; link to risk and assertion", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Exceptions", "Failed items out of sample", "e.g. 3 of 25", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Dependent controls affected", "ITAC / IPE / ITDM relying on this ITGC", "From W05 ITGC_Dependency", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Compensating controls", "Other controls reducing the exposure", "CTRL-ID list", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Compensating tested effective?", "Result of testing the compensating control", "Y / N / Pending", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Severity", "Evaluated level", "Control deficiency / Significant deficiency / Material weakness", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Group ID", "Aggregation group", "From Groups tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Management response", "Owner's agreed action", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Remediation owner", "Who fixes it", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Due date", "Remediation due", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Retest TEST-ID", "Test confirming the fix", "e.g. <CTRL-ID>-RT1", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Status", "Progress", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Group ID", "Unique key", "GR-x", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Group name", "Short name", "e.g. Privileged access governance", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Common factor", "What links the deficiencies", "Same user, date, process, root cause", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Evaluated exposure", "Combined exposure after compensating controls and follow-up", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Group severity", "Severity of the group", "Reviewer approved", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Rationale", "Why this severity", "Magnitude, likelihood, compensating controls, reliance", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "DEFs in group", "Count", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Highest individual severity", "Highest rating among its DEFs", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 565, "id": "w13-deficiency-remediation-log-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W13_Deficiency_Remediation_Log.xlsx", "filename": "W13_Deficiency_Remediation_Log.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "W14 Scoping Workpaper Coverage Dashboard — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W14 — Scoping Workpaper & Coverage Dashboard"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The master record and a reviewer's first stop. At scoping stage it summarises what is in scope, what is confirmed, what is unconfirmed and which dependencies must be resolved. At testing stage it becomes the coverage dashboard: every risk covered, every key control tested, every deficiency evaluated."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Complete Engagement_Summary (client, systems, processes, period, materiality, stakeholders) and set Stage = Scoping."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Log known, unconfirmed and outstanding items, and access/evidence dependencies."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Paste the summary rows from W04/W05 (Ref_Risk_Control, Ref_Controls, Ref_Reliance), W10/W12 (Ref_Tests) and W13 (Ref_Deficiencies). No figure on the dashboard is typed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. At scoping stage the conclusion reads \"No conclusion at scoping stage\" and coverage checks 2–7 show SCOPING. Switch Stage to Testing when fieldwork starts."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Read the Checks tab and Coverage_Matrix before issuing any report."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = you enter data here.   Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite).   Row 5 of each register is a worked ABC Manufacturing example: overwrite it with your first record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Scoping stage: in-scope items, known/unconfirmed/outstanding and dependencies are complete, and the conclusion reads \"No conclusion at scoping stage\"."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Testing stage: every coverage check passes or the gap is explained."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "All figures are formulas linked to the pasted source data."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Sessions 15–16 (W14); Part 4, Session 38 (reporting)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Item ID", "Unique key", "KU-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Category", "Area", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Item", "The fact, unconfirmed matter or outstanding item", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Status", "Known = verified; Unconfirmed = reported but not verified; Outstanding = still to obtain", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Source", "Workbook and key", "e.g. W01 SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Owner", "Person to resolve", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Next step", "Action to resolve", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Known_Unconfirmed_Outstanding", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Dep ID", "Unique key", "DEP-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Type", "Kind of dependency", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Description", "What must be resolved before testing", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Source key", "Workbook and key", "e.g. W07 C-ITGC-AC-004", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Owner", "Person to resolve", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Target date", "Needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Blocks", "Gate or test it blocks", "e.g. G5; WP-AC-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Days overdue", "Today minus target while not closed", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dependencies", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 466, "id": "w14-scoping-workpaper-coverage-dashboard-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/06_Reference_Manuals/Blank_Workbook_Templates/W14_Scoping_Workpaper_Coverage_Dashboard.xlsx", "filename": "W14_Scoping_Workpaper_Coverage_Dashboard.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "Corvane Weekly Activity Plan — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Corvane Weekly Activity Plan | Corvane Energy Group"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Converts the Corvane Story into discrete activities grouped by week for the 38 calendar weeks from 12 January to 30 September 2026. Each activity has an owner, supporters, client stakeholders, the workbook where it is recorded and the output that shows it is done. Week_Summary gives the focus and gate of each week; Pod_Activity shows how work moves between the pods."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Filter Weekly_Plan by Week to see what the team does that week, or by Owner or Pod to follow one person or pod across the project."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Read Week_Summary for the phase, gate and focus of each week."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Read Pod_Activity to see when each pod is busy and when it is free to help another."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. To reuse on a new project, change the start date in Setup, replace the activities and re-read Checks."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). Weekly_Plan holds one discrete activity per row, grouped by project week; Week_Summary, Team and Pod_Activity read it."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every week has at least one discrete activity with an owner and an output."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Gates G1 to G9 each fall in a stated week."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every owner is a member of the team."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The Corvane Story, chapters 1 to 12; Controls Lead Playbook sections 2, 3 and 5."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Week", "Project week, 1 to 38", "Number 1 to 38", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Activity ID", "Auto number within the week", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Week commencing", "Monday of the week", "Formula from the project start date", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Phase", "Phase of the week", "Formula from Week_Summary", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Gate / milestone", "Gate or milestone falling in the week", "Formula from Week_Summary", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Activity", "One discrete activity, started and finished in the week", "Verb first; one deliverable", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Owner", "Person accountable for the activity", "Person in the Team tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Pod", "Owner pod", "Formula from Team", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Supported by", "Others who help", "First names", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Client stakeholder(s)", "Client people involved", "Names", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Workbook / artefact", "Where the work is recorded", "Workbook and key", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Output (done when)", "Evidence the activity is finished", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Story chapter", "Chapter of the Corvane Story", "Formula from Week_Summary", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Weekly_Plan", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Week", "Project week", "1 to 38", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Week commencing", "Monday of the week", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Phase", "Phase of the project", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Gate / milestone", "Gate or milestone in the week", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Focus of the week", "What the week is for", "One line", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Story chapter", "Chapter of the Corvane Story", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Activities", "Count of activities in the week", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Steering this week?", "Fortnightly steering on the Friday of odd weeks", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Week_Summary", "Row check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Team", "Person", "Name", "Full name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Team", "Role", "Role on the project", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Team", "Pod", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 563, "id": "corvane-weekly-activity-plan-instructions-2", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/07_Project_Story_and_Lead_Playbook/Corvane_Weekly_Activity_Plan.xlsx", "filename": "Corvane_Weekly_Activity_Plan.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "P01 W01 Landscape System Inventory — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W01 — Landscape & System Inventory | PRACTICE CASE: CORVANE LITE"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records every SAP system, client and supporting component that holds financial data or processes business transactions, and states whether each is in scope and whether it has actually been validated. W01 is the root of the golden thread: every other workbook points back to a SYS-ID."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Enter the client-provided landscape in System_Inventory with Validation status = Documented only."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Validate directly (System > Status, SCC4, SM51, DB02). Upgrade the status to Validated and add the evidence ref."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Record application servers and hosts in Server_Detail, and the STMS route in Transport_Routes."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Decide scope and record a rationale for every exclusion."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Log every unresolved question in Open_Items. Finish by reading the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is a PRACTICE copy: the registers are empty. Enter the facts from the Practice Case Brief, then read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every known component is listed with SID, client and environment."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every row has In scope = Y/N with a rationale for N."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every Validated row has an evidence reference; every other row has an open question."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The PRD transport route is recorded."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 2 (W01)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "SYS-ID", "Unique key for the system/client", "SYS-nn; unique; never reused", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "SID", "SAP system identifier", "3 characters; must match System > Status", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Client", "Logical client number", "3 digits; must exist in SCC4", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Environment", "Landscape tier", "DEV / QAS / PRD / SBX / TRN", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Product & release", "Installed SAP product and release", "e.g. S/4HANA 2022 FPS02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Component type", "Technical role of the component", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Hosting model", "Where it runs; drives which ITGCs ABC vs the provider performs", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Database & OS", "Underlying platform", "From DB02 / Basis", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Primary purpose", "Business use the system supports", "One line", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Financially relevant?", "Holds or processes data reaching the financial statements", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "In scope?", "Included in this engagement", "Y / N / TBC; PRD financial system = Y unless justified", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Scope rationale", "Reason for inclusion or exclusion", "Mandatory if In scope = N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "System owner", "Accountable owner (usually Basis/IT lead)", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Validation method", "How the row was confirmed", "System > Status / SCC4 / SM51 / client inventory / interview", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Validation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Validated requires an evidence ref", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Evidence ref", "Screenshot or document supporting the row", "EV-ID or filename; mandatory if Validated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Open question", "Anything still to confirm", "Mirror into Open_Items", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Server ID", "Unique key for the server record", "SRV-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "SYS-ID", "System the server belongs to", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "SID (lookup)", "SID pulled from System_Inventory", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Host name", "Server host name", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Role", "Server role", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "OS", "Operating system and version", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Location / hosting", "Data centre, site or cloud region", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Owner", "Accountable infrastructure owner", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Validated via", "Evidence source", "SM51 / DB02 / inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Evidence ref", "Supporting evidence", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Route ID", "Unique route reference", "TR-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Landscape", "Landscape / transport group", "e.g. ECC-S4 landscape", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "From SYS-ID", "Source system", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "From env (lookup)", "Environment of source", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "To SYS-ID", "Target system", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "To env (lookup)", "Environment of target", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Route type", "STMS route type", "Consolidation / Delivery", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Direct DEV→PRD?", "Flags a route that bypasses QAS", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Evidence ref", "STMS route display screenshot", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 796, "id": "p01-w01-landscape-system-inventory-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/01_Practice_Case_Blank/P01_W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx", "filename": "P01_W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "P02 W02 Interface Data Flow Inventory — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W02 — Interface & Data Flow Inventory | PRACTICE CASE: CORVANE LITE"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Identifies each financially relevant interface, how it works, how errors are caught and who owns it. Also captures RFC destinations and service users, which are privileged access paths in their own right."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste the in-scope SYS-IDs from W01 into the Ref_SYS tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Ask by process, not technology: what comes in from outside SAP, and what leaves? Enter each flow in Interface_Register."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Confirm mechanism and technical user with Basis (WE20, SM59, PI/PO or CPI lists, SM37 file jobs)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Record honestly how failures are detected, including \"None\"."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Complete RFC_Service_Users and Data_Flow_Map; back-fill BP-IDs after W03."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is a PRACTICE copy: the registers are empty. Enter the facts from the Practice Case Brief, then read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every financially relevant inbound and outbound flow is listed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every interface has a technology, a monitoring answer and an owner (or TBC)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "RFC / service users are listed and cross-referenced."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Interfaces with no monitoring or reconciliation are visible for W04."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 3 (W02)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "INT-ID", "Unique key", "INT-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Interface name", "Business name", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Source", "Where data originates", "SYS-ID or named external system", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Target", "Where data lands", "SYS-ID or named external system; at least one side must be an in-scope SYS-ID", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Direction", "Flow direction", "Inbound / Outbound / Bidirectional", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Technology", "How data moves", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Data object", "What is transferred", "e.g. MT940 statements, ORDERS05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "BP-ID", "Process the data feeds", "Back-fill after W03", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Frequency & volume", "How often and how much", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Financially relevant?", "Data reaches the financial statements", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Error monitoring", "How failures are detected", "\"None\" is a valid, important answer", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Reconciliation control?", "Completeness/accuracy check between source and target", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Interface owner", "Accountable person", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "RFC / service user", "Technical user used", "User ID; cross-ref RFC_Service_Users", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Evidence ref", "Supporting document", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Open question / rationale", "Still to confirm, or reason for exclusion", "Mandatory if In scope = N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Risk flag", "Whether the interface feeds a W04 risk", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interface_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "RFC ID", "Unique key", "RFC-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "INT-ID", "Interface using the connection", "Must exist in Interface_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Interface (lookup)", "Interface name", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "RFC destination / connection", "SM59 destination or connection name", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Technical user", "User the connection logs on with", "User ID", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "User type", "USR02 user type", "Dialog / System / Communication / Service", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Authorisation level", "Roles or profiles held", "e.g. SAP_ALL, narrow technical role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Stored credentials?", "Password stored in the destination", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Review flag", "Link to W06 sensitive access", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC_Service_Users", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Flow ID", "Unique key", "FL-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "BP-ID", "Process the flow belongs to", "From W03", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Flow description", "What data moves and why", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Source", "Where it starts", "System or external", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Target", "Where it ends", "System or external", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "INT-ID", "Interface carrying it", "INT-ID, or N/A if entirely in-system", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Coverage", "Is the flow covered by the register", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Data_Flow_Map", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 737, "id": "p02-w02-interface-data-flow-inventory-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/01_Practice_Case_Blank/P02_W02_Interface_Data_Flow_Inventory.xlsx", "filename": "P02_W02_Interface_Data_Flow_Inventory.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "P03 W03 Module Business Process Scope — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W03 — Module & Business Process Scope | PRACTICE CASE: CORVANE LITE"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Separates the SAP module from the business process and ties each process to its systems, interfaces and the financial statement areas it affects. The BP-ID created here anchors every risk in W04."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Start from the financial statements: enter significant accounts in FSLI_Map."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Map each account to the process that creates its balance; break each process into sub-processes with a BP-ID in Process_Scope."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Record modules, SYS-IDs (W01), INT-IDs (W02), owners and indicative transactions."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Add the technical areas BP-TECH-01 to 03 in Technical_Areas."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Mark confirmation status honestly. Unspecified processes are Outstanding, never guessed."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is a PRACTICE copy: the registers are empty. Enter the facts from the Practice Case Brief, then read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every significant FSLI maps to at least one in-scope process."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every sub-process has a BP-ID, modules, SYS-IDs and a confirmation status."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Technical areas BP-TECH-01 to 03 exist."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Unspecified processes are marked Outstanding."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 4 (W03)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "BP-ID", "Unique key for the sub-process", "BP-…; unique", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Business process", "End-to-end process", "R2R / P2P / O2C / INV / H2R / TECH", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Sub-process", "Specific activity within the process", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Module(s)", "SAP modules or functional areas used", "FI, CO, MM, SD, HCM, Basis/Security", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Supporting SYS-ID(s)", "Systems the sub-process runs on", "SYS-IDs from W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Related INT-ID(s)", "Interfaces feeding the sub-process", "INT-IDs from W02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Significant FSLI / accounts", "Financial statement areas affected", "From FSLI_Map", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Significance", "Why it matters to the audit", "High / Medium / Low", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Process owner", "Accountable business owner", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Indicative transactions", "Key t-codes / Fiori apps", "Used later for SoD and access testing", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Confirmation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Source-identified / Confirmed / Partially confirmed / Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Scoping note", "Reason for inclusion or open question", "Mandatory if not in scope or Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Process_Scope", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "BP-ID", "Unique key for the sub-process", "BP-TECH-…; unique", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Business process", "End-to-end process", "R2R / P2P / O2C / INV / H2R / TECH", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Sub-process", "Specific activity within the process", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Module(s)", "SAP modules or functional areas used", "FI, CO, MM, SD, HCM, Basis/Security", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Supporting SYS-ID(s)", "Systems the sub-process runs on", "SYS-IDs from W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Related INT-ID(s)", "Interfaces feeding the sub-process", "INT-IDs from W02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Significant FSLI / accounts", "Financial statement areas affected", "From FSLI_Map", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Significance", "Why it matters to the audit", "High / Medium / Low", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "In scope?", "Included in engagement", "Y / N / TBC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Process owner", "Accountable business owner", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Indicative transactions", "Key t-codes / Fiori apps", "Used later for SoD and access testing", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Confirmation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Source-identified / Confirmed / Partially confirmed / Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Scoping note", "Reason for inclusion or open question", "Mandatory if not in scope or Outstanding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Technical_Areas", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "FSLI ID", "Unique key", "FSLI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "FSLI / account", "Significant line item or account", "From audit plan", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Significant?", "Above scoping threshold", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Primary process", "Process that creates the balance", "R2R / P2P / O2C / INV / H2R", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Mapped BP-ID(s)", "Sub-processes that feed the account", "BP-IDs from Process_Scope, comma separated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Assertions", "Relevant assertions", "Existence, Completeness, Accuracy, …", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI_Map", "Check", "Is the account mapped to a process", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 775, "id": "p03-w03-module-business-process-scope-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/01_Practice_Case_Blank/P03_W03_Module_Business_Process_Scope.xlsx", "filename": "P03_W03_Module_Business_Process_Scope.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "P04 W04 Risk Register — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W04 — Risk Register | PRACTICE CASE: CORVANE LITE"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "States, for each in-scope process and technical area, what could go wrong, who owns the risk and how significant it is. A risk is an exposure, never a finding: nothing here says a control has failed."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste the BP-IDs from W03 (BP_Index tab, values only) into the Ref_BP tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Start from the TIB risk library or the client RCM; tailor to each process and technical area."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Write each risk with the formula: There is a risk that … because … resulting in …"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Assign category, assertions, owner and likelihood/impact. Mark SOX-relevant risks."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Agree owners with the business and read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is a PRACTICE copy: the registers are empty. Enter the facts from the Practice Case Brief, then read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every in-scope BP-ID, including technical areas, has at least one risk."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every risk follows the formula, has a category, assertions and a risk owner (or TBC)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "SOX-relevant risks are flagged."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "No risk statement contains an effectiveness conclusion."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 5 (W04)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "RISK-ID", "Unique key", "R-<process>-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "BP-ID", "Process or technical area the risk belongs to", "Must exist in W03 (Ref_BP)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "SYS-ID / INT-ID", "System or interface where the risk arises", "Keys from W01 / W02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk category", "Type of risk; guides control category in W05", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk statement", "What could go wrong", "\"There is a risk that … because … resulting in …\"; never a finding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Assertion(s)", "Financial statement assertions affected", "Occurrence, Completeness, Accuracy, Cut-off, Valuation, Existence, Rights & obligations, Presentation", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk owner", "Accountable for the outcome", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent likelihood", "Before controls", "1 Low / 2 Medium / 3 High", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent impact", "Before controls", "1 Low / 2 Medium / 3 High", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent rating", "Likelihood × impact", "Formula; 1–9", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Rating band", "High ≥ 6, Medium 3–5, Low < 3", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "SOX relevant?", "Could lead to a material misstatement", "Y / N; drives key-control requirement in W05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk source", "Where the risk came from", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Status", "Agreement status", "No \"Failed\" value exists", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Notes", "Limitations or follow-ups", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 471, "id": "p04-w04-risk-register-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/01_Practice_Case_Blank/P04_W04_Risk_Register.xlsx", "filename": "P04_W04_Risk_Register.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "P05 W05 Risk Control Matrix — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W05 — Risk & Control Matrix (RCM) | PRACTICE CASE: CORVANE LITE"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The centre of the foundation. Defines the specific control that answers each risk, classifies it, names its owner, and records the SAP mechanism, expected evidence and test approach. Everything in the Test phase (W10 to W12) is generated from RCM rows."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste RISK-IDs and SOX flags from W04 into the Ref_RISK tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. For each risk, record what actually prevents or detects it. Write each control in the five-part form in Control_Register."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Link controls to risks in Risk_Control_Map (one row per pair; the register is many-to-many)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. For every ITAC, IPE and ITDM control, list the ITGCs it relies on in ITGC_Dependency."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Read Coverage_Check: any SOX risk without a key control is a design gap."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is a PRACTICE copy: the registers are empty. Enter the facts from the Practice Case Brief, then read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every risk in W04 maps to at least one control; every SOX-relevant risk maps to at least one key control."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every control has a five-part description, single category, nature, frequency and owner."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every ITAC, IPE and ITDM control lists its dependent ITGCs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Expected evidence is defined for every control, ready to drive W08."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 6 (W05); Part 3B, Session 33 (reliance)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "CTRL-ID", "Unique key", "C-<category>-nnn, e.g. C-ITGC-AC-002, C-ITAC-004", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control title", "Short name", "e.g. Timely removal of leavers", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control description", "Who does what, when, how, with what evidence", "Five-part description: who / what / when / how / evidence", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control area", "Grouping", "From Control_Areas tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control category", "Classification", "One value only", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Type", "When it acts", "Preventive / Detective", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Nature", "How it operates", "Must agree with category; ITAC = Automated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Frequency", "How often it operates; drives sample size in W10", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Key control?", "Relied on to address a SOX risk", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SOX in scope?", "Requires formal TOD and TOE", "Y if key control for a SOX-relevant risk", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control owner", "Person or role performing the control", "Name / role; carried into W07", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SYS-ID", "Where the control operates", "From W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SAP mechanism", "Where the control lives in SAP", "t-code, config path, table, GRC component", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Dependent ITGCs", "ITGCs this control relies on", "CTRL-ID list; mandatory for ITAC, IPE, ITDM", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "IPE used", "Reports the control relies on", "Each IPE needs completeness & accuracy testing", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Expected evidence", "What the control produces", "Feeds W08 PBC requests", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Test approach", "Planned procedure", "Inquiry / Observation / Inspection / Reperformance; TOD + TOE", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Planned session", "Where it is tested in the course", "Part 3 session reference", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Risks mapped", "Number of risks linked in Risk_Control_Map", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Map ID", "Unique key for the link", "M-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "RISK-ID", "Risk from W04", "Must exist in Ref_RISK", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "CTRL-ID", "Control from Control_Register", "Must exist in Control_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Coverage", "Does the control fully address the risk", "Full / Partial", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Rationale", "Why this control answers this risk", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Risk SOX? (lookup)", "SOX flag of the risk (W04)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Key control? (lookup)", "Key flag of the control", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dep ID", "Unique key", "D-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dependent CTRL-ID", "ITAC / IPE / ITDM control that relies on an ITGC", "Must exist in Control_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dependent category", "Category (lookup)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC CTRL-ID", "ITGC being relied on", "CTRL-ID beginning C-ITGC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC?", "Is the dependency really an ITGC", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC test result", "Result from W10 / W11", "Updated in Part 3 sessions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Additional procedures", "Logs, usage analysis, etc. used to restore reliance", "Free text; see Session 33", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Reliance decision", "Outcome for the dependent control", "Pending until ITGC tested", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 744, "id": "p05-w05-risk-control-matrix-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/01_Practice_Case_Blank/P05_W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx", "filename": "P05_W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "X01 W01 Landscape System Inventory — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W01 — Landscape & System Inventory | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records every SAP system, client and supporting component that holds financial data or processes business transactions, and states whether each is in scope and whether it has actually been validated. W01 is the root of the golden thread: every other workbook points back to a SYS-ID."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Enter the client-provided landscape in System_Inventory with Validation status = Documented only."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Validate directly (System > Status, SCC4, SM51, DB02). Upgrade the status to Validated and add the evidence ref."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Record application servers and hosts in Server_Detail, and the STMS route in Transport_Routes."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Decide scope and record a rationale for every exclusion."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Log every unresolved question in Open_Items. Finish by reading the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every known component is listed with SID, client and environment."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every row has In scope = Y/N with a rationale for N."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every Validated row has an evidence reference; every other row has an open question."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The PRD transport route is recorded."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 2 (W01)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "SYS-ID", "Unique key for the system/client", "SYS-nn; unique; never reused", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "SID", "SAP system identifier", "3 characters; must match System > Status", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Client", "Logical client number", "3 digits; must exist in SCC4", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Environment", "Landscape tier", "DEV / QAS / PRD / SBX / TRN", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Product & release", "Installed SAP product and release", "e.g. S/4HANA 2022 FPS02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Component type", "Technical role of the component", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Hosting model", "Where it runs; drives which ITGCs ABC vs the provider performs", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Database & OS", "Underlying platform", "From DB02 / Basis", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Primary purpose", "Business use the system supports", "One line", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Financially relevant?", "Holds or processes data reaching the financial statements", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "In scope?", "Included in this engagement", "Y / N / TBC; PRD financial system = Y unless justified", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Scope rationale", "Reason for inclusion or exclusion", "Mandatory if In scope = N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "System owner", "Accountable owner (usually Basis/IT lead)", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Validation method", "How the row was confirmed", "System > Status / SCC4 / SM51 / client inventory / interview", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Validation status", "Strength of confirmation", "Validated requires an evidence ref", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Evidence ref", "Screenshot or document supporting the row", "EV-ID or filename; mandatory if Validated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Open question", "Anything still to confirm", "Mirror into Open_Items", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["System_Inventory", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Server ID", "Unique key for the server record", "SRV-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "SYS-ID", "System the server belongs to", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "SID (lookup)", "SID pulled from System_Inventory", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Host name", "Server host name", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Role", "Server role", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "OS", "Operating system and version", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Location / hosting", "Data centre, site or cloud region", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Owner", "Accountable infrastructure owner", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Validated via", "Evidence source", "SM51 / DB02 / inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Evidence ref", "Supporting evidence", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server_Detail", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Route ID", "Unique route reference", "TR-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Landscape", "Landscape / transport group", "e.g. ECC-S4 landscape", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "From SYS-ID", "Source system", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "From env (lookup)", "Environment of source", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "To SYS-ID", "Target system", "Must exist in System_Inventory", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "To env (lookup)", "Environment of target", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Route type", "STMS route type", "Consolidation / Delivery", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Direct DEV→PRD?", "Flags a route that bypasses QAS", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Evidence ref", "STMS route display screenshot", "EV-ID or filename", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Transport_Routes", "Note", "Anything relevant", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Item ID", "Unique open-item reference", "OI-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Source", "Workbook and key the question comes from", "e.g. SYS-05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Question / dependency", "What must still be confirmed", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Owner", "Person who can answer", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Raised", "Date raised", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Target date", "Date needed by", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Status", "Progress", "Open / In progress / Closed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Open_Items", "Resolution", "How it was resolved", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 791, "id": "x01-w01-landscape-system-inventory-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/03_Find_the_Errors/X01_W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx", "filename": "X01_W01_Landscape_System_Inventory.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "X02 W04 Risk Register — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W04 — Risk Register | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "States, for each in-scope process and technical area, what could go wrong, who owns the risk and how significant it is. A risk is an exposure, never a finding: nothing here says a control has failed."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste the BP-IDs from W03 (BP_Index tab, values only) into the Ref_BP tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Start from the TIB risk library or the client RCM; tailor to each process and technical area."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Write each risk with the formula: There is a risk that … because … resulting in …"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Assign category, assertions, owner and likelihood/impact. Mark SOX-relevant risks."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Agree owners with the business and read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every in-scope BP-ID, including technical areas, has at least one risk."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every risk follows the formula, has a category, assertions and a risk owner (or TBC)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "SOX-relevant risks are flagged."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "No risk statement contains an effectiveness conclusion."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 5 (W04)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "RISK-ID", "Unique key", "R-<process>-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "BP-ID", "Process or technical area the risk belongs to", "Must exist in W03 (Ref_BP)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "SYS-ID / INT-ID", "System or interface where the risk arises", "Keys from W01 / W02", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk category", "Type of risk; guides control category in W05", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk statement", "What could go wrong", "\"There is a risk that … because … resulting in …\"; never a finding", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Assertion(s)", "Financial statement assertions affected", "Occurrence, Completeness, Accuracy, Cut-off, Valuation, Existence, Rights & obligations, Presentation", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk owner", "Accountable for the outcome", "Name / role; TBC allowed", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent likelihood", "Before controls", "1 Low / 2 Medium / 3 High", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent impact", "Before controls", "1 Low / 2 Medium / 3 High", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Inherent rating", "Likelihood × impact", "Formula; 1–9", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Rating band", "High ≥ 6, Medium 3–5, Low < 3", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "SOX relevant?", "Could lead to a material misstatement", "Y / N; drives key-control requirement in W05", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Risk source", "Where the risk came from", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Status", "Agreement status", "No \"Failed\" value exists", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Notes", "Limitations or follow-ups", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 466, "id": "x02-w04-risk-register-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/03_Find_the_Errors/X02_W04_Risk_Register.xlsx", "filename": "X02_W04_Risk_Register.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "X03 W05 Risk Control Matrix — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W05 — Risk & Control Matrix (RCM) | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The centre of the foundation. Defines the specific control that answers each risk, classifies it, names its owner, and records the SAP mechanism, expected evidence and test approach. Everything in the Test phase (W10 to W12) is generated from RCM rows."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste RISK-IDs and SOX flags from W04 into the Ref_RISK tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. For each risk, record what actually prevents or detects it. Write each control in the five-part form in Control_Register."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Link controls to risks in Risk_Control_Map (one row per pair; the register is many-to-many)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. For every ITAC, IPE and ITDM control, list the ITGCs it relies on in ITGC_Dependency."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Read Coverage_Check: any SOX risk without a key control is a design gap."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every risk in W04 maps to at least one control; every SOX-relevant risk maps to at least one key control."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every control has a five-part description, single category, nature, frequency and owner."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every ITAC, IPE and ITDM control lists its dependent ITGCs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Expected evidence is defined for every control, ready to drive W08."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 1, Session 6 (W05); Part 3B, Session 33 (reliance)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "CTRL-ID", "Unique key", "C-<category>-nnn, e.g. C-ITGC-AC-002, C-ITAC-004", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control title", "Short name", "e.g. Timely removal of leavers", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control description", "Who does what, when, how, with what evidence", "Five-part description: who / what / when / how / evidence", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control area", "Grouping", "From Control_Areas tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control category", "Classification", "One value only", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Type", "When it acts", "Preventive / Detective", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Nature", "How it operates", "Must agree with category; ITAC = Automated", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Frequency", "How often it operates; drives sample size in W10", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Key control?", "Relied on to address a SOX risk", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SOX in scope?", "Requires formal TOD and TOE", "Y if key control for a SOX-relevant risk", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Control owner", "Person or role performing the control", "Name / role; carried into W07", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SYS-ID", "Where the control operates", "From W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "SAP mechanism", "Where the control lives in SAP", "t-code, config path, table, GRC component", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Dependent ITGCs", "ITGCs this control relies on", "CTRL-ID list; mandatory for ITAC, IPE, ITDM", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "IPE used", "Reports the control relies on", "Each IPE needs completeness & accuracy testing", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Expected evidence", "What the control produces", "Feeds W08 PBC requests", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Test approach", "Planned procedure", "Inquiry / Observation / Inspection / Reperformance; TOD + TOE", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Planned session", "Where it is tested in the course", "Part 3 session reference", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Risks mapped", "Number of risks linked in Risk_Control_Map", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Control_Register", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Map ID", "Unique key for the link", "M-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "RISK-ID", "Risk from W04", "Must exist in Ref_RISK", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "CTRL-ID", "Control from Control_Register", "Must exist in Control_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Coverage", "Does the control fully address the risk", "Full / Partial", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Rationale", "Why this control answers this risk", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Risk SOX? (lookup)", "SOX flag of the risk (W04)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Key control? (lookup)", "Key flag of the control", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk_Control_Map", "Check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dep ID", "Unique key", "D-nn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dependent CTRL-ID", "ITAC / IPE / ITDM control that relies on an ITGC", "Must exist in Control_Register", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Dependent category", "Category (lookup)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC CTRL-ID", "ITGC being relied on", "CTRL-ID beginning C-ITGC", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC?", "Is the dependency really an ITGC", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "ITGC test result", "Result from W10 / W11", "Updated in Part 3 sessions", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Additional procedures", "Logs, usage analysis, etc. used to restore reliance", "Free text; see Session 33", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Reliance decision", "Outcome for the dependent control", "Pending until ITGC tested", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["ITGC_Dependency", "Check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 739, "id": "x03-w05-risk-control-matrix-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/03_Find_the_Errors/X03_W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx", "filename": "X03_W05_Risk_Control_Matrix.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "X04 W09 Evidence Index IPE Log — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W09 — Evidence Index & IPE Log | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The filing system. Every item received gets an EV-ID, its source and extraction details are recorded, the original is preserved, and population evidence is checked for completeness and accuracy before any sample is drawn."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste PBC-ID, period from, period to and evidence type from W08 into Ref_PBC."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Log every item received in Evidence_Index using the filename convention EV-ID_SYS_description_date.ext. Keep the raw original untouched."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. For each population, record the completeness and accuracy work in IPE_Log."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Set the IPE result. No sample may be drawn until the result is Pass."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Back-fill TEST-IDs once W10 exists. Update W08 status with EV-IDs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every received item has an EV-ID, standard filename and preserved original."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every population has an IPE check recorded."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "No sample is drawn from a population with an IPE result other than Pass."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W08 status is updated with EV-IDs."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 10 (W09)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "EV-ID", "Unique key", "EV-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "PBC-ID", "Request it answers", "Must exist in W08 (Ref_PBC)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "PBC period from", "Requested period start (lookup)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "PBC period to", "Requested period end (lookup)", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Filename", "Stored file name", "<EV-ID>_<SYS>_<description>_<date>.ext", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Description", "What the file contains", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Source SYS-ID", "System it was extracted from", "From W01", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Extraction method", "How it was produced", "t-code / report / table / GRC report / ITSM export / document", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Extracted by", "Who ran it", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Extraction date", "When it was run", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Period covered from", "Start date in the file", "Date; must cover PBC period", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Period covered to", "End date in the file", "Date; must cover PBC period", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Record count", "Rows in the population", "Number; used for completeness check", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Received date", "Date logged", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Original preserved?", "Raw copy stored read-only", "Must be Y", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "IPE required?", "Population requests need IPE checks", "Formula from W08 evidence type", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "IPE result", "Outcome of completeness & accuracy check", "Pass required before sampling", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Used by TEST-ID(s)", "Tests relying on the file", "Back-filled from W10", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Evidence_Index", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "IPE-ID", "Unique key", "IPE-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "EV-ID", "Population being tested", "Must exist in Evidence_Index", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Population (lookup)", "Description from Evidence_Index", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Record count (lookup)", "Rows in the file", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Completeness procedure", "How completeness was established", "Observed extraction, parameter screenshot, no filters", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Reconciled to", "Independent source used", "e.g. SUIM total, STMS queue, HR headcount report", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Source count", "Count in the independent source", "Number", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Count difference", "File count minus source count", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Accuracy procedure", "How accuracy was tested", "Records traced both ways", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Records traced", "Number of records traced", "Number", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Parameters evidenced?", "Selection parameters captured", "Y / N", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Tester", "Who performed it", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Test date", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Result", "Outcome", "Pass / Fail / Pending", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["IPE_Log", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 607, "id": "x04-w09-evidence-index-ipe-log-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/03_Find_the_Errors/X04_W09_Evidence_Index_IPE_Log.xlsx", "filename": "X04_W09_Evidence_Index_IPE_Log.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "X05 W10 Test Plan — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W10 — Test Plan / Testing Program | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Converts the RCM into a schedule of tests. Every in-scope key control gets a test of design (TOD) and a test of operating effectiveness (TOE) with attributes, procedure, population, sample size, period, tester, reviewer and dates."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste controls from W05 into Ref_CTRL, and evidence IDs with IPE result and record count from W09 into Ref_EV."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Filter W05 for SOX in scope = Y. Create a TOD and a TOE row for each key control; derive attributes from the control description."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Set sample size from the Sample_Guide (edit the guide to match engagement methodology). Mark ITGC reliance."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Assign testers and reviewers (different people). Schedule ITGC tests before dependent ITAC/IPE tests."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Update Status and Result only after review. Read the Checks tab."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every SOX in-scope key control has a TOD and a TOE row."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every row has attributes derived from the control description."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Sample sizes follow the guide or a justified deviation."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "ITGC tests are scheduled before dependent ITAC/IPE tests."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 2, Session 11 (W10)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "TEST-ID", "Unique key", "<CTRL-ID>-TOD or -TOE", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "CTRL-ID", "Control being tested", "From W05 (Ref_CTRL)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Control title", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Control category", "Lookup; drives workpaper template", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Frequency", "Lookup; drives sample size", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test type", "TOD or TOE", "One TOD and one TOE per key control", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test attributes", "Conditions each sample item must meet", "Lettered A, B, C…; derived from the control description", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test procedure", "What the tester does", "Inquiry / Observation / Inspection / Reperformance; inquiry alone is never enough", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Population EV-ID", "Population sampled from (TOE)", "IPE must be Pass", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "IPE result", "Lookup from W09", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Population size", "Lookup from W09", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Guide sample", "Typical range for the frequency", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Sample size", "Items to test", "Number; justify deviations from the guide", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test period from", "Start of period", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Test period to", "End of period", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "ITGC reliance needed?", "Dependent controls need effective ITGCs", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Tester", "Who performs", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Reviewer", "Who reviews; must differ from tester", "Name", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Planned start", "Schedule", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Planned end", "Schedule", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Workpaper ref", "W11 workpaper", "File name or WP ref", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Status", "Progress", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Result", "Outcome (only after review)", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test_Plan", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 509, "id": "x05-w10-test-plan-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/03_Find_the_Errors/X05_W10_Test_Plan.xlsx", "filename": "X05_W10_Test_Plan.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "X06 W13 Deficiency Remediation Log — Instructions", "sections": [{"title": "Instructions", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC  |  SAP Controls Project Foundation Workbook Set"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "W13 — Deficiency & Remediation Log | Corvane Energy Group (completed)"}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Purpose"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Records every confirmed test exception, evaluates its severity (condition, criteria, cause, effect), links it back through the thread to the risk and process, groups related deficiencies, and tracks remediation and retest."}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "How to use this workbook"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "1. Paste TEST-ID, CTRL-ID, RISK-ID, BP-ID and the W10 result into Ref_TEST."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "2. Validate each exception first: false positive, wrong population or later evidence? Only confirmed exceptions get a DEF-ID."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "3. Write the four-part statement, record compensating controls and dependent controls affected, and set severity."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "4. Aggregate related deficiencies in Groups (same user, date, process or cause) and evaluate the group."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "5. Record management response, owner and due date; add the retest TEST-ID. A deficiency closes only after a successful retest."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Colour legend"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Yellow cells with blue text = input. Grey cells = formulas (do not overwrite). This is the completed Corvane Energy Group project file: every register is populated with the project record."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Completion gate"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Every confirmed exception has a DEF-ID traced to test, control, risk and process."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Severity is evaluated, including impact on dependent controls and compensating controls."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Management response, owner and due date are recorded."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Retest is planned for every remediated deficiency."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Manual reference"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Part 4, Sessions 35–37 (W13)."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Column dictionary"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Tab", "Field", "Definition", "Allowed values / rule", "Type"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "DEF-ID", "Unique key", "DEF-nnn", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "TEST-ID", "Test where it was found", "From W10 (Ref_TEST)", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "CTRL-ID", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "RISK-ID", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "BP-ID", "Lookup", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Deficiency type", "Design or operating", "Design / Operating", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Condition", "What was found (facts, numbers, dates)", "Four-part statement", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Criteria", "What should have happened", "Control description or policy", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Cause", "Why it happened (from inquiry)", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Effect", "What could go wrong; link to risk and assertion", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Exceptions", "Failed items out of sample", "e.g. 3 of 25", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Dependent controls affected", "ITAC / IPE / ITDM relying on this ITGC", "From W05 ITGC_Dependency", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Compensating controls", "Other controls reducing the exposure", "CTRL-ID list", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Compensating tested effective?", "Result of testing the compensating control", "Y / N / Pending", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Severity", "Evaluated level", "Control deficiency / Significant deficiency / Material weakness", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Group ID", "Aggregation group", "From Groups tab", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Management response", "Owner's agreed action", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Remediation owner", "Who fixes it", "Name / role", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Due date", "Remediation due", "Date", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Retest TEST-ID", "Test confirming the fix", "e.g. <CTRL-ID>-RT1", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Status", "Progress", "Dropdown", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Deficiency_Log", "Row check", "Automatic completeness test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Group ID", "Unique key", "GR-x", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Group name", "Short name", "e.g. Privileged access governance", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Common factor", "What links the deficiencies", "Same user, date, process, root cause", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Evaluated exposure", "Combined exposure after compensating controls and follow-up", "Free text", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Group severity", "Severity of the group", "Reviewer approved", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Rationale", "Why this severity", "Magnitude, likelihood, compensating controls, reliance", "Input"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "DEFs in group", "Count", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Highest individual severity", "Highest rating among its DEFs", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Groups", "Check", "Automatic test", "Formula"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Version history"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "0.1 — October 2026 — initial template (TIB Systems LLC)"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Cross-workbook links: keys from other workbooks are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs, so this file works on its own and never breaks when files are moved."}]}], "word_count": 567, "id": "x06-w13-deficiency-remediation-log-instructions", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/03_Find_the_Errors/X06_W13_Deficiency_Remediation_Log.xlsx", "filename": "X06_W13_Deficiency_Remediation_Log.xlsx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "workbook_instructions", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Practice Case Brief: Corvane Lite"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Hands-on practice for the prerequisite course \"Using the Foundation Workbooks\""}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Practice package  |  Fill in W01 to W05 from the case facts, then find the errors in six workbooks  |  Version 0.1 (Draft)  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Corvane Lite is a small slice of the fictitious Corvane Energy Group case. All names and figures are invented."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Right-click the table above and choose Update Field if page numbers do not appear."}]}, {"title": "1. How to Use This Package", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "1. How to Use This Package"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "The package has three folders. Work through them in order after you finish the course manual (Parts 1 to 3). Allow about four hours in all."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Folder", "What is in it", "What you do"], ["01_Practice_Case_Blank", "P01 to P05: empty copies of W01 to W05 for the Corvane Lite case", "Enter the case facts from this brief; paste keys between the files; read the Checks tabs"], ["02_Practice_Case_Answer_Key", "The same five workbooks, filled in", "Compare with your own work after Task 5; do not open before"], ["03_Find_the_Errors", "X01 to X06: six Corvane workbooks with 18 errors seeded; Error_Log_Template; the answer key", "Task 7: find the errors and log them"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["GROUND RULES Work in copies. Type exactly what the tables show; keys must match. Paste values only into Ref_ tabs. A blank workbook can read ALL CHECKS PASS because there is nothing to fail, so enter data first and then judge the Checks tab."]]}]}, {"title": "2. The Case in One Page", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "2. The Case in One Page"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Corvane Energy Group runs one production S/4HANA system for all four company codes. The project lead has asked you to record a small slice of the foundation: the vendor payment and treasury processes and the access control that protects them. The slice has six systems, two bank interfaces, four processes (one of them technical), two financial statement accounts, five risks and five controls. The process owners are the Head of AP, the Treasury Manager and the IT Security Manager."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Enter the facts in the order of the tasks. Where a table has a \"Notes\" column, the note is part of the record. Dates are 2026."}]}, {"title": "3. The Case Facts", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3. The Case Facts"}]}, {"title": "3.1 Systems (for W01 System_Inventory)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.1 Systems (for W01 System_Inventory)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["SYS-ID", "SID / client", "Env", "Product", "Component", "Hosting", "Database and OS", "Purpose", "Fin. relevant / In scope", "Owner", "Validation method; status; evidence"], ["SYS-01", "CED / 100", "DEV", "S/4HANA 2023 FPS01", "ABAP back end", "On-premise", "HANA 2.0 SPS07 / SLES 15", "Development client: configuration and custom code; origin of all transports", "N / Y", "Basis Lead", "System > Status; SCC4; STMS route; Validated; EV-001"], ["SYS-02", "CEQ / 200", "QAS", "S/4HANA 2023 FPS01", "ABAP back end", "On-premise", "HANA 2.0 SPS07 / SLES 15", "Quality client: testing and business sign-off before production", "N / Y", "Basis Lead", "System > Status; SCC4; Validated; EV-002"], ["SYS-03", "CEP / 300", "PRD", "S/4HANA 2023 FPS01", "ABAP back end", "On-premise", "HANA 2.0 SPS07 / SLES 15", "Primary financial system: FI/CO, MM, SD, PM, PS, JVA and HCM posting for all four company codes", "Y / Y", "Head of IT Applications", "System > Status; SCC4; SM51; SE06; Validated; EV-003"], ["SYS-04", "GRP / 001", "PRD", "GRC Access Control 12.0 SP22", "GRC", "On-premise", "HANA 2.0 SPS07 / SLES 15", "Access requests, SoD risk analysis, Firefighter (EAM) and user access review", "Y / Y", "IT Security Manager", "System > Status; GRC administration screens; Validated; EV-004"], ["SYS-06", "HDP / n/a", "PRD", "SAP HANA 2.0 SPS07", "HANA DB", "On-premise", "SLES 15", "Database for CEP; database-level privileged users can bypass SAP security", "Y / Y", "Basis Lead / DBA", "HANA Cockpit user and role list; Validated; EV-006"], ["SYS-10", "CES / 400", "SBX", "S/4HANA 2023 FPS01 (sandbox)", "ABAP back end", "On-premise", "HANA 2.0 SPS07 / SLES 15", "Sandbox for experimentation; no production data", "N / N. Rationale: Sandbox: no financial data; STMS route display shows no transport path to CEQ or CEP", "Basis Lead", "STMS route display; SCC4; Validated; EV-010"]]}]}, {"title": "3.2 Servers, transport routes and an open item (W01)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.2 Servers, transport routes and an open item (W01)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Server ID", "SYS-ID", "Host", "Role", "OS", "Location", "Owner", "Validated via", "Evidence", "Note"], ["SRV-01", "SYS-03", "cep-app01", "Application server", "SLES 15", "Houston DC1", "Basis Lead", "SM51", "EV-003", "Dialog instance"], ["SRV-02", "SYS-03", "cep-app02", "Application server", "SLES 15", "Houston DC1", "Basis Lead", "SM51", "EV-003", "Dialog instance"], ["SRV-06", "SYS-06", "cep-hdb01", "Database host", "SLES 15", "Houston DC1", "Basis Lead / DBA", "HANA Cockpit", "EV-006", "Primary database"], ["SRV-08", "SYS-04", "grp-app01", "Application server", "SLES 15", "Houston DC1", "IT Security Manager", "SM51", "EV-004", ""], ["SRV-13", "SYS-01", "ced-app01", "Application server", "SLES 15", "Houston DC1", "Basis Lead", "SM51", "EV-001", ""], ["SRV-14", "SYS-02", "ceq-app01", "Application server", "SLES 15", "Houston DC1", "Basis Lead", "SM51", "EV-002", ""]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Route ID", "Landscape", "From", "To", "Route type", "Evidence", "Note"], ["TR-01", "S/4HANA core (CED to CEQ to CEP)", "SYS-01", "SYS-02", "Consolidation", "EV-012", "DEV to QAS; no other route leaves DEV"], ["TR-02", "S/4HANA core (CED to CEQ to CEP)", "SYS-02", "SYS-03", "Delivery", "EV-012", "QAS to PRD; the only path to production"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Source", "Question", "Owner", "Raised", "Target", "Status", "Resolution"], ["OI-02", "SYS-10", "Is there any transport route from the sandbox to QAS or PRD?", "Basis Lead", "14 Jan 2026", "28 Jan 2026", "Closed", "None: STMS route display (EV-010); sandbox excluded"]]}]}, {"title": "3.3 Interfaces, RFC users and data flows (W02)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.3 Interfaces, RFC users and data flows (W02)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["INT-ID", "Name", "Source", "Target", "Direction", "Technology", "Data object", "Process", "Frequency", "Fin. rel.", "Monitoring", "Reconc.", "Owner", "Technical user", "Scope", "Evidence", "Note"], ["INT-01", "Bank statements inbound", "Banks (host-to-host)", "SYS-03", "Inbound", "CPI", "MT940 / camt.053 statements", "BP-TRE-01", "Daily, about 1,800 lines", "Y", "CPI monitor", "Y", "Treasury Manager", "CPI_BANK_SVC", "Y", "EV-013", ""], ["INT-02", "Vendor payment files outbound", "SYS-03", "Banks (host-to-host)", "Outbound", "CPI", "pain.001 payment instructions", "BP-P2P-03", "Three payment runs per week", "Y", "Other documented", "N", "Treasury Manager", "CPI_PAY_SVC", "Y", "EV-014", "At scoping: no alert on file change and no bank-confirmation reconciliation; carried to W04 R-P2P-006"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RFC ID", "INT-ID", "Destination", "Technical user", "User type", "Authorisation", "Stored credentials", "Note"], ["RFC-04", "INT-01", "CPI_BANK", "CPI_BANK_SVC", "Communication", "Bank statement posting role (narrow)", "Y", ""], ["RFC-05", "INT-02", "CPI_PAY", "CPI_PAY_SVC", "Communication", "Payment file read role (narrow)", "Y", ""]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Flow ID", "Process", "Description", "Source", "Target", "INT-ID", "Note"], ["FL-01", "BP-TRE-01", "Bank statements into cash accounts", "Banks", "SYS-03", "INT-01", ""], ["FL-02", "BP-P2P-03", "Payment instructions to banks after F110", "SYS-03", "Banks", "INT-02", ""]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Source", "Question", "Owner", "Raised", "Target", "Status", "Resolution"], ["OI-02", "INT-02", "Who can write to the payment file directory after creation?", "Basis Lead", "22 Jan 2026", "3 Feb 2026", "Closed", "Four AP users had write access at scoping; carried to R-P2P-006"]]}]}, {"title": "3.4 Processes, technical area and accounts (W03)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.4 Processes, technical area and accounts (W03)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["BP-ID", "Process", "Sub-process", "Modules", "Systems", "Interfaces", "Accounts", "Signif.", "Scope", "Owner", "Transactions", "Status"], ["BP-P2P-01", "P2P", "Vendor master maintenance", "MM, FI", "SYS-03", "", "Trade payables", "High", "Y", "Head of AP", "BP, XK01, XK02, MK01", "Confirmed"], ["BP-P2P-03", "P2P", "Vendor payment", "FI", "SYS-03", "INT-02", "Cash; trade payables", "High", "Y", "Treasury Manager", "F110, FBZP, F111", "Confirmed"], ["BP-TRE-01", "TRE", "Bank and treasury: statements and bank reconciliation", "FI-BL", "SYS-03", "INT-01", "Cash and cash equivalents", "High", "Y", "Treasury Manager", "FF_5, FEBAN, FF67", "Confirmed"], ["BP-TECH-01", "TECH", "User administration and access", "Basis/Security", "SYS-03, SYS-04, SYS-06", "INT-07", "All financial accounts", "High", "Y", "IT Security Manager", "SU01, PFCG, SUIM, GRC ARM / ARA / EAM", "Confirmed"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "BP-TECH-01 goes on the Technical_Areas tab; the other three go on Process_Scope."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["FSLI ID", "Account", "Significant?", "Primary process", "Mapped BP-IDs", "Assertions"], ["FSLI-01", "Cash and cash equivalents", "Y", "TRE", "BP-TRE-01, BP-P2P-03", "Existence, Completeness"], ["FSLI-07", "Trade payables and accruals", "Y", "P2P", "BP-P2P-01, BP-P2P-03", "Completeness, Occurrence"]]}]}, {"title": "3.5 Risks (W04)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.5 Risks (W04)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["RISK-ID", "BP-ID", "Sys / INT", "Category", "Risk statement", "Assertions", "Owner", "L", "I", "SOX", "Source", "Status"], ["R-P2P-001", "BP-P2P-01", "SYS-03", "SoD", "There is a risk that a fictitious vendor is created and paid because one user can both maintain vendor master data and execute the payment run, resulting in fraudulent payments and overstated expenses.", "Occurrence", "Head of AP", "3", "3", "Y", "Client RCM", "Agreed with owner"], ["R-P2P-002", "BP-P2P-01", "SYS-03", "Business process", "There is a risk that vendor bank details are changed to divert payments because bank-detail changes are not independently verified, resulting in misappropriation of funds and overstated payables or expenses.", "Occurrence", "Head of AP", "3", "3", "Y", "TIB risk library", "Agreed with owner"], ["R-P2P-006", "BP-P2P-03", "INT-02", "Interface", "There is a risk that the payment file is altered between creation and bank transmission because file directory permissions and file integrity are not controlled, resulting in payments to unauthorised accounts.", "Occurrence", "Treasury Manager", "2", "3", "Y", "Walkthrough", "Agreed with owner"], ["R-TRE-001", "BP-TRE-01", "INT-01", "Interface", "There is a risk that bank transactions are missing from the ledger because bank statement loads fail without detection, resulting in misstated cash and unrecorded receipts or payments.", "Completeness, Existence", "Treasury Manager", "2", "3", "Y", "TIB risk library", "Agreed with owner"], ["R-TECH-001", "BP-TECH-01", "SYS-03", "ITGC-APD", "There is a risk that leavers retain SAP access because accounts are not locked promptly after termination, resulting in unauthorised transactions.", "Occurrence", "IT Security Manager", "3", "3", "Y", "Prior-year audit issue", "Agreed with owner"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Notes: R-P2P-006 was raised from the payment-file open item in W02. The inherent rating and band are formulas: do not type them."}]}, {"title": "3.6 Controls, links and dependencies (W05)", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "3.6 Controls, links and dependencies (W05)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CTRL-ID", "Title", "Area", "Category", "Type", "Nature", "Frequency", "Key", "Owner", "System", "SAP mechanism", "Dependent ITGCs", "Expected evidence"], ["C-ITGC-AC-002", "Timely removal of leavers", "Termination", "ITGC-APD", "Preventive", "Manual", "Event-driven", "Y", "IT Security Manager", "SYS-03", "HR leavers report; SU01; USR02; RSUSR100N", "", "HR leavers report, lock dates, SU01 change history"], ["C-ITGC-SOD-001", "SoD analysis at provisioning and monthly", "Segregation of duties", "ITGC-APD", "Preventive", "IT-dependent manual", "Event-driven", "Y", "IT Security Manager", "SYS-04", "GRC ARA user-level and permission-level analysis; mitigation master data", "", "ARA output, mitigation assignments, approved ruleset version"], ["C-BPC-P2P-002", "Vendor bank change call-back verification", "Business process control", "Manual BPC", "Preventive", "Manual", "Event-driven", "Y", "Head of AP", "SYS-03", "Call-back log; vendor change request form", "", "Call-back log, signed change request"], ["C-BPC-P2P-003", "Payment file integrity and reconciliation", "Business process control", "ITDM", "Detective", "IT-dependent manual", "Event-driven", "Y", "Treasury Manager", "SYS-03", "F110 log; CPI hash alert; AL11 and OS permissions", "C-ITGC-CO-002, C-ITGC-AC-003, C-ITGC-PC-001", "Hash alert, reconciliation, directory permission listing"], ["C-BPC-TRE-001", "Bank statement load monitoring", "Business process control", "ITDM", "Detective", "IT-dependent manual", "Daily", "Y", "Treasury Manager", "SYS-03", "FF_5 log; FEBAN", "C-ITGC-CO-002", "Daily load checklist"]]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "Control descriptions (the Control description column)"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["CTRL-ID", "Description"], ["C-ITGC-AC-002", "The IT Security Administrator (who) locks and end-dates the SAP user of every leaver (what) within two business days of the HR termination date (when), using the daily HR leavers report and SU01 or GRC (how). Evidence is the leavers report annotated with lock dates and the SU01 change history (evidence)."], ["C-ITGC-SOD-001", "The GRC administrator (who) runs access risk analysis at every provisioning request and monthly for all users against the approved ruleset (what), so conflicts are removed or assigned an approved, tested mitigating control (when and how, in GRC ARA). Evidence is the risk analysis output and the mitigation assignments (evidence)."], ["C-BPC-P2P-002", "The AP Analyst (who) verifies every vendor bank-detail change by calling the vendor on an independently sourced number before the change is saved (what) for every change (when) using the call-back procedure and log (how). Evidence is the call-back log with approver (evidence)."], ["C-BPC-P2P-003", "Treasury (who) confirms for every payment run that the file hash received by the bank equals the F110 file, restricts write access to the file directory, and reconciles the bank confirmation total to the F110 total (what) at every payment run (when) using the CPI file-hash alert and the bank confirmation (how). File-hash alert introduced during remediation in August 2026. Evidence is the hash alert, reconciliation and directory permission listing (evidence)."], ["C-BPC-TRE-001", "The Treasury Analyst (who) confirms that every bank statement expected for the day was loaded in FF_5 and chases any missing statement (what) every day (when) using the statement load log and the bank list (how). Evidence is the daily load checklist (evidence)."]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Map ID", "RISK-ID", "CTRL-ID", "Coverage", "Rationale"], ["M-01", "R-P2P-001", "C-ITGC-SOD-001", "Full", "Removes or mitigates create-and-pay conflict"], ["M-02", "R-P2P-001", "C-BPC-P2P-002", "Partial", "Verifies bank changes"], ["M-03", "R-P2P-002", "C-BPC-P2P-002", "Full", "Call-back verification"], ["M-04", "R-P2P-006", "C-BPC-P2P-003", "Full", "File hash and reconciliation"], ["M-05", "R-TRE-001", "C-BPC-TRE-001", "Full", "Daily load check"], ["M-06", "R-TECH-001", "C-ITGC-AC-002", "Full", "Timely lock"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dep ID", "Dependent control", "ITGC", "ITGC result", "Additional procedures", "Reliance decision"], ["D-01", "C-BPC-P2P-003", "C-ITGC-CO-002", "Exception noted", "No reliance for Jan to May 2026: all vendor bank changes and payment runs examined substantively; controls remediated Aug 2026 and effective from retest", "Not supported"], ["D-02", "C-BPC-P2P-003", "C-ITGC-AC-003", "Effective", "", "Supported"], ["D-03", "C-BPC-P2P-003", "C-ITGC-PC-001", "Exception noted", "Full-period transport population compared to approved tickets; transports touching ITAC tables inspected individually", "Supported with additional procedures"], ["D-04", "C-BPC-TRE-001", "C-ITGC-CO-002", "Exception noted", "Aged interface errors and reconciliations inspected for the period; unposted items valued and posted", "Supported with additional procedures"]]}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Control_Areas list: Configuration (ITAC), Business process control, Termination, Segregation of duties. Test approach: Inquiry and inspection of review evidence; TOD + TOE for the business-process controls, and Inspection and reperformance; TOD + TOE for the access and SoD controls. Planned session: any session reference."}]}, {"title": "4. The Tasks", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "4. The Tasks"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Task", "Workbook", "What to do", "Target result"], ["1", "P01 (W01)", "Enter the six systems, six servers, two transport routes and the open item. Use the dropdowns. Read the Row check column and the Checks tab", "All row checks OK; Checks tab ALL CHECKS PASS"], ["2", "P02 (W02)", "Paste the six SYS-IDs from P01 into Ref_SYS (values only, A2). Enter the two interfaces, two RFC users, two data flows and the open item", "All row checks OK; the Risk flag for INT-02 reads \"Carry to W04 as risk\"; ALL CHECKS PASS"], ["3", "P03 (W03)", "Enter three processes on Process_Scope, BP-TECH-01 on Technical_Areas, and the two accounts. Check BP_Index lists four keys", "ALL CHECKS PASS"], ["4", "P04 (W04)", "Paste the four BP-IDs from P03 into Ref_BP (business keys first, then the technical key). Enter the five risks", "All row checks OK; Coverage_By_BP shows at least one risk for each process; ALL CHECKS PASS"], ["5", "P05 (W05)", "Paste RISK-ID and SOX flag from P04 into Ref_RISK. Enter the five controls, the six links and the four dependencies", "Coverage_Check shows every SOX risk with a key control; ALL CHECKS PASS"], ["6", "All", "Open the answer-key workbooks and compare each of your registers. Note every difference and say whether it matters", "You can explain each difference"], ["7", "X01 to X06", "Find the 18 seeded errors in the Find_the_Errors folder and record them in Error_Log_Template.xlsx", "At least 15 of 18 found"]]}]}, {"title": "Task 7: Find the errors", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Task 7: Find the errors"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Six Corvane workbooks (X01 to X06) have had errors seeded into them. Fifteen of the errors make a check show a message; three are silent and can only be found by reading the data and thinking about whether it makes sense. For each error, log the workbook, tab, key, what is wrong, the message shown (if any), how you found it, and the fix."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Workbook", "Errors hidden", "Hint"], ["X01 (W01)", "2", "One row claims more than its evidence shows; one key is not what it should be"], ["X02 (W04)", "4", "Look at statements, keys and owners; one error is invisible to the checks"], ["X03 (W05)", "4", "A short description, a missing dependency, a broken link, and one silent error"], ["X04 (W09)", "2", "A file name and a result in the evidence index"], ["X05 (W10)", "3", "People, sample size and status"], ["X06 (W13)", "3", "Closure, group rating and one silent error"]]}]}, {"title": "Marking", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Marking"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Result", "Score"], ["Each error found, logged with the right key and a fix", "1 point"], ["18 of 18", "Excellent"], ["15 to 17", "Pass"], ["Fewer than 15", "Repeat the relevant course sessions"]]}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["A WORD ON THE SILENT ERRORS Checks measure completeness, not correctness. A workbook can read OK on every row and still carry a wrong owner, a wrong frequency or a wrong group. Reading for sense is part of the job, not an extra."]]}]}], "word_count": 435, "id": "practice-case-brief-corvane-lite", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/08_Practice_Workbooks/Practice_Case_Brief_Corvane_Lite.docx", "filename": "Practice_Case_Brief_Corvane_Lite.docx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "document", "printable": true}, {"title": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC", "sections": [{"title": "Start", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 3, "text": "TIB SYSTEMS LLC"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Corvane Energy Group"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "SAP Controls Project: Completed Case File"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "README and navigation guide  |  Training package for a new controls lead  |  Version 0.1  |  October 2026"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Corvane Energy Group is a fictitious integrated oil and gas company. All people, figures and events are invented and built to be internally consistent across the 14 workbooks, the story and the playbook."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "© TIB Systems LLC. Atlanta, GA | Hyderabad, India | RAK, UAE. For training use."}]}, {"title": "1. What This Package Is", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "1. What This Package Is"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "A complete, worked example of a SOX-focused SAP controls project, from the landscape to the conclusion, for an oil and gas company running S/4HANA across four company codes. It contains the 14 foundation workbooks filled in end to end, the story of how the project was run, a playbook for leading the team and the stakeholders, and the reference manuals. The headline result: 30 deficiencies were logged in the baseline cycle; 21 were closed after a successful retest and 9 remain open, a 70 percent reduction. Both significant-deficiency groups were closed and there is no material weakness."}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Use it to train a new controls lead who is about to run a similar project with a team of 15 and a set of senior stakeholders."}]}, {"title": "2. Folder Map", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "2. Folder Map"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Folder", "Contents", "Start here if you want to"], ["01_Environment", "W01 Landscape and System Inventory; W02 Interfaces and Data Flows; W03 Modules and Business Process Scope", "See how the scope was built from facts"], ["02_Risk_and_Control_Design", "W04 Risk Register; W05 Risk and Control Matrix; W06 SoD and Sensitive Access; W07 Control and Evidence Owners", "Understand risk to control to owner"], ["03_Evidence", "W08 PBC Evidence Requests; W09 Evidence Index and IPE Log", "See how evidence was requested and proved complete"], ["04_Testing", "W10 Test Plan (with retests); W11 Test Workpapers (six worked examples); W12 Sample Selection Log", "See exactly how tests were planned, sampled and performed"], ["05_Conclusion", "W13 Deficiency and Remediation Log; W14 Scoping Workpaper and Coverage Dashboard", "See how findings became a conclusion"], ["06_Reference_Manuals", "Manual Parts 1 to 4 (including 3A and 3B); Deployment Guide; Video Outline; Blank_Workbook_Templates (the 14 empty templates)", "Learn the method or start a new engagement"], ["08_Practice_Workbooks", "Practice_Case_Brief_Corvane_Lite; blank practice workbooks P01 to P05 and their answer keys; six workbooks with 18 seeded errors (X01 to X06), an error log template and its answer key", "Practise the workbooks hands-on after the course manual"], ["07_Project_Story_and_Lead_Playbook", "The Corvane Story (the Controls Lead's narrative); The Controls Lead Playbook; the Weekly Activity Plan (287 discrete activities grouped by week, 12 Jan to 30 Sep 2026)", "Learn how to lead the team and the stakeholders, and see what happens each week"]]}]}, {"title": "3. Suggested Study Path for a New Lead", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "3. Suggested Study Path for a New Lead"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Step", "Read or open", "Time (about)", "What to take away"], ["1", "The Corvane Story, chapters 1 to 4", "1.5 hours", "How the lead listened, built the team, and scoped from facts"], ["2", "Manual Part 1 and W01 to W05", "3 hours", "The golden thread; how risks become controls"], ["3", "Manual Part 2 and W06 to W09", "3 hours", "SoD rules, owners, evidence requests and population checks"], ["4", "Manual Part 3A, 3B and W10 to W12; the six worked workpapers in W11", "4 hours", "How each test is planned, sampled and performed"], ["5", "Manual Part 4, W13 and W14; Story chapters 8 to 12", "3 hours", "Evaluating, grouping, remediating and reporting"], ["6", "The Controls Lead Playbook", "2 hours", "Team design, stakeholder management and your first 90 days"], ["7", "Trace exercise (Manual Part 4, Session 39) using the Corvane workbooks", "2 hours", "Follow any deficiency back to a server, and any server forward to a conclusion"]]}]}, {"title": "4. How the Workbooks Connect", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "4. How the Workbooks Connect"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "Each workbook has a unique key and the next workbook refers to it. The thread is: system, interface, process, risk, control, owner, evidence request, evidence item, test, sample, deficiency, conclusion. The keys are pasted as values into the Ref_ tabs of the next workbook (see Deployment Guide section 4), so every file stands alone. In this package every Ref_ tab matches its source workbook exactly."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Key chain", "Example from the Corvane case"], ["SYS-03 (CEP / 300, production)", "The primary financial system"], ["INT-02 (payment file to banks)", "Four AP users could write to the file directory"], ["BP-P2P-03 (vendor payment)", "Process and owner: Treasury Manager"], ["R-P2P-006", "Payment file altered between creation and bank"], ["C-BPC-P2P-003", "Payment file integrity and reconciliation"], ["PBC and EV items; IPE check", "Evidence requested, indexed and proven complete"], ["C-BPC-P2P-003-TOD", "Design walkthrough found a gap"], ["DEF-007 (group GR-B)", "Design deficiency; closed after retest C-BPC-P2P-003-RT1"]]}]}, {"title": "5. Key Numbers", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "5. Key Numbers"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Item", "Count"], ["Systems / interfaces", "11 systems (9 in scope) / 11 interfaces"], ["Processes", "14 business processes plus 4 technical areas"], ["Risks / controls", "36 risks (32 SOX-relevant) / 47 controls (38 key)"], ["General-control dependency links", "61 (11 supported, 49 supported with additional procedures, 1 not relied on)"], ["SoD functions / rules", "20 / 16"], ["Evidence requests / items", "95 / 109; 45 population checks, all Pass"], ["Tests", "76 baseline plus 28 retests (19 completed, 9 planned)"], ["Samples / items", "37 samples (25 baseline, 12 retest) / 601 items"], ["Deficiencies", "30 logged; 21 closed; 9 open; 7 groups"], ["Conclusion", "No material weakness; no significant deficiency remaining; control deficiencies identified"]]}]}, {"title": "6. Status of the Files", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "6. Status of the Files"}, {"type": "p", "level": 0, "text": "All 14 workbooks were recalculated and checked: every Checks tab reads ALL CHECKS PASS, there are no formula errors, and the Ref_ tabs agree with their source workbooks (risks, controls, tests, evidence, requests, deficiencies, processes and systems). No placeholder text from the blank templates remains in the Corvane files."}]}, {"title": "Points to know", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Points to know"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The nine open deficiencies are deliberate. They need further operating cycles or an event (an emergency change) before a retest is meaningful. Their owners and due dates (27 November to 18 December 2026) are in W13, and the planned retests are in W10."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Dates and counts depend on the as-of date of 30 September 2026. Some formulas use today's date (days overdue), so these figures change as time passes."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "The six worked workpapers in W11 (leavers, transports, Firefighter sessions, vendor call-backs, interface monitoring and the three-way match) carry real sample items from W12; other tests are logged in the W11 register with their results and deficiency links."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Everything is fictitious. Names of people, systems, figures and companies are invented for training. Do not rely on any figure as a benchmark."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Method versus case. The blank templates and manuals (folder 06) are generic. The Corvane files are one worked example, not a standard."}]}, {"title": "7. How to Reuse the Package", "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "level": 1, "text": "7. How to Reuse the Package"}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Run a training lab: give trainees the blank templates and the Corvane story and ask them to rebuild W01 to W05 from the case facts, then compare to the completed files."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Run the trace exercise: pick any deficiency in W13 and trace it back through W11, W10, W09, W08, W05, W04 and W01."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Start a real engagement: copy Blank_Workbook_Templates into a new engagement folder and follow the Deployment Guide."}, {"type": "number", "level": 0, "text": "Brief a new lead: read the Story and Playbook, then work through the first 30, 60 and 90 days plan."}]}], "word_count": 595, "id": "readme-corvane-case-file", "source_path": "PM_to_Controls_Lead_Course/07_Corvane_Case_File/Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project.zip::Corvane_Energy_SAP_Controls_Project/README_Corvane_Case_File.docx", "filename": "README_Corvane_Case_File.docx", "category": "Case Library", "kind": "document", "printable": true}]}